window.LT_INDEX=[{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"Not a tool: no interface, no pipeline, no model. ADTLib is the code the twenty modules import, and almost every one of its files exists because the same function had been copied between two and fifteen times. Each module header records how many copies it replaced and what the copies had started to disagree about: that count is the point of this page. Note This page describes ADT/ADTLib on upstream/main , and it is the only page of this atlas that documents code no user ever opens. It is here because module behaviour now depends on it: a reader chasing what AREG does with a surface ends up in…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#situation","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"1. Where it sits","x":"Under everything, and inside no pipeline. ADTLib is declared first in the extension's CMakeLists.txt , \"Shared code, declared before the modules that import it\" , and the twenty tool directories import from it. It has no Process of its own, nothing to run, and nothing to download. Its own measure of itself, from the module headers: twenty-one modules, about 3 300 lines, replacing copies that together ran to several thousand . The header of theming.py alone accounts for 1 926 lines of duplicate, and env/conda.py for 656 across forty functions."},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#import","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"2. How a module reaches it","x":"Slicer puts each extension's qt-scripted-modules directory on sys.path , so a plain import ADTLib works from any module of the extension and from a scripted CLI. A third route matters more than it looks: once the modules publish Slicer's module search paths into the shapeaxi Conda environment, ADTLib is importable there too . That is what ADTLib.env.install_pytorch relies on: it is never imported by a widget, it is run as python -m ADTLib.env.install_pytorch by the Conda interpreter. Pitfall ADTLib/__init__.py and io/__init__.py must stay free of imports. They run in the lean Conda…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#contrat","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"3. The shared contract","x":"Three modules carry what the tools promise each other, rather than what they compute. Module What it replaced What it settles method.py six Method(ABC) declarations, 934 lines mostly contract rather than code a core ( Process , TestProcess , NumberScan , search ) plus three mixins, landmarks, checkboxes, DICOM, because the six did not overlap at random: measured side by side, three clusters stood out sharply requests.py nothing; it names what was never written down 473 kwargs[\"...\"] accesses with no list anywhere. Each family now declares its fields, an unknown key is rejected at…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#environnement","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"4. The environment","x":"Four modules, and the only part of ADTLib that reaches outside the process. Module What it replaced env/conda.py six copies of each function, forty functions, 656 lines , drifted into three shapes, one still holding an unreachable return in check_lib_wsl that read two names nothing had bound env/deps.py nine copies of check_lib_installed under three incompatible answers to the same question : AREG and FlexReg wrote if required_version: pass , true as soon as the library was installed at all, the constraint was decoration env/cuda.py the architecture-list problem: a torch wheel carries…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#io","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"5. Reading, writing, naming","x":"Module Copies replaced What was decided io/fs.py search in fifteen copies, six variants six were a search(self, …) method and three the same thing as a free function: identical byte for byte once self is removed. The only two substantive differences became parameters io/landmarks.py LoadJsonLandmarks ×3 strictly identical, WriteJson ×2 the variants that genuinely diverge, ASO_CBCT's, ALI_CBCT's, ALI_IOS's, stay where they are io/surface.py ReadSurf ×5, WriteSurf ×4 unlike the rest, these had diverged ; the differences were measured before one was kept labels.py GetLabelSurface and…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#reste","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"6. The rest","x":"Module What it carries logging_setup.py 151 files carried the same ten lines , each setting propagate = False and attaching its own StreamHandler, which is exactly what made the extension's logging impossible to steer: raising a level or sending a run to a file meant editing all of them theming.py eleven copies of the recursive pass and nine of applyDarkModeStyles , 1 926 lines , already drifting: an inert !important here, and one copy missing the hasattr guard that keeps the recursion off a parent widget testdata.py seven copies of DownloadUnzip with three distinct ways of being wrong, all…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#tests","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"7. What the tests pin down","x":"ADTLib ships 22 tests in ADT/Testing/Python/ , up from two. They are the closest thing to a specification this shared layer has, and several were written because grepping was not enough. test_pip_requirements.py says why in its own header: every (library, version) pair the repository hands to pip must parse, because f'{lib}=={version}' is correct only while every entry carries a bare version, and three modules had quietly gained a constraint with an operator, producing dicom2nifti==>=2.6.2 , which pip refuses outright. The defect is invisible on a machine that already has the library , so no…"},{"u":"ADTLib/ADTLib.html#lire","p":"ADTLib","t":"ADTLib","c":"","s":"8. Reading this layer","x":"Two habits make it legible. First, every module header states what it replaced and what the copies disagreed about : that is where the reasoning lives, not in this page. Second, the line that recurs across those headers is what was left out : the variants that genuinely diverge stay in their tool. TestScan and TestModel are not in method.py because their arities plainly differ from one tool to the next, up to four arguments in AREG, and getModelUrl returns a per-tool selection. Lifting them would have meant inventing a contract none of the six actually honoured."},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"ALI does not compute anything itself: it is a dispatch widget that chooses between two backends with no shared code, ALI_CBCT ( reinforcement-learning agents that move through a volume) and ALI_IOS (multi-view rendering and per-pixel segmentation on a mesh), to place anatomical landmarks in LPS . Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, is described in ALI on VISOR . Notes added to the callouts say whether each finding still holds in VISOR; they cite…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#situation","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"ALI is a landmark provider for almost the whole repository. Nobody calls the widget itself; both CLIs are called, though. Caller Backend How Why ALI.py:999 ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.ali_cbct, …) direct use ALI.py:1246 ALI_IOS conda run -n shapeaxi python -m ALI_IOS direct use ASO_Method/CBCT.py:515 ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run landmarks for SEMI_ASO orientation AREG_Method/IOSCBCT.py:300 both slicer.cli.run 12 occlusal points on CBCT and on IOS, to register one onto the other GreedyReg_Method/Logic.py:495 ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run \"Distant Registration\": landmark-based registration…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#fichiers","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Note · Legacy only Part of this module now lives in the shared layer. ADTLib centralises what several modules carried in copies: from here, geometry.py , io/landmarks.py , testdata.py and the contract of method.py . Its page records how many copies each file replaced. Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR : the server tools do not import ADTLib. What they share is the small sadt-ali-common package ( SADT-VISOR tools/ALI/common/src/sadt_ali_common/ : discovery.py , markups.py ). File Role Where it runs ALI/ALI.py widget, 2286 lines: dispatch, UI, weight download, conda bootstrap Slicer…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#ali-cbct","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"3. ALI_CBCT: agents inside the volume","x":"ALI_CBCT: one agent per landmark, reused for every patient, walks voxel by voxel until it oscillates, changes scale, then averages six refinements ( Focus ). Drag to rotate · scroll to zoom Basion Ba The anterior margin of the foramen magnum, on the skull base. Sella S The centre of the sella turcica, the hollow that holds the pituitary. Nasion N The bridge of the nose, where the frontal and nasal bones meet. Replay the search walking, one voxel per step oscillated: arrived. Scale now steps to reach the landmark The real path, not a reconstruction: ali_trace.py reads it out of the agent’s…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#ali-ios","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"4. ALI_IOS: multi-view rendering","x":"ALI_IOS: the network (2D UNet) segments each rendered view at the pixel level; everything else, camera placement, pixel→vertex mapping, scaling back to the original size, is geometric. Drag to rotate · click a tooth Run the camera pass dentalmodelseg numbers the teeth first: camera placed on tooth UNet segmenting the render of tooth landmarks placed, one camera pass per tooth The published test arch (ASO_IOS). It starts as plain surface: the numbering comes first, from dentalmodelseg , and everything depends on it , the label array is what gives each tooth’s centroid, and therefore where the…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#predit-calcule","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"5. What's predicted vs. what's computed","x":"Block Nature Where Histogram windowing, resampling computed geometric (SimpleITK / itk) CBCT CLI, Slicer Python Agent's move direction predicted DenseNet3D + MLP network, 6 classes CBCT CLI Stopping criterion, scale-up, Focus computed geometric (oscillation detection, averaging) CBCT CLI Crown segmentation predicted multi-view network (DentalModelSeg) conda shapeaxi Arch leveling (MG) computed geometric, SVD on centroids IOS CLI Camera placement computed geometric (centroids, arch tangent, MG_AIM_OFFSET prior) IOS CLI Per-pixel class map predicted monai 2D UNet network IOS CLI Pixel → face →…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#environnement","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Source CBCT backend Slicer Python + itk , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 , monai ( 1.3.2 if Python ≥ 3.10, otherwise 0.7.0 ) ALI.py:874 IOS backend conda env shapeaxi , shared with AREG, ASO , FlexReg , DOCShapeAXI ALI.py:2074 Env Python 3.12 ALI.py:2076 Env creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK ALI.py:2098 pytorch3d wheel chosen based on the installed torch version, PEP 503 index ImageMindAnalytics.github.io/pytorch3d-wheels install_pytorch.py shapeaxi >=2.0.2 , installed after pytorch3d install_pytorch.py:189 Windows conda via WSL ( CondaSetUpCallWsl ), checks…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#pieges","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"7. Pitfalls and fragile spots","x":"Focus() had no safeguard in the revision this page was written against. Superseded: bounded at 3c67957 ( agent.py:325-341 ) and in VISOR ( CB/agent.py:238-243 ), see the note in 3.4. Move() tests its bounds wrong : if new_pos.all() > 0 and (...) , .all() returns a boolean, compared against 0 . The test checks \"no coordinate is zero,\" not \"no coordinate is negative\" ( agent.py:242 ). Superseded: since 7d901e4 (2026-09-18) the legacy tests the padded sampled zone coordinate by coordinate ( ALI_CBCT_utils/agent.py:114 , :279-284 ), and VISOR tests >= 0 and < size ( CB/agent.py:135-141 ). The…"},{"u":"ALI/ALI.html#litterature","p":"ALI","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"8. Literature","x":"Both backends have their own paper, and the code matches what they describe: with one reservation for ALI_IOS. ALI_CBCT : Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography , Gillot, Baquero, Cevidanes et al., Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research , 2023. Detection is reframed as a classification problem : a virtual agent placed in the volume learns to navigate a multi-scale space toward the landmark. Verified · Also true in VISOR for the algorithm Reported mean error 1.54 ± 0.87 mm over 32 landmarks, 4.2 s inference. That's exactly what the code does, including the two…"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html","p":"ALI: sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"ALI's bibliography: what has been archived in this folder, what's left to read online, and the neighborhood, related work that doesn't describe the code. ALI is the best-published module in the repository: each of the two backends has its own paper, and those papers describe the code well. ALI_CBCT is covered by an open-access journal article (Orthod Craniofac Res 2023), ALI_IOS by a paywalled LNCS chapter (CLIP 2022) with an author's version in circulation. The sheet's reservation holds up: the MG path (mucogingival line) of the ALI_IOS code is not described in any publication , no article…"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"ALI: sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2023_Gillot_ALICBCT_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research, 2023, 26(4), 560-567 Journal article: the ALI_CBCT paper 10.1111/ocr.12642 · PMID 36811276 · PMCID PMC10440369 · CC BY-NC-ND 2022_Baquero_ALIIOS_landmark_intraoral_scans.pdf Baquero B, Gillot M, Cevidanes L, Al Turkestani…"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"ALI: sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Leclercq M, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Styner M, Paniagua B, Prieto JC. DentalModelSeg: fully automated segmentation of upper and lower 3D intra-oral surfaces. Proc. IEEE ISBI 2023 Freely readable on Europe PMC PMC10949221 ; neither JATS nor BioC available, PDF behind Cloudflare 10.1109/ISBI53787.2023.10230397 Baquero B, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, et al. ALIDDM: Automatic Landmark Identification in Digital Dental Models. 51st AADOCR/CADR Annual Meeting, Atlanta (hybrid), March 2022, abstract 3668353 IADR Abstract Archives : IADR account required –…"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#voie-mg","p":"ALI: sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"What was searched for the MG path","x":"Verified The sheet claims the mucogingival line is published nowhere. Verified, and nothing contradicts it: Europe PMC: \"mucogingival\" AND AUTH:\"Cevidanes L\" (13 results, none about automatic landmark identification), \"mucogingival\" AND AUTH:\"Prieto JC\" (1 result, unrelated), \"mucogingival\" AND \"intraoral scan\" AND \"deep learning\" (3 results, all unrelated abstract collections). OpenAlex: ALIIOS , mucogingival line automatic landmark identification , gingival margin landmark detection intraoral scan neural network , nothing from the DCBIA group. The CLIP paper itself does not contain a…"},{"u":"ALI/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"ALI: sources","t":"ALI","c":"Landmarks","s":"Neighborhood","x":"Related work that does not describe ALI but sheds light on the problem. Alansary et al. 2019 (archived here): a comparative evaluation of reinforcement-learning agents for anatomical landmark detection. This is the general reference for the moving-agent mechanism described in the sheet. Ghesu et al. 2016 (MICCAI) and 2019 (TPAMI) : the origin of this mechanism, an artificial agent followed by multi-scale deep reinforcement learning. This is reference 20 of the ALI_CBCT paper, the only one it cites for the agent part. Maxime Gillot has no prior work of his own on the subject : a review of his…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"Automatic multi-structure segmentation of craniofacial CBCT scans: one binary nnU-Net v2 network per anatomical structure, assembled and merged by the CLI. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, is described on its own page, AMASSS on VISOR ."},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#situation","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"1. Where it sits in the chain","x":"AMASSS is both a terminal module (the AMASSS widget) and a building block reused by three other tools , all of which call the same CLI through slicer.modules.amasss_cli . Caller File Usage AMASSS widget AMASSS.py:1659-1667 operator-driven segmentation AREG_CBCT (semi-auto, fully-auto, oriented) CBCT.py:337 , 469 , 554 two distinct roles: registration masks ( merge=SEPARATE , genVtk=False ) before AREG, then deliverable segmentations afterwards VFACE createlistprocess.py:126 segmentation within the asymmetry-analysis chain AMASSS calls nothing else: no conda subprocess, no other module. Its…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#fichiers","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AMASSS/AMASSS.py Slicer widget, 1688 lines, of which ~450 are light/dark stylesheets Slicer's Python AMASSS_CLI/AMASSS_CLI.py all the logic: model discovery, inference, output assembly Slicer's Python, via slicer.cli.run AMASSS_CLI/AMASSS_CLI.xml declaration of the 12 positional parameters – The CLI is not launched inside a conda environment: slicer.cli.run starts it in Slicer's Python, and since the September 2026 port ( 109e1ed ) nnU-Net inference runs in this same process , no longer in a child nnUNetv2_predict . CUDA is not required: ResolveDevice()…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#entrees","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 File discovery main() accepts a file or a folder, walked with a recursive os.walk ( AMASSS_CLI.py:766 ): necessary because AREG writes its registered scans to <output>/<Region>/<patient>_OutReg/ . Extensions kept: .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz . Two filters: any name containing MASK is skipped, and if isSegmentInput is false, any name containing _<prediction_ID>_ is skipped too, it is the output of a previous run, and re-segmenting it would produce an empty mask. Pitfall (legacy only) The widget, for its part, counts files with a different extension list ( .gipl included) and…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#modeles","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"4. The models","x":"4.1 The bundle and its discovery FindModelFolder() ( AMASSS_CLI.py:87 ) looks, for each structure code, for: <modelDirectory>/<CODE>/**/<Dataset...>__nnUNetPlans__3d_fullres/fold_0/checkpoint_final.pth A candidate is only accepted once the fold_0 checkpoint is confirmed present ; otherwise the structure is declared unavailable and the run continues with the others. Discovery happens once for the whole run . Download: the Download latest models button downloads MODEL_LINK , the archive AMASSS_Models.zip (about 2 GB), once, into <Documents>/<Slicer>Downloads/AMASSS/Models , and fills in the…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#coeur","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"5. The algorithmic core (not learned)","x":"5.1 GPU resampling EnableGpuResampling() ( AMASSS_CLI.py:148 ) is the CLI's main optimization. According to the code comment, single-core scipy resampling costs roughly seven times as much as the network. The mechanism: nnU-Net resolves its resampling functions by name , through recursive_find_resampling_fn_by_name , from the configuration dict. Rewriting the two names redirects both ends, with no monkeypatch: configuration[\"resampling_fn_data\"] = \"resample_torch_fornnunet\" configuration[\"resampling_fn_probabilities\"] = \"resample_torch_fornnunet\" configuration[f\"{key}_kwargs\"] = {\"is_seg\"…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#sorties","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"Naming, base = scan name without extension, ext = original extension: Mode File Content SEPARATE <base>_<prediction_ID>_<STRUCT><ext> computed binary int16 mask, one per structure MERGE <base>_<prediction_ID>_MERGED<ext> computed int16 label map per LABELS[\"LARGE\"] genVtk <same name>.vtk computed colored surface(s) With save_in_folder , everything goes into <output_folder>/<base>_<prediction_ID>_SegOut/ ; otherwise directly into <output_folder> . Confirmed bug (legacy only) The temporary folder is wiped and recreated at both startup and the end ( AMASSS_CLI.py:808 , AMASSS_CLI.py:887 ).…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#predit-calcule","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Preprocessing (resampling, CTNormalization ) computed deterministic, driven by plans.json nnU-Net, in-process Segmentation of each structure predicted 3D PlainConvUNet network, one per structure, binary nnU-Net, in-process, GPU if available Sliding window, gaussian, step 0.5 computed deterministic nnU-Net Output resampling computed deterministic, torch (GPU) or scipy nnU-Net, resampler substituted by AMASSS Binarization + label merging computed trivial geometry ( np.where ) CLI Geometry registration to the scan computed ITK, nearest neighbor, a no-op in practice CLI VTK…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#environnement","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"8. Environment","x":"Everything runs in Slicer's Python. Installation is triggered by the Predict button ( AMASSS.py:869-890 ), not when the module opens, and goes through a confirmation dialog. Package Version pinned Reason readable in the code torch , torchvision , torchaudio unpinned the CUDA build is read off the GPU by torch_install_arguments() (ADTLib); a version pin had flagged the cu128 build an RTX 50 series needs ( AMASSS.py:878-882 ) nnunetv2 >=2.8.0 inference backend numpy 1.26.4 ( NUMPY_PINNED_VERSION ) according to the comment, torch 2.2.0 is built against numpy 1.x; numpy ≥ 2 breaks every torch…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#pieges","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug: small FOV broken (legacy only) highDefinition / \"small FOV\" no longer does anything it claims to. The smallFOVCheckBox switches the offered structure list to GROUPS_HD_SEG ( AMASSS.py:252 , AMASSS.py:769 ): mandible, maxilla, teeth, root canal, mandibular canal, three of which have no model. The param[\"highDefinition\"] flag is not declared in the XML , slicer.cli.run drops it, and the CLI no longer has any SMALL code path anyway (spacing [0.16, 0.16, 0.32] and MODELS_GROUP[\"SMALL\"] disappeared with the port). Checking the box only changes the list of checkboxes. Legacy…"},{"u":"AMASSS/AMASSS.html#litterature","p":"AMASSS","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"10. Literature","x":"AMASSS is published once: Gillot M., Baquero B., Le C., Deleat-Besson R., Bianchi J., Ruellas A., …, Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic multi-anatomical skull structure segmentation of cone-beam computed tomography scans using 3D UNETR. PLOS ONE 17(10):e0275033, 2022. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0275033 , PMID 36223330, PMC9555672 Code / paper divergence (also true in VISOR) Also true in VISOR (nnU-Net v2 checkpoints, no MONAI: SADT-VISOR sadt_amasss/nnunet_runner.py:33-34, 208-212 ). The code has diverged from the paper, completely. The paper describes a UNETR (transformer encoder +…"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html","p":"AMASSS: sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for this tool: what is deposited in this folder, what is meant to be read online, and the neighborhood. AMASSS is published once, open access (PLOS ONE 2022), but the paper describes a dead version of the module : a UNETR MONAI model with a 128³ sliding window, whereas the code that runs today is an nnU-Net v2 (one binary network per structure, PlainConvUNet architecture coming from the weights' plans.json ). The switch dates to commit 61bbb15 \"ENH: AMASSS with nnunet\" (2025-07-30); commit 109e1ed (2026-09-09) later replaced the subprocess call with nnU-Net's Python API. Code /…"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AMASSS: sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2022_Gillot_AMASSS_skull_segmentation.pdf Gillot M., Baquero B., Le C., Deleat-Besson R., Bianchi J., Ruellas A., Gurgel M., Yatabe M., Al Turkestani N., Najarian K., Soroushmehr R., Pieper S., Kikinis R., Paniagua B., Gryak J., Ioshida M., Massaro C., Gomes L., Oh H., Evangelista K., Chaves Junior C.M., Garib D., Costa F., Benavides E., Soki F., Fillion-Robin J.-C., Joshi H., Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic multi-anatomical skull structure segmentation of cone-beam computed tomography scans using 3D UNETR. PLOS ONE 17(10):e0275033, 2022, 12 p. Journal…"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AMASSS: sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Isensee F., Jaeger P.F., Kohl S.A.A., Petersen J., Maier-Hein K.H. nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image segmentation. Nature Methods 18(2):203-211, 2021. Reference version to cite ; only the arXiv preprint is freely redistributable. nature.com 10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z"},{"u":"AMASSS/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AMASSS: sources","t":"AMASSS","c":"Segmentation","s":"Neighborhood","x":"These works do not describe AMASSS ; they document its environment or its building blocks. nnU-Net (Isensee et al. 2021, above): the foundation of the module's current version. It describes the self-configuring method, not the AMASSS model or its weights, whose training is documented nowhere. UNETR (Hatamizadeh et al. 2022): the architecture of the PLOS ONE paper, so of the historical version of the CLI. Unrelated to what runs today. Maxlo24/AMASSS_CBCT : the original repository cited by the paper; research code, not the Slicer module. DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools : the module's…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Rigid T1/T2 registration of CBCT scans onto a reference bone region (cranial base, mandible, or maxilla): plain elastix , mutual information, no network, everything learned sits upstream, in the masks and the landmarks. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, has its own page: AREG_CBCT on VISOR . The notes that open some boxes below (“Legacy only”, “Also true in VISOR”) compare with that port at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78; in them cbct/ stands for SADT-VISOR…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#situation","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"AREG/AREG.py is a single widget that drives three modalities: CBCT ( AREG/AREG_Method/CBCT.py ), IOS ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py ) and IOS-on-CBCT ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOSCBCT.py ). It computes nothing itself: each Method.Process() returns a list of step dictionaries , and the widget runs them one after another. A step always has the following shape: {\"Process\": slicer.modules.areg_cbct, # Slicer CLI module \"Parameter\": {...}, # dict passed as-is to slicer.cli.run \"Module\": \"AREG_CBCT for Mandible\", # label shown in the UI \"ReviewId\": \"cbct_registration\", # optional review pause \"ReviewFolder\"…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#fichiers","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Note Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . ADTLib belongs to the legacy Slicer code base. The three VISOR engines of AREG share a package of their own, sadt_areg_common , declared with no dependency at all ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/common/pyproject.toml:6-13 ). Part of this module now lives in the shared layer. ADTLib centralises what several modules carried in copies: from here, theming.py , io/landmarks.py , env/conda.py and the contract of method.py . Its page records how many copies each file replaced. File Role Where it runs AREG/AREG.py widget, 3336 lines: UI, step chaining…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#modes","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"3. The three modes and the exact chain","x":"The mode is chosen by CbModeType ; SwitchType() ( AREG.py:1093 ) instantiates the matching class. All three share the same tail end, center T2, run AREG per region, final AMASSS , and only differ upstream: Mode Class Upstream T1 masks Semi-Automated Semi_CBCT ( CBCT.py:270 ) none supplied by the operator (\"T1 Masks\" field) Fully-Automated Auto_CBCT ( CBCT.py:445 ) none AMASSS CBMASK / MANDMASK / MAXMASK , written into the input T1 folder predicted Orientation & Registration Or_Auto_CBCT ( CBCT.py:613 ) PRE_ASO → ALI_CBCT → SEMI_ASO on T1, into <T1 folder>Or AMASSS on <T1 folder>Or predicted…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#entrees","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Inputs, pairing, preprocessing","x":"Formats. .nii.gz , .nii , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz . For DICOM input, convertdicom2nifti ( utils.py:723 ) converts a per-patient subfolder via sitk.ImageSeriesReader , falling back to dicom2nifti , into <folder>/NIFTI/<patient>.nii.gz . T1/T2 pairing : GetPatients ( utils.py:80 ). The patient identifier is whatever remains of the file name after a chain of split() calls on a fixed list of markers ( _Scan , _Or , _MAND , _CB , _lm , _T2 , _T1 , _Cl …). Then: an image file whose name contains mask , seg or pred → segmentation; otherwise → scanT1 / scanT2 ; if segmentationType is…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#recalage","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"5. The algorithmic core: the registration","x":"Cranial base Mandible Maxilla Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again 0. upstream : ALI_CBCT places the landmarks 1. read the moving T2 as itk.F 2. AMASSS predicts the mask 3. mask the fixed T1: this is all elastix will see 4. one elastix pass: Mattes MI, 3 pyramid levels, 1500 iterations each 5. MatrixRetrieval → Euler3DTransform 6. resample T2: on its own grid, not T1's ALI_CBCT places the landmarks the orientation needs, read the moving T2 as itk.F AMASSS predicts the mask mask the fixed T1, this is all elastix will see one elastix pass: Mattes MI, 3 pyramid levels, 1500…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#amont","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"6. What runs upstream: exact arguments","x":"AMASSS , registration masks ( CBCT.py:456 ): {\"inputVolume\": <T1 folder>, # or <T1 folder>Or \"modelDirectory\": <model_folder_1>/AMASSS_Models, \"skullStructure\": \"CBMASK,MANDMASK,MAXMASK\", # depending on checked boxes \"merge\": \"SEPARATE\", \"genVtk\": False, \"save_in_folder\": False, \"output_folder\": <T1 folder>, # next to the scans \"vtk_smooth\": 5, \"prediction_ID\": \"seg\", \"temp_fold\": <Documents>/Slicer_temp_AMASSS, \"SegmentInput\": False, \"DCMInput\": False} The name written is <base>_seg_<STRUCT>.<ext> ( AMASSS_CLI.py:600 ), so P1_T1_seg_MANDMASK.nii.gz , which the second pass of GetPatients…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#sorties","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Outputs","x":"Written by AREG_CBCT.py:48 : <output>/<Cranial Base|Mandible|Maxilla>/<patient>_OutReg/ <patient>_<CB|MAND|MAX>Scan<add_name>.nii.gz # registered T2, int16 <patient>_<CB|MAND|MAX><add_name>_matrix.tfm # the transform The subfolder name comes from translate() ( utils.py:595 ): CB → Cranial Base . add_name is concatenated with no separator . Conventions of the .tfm : sitk.WriteTransform of an Euler3DTransform , so LPS and the ITK convention (the transform maps output space to input space). No flip is needed anywhere in the CLI, everything there is SimpleITK/elastix; the only place in the CBCT…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#revue","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Review pauses and batch navigation","x":"Added by commit 2609bc4 (\"Pause VFACE at chosen steps\"… and AREG before it). In \"Review and manual adjustment,\" the operator checks the steps where the run should stop; the selection is stored in QSettings under AREG/ReviewSteps ( AREG.py:2122 ). The breakpoints available in CBCT ( getReviewSteps , CBCT.py:254 ), and what they allow ( CATALOGUE , Review.py:578 ): Id After Nature cbct_landmarks_orientation ALI_CBCT (Orientation mode) editable : points can be moved and are rewritten cbct_oriented SEMI_ASO view only cbct_masks AMASSS masks view only cbct_centered_t2 PRE_ASO T2 view only…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#predit-calcule","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"9. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where T1/T2 centering (PRE_ASO) computed geometric, translation of the center Slicer CLI Orientation landmarks ( ALI_CBCT ) predicted 3D DenseNet network, multi-scale RL agents Slicer CLI, CUDA if available Orientation (SEMI_ASO) computed rigid VTK ICP on landmarks Slicer CLI Reference-region masks ( AMASSS ) predicted nnU-Net v2 3d_fullres network Slicer CLI, GPU T1/T2 registration (AREG_CBCT) computed rigid elastix, mutual information Slicer CLI, CPU Final segmentations (AMASSS) predicted nnU-Net v2 network Slicer CLI, GPU Manual correction computed Slicer transform composed…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#environnement","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Environment","x":"Everything is installed into Slicer's Python by install_function ( AREG.py:155 ) when the launch button is clicked, after a confirmation dialog: itk==5.4.0 , itk-elastix==0.19.2 (the registration engine), dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , torch , no longer pinned: see below, monai==1.3.2 , nnunetv2>=2.8.0 and torchvision ( Linux/macOS only ), numpy<2.0.0 re-forced afterward if numpy ≥ 2 is detected, plus connected-components-3d , einops , nibabel , pandas . pydicom is pinned to ==3.0.2 , Slicer's own version: downgrading it breaks dicomweb-client and highdicom , hence every DICOM module in Slicer.…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#pieges","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Traps and fragile spots","x":"The _T1 / _T2 suffixes. Commit 05d1fec flagged every site with the keyword TIMEPOINT-SUFFIX . The reference note sits above GetPatients ( utils.py:68 ). Trap Also true in VISOR , but no longer silent. Observed on 6ba2a78: P001_T3.nii.gz keeps the id P001_T3 and P001_T10.nii.gz is not cut to P001 ; only t1 , t2 and t0 are timepoint tokens ( SADT-VISOR common/catalogs.py:148 ). The orphan is listed under unmatched in AREG_report.json ( cbct/dispatch.py:259-260 ) and a run in which nothing pairs stops with an error that states the rule ( cbct/dispatch.py:261-268 ). Legacy side…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#litterature","p":"AREG_CBCT","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Literature","x":"The reference paper is Anchling, Hutin, Huang, Barone, Roberts, Miranda, Gurgel, Al Turkestani, Tinawi, Bianchi, Yatabe, Ruellas, Prieto, Cevidanes, \"Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans,\" LNCS 14242, 2023, p. 43-58 : Springer , PMC11104011 . Reported accuracy: < 3° and < 2 mm against experts, under 5 min per case. The code diverges from the paper on three verified points: the paper describes a two-stage registration: \"VBR between the full images, then with the masked image as a refinement step,\" 10,000 iterations on the masked pass. The code now runs…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html","p":"AREG_CBCT: sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for AREG_CBCT: what is archived in this folder, what is still to be checked online, and the neighboring work that does not describe the code. AREG_CBCT is published only once , in a MICCAI workshop proceedings chapter (LNCS 14242, 2023) that it shares with ASO : the same text describes orientation (ASO) and registration (AREG). This chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript is freely readable on PMC (PMC11104011); PMC, however, blocks automated PDF download, so it could not be archived here (see \"To check online\"). Trap The code has diverged from the paper on…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AREG_CBCT: sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Barone_VSP_ClasseIII_voxel_based.xml / .txt Barone S., Cevidanes L., Miranda F., Gurgel M.L., Anchling L., Hutin N., Bianchi J., Goncalves J.R., Giudice A. Enhancing skeletal stability and Class III correction through active orthodontist engagement in virtual surgical planning: A voxel-based 3-dimensional analysis. American Journal of Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopedics, 165(3):321-331, 2024. Journal article, full JATS text (Europe PMC) + plain text: CC-BY license 10.1016/j.ajodo.2023.09.016 : PMID 38010236, PMCID PMC10923113…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AREG_CBCT: sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Anchling L., Hutin N., Huang Y., Barone S., Roberts S., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Tinawi S., Bianchi J., Yatabe M., Ruellas A., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L. Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans. Lecture Notes in Computer Science 14242, Springer, Cham, 2023, p. 43-58. Joint proceedings of CLIP 2023 (12th International Workshop on Clinical Image-Based Procedures), FAIMI 2023 and EPIMI 2023, held with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8-12, 2023. Eds. Wesarg, Puyol Antón, Baxter et al. ISBN 978-3-031-45248-2 /…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AREG_CBCT: sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighboring work","x":"These works do not describe AREG_CBCT ; they underpin or surround the method. Cevidanes 2005 (DMFR) and Nada 2011 (PLOS ONE) : voxel-based superimposition on the anterior cranial base. Note: contrary to what the AREG_CBCT.md page might suggest, the 2011 PLOS ONE paper is not from the Cevidanes lab: it is signed by Nada, Maal, Kuijpers-Jagtman et al. (Radboud UMC, Nijmegen). It is an independent validation of the method, which makes it more useful, not less. Ruellas 2016 (mandible) and Ruellas 2016 (maxilla) : the two papers that established the reference regions used by regional…"},{"u":"AREG_CBCT/SOURCES.html#recuperation","p":"AREG_CBCT: sources","t":"AREG_CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"How the full text was retrieved","x":"The chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript deposited with the NIH is freely accessible. Two approaches fail on it and one works, noted here to avoid retracing the whole search: Route Result on this PMCID pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/.../pdf/ anti-bot HTML BioC API pmcoa.cgi \"No result can be found\": it only serves the open access subset eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=<number> full JATS, body included curl -sL \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=11104011\" This is how the .xml and .txt files in this folder were…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Longitudinal T1/T2 registration of Intra Oral Scans by ICP restricted to a stable region: a palatal patch predicted by a network (Butterfly) or a mucogingival band computed from predicted landmarks (MGL), processed in batches of folders. Longitudinal T1/T2 registration of Intra Oral Scans. Same principle as FlexReg.md, a rigid ICP restricted to a stable region, but the region is predicted by a network instead of being drawn by the operator, and processing runs in batches of folders instead of patient by patient. Two modes coexist in the same CLI: Butterfly : upper arch, palatal patch…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#situation","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"AREG_IOS is a CLI. Its only caller in the repository is the AREG module, via AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py . It doesn't call any other module itself: everything it needs (segmentation, orientation, landmarks) is produced upstream by the steps the orchestrator stacks ahead of it. Upstream preprocessing depends on the mode (Butterfly vs MGL); the CLI then produces either a palatal patch predicted by a network, or an MGL band computed , before the rigid ICP. AREG mode Steps stacked before AREG_IOS Source Auto_IOS / Butterfly CrownSegmentationcli T1 and T2 → PRE_ASO_IOS T1 and T2…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#fichiers","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Note Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . ADTLib belongs to the legacy Slicer code base. The three VISOR engines of AREG share sadt_areg_common , which has no dependency ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/common/pyproject.toml:6-13 ). Part of this module now lives in the shared layer. ADTLib centralises what several modules carried in copies: from here, geometry.py , io/landmarks.py , labels.py and the contract of method.py . Its page records how many copies each file replaced. File Role Where it runs AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.py the CLI, 451 lines: Butterfly loop, RunMGL , writing .tfm files conda…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#entrees","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 T1/T2 pairing Sort() ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:89 ) and sort() ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:258 ): glob over both folders, filtered to .vtk .vtp .stl .obj (the .tfm files are explicitly excluded); files are classified Upper/Lower by substring search: [\"Lower\",\"_L\",\"L_\",\"Mandibule\",\"Md\"] and [\"Upper\",\"_U\",\"U_\",\"Maxilla\",\"Mx\"] ; pairing works by literally stripping the T1 / T2 substring from the basename and comparing what's left; matching Upper↔Lower for the same patient works by additionally stripping the words Upper/Lower and comparing. Pitfall Legacy (3c67957)…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#modele","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The model: MonaiUNetHRes","x":"Weights file: a single .ckpt in the model folder, selected by getModel(..., extension=\"ckpt\") ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:271 ). Source: AREG_model.zip , release HUTIN1/AREG v1.0.0 ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:272 ). Loaded via torch.load(path)[\"state_dict\"] : a PyTorch Lightning checkpoint. Multi-view rendering, a per-view 2D UNet predicted , reprojection onto the mesh by voting, then a morphological closing computed to fill in the holes left by a degenerate vote (writes only to the first vertex of each triangle). 4.1 Architecture AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/net.py:52 . A LightningModule that…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#mgl","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"5. MGL mode: lower arch","x":"The palate gives a stable plateau up top; the mandible has none. The substitute is the mucogingival line. This mode is the CLI port of path C of FlexReg (see FlexReg.md §5.3), and it involves no AREG network at all: the palatal .ckpt isn't even loaded ( parameter_reg[\"model\"] = \"None\" , AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:92 , and RunMGL returns before PredPatch ). Mucogingival landmarks predicted by ALI_IOS, filtering out doubtful points, then computed construction of the band via spline and geodesic growth. 5.1 The landmarks MGLProcess() ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:41 ) stacks two ALI_IOS calls (T1 then…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#icp","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"6. The ICP","x":"Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom 1. the two time points, still apart 2. the Butterfly patch, predicted on the palate 3. rigid ICP, on the patch alone 4. registered mean gap on the patch Register again the two time points, still apart the Butterfly patch, predicted on the palate, rigid ICP, on the patch alone registered, mean gap on the patch T1 in blue, T2 in orange, the patch in green. AREG_IOS does not register on the whole arch: it registers on a patch , here the Butterfly, predicted on the palate, 9529 of 75867 vertices , about an eighth of the mesh. That array is in the output…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#sorties","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Post-processing and outputs","x":"WriteSurf(surf, output_folder, name, inname) ( ADTLib/io/surface.py:192 ) writes <name without extension><suffix><extension> . File Content Mode <T1><suffix>.vtk T1 geometrically unchanged, with the Butterfly or Bottom_MGL array both <T2><suffix>.vtk T2 registered both <Lower_T1><suffix>.vtk lower arch T1, copied unchanged Butterfly, if a lower pair exists <Lower_T2><suffix>.vtk lower arch T2, with the upper arch's matrix applied to it Butterfly, if a lower pair exists <pid>_T1_SegOr.tfm copy of <pid>_SegOr.tfm written by PRE_ASO_IOS areg_mode == \"Auto_IOS\" only <pid>_T2_SegOr<suffix>.tfm…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#predit-calcule","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"8. What's predicted vs what's computed","x":"Block Nature Where Crown segmentation network multi-view ( DentalModelSeg , latest ) conda run -n shapeaxi -m CrownSegmentationcli ASO orientation (Butterfly, Auto) geometric + ICP against a gold file slicer.cli.run(pre_aso_ios) Internal canonical orientation geometric , 4 centroids, 2 rotations CLI, inside DatasetPatch 7-view rendering geometric (PyTorch3D) CLI, GPU Palatal patch network 2D MONAI UNet over the 7 views CLI, GPU Reprojection + vote geometric ( pix_to_face , argmax) CLI, GPU Morphological closing geometric , edge graph CLI, CPU MG landmarks network multi-view ( ALI_IOS…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#comparaison","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"9. FlexReg vs AREG_IOS: the table","x":"Same output, same arrays, same ICP. The dividing line is: who decides the patch's shape . Step FlexReg AREG_IOS Granularity one patient, interactive T1/T2 folders, batch Where the heavy computation runs Slicer's Python ( slicer.cli.run ) conda env ( python -m AREG_IOS ) Crown segmentation network, on demand if the array is missing network, a step stacked by AREG Orientation geometric, teeth 3/5/12/14, in the CLI ASO (upstream) then geometric, teeth 3/8/9/14, in the dataset Upper patch built by hand : 4 centroids, Bézier curves mirrored across the chord, GPU geodesic flood fill predicted : 7…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#environnement","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Environment","x":"Element Value Source Conda env shapeaxi , shared with ALI , ASO , FlexReg , DOCShapeAXI AREG/AREG.py:3366 Python 3.12 AREG/AREG.py:3368 Env creation SimpleITK only: torch and everything built on it come afterwards AREG/AREG.py:3396 torch, ocnn==2.2.1 , pytorch3d + shapeaxi installed by ADTLib/env/install_pytorch.py , torch kept inside torch>=2.8,<2.13 ( install_pytorch.py:83 , ocnn at install_pytorch.py:88 ) AREG/AREG.py:3415 Slicer side (IOS) tqdm , vtk , pandas , monai==1.3.2 AREG/AREG.py:1682 Windows conda inside WSL, plus libxrender1 , libgl1 / libgl1-mesa-glx , libglx-mesa0…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#pieges","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . VISOR classifies the jaw on whole tokens of the base name ( SADT-VISOR ios/surfaces.py:113-130 ), so a folder name cannot change it. isLowerUpper() tests the full path , not the basename ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:232 ; called with file as a full path from Sort and SortLower ). A folder containing _U , L_ , Md … misclassifies every arch. And the words are tested case-sensitively here, whereas Method.IsLower on the widget side ( AREG/AREG_Method/IOS.py:146 ) lowercases the basename first: the two don't classify the same…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#litterature","p":"AREG_IOS","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Literature","x":"The published foundation is AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOral Scans (Hutin, Anchling, Cevidanes et al., Lecture Notes in Computer Science , 2023, MICCAI): crown segmentation by DentalModelSeg , initial alignment via shared centroids, palatal ROI segmented by multi-view 3D shape analysis, then ICP. Two discrepancies between the paper and this repository's code: the paper describes an initial alignment by shared crown centroids ; in the code, that role is played by upstream PRE_ASO_IOS plus VTK's StartByMatchingCentroidsOn() : there is no centroid-alignment step inside AREG_IOS…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html","p":"AREG_IOS: sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for AREG_IOS: what has been deposited in this folder, what remains paywalled with no author version, and the methodological neighborhood that does not describe this code. The tool is published , once: AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans (ShapeMI 2023, LNCS 14350). The paper describes the automatic palatal path. This repository's code departs from it on two points flagged on the pipeline page: the initial alignment by crown centroids is moved upstream into PRE_ASO_IOS + StartByMatchingCentroidsOn() , and the MGL path (mucogingival line) appears nowhere in the paper…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AREG_IOS: sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2021_Boubolo_FlyBy_CNN_surface_segmentation.xml / .txt Boubolo L, Dumont M, Brosset S, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel ML, Massaro C, Aliaga-Del Castillo A, Ioshida M, Yatabe MS, Benavides E, Rios H, Soki F, Neiva G, Paniagua B, Cevidanes L, Styner M, Prieto JC. « FlyBy CNN: a 3D surface segmentation framework ». Proc. SPIE 11596, Medical Imaging 2021: Image Processing , 115962B, 2021. SPIE proceedings, PMC full text (author manuscript) 10.1117/12.2582205 : PMC7983301 2023_Leclercq_DentalModelSeg_intraoral_surfaces.xml / .txt Leclercq M, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Yatabe…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AREG_IOS: sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Hutin N, Anchling L, Cevidanes L, Miranda F, Curado D, Gurgel M, Barone S, Bianchi J, Al Turkestani N, Ruellas A, Eason M, Mavani K, Prieto JC, Aliaga A. « AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans ». In: Shape in Medical Imaging, ShapeMI 2023, held jointly with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8, 2023 , Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14350, Springer, 2023, p. 223–235. link.springer.com : paywalled, no deposited author version 10.1007/978-3-031-46914-5_18 Eason M, Tai SK, Loh CT, Del Castillo AA, Teixeira R, Hutin N, Leroux G, Gurgel ML, Mattos CT, de…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AREG_IOS: sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighborhood","x":"Related work that does not describe this code . Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. « Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography ». Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research 2023;26(4):560–567. 10.1111/ocr.12642 : ALI on the CBCT side. AREG_IOS does not use ALI_CBCT ; it's the volumetric counterpart of ALI_IOS. Anchling L, Hutin N, Huang Y, Barone S, Roberts S, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Tinawi S, Bianchi J…"},{"u":"AREG_IOS/SOURCES.html#recherche-vide","p":"AREG_IOS: sources","t":"AREG_IOS","c":"Recalage","s":"Searched with nothing found","x":"Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC ( AReg IOS , IntraOralScans , AUTH:Hutin N ), Unpaywall, HAL/DUMAS, arXiv, NA-MIC Project Week 36 through 43 repositories, web search of IADR/AADOCR abstracts. No published work on the MGL path nor on the \"4.2 mm vs 1.2 mm over 364 predictions\" figures cited in the code's comments. No open-access author version of AReg IOS: the only remaining place to check by hand is Deep Blue (deepblue.lib.umich.edu), inaccessible by script."},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Multimodal registration: placing an Intra Oral Scan into the frame of a CBCT from the same patient. The CLI is entirely geometric: it loads no weights at all. Nothing to do with AREG_IOS , which is longitudinal (T1 → T2, same modality): here the two point clouds come from different sensors, and there is no common \"stable patch\". Two successive matching steps, no network inside the CLI : 12 occlusal landmarks predicted on both sides by two different networks ( ALI_CBCT on the volume, ALI_IOS on the mesh) → rigid Procrustes transform ( vtkLandmarkTransform ) that serves as pre-alignment; ICP…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#situation","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Place in the chain","x":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.py is called only by AREG/AREG_Method/IOSCBCT.py , which defines three modes. It calls nothing itself. Mode Class Steps stacked before AREG_IOSCBCT Registration Reg_IOSCBCT ( IOSCBCT.py:383 ) none : the user supplies IOS, CBCT and both landmark folders Semi-Automated Semi_IOSCBCT ( IOSCBCT.py:149 ) CrownSegmentationcli → ALI_CBCT → ALI_IOS Fully-Automated Auto_IOSCBCT ( IOSCBCT.py:460 ) 8 steps, see the full sequence So this is indeed an orchestrator with a thin terminal CLI . The real pipeline is the list of processes built by Process() . Full sequence of the…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#ou-ca-tourne","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Where it runs","x":"Element Value Execution slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.areg_ioscbct, ...) : Slicer's Python , not conda Exception the CrownSegmentationcli step goes through run_conda_tool(\"seg\") ( AREG/AREG.py:2865 ) Dependencies installed in Slicer pyvista==0.47.3 , scipy , numpy , SimpleITK ( AREG/AREG.py:1703 ) GPU not required in the CLI: no torch is imported Parameter passing via AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.xml , 5 positional arguments, this one is up to date IOS_folder CBCT_folder IOS_lm_folder CBCT_lm_folder output This is the structural difference from AREG_IOS : AREG_IOS is a conda module launched by…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#appariement","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Matching the files","x":"getPatients() ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:1051 ) scans the four folders and builds a <patient_id>_<timepoint> key. Extraction Regex Note Timepoint [Tt]([0-2]) T0, T1, T2 only . A _T3 matches nothing → patient silently ignored. The pattern is not anchored, so _T10 is read as T1 and _T20 as T2 ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:1061-1064 ) Arch (?:^|_)(?:u|upper)(?=_|\\.|$) and the same for l/lower the letter must be a delimited token. An isolated u in the middle of the name no longer counts: otherwise P001_T1_L_Surface.vtk was read as \"upper\" and registered against the wrong landmarks Patient…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#correspondance","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Matching the two modalities","x":"Lower arch Upper arch Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom 1. the CBCT surface, marching cubes at a level read off the scan 2. ALI places the same six occlusal points on each modality 3. paired by label, never by position 4. rigid pre-alignment, vtkLandmarkTransform 5. then ICP, inside a 1 mm window not replayed here Register again the CBCT surface; this demo contours it at the fallback level, ALI places the same six occlusal points on each modality paired by label, never by position, rigid pre-alignment, vtkLandmarkTransform then ICP, inside a 1 mm window, not replayed here Two…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#sorties","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"5. Outputs","x":"save_registered_ios() ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:273 ) and apply_matrix_and_save_landmarks() ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:279 ), all within <output> (= <folder_output>/Registered IOS in the Semi and Auto modes): File Content <pid>_Reg_U.vtk upper IOS, landmark transform then ICP applied <pid>_Reg_L.vtk same, lower <pid>_lm_Reg_U.mrk.json the CBCT json , with each position replaced by that of the registered IOS landmark of the same label <pid>_lm_Reg_L.mrk.json same <pid> is the normalized identifier ( P1 , not P_0001 ): the output names therefore do not resemble the input names, hence _matchAcrossNaming() in…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#predit-calcule","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"6. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where CBCT resampling geometric slicer.cli.run(mri2cbct_resample_cbct_mri) CBCT pre-orientation network (PreASO) slicer.cli.run(pre_aso_cbct) CBCT landmarks (×2 passes) network ( ALI_CBCT , RL agents) slicer.cli.run(ali_cbct) CBCT orientation geometric , gold file slicer.cli.run(semi_aso_cbct) IOS crown segmentation network ( DentalModelSeg ) conda run -n shapeaxi IOS orientation geometric , 4 centroids + gold ICP slicer.cli.run(pre_aso_ios) IOS landmarks network ( ALI_IOS multi-view) slicer.cli.run(ali_ios) CBCT isosurface geometric , marching cubes at a level read off the scan…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#pieges","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Corrected: the isosurface level is read off the scan (3c67957) An earlier version of this callout said that the 400 threshold is hard-coded and never exposed. At 3c67957 it is neither: the level is the half-way point between enamel and soft tissue measured on the scan, 400 is the fallback, and AREG_CBCT_SURFACE_THRESHOLD overrides it ( AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.py:92,847-966 ). The sensitivity to the reconstruction still applies in the fallback case, which the log reports as a warning. In VISOR the same rule is kept ( SADT-VISOR ioscbct/volume.py:96-117,148-175 ) and the fallback is 400…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#litterature","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: full pipeline","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Literature","x":"Unpublished No paper covers AREG_IOSCBCT as implemented here. The lineage published by the group covers CBCT orientation and registration ( Automated Orientation and Registration of CBCT Scans, LNCS 2023 ) and longitudinal IOS registration (AReg IOS, LNCS 2023); the landmark modules it consumes each have their own ( ALI_CBCT ). The multimodal IOS↔CBCT part appears to be an internal, unpublished assembly. The methodological neighborhood in the literature, for comparison, not as a description of this code: Novel Procedure for Automatic Registration between CBCT and Intraoral Scan Data…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"No publication describes this module. What is published are the upstream building blocks that the orchestrator stacks in front of the CLI. Verification The sheet said so, and the verification confirms it: neither Crossref, nor OpenAlex, nor Europe PMC, nor arXiv, nor HAL, nor NA-MIC project weeks 36 through 43 contain anything about an IOS↔CBCT registration by occlusal landmarks + ICP on an isosurface signed by this group. The two NA-MIC projects titled \"Multimodal Registration\" (PW40 2024, PW41 2024) deal with MRI↔CBCT , not IOS↔CBCT. What is published are the upstream building blocks that…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID .xml / .txt Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. \"Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography\". Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research 2023;26(4):560–567. article, open access (CC BY-NC-ND) 10.1111/ocr.12642 : PMC10440369 .xml / .txt Boubolo L, Dumont M, Brosset S, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel ML, Massaro C, Aliaga-Del Castillo A, Ioshida M, Yatabe MS, Benavides E, Rios H, Soki F, Neiva G…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Zheng Q, Wu Y, Chen J, Wang X, Zhou M, Li H, Lin J, Zhang W, Chen X. \"Automatic multimodal registration of cone-beam computed tomography and intraoral scans: a systematic review and meta-analysis\". Clinical Oral Investigations 2025;29(2):97. : systematic review of the problem; places geometric approaches against learned approaches. Does not mention this code. link.springer.com : paywalled 10.1007/s00784-025-06183-x Elgarba BM, Fontenele RC, Ali S, Swaity A, Meeus J, Shujaat S, Jacobs R. \"Validation of a novel AI-based automated multimodal image registration of CBCT and…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighborhood","x":"Work that addresses the same problem but does not describe this code . Kim 2023 ( 2023_Kim_registration_CBCT_intraoral_scan.pdf ): same objective, but with tooth segmentation on both sides , then RANSAC + ICP. Reported error 0.234 ± 0.019 mm. AREG_IOSCBCT takes the lighter path: landmarks instead of volumetric segmentation, thresholded isosurface instead of segmented teeth. Jang 2024 ( 2024_Jang_CBCT_intraoral_impressions_integration.pdf ): tooth-by-tooth identification and correction of the impression's stitching error. Fully learned pipeline, unrelated to the implementation here. Zheng…"},{"u":"AREG_IOSCBCT/SOURCES.html#rien-trouve","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: sources","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Searched with nothing found","x":"Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, arXiv, HAL, Unpaywall on every combination of AREG / IOSCBCT / IOS-CBCT / multimodal registration + Cevidanes, Prieto, Anchling, Hutin, Leroux, Baquero; listing of NA-MIC Project Week 36 → 43 projects (the two \"Multimodal Registration\" ones are MRI↔CBCT); targeted web search. Nothing on the multimodal IOS↔CBCT part of this repository."},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"Looks for a rigid rotation that brings a CBCT or an intraoral scan into the frame of a reference case (the \"gold\"), through a 3-point initialization followed by ICP : never an optimal least-squares registration. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, is described in ASO on VISOR . Notes added to the callouts say whether each finding still holds in VISOR; they cite SADT-VISOR at 6ba2a78, with ASO/ for tools/ASO/src/sadt_aso/ when the path is not…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#situation","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls ASO. ASO, however, calls three tools, and AREG_IOS consumes its output directly : AREG_IOS.py:324 looks for <patient>_SegOr.tfm in the T1 and T2 folders, exactly the name written by PRE_ASO_IOS.py:422 , and composes inv(areg_matrix @ inv(matrix_aso)) to produce a single matrix from raw scan to registered. Legacy only. In VISOR ASO calls one tool, ALI_CBCT , and is called by four: AREG_CBCT, AREG_IOS, AREG_IOSCBCT and VFACE (see Status and where it lives ). Called When How slicer.modules.pre_aso_cbct CBCT, Fully-Automated mode only slicer.cli.run…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#fichiers","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Note · Legacy only Part of this module now lives in the shared layer. ADTLib centralises what several modules carried in copies: from here, io/surface.py , io/landmarks.py , theming.py and the contract of method.py . Its page records how many copies each file replaced. Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR : the server tool has no ADTLib. Its file-name vocabulary comes from the small sadt-naming package ( SADT-VISOR tools/ASO/pyproject.toml:51 ). File Role Where it runs ASO/ASO.py widget, 2590 lines: UI, downloads, chaining the CLIs, conda Slicer's Python ASO_Method/CBCT.py Semi_CBCT /…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#principe","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"3. The shared geometric principle","x":"The target is not an analytical frame (\" occlusal plane = z=0\") but a reference patient case , downloaded from a release: scan + fiducials for the CBCT, two meshes + two jsons for the IOS. The choice of frame is thus offloaded into the data: Occlusal and Midsagittal Plane or Frankfurt Horizontal and Midsagittal Plane ( CBCT.py:68 ) are two different golds, the same code producing two different orientations. In both backends the estimation follows the same two-stage scheme: A 3-point initialization : a translation followed by two rotations around hand-built axes, never a least-squares…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#aso-cbct","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"4. ASO_CBCT","x":"4.1 The two modes Semi-Automated Fully-Automated Input scans + fiducials .mrk.json scans alone (or DICOM) Steps SEMI_ASO_CBCT PRE_ASO_CBCT → ALI_CBCT → SEMI_ASO_CBCT Working folder the user's folder two slicer.util.tempDirectory() The automatic mode is therefore just the semi mode preceded by generating the missing landmarks ( CBCT.py:490 ). ASO_CBCT: the Fully-Automated mode only adds the recentering and landmark prediction by ALI_CBCT ( network ) ahead of an identical orientation ( geometric ). 4.2 PRE_ASO_CBCT: recentering, no more network computed Despite its name and its six arguments…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#aso-ios","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"5. ASO_IOS","x":"5.1 Automatic mode: crown centroids a) Segmentation bypass. __BypassCrownseg__ ( IOS.py:196 ) opens each mesh and looks for a PredictedID , UniversalID or Universal_ID array. Already-segmented meshes are copied directly into the segmentation's output folder with the suffix _Seg ; the others go to the input folder to be segmented, with exotic formats converted to .vtk along the way. b) DentalModelSeg predicted , via conda ( IOS.py:288 ): conda run -n shapeaxi python -m CrownSegmentationcli \\ None <csv> <out> 0 latest 0 Universal_ID 0 Seg <vtk_folder> <dentalmodelseg_path> Argument order: surf…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#flexreg","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"6. Comparison with FlexReg's orientation","x":"FlexReg_Method/orientation.py and ASO_IOS_utils/pre_icp.py are the same algorithm, copy-pasted . make_vector is identical character for character; the bodies of orientation() and PrePreAso() differ only on three points: ASO_IOS PrePreAso FlexReg orientation Target the gold mesh's centroids , read with the same vtkMeanTeeth three hard-coded points, [[-0.5,-0.5,0], [0,0,0], [0.5,-0.5,0]] Teeth 3 or 4 chosen by the operator, sorted by organizeLandmark 3, 5, 12, 14 hard-coded (UR6, UR4, UL4, UL6) Return (mesh, matrix) mesh alone transformation.py is likewise duplicated between the two (same…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#predit-calcule","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where CBCT recentering geometric (translation) PRE_ASO_CBCT CBCT landmarks (auto mode) network , multi-scale search agents ALI_CBCT Pruning outlier landmarks geometric , distances + angles SEMI_ASO_CBCT Optimal triplet + rotations geometric , random search ≤ 2500 draws SEMI_ASO_CBCT / PRE_ASO_IOS Refinement geometric , rigid VTK ICP same IOS crown segmentation network , multi-view ( DentalModelSeg ) conda run -n shapeaxi Tooth centroids geometric , vertex averaging PRE_ASO_IOS IOS orientation geometric , 2 rotations + translation PrePreAso The two networks that are called are…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#environnement","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"8. Environment","x":"Two distinct regimes, and this is a source of confusion. The CLIs run in Slicer's Python. install_function ( ASO.py:74 ) pip_install s whatever is missing there: itk , torch==2.2.0 , pytorch_lightning , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 and monai at 1.3.2 (Python ≥ 3.10) or 0.7.0 otherwise. Pitfall · Legacy only torch , pytorch_lightning and monai are only used by Net.py and by ALI_CBCT : in semi-CBCT mode and in IOS, ASO installs them for nothing, with the risk of overwriting another module's version of torch (the dialog says so itself: \"Doing it could break other modules\"). Legacy…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#pieges","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug · First half superseded at 3c67957, second half Legacy only The .tfm requirement is fixed in the legacy. An earlier revision of this page said SEMI_ASO_CBCT.py required data[\"tfm\"] and failed with a KeyError on every patient without a .tfm . At 3c67957 it reads data.get(\"tfm\") and registers from the landmarks alone, with an identity as the prior transform ( SEMI_ASO_CBCT/SEMI_ASO_CBCT.py:52-59 , ASO_CBCT_utils/utils.py:666-676 ; commit e703ec0, 2026-09-29). In VISOR no .tfm is read at all and the recentering is folded into the transform it writes ( SADT-VISOR…"},{"u":"ASO/ASO.html#litterature","p":"ASO","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"10. Literature","x":"The reference paper for ASO_CBCT is Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans (Anchling, Hutin, Cevidanes et al., Lecture Notes in Computer Science, 2023). Its description matches the code: ALI_CBCT identifies the landmarks regardless of the input orientation, then the orientation is obtained \"by first aligning 3 randomly chosen landmarks, then refining with ICP.\" Reported errors: < 3° and < 2 mm against experts. The paper does not mention the DenseNet pre-orienter, consistent with the fact that it is no longer connected. For ALI_CBCT itself: Automatic…"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html","p":"ASO: sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"ASO's bibliography: what has been deposited in this folder, what is still to be read online, and the neighborhood, the works that underpin the method without describing the code. ASO_CBCT is published , in a MICCAI workshop proceedings chapter (LNCS 14242, 2023) that it shares with AREG_CBCT : the same text describes the orientation (ASO) and the registration (AREG). The chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript is freely readable on PMC (PMC11104011); PMC blocks automated PDF downloads, so it could not be deposited here (see \"To read online\" ). ASO_IOS has no paper of its…"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"ASO: sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2023_Gillot_ALI_CBCT_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M., Miranda F., Baquero B., Ruellas A., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Anchling L., Hutin N., Biggs E., Yatabe M., Paniagua B., Fillion-Robin J.-C., Allemang D., Bianchi J., Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research, 26(4):560-567, 2023. Journal article, full JATS text (Europe PMC) + plain text: CC-BY-NC-ND 10.1111/ocr.12642 : PMID 36811276, PMCID PMC10440369 2016_Ruellas_common_coordinate_system.pdf Ruellas A.C.O.…"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"ASO: sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Anchling L., Hutin N., Huang Y., Barone S., Roberts S., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Tinawi S., Bianchi J., Yatabe M., Ruellas A., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L. Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans. Lecture Notes in Computer Science 14242, Springer, Cham, 2023, p. 43-58. Joint proceedings of CLIP 2023 (12th International Workshop on Clinical Image-Based Procedures), FAIMI 2023 and EPIMI 2023, held with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8-12, 2023. Eds. Wesarg, Puyol Antón, Baxter et al. ISBN 978-3-031-45248-2 /…"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"ASO: sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"Neighborhood","x":"These works do not describe ASO ; they underpin the method or show it in use. Ruellas 2016, Common 3-dimensional coordinate system . This is the article that defines the common coordinate system toward which ASO orients: it first shows that head orientation changes the displacement values measured in 3D, then proposes building a common frame from landmarks. ASO's orientation \"gold standard,\" and the reference landmarks shipped with the module, come from this convention. It speaks of neither ASO nor deep learning: everything in it is manual. Gillot 2023, ALI_CBCT . The reinforcement-learning…"},{"u":"ASO/SOURCES.html#methode","p":"ASO: sources","t":"ASO","c":"Landmarks","s":"How the full text was retrieved","x":"The chapter is paywalled at Springer, but the author manuscript deposited with the NIH is open access. Two recipes fail on it and one works, noted here to avoid retracing the steps: Route Result on this PMCID pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/.../pdf/ anti-bot HTML BioC API pmcoa.cgi \"No result can be found\": it only serves the open access subset eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=<number> full JATS, body included curl -sL \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=11104011\" Verified This is how the .xml and .txt files in this folder were obtained. The…"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Project report: 45th NA-MIC Project Week, Boston, 2026. Category: Infrastructure. A statement of intent, not of state This report announces llama3:latest where the code hardcodes qwen3:8b , and its \"next steps\" (LLM integration, deployment inside Slicer) are already achieved in the repository. Read it as a dated statement of intent, not as a description of the module as it stands. See the pipeline page for the actual state. Investigators Name Affiliation Country Paul Dumont University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Alexandre Buisson University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Juan…"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#github","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Github Repository","x":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#financement","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Funding Source(s)","x":"R01DE024450"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#description","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Project Description","x":"Problem : The Slicer Automated Dental Tools extension provides robust craniofacial analysis, but complex module selection and parameter tuning create a steep learning curve for users. Solution : This project introduces an AI Agent and chatbot UI integrated into 3D Slicer to streamline the workflow. By allowing drag-and-drop inputs and natural language prompts, users can easily request complex tasks (e.g., segmentation, landmarking, or orientation on CBCT/IOS). The agent autonomously translates these requests into actions, selecting the right tools and automatically configuring the parameters…"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#objectif","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Objective","x":"Local LLM Integration: Uses Ollama to run a lightweight local model (currently llama3:latest ). Cross-Encoder Retrieval: the system leverages a Cross-Encoder. It directly scores the semantic relevance between the user's query and tool use cases to retrieve the top 3 most appropriate modules. Autonomous Execution: The LLM analyzes the retrieved context, selects the optimal tool, extracts the required parameters from the user's text, and automatically triggers the execution within Slicer."},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#approche","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Build the core backend pipeline integrating the Cross-Encoder retrieval model and the local LLM. Deploy the pipeline within 3D Slicer and connect it to the user-facing chatbot UI. Implement an autonomous feedback loop. The model will verify if the selected Slicer tools executed successfully or encountered an error, and provide real-time feedback to the user."},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#progres","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Current Progress The backend retrieval system is completely operational. The Cross-Encoder model reliably identifies and selects the appropriate Slicer tool from natural language input. Next Steps LLM Integration: Implement the logic for the local LLM to parse the user's prompt, auto-fill the required parameters, and trigger the tool execution. Slicer Deployment: Embed the interactive UI and connect the entire AI pipeline directly within the 3D Slicer environment."},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#illustrations","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Illustrations","x":"Media hosted offsite The screenshots and video are hosted on github.com/user-attachments . They are given as links rather than embedded, so this site stays browsable offline. What would the Slicer user interface look like? : image (504 × 934) Slicer Automated Dental Tools Overview: image (width 800) Tool demo: video Results comparing local LLMs and Cloud-based Models: screenshot from June 26, 2026 (1629 × 82)"},{"u":"Agent/2026_NAMIC_PW45_ai_agent_slicer_dental_tools.html#references","p":"AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Background and References","x":"Github link: https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools This work was supported by the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR) of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01DE024450 ."},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"A natural-language assistant that picks a module from the extension, extracts its parameters, builds the command line, and launches it. No medical neural network is called here: the Agent only does routing and form-filling. Everything runs locally: an 8B LLM served by Ollama, plus a small cross-encoder to preselect candidate tools. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). VISOR has a server tool called Agent , a router over an external Ollama endpoint, but no client calls it : the AgentRemote panel…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#situation","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Nobody calls Agent. A grep for slicer.modules.agent turns up no caller outside the module itself. It is a terminal entry point. Outbound, there are two levels: Called How Where Agent_CLI slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.agent_cli, ...) ( Agent/Agent.py:778 ) separate CLI process, Slicer Python the target module (ALI_CBCT.py, AMASSS_CLI.py, …) subprocess.run(cli_args) ( Agent/Agent.py:962 ) child process of the widget, Slicer Python Ollama python client ollama , HTTP on 127.0.0.1:11434 local server, launched/installed by the module The inventory of targets is purely declarative: it lives in…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#fichiers","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs Agent/Agent.py widget: chat, drop zone, Ollama installation, confirmation, actual execution, repair loop Slicer Python Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI.py routing + extraction; prints JSON to stdout , executes nothing CLI process, Slicer Python Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI_utils/utils.py manifest, cross-encoder, Ollama call, path resolution, command building same parameter_extraction_improved.py building the extraction prompt, nothing else same parameter_validator.py type coercion, required / enum / range / extension checks same Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml the 16 addressable tools, their…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#modeles","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"3. The models","x":"3.1 The LLM, qwen3:8b via Ollama A single constant, duplicated at both ends: DEFAULT_MODEL = \"qwen3:8b\" ( utils.py:21 ) and MODEL_NAME = \"qwen3:8b\" ( Agent.py:29 ). get_router_model() honors a ROUTER_MODEL environment variable that lets you swap in any Ollama model without touching the code. The same model serves all three calls : routing, parameter extraction, repair. No specialized model, no fine-tuning. Point Value Source Inference engine Ollama (so llama.cpp underneath), local HTTP API import ollama , port 11434 Weight format GGUF : imposed by Ollama, no transformers checkpoint inferred…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#routage","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"4. Routing","x":"Two stages, and the LLM only steps in at the second one. Stage 1, cross-encoder preselection. cross_encoder_retrieve_candidates(manifest, prompt, k=3) builds one document per tool: docs.append(f\"{s['name']}: {s['description']} (tags: {tags})\") scores = model.predict([(user_text, doc) for doc in docs]) ranked = sorted(zip(scores, scripts), reverse=True) return [s for _, s in ranked[:k]] 16 pairs scored, sorted in descending order, hardcoded top-3 ( k=3 is never parameterized). The manifest's priority field (values 1 to 23, with gaps) is never read : the line that would read it is commented…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#extraction","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"5. Parameter extraction","x":"A second LLM call, with a tool already chosen, inside extract_parameters ( utils.py:220 ). Masking technical parameters. Parameters named temp_fold , tmp_folder , temp_folder , log_path or logPath are removed from the spec before building the prompt: no point asking the model to invent a temporary folder. They are reinjected afterward with the value input.temp_folder , i.e. the slicer.util.tempDirectory() created by the widget on every click ( Agent_CLI.py:153 ), and removed from missing_required . The extraction prompt ( parameter_extraction_improved.py:40 ) lists each parameter as - name…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#execution","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"6. Building and executing the command","x":"Yes, the module is actually launched. Unambiguously. The CLI only builds the command. build_cli_args ( utils.py:350 ) merges the manifest's default values with the extracted parameters, then: return [sys.executable, tool_path] + cli sys.executable is the Agent_CLI process's interpreter, i.e. Slicer's Python. For cli_style: positional (14 tools out of 16), the values are concatenated in the manifest's declaration order: positional_order is supported but no entry sets it . For any other cli_style , execution falls into the --flag value branch, iterating over merged : hallucinated out-of-spec…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#reparation","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"7. The repair loop","x":"On a nonzero return code, runToolWithRepair ( Agent.py:943 ) asks the LLM to fix the parameters, at most MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS = 2 times. build_repair_prompt ( utils.py:282 ) sends the parameter definitions, the values used, the full command, and the last 2000 characters of stderr , and asks it to return only the parameters to change. This path runs inside the Slicer process , not the CLI: _proposeRepair adds the Agent_CLI folder to sys.path and reimports Agent_CLI_utils so as not to duplicate the logic. The fix goes back through ParameterValidator then build_cli_args , an old -> new diff is…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#modules","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"8. Addressable modules, and their real state","x":"The 16 entries of Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml (counted with a YAML parser on the file, 2026-11-09). The right-hand column compares the manifest's positional order against the target script's actual argparse signature, checked file by file. Tool (manifest) Target script Manifest / actual params Verdict ali_cbct ALI_CBCT/ALI_CBCT.py 6 / 10 missing spacing , speed_per_scale , agent_fov , spawn_radius ali_ios ALI_IOS/ALI_IOS.py 6 / 10 shift starting at position 5 ( teeth_mg missing) amasss_cli AMASSS_CLI/AMASSS_CLI.py 5 / 12 the script refuses: \"Expected 13 arguments\" areg_cbct…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#modes","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"9. The two modes","x":"Mode ( comboBox ) Agent_CLI output Execution Agent (Automated) JSON {tool, tool_confidence, parameters_confidence, parameters, missing_required, command} yes, after confirmation Ask (Interactive) free text none In Ask mode, build_candidates_block(..., include_parameters=True) appends | parameters:<raw YAML list of dicts> to each line: the Python repr of the manifest's structure gets injected as-is into the system prompt of an \"expert medical image analysis consultant.\" No format=\"json\" ; the response is displayed after stripping the * and # characters. Three names for two modes · partly true…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#garde-fous","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"10. Guardrails","x":"What exists: A mandatory modal confirmation before every execution, listing all the parameters, and a second one before every repair attempt. Blocking on missing required parameters : nothing is launched while missing_required is not empty. Type coercion and choices / min / max / extension-on-folder checks. Masking of temporary folders , filled in from slicer.util.tempDirectory() rather than by the model. Bounding : MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS = 2 , history truncated to MAX_HISTORY_ENTRIES = 40 turns. Global try in main() : stdout always stays parsable JSON, the traceback goes to stderr. What does…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#environnement","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"11. Environment","x":"CheckDependencies ( Agent.py:16 ) runs a slicer.util.pip_install inside Slicer's Python, in this order, which matters : Package Reason for the pin ollama python client only: pip does not provide the server pyyaml reading the manifest transformers<4.53.0 regression NameError: name 'nn' is not defined in integrations/accelerate.py , breaks the cross-encoder import numpy<2 numpy ≥ 2 breaks the ABI of the pip torch wheels ( RuntimeError: Numpy is not available ) sentence-transformers pulls in torch; installed last so as not to bump the pins Agent_CLI.py runs in a separate process but shares…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#pieges","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"12. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"resolve_tool_path finds nothing in this repository (§8). This is blocker number one. Possible fixes: create a CLI files folder with links, set AGENT_CLI_TOOLS_DIR , or put repository-relative paths in the manifest and extend the search. 14 signatures out of 16 are wrong , and the shift is silent. batchdentalseg and clic are not CLIs. cli_style: ui_module is handled nowhere: it falls into the --flag branch, which produces a command that cannot work on a scripted Slicer module. input.folders.split(\",\") breaks any path containing a comma, both for the folder list and for the join(\",\") on the…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#predit-calcule","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"13. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Preselection of the 3 candidates network (cross-encoder MiniLM-L6, 22.7 M) Agent_CLI process, CPU or GPU depending on torch Final choice of tool LLM qwen3:8b, constrained JSON Ollama server Parameter extraction LLM qwen3:8b, constrained JSON Ollama server Path resolution LLM , from FOLDERS_CONTEXT Ollama server Validation / coercion deterministic , pure Python Agent_CLI Temporary folders deterministic , slicer.util.tempDirectory() widget Command building deterministic , manifest order Agent_CLI Repair after failure LLM qwen3:8b, from stderr Slicer process Environment error…"},{"u":"Agent/Agent.html#litterature","p":"Agent: full pipeline","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"14. Literature","x":"No paper · unchanged Also true in VISOR . The server tool is the same approach (routing and form filling). Its README gives a provenance section naming the legacy files it was ported from, not a publication ( SADT-VISOR tools/Agent/README.md:316-324 ). Neither on this module nor on the approach. A search for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools + LLM agent turns up only the repository's documentation and general literature on LLMs in dentistry, unrelated to this code. The only real external references are those of the two models used, both generic and not trained for this domain. Qwen3 on Ollama…"},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html","p":"Agent: sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Unpublished. No paper covers this module or its approach. There is, however, an official record that had to be found: a NA-MIC project report. The report describes the intent, not the state A NA-MIC Project Week report (PW45, Boston, 2026) signed by its authors was retrieved here. It describes the module in its intent, not in its actual state: it announces llama3:latest where the code hardcodes qwen3:8b , and its \"next step\" (LLM integration, deployment inside Slicer) is already done in the repository. The other useful references are purely technical: the model served by Ollama , the…"},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"Agent: sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID PW45 report Dumont P., Buisson A., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L., Pieper S. AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. 45th NA-MIC Project Week, Boston, 2026. Funded by NIDCR R01DE024450. NA-MIC project report projectweek.na-mic.org/PW45_2026_Boston .pdf Qwen Team. Qwen3 Technical Report. arXiv:2505.09388, 14 May 2025. The routing and extraction model ( qwen3:8b , default Ollama tag: 5.2 GB, Q4_K_M, 8.19 G parameters). Technical report / arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.09388 .pdf Wang W., Wei F., Dong L., Bao H., Yang N., Zhou M. MiniLM: Deep Self-Attention Distillation for…"},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"Agent: sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Nothing. No paywalled work was identified for this module. Model cards, worth reading alongside this page: ollama.com/library/qwen3 (tags, sizes, quantizations) and huggingface.co/cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 ."},{"u":"Agent/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"Agent: sources","t":"Agent","c":"Texte","s":"Neighborhood","x":"None of the following works describe this module . MS MARCO (Bajaj, retrieved): the reranker was trained to rank web passages for Bing queries. Here it is fed Slicer module cards and operator prompts: a domain and a register it was not trained for, and for which no evaluation exists in the repository. Qwen3 (retrieved): the generic model's technical report. Neither the module's system prompt, nor its constrained JSON output format, nor its repair loop appear in it, and no temperature , seed , num_ctx , or think is passed by the code. Ollama and llama.cpp , the server and inference engine…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"Batch-crops a folder of volumes to a ROI box ( .mrk.json ) by extracting indices in the original grid: no interpolation, no network, no conda. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, is described in VISOR: how this tool runs now . VISOR references read SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D/pipeline.py:272 : that is tools/AutoCrop3D/src/sadt_autocrop3d/pipeline.py at 6ba2a78. VISOR-serve is e9a7e5d, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote is 19b95e8. Legacy lines are written…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#situation","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls AutoCrop3D, and AutoCrop3D calls nothing but Slicer. It is declared in CMakeLists.txt:41 and that's it. In practice it sits upstream of the segmenters ( AMASSS , BATCHDENTALSEG) or downstream of them: the \"generate VTK\" mode only triggers if the output file name contains seg , which presupposes it's being handed labelmaps already produced elsewhere. AutoCrop3D is called by no other module in the repository and calls nothing but Slicer; its place upstream or downstream of the segmenters is a matter of usage, not code. No step is predicted by a network: both…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#fichiers","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs AutoCrop3D.py widget, input validation, Crop Volume path Slicer's Python AutoCrop3D_CLI.py the main path, 168 lines, all the cropping Slicer's Python ( SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI ) FilesType.py Search (extension glob) and ChangeKeyDict (patient→ROI pairing) same GenerateVTKfromSeg.py marching cubes on the cropped labelmap same CropCBCT.py dead code , says so itself: !!! UNUSED !!! – The CLI is a scripted CLI ( AutoCrop3D/Crop_Volumes_CLI/CMakeLists.txt ), so it's launched by slicer.cli.run inside Slicer's Python. No conda environment, unlike AMASSS or ALI."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#boite-crop","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"3. What defines the crop box","x":"A markups ROI json file ( .mrk.json ), read as follows ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:63-68 ): ROI = json.load(open(roi_path))['markups'][0] roi_center = np.array(ROI['center']) roi_size = np.array(ROI['size']) lower = roi_center - roi_size / 2 upper = roi_center + roi_size / 2 Three fields of the json are ignored , and that's the main source of surprises: coordinateSystem . The test file ROI.mrk.zip (in AutoCrop3D/Crop_Volumes_UI/Resources/testfiles/AutoCrop3D/ ) declares \"coordinateSystem\": \"LPS\" , which is the repository's convention (every markups writer hardcodes \"LPS\" : ALI_CBCT , ASO_CBCT…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#application","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"4. How the box is applied: index extraction, no resampling","x":"This is the key point: no interpolation . The physical → index conversion followed by the crop ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:70-80 , slice at :96-98 ): lower = np.array(img.TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex(lower)).astype(int) upper = np.array(img.TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex(upper)).astype(int) for i in range(3): if lower[i] > upper[i]: lower[i], upper[i] = upper[i], lower[i] lower = [max(0, l) for l in lower] upper = [min(img_size[i], u) for i, u in enumerate(upper)] img_roi = img[lower[0]:upper[0], lower[1]:upper[1], lower[2]:upper[2]] The swap is not cosmetic.…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#crop-volume","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"5. The alternative path: Slicer's Crop Volume module","x":"processCropVolume() ( AutoCrop3D.py:723-808 ), activated by the \"Use module 'Crop Volume' for tilted images\" checkbox (commit 9d2bbe8 ). It runs inside the widget , not in the CLI: crop_volume_logic= slicer.modules.cropvolume.logic() parameters = slicer.vtkMRMLCropVolumeParametersNode() parameters.SetInputVolumeNodeID(input_volume.GetID()) parameters.SetROINodeID(roi_node.GetID()) parameters.SetOutputVolumeNodeID(output_volume.GetID()) slicer.mrmlScene.AddNode(parameters) crop_volume_logic.Apply(parameters) ( AutoCrop3D.py:769-778 .) Only the three IDs are set: interpolation, isotropy, and…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#batch","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"6. Batch mode and patient ↔ ROI pairing","x":"Two regimes, discriminated by len(roi_list['.mrk.json']) >1 ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:46,56 ): a single ROI → applied to every volume found; several ROIs → ChangeKeyDict builds {basename.split('_')[0]: path} ( FilesType.py:75-78 ) and each volume looks up its key; on failure → logger.warning('No ROI for patient:') and continue ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:57-61 ). Both sides compute the key differently : Side Rule Example MA_0001_T1_Scan.nii.gz / MA_0001_ROI.mrk.json volume chain of .split() ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:52 ) on _Scan _scan _Seg _seg _Or _OR _MAND _MD _MAX _MX _CB _lm _T2 _T1 _Cl then .split('.')[0]…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#vtk","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"7. Generating a VTK mesh from a segmentation","x":"Triggered by if \"seg\" in scan_out_path.lower() ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:143 ): the test is against the full output path , so a parent folder named Segmentations/ is enough to trigger it on every file. A failure is caught by except Exception and only logged at debug level ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:144-147 ). convertNiftiToVTK ( GenerateVTKfromSeg.py:15 ): padding() : sitk.ConstantPad(input_image, padding_size, [0]*input_image.GetDimension()) with padding_size = [50, 50, 10] ( GenerateVTKfromSeg.py:83-85 ). The actual signature verified: ConstantPad(image, padLowerBound, padUpperBound, constant) , the…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#sorties","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"8. Outputs","x":"Path Name produced Construction CLI <stem>_<suffix><ext> basename.split('.')[0] + \"_\" + suffix + key , where key is the matched search extension ( .nii.gz , …) CLI, segmentation <stem>_<suffix>_vtk.vtk same Crop Volume <basename with .nii.gz → _<suffix>.nii.gz> basename.replace('.nii.gz', f'_{suffix}.nii.gz') : hardcoded to .nii.gz Default suffix: cropped ( AutoCrop3D.ui:331 ). The CLI names are built at AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:118-124 , the Crop Volume name at AutoCrop3D.py:699 . The input folder tree is reproduced in the output folder via os.path.relpath . No transform file is produced."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#predit-calcule","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"9. Predicted vs. computed","x":"Block Nature Where Reading the ROI computed json, no computation CLI Physical → index computed TransformPhysicalPointToContinuousIndex (ITK) CLI Cropping computed SimpleITK slicing, no interpolation CLI \"Tilted\" cropping computed resampling via cropVolumeLogic.Apply widget VTK surface computed marching cubes + laplacian smoothing CLI No network. No part of this module learns or predicts anything."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#environnement","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"10. Environment","x":"Nothing to install. The module uses only what Slicer already ships with: SimpleITK , numpy , vtk , qt . pip_install was imported but never called when this page was written; checked again on 2026-10-12, the import has been removed. No CUDA, no WSL, no conda."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#pieges","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"11. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Pitfall: ROI folder with a single file Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . Legacy: the table is built only if more than one ROI is found, then json.load(open(roi_path)) receives the folder ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:46,56,63@3c67957 ). VISOR: a file, or a folder holding exactly one ROI, is applied to every scan ( SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D/__init__.py:342-343 ). A ROI folder containing exactly one file → crash. ROI_dict is only built if len(ROIList) > 1 ; otherwise ROI_Path stays the folder , and json.load(open(ROI_Path)) raises IsADirectoryError . The Crop Volume path, by contrast, tests…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#litterature","p":"AutoCrop3D","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"12. Literature","x":"No paper covers AutoCrop3D. It's a batch-processing utility written for the lab (Jeanne Claret, UoM/DCBIA), documented only by the README.md:331 section and by the help of Slicer's Crop Volume module, whose semantics it reuses: Crop Volume: Slicer documentation Crop Volume Sequence, Slicer documentation (cited by the README as the limitation AutoCrop3D works around: files too heavy to load as a sequence) SlicerAutomatedDentalTools The CLI's documentation-url points to https://github.com/Jeanneclre/DCBIA-code , a personal repository that is not the project's own. Full bibliography…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html","p":"AutoCrop3D: sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoCrop3D bibliography: nothing describes it, two articles cite it in a single sentence. Unpublished. No article, abstract, poster, or Project Week report describes AutoCrop3D. Two articles cite it in passing as a tool they used, one sentence each, without describing either how it works or its parameters; they are retrieved here because they are the only published trace of the module's existence. The documentation remains that of the repository and that of Slicer's Crop Volume module, whose semantics AutoCrop3D reuses."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AutoCrop3D: sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Barone_condylar_remodelling_class_III.xml / .txt Barone S., Cevidanes L., Bianchi J., Gonçalves J.R., Giudice A. Deep Learning-Based Three-Dimensional Analysis Reveals Distinct Patterns of Condylar Remodelling After Orthognathic Surgery in Skeletal Class III Patients. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research 28(3):441-448, 2025. Journal article, full JATS text (Europe PMC, CC BY-NC-ND) 10.1111/ocr.12895 : PMCID PMC12056474 2025_Caleme_MR2CBCT_TMJ_case_series.xml / .txt Caleme E.D., Cevidanes L., Mattos C., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Barone S., Gaydamour A., Tulissi…"},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AutoCrop3D: sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Nothing. No paywalled work was identified on this module."},{"u":"AutoCrop3D/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AutoCrop3D: sources","t":"AutoCrop3D","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Neighboring resources","x":"These resources do not describe AutoCrop3D ; they document what it reuses the semantics of, or what it works around. Crop Volume, Slicer documentation : the reference for the expected behavior (ROI, spacing scale , interpolation, isotropic ). Crop Volume Sequence, Slicer documentation : cited by the repository's README as the limitation AutoCrop3D works around (files too heavy to load as a sequence). DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools : the module's repository. The CLI's documentation-url , meanwhile, points to Jeanneclre/DCBIA-code , the author's personal repository, which is not the…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"Applies one or more ITK transformations, already computed elsewhere, in batch to a folder of volumes and landmark files: no network, no conda, no GPU. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, is described in VISOR: how this tool runs now . VISOR references read SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pipeline.py:127 : that is tools/AutoMatrix/src/sadt_automatrix/pipeline.py at 6ba2a78. VISOR-serve is e9a7e5d, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote is 19b95e8. Legacy lines are…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#situation","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"AutoMatrix is called by no other module in the repository. It is, however, a consumer of the .tfm files produced by the registration modules: Producer File produced Transformation type AREG_CBCT.py:52 <out>/<Region>/<patient>_OutReg/<patient>_<CB|MAND|MAX><add_name>_matrix.tfm affine / rigid SEMI_ASO_CBCT.py:122 <patient>_<add_name>_transform.tfm sitk.CompositeTransform ( ASO_CBCT/ASO_CBCT_utils/utils.py:719-723 ) MRI2CBCT_REGISTER.py:96 <pid>/<pid>_<side>_MRI_transform[_tag].tfm ( :34-36 ) affine, already RAS→LPS-conjugated and inverted ( MRI2CBCT_core/frames.py:39-50 ) Review.py:607 and…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#fichiers","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Note Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . SADT-VISOR has no ADTLib. The patient key comes from sadt_areg_common.pairing ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pyproject.toml:16 , SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pipeline.py:40-42 ) and progress.py is a copy kept inside the tool ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/progress.py:8-9 ). Part of this module now lives in the shared layer. ADTLib centralises what several modules carried in copies: from here, theming.py , method.py , io/fs.py and the contract of method.py . Its page records how many copies each file replaced. File Role Where it runs AutoMatrix.py widget…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#formats","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"3. Transformation formats read, and those that aren't","x":"The UI ( AutoMatrix.py:829,832 ) and the README ( README.md:416-418 ) advertise .npy , .h5 , .tfm , .mat , .txt . GetPatients collects all of them. The CLI then does, with no distinction ( Automatrix_CLI.py:257-261 ): try: tfm = sitk.ReadTransform(matrix) except Exception as e: logger.error(f\"ERROR reading transform {matrix}: {e}\") continue sitk.ReadTransform only knows ITK's TransformIO classes. Tested with Slicer 5.12's SimpleITK 2.5.5: Extension Actual result .tfm , .txt OK if and only if it's an Insight Transform File V1.0 . A text file containing a raw 4×4 matrix is rejected: ITK ERROR…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#convention","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"4. The transformation convention: the point that breaks everything","x":"4.1 Direction of the transformation ITK is fixed → moving : a sitk.Transform passed to a resampler maps the output physical point to the input physical point to sample from. The visible content therefore moves according to T⁻¹ . The code exploits this on both sides, and it is consistent: Volumes ( Automatrix_CLI.py:182 ): resampler.SetTransform(transform) , the transformation is passed as-is, the content moves by T⁻¹ . Landmarks ( Automatrix_CLI.py:149,156 ): tfm_inverted = transform.GetInverse() point['position'] = list(tfm_inverted.TransformPoint(point['position'])) T⁻¹ is explicitly…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#types-donnees","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"5. Data types handled","x":"GetPatients collects .vtk .vtp .stl .off .obj .nii .nii.gz .nrrd .mrk.json ( Automatrix_CLI.py:45 ). The CLI then only distinguishes two cases ( :220 , :275 ): is_landmark = scan.endswith(\".mrk.json\") ... image = sitk.ReadImage(scan) # everything else Confirmed bug Legacy (3c67957); changed in VISOR . The legacy still accepts .vtk .vtp .stl .off .obj in its search ( Automatrix_CLI.py:45@3c67957 ) and logs the failure ( :283-285 ). The port has no mesh extension ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pipeline.py:54 ), no panel offers them and no counter counts them. A folder holding only meshes raises No…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#reechantillonnage","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"6. Resampling and reference image","x":"resampler.SetInterpolator(sitk.sitkNearestNeighbor if is_seg else sitk.sitkLinear) resampler.SetDefaultPixelValue(0) if reference is not None: resampler.SetReferenceImage(reference) ( Automatrix_CLI.py:183-187 .) The interpolator comes from the \"Check this box if using segmentations\" checkbox ( is_seg , :206 ). Nearest neighbor for a labelmap, linear otherwise: correct, and it's up to the user to declare it, nothing is auto-detected. The output grid is chosen as follows ( Automatrix_CLI.py:165-175 and :276-279 ), in decreasing priority: Composite transformation → the reference is guessed…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#mirror","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"7. Mirror mode","x":"Mirror() ( AutoMatrix.py:307 ) and DownloadMirror() ( :384-405 ) download Mirror.zip from a personal GitHub release ( GaelleLeroux/DCBIA_Apply_matrix , ADT/ADTLib/model_registry.py:80-82 ), unzip it into <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/Mirror_matrix/ and fills the matrix field with Mirror/Matrix_mirror.tfm . The file's actual content: #Insight Transform File V1.0 Transform: AffineTransform_double_3_3 Parameters: -1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 FixedParameters: 0 0 0 Pitfall Legacy statement (3c67957); a second consequence in VISOR. In the port a reflection applied with no reference leaves the output…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#appariement","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"8. File ↔ matrix matching","x":"GetPatients ( Automatrix_CLI.py:33-142 ) builds {patient_key: {'scan': [...], 'matrix': [...]}} . The key is obtained through a chain of .split() calls that is not the same on both sides : Tokens removed (in order) scan ( :77-79 ) _Seg _seg _Scan _scan _Or _OR _MAND _MD _MAX _MX _CB _lm _T2 _T1 _Cl _MR , then .split('.')[0] , then _T0 … _T49 matrix ( :130-133 ) _SegOr _Left _left _Right _right _Or _OR _MAND _MD _MAX _MX _CB _lm _T2 _T1 _Cl _MA _Mir _mir _Mirror _mirror _MR , then .split('.')[0] , then _T0 … _T49 GetPatients removes different tokens from the scan name and the matrix name to…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#sorties","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"9. Outputs","x":"matrix_suffix = f\"_{Path(matrix).stem}\" if args.matrix_name == \"True\" else \"\" out_suffix = f\"{args.suffix}{matrix_suffix}\" out_file = outpath.split(extension_scan)[0] + out_suffix + extension_scan ( Automatrix_CLI.py:263-264,281 .) The input directory tree is reproduced in the output folder ( scan.replace(args.input_patient, args.output_folder) , :224 ). Default suffix _apply ( AutoMatrix.ui:325 ), checkBoxMatrixName checked by default ( AutoMatrix.ui:337-344 ). Pitfall Also true in VISOR , with a different default. The whole transform stem is appended ( SADT-VISOR…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#predit-calcule","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"10. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Transform reading computed ITK TransformIO CLI File ↔ matrix matching computed string splitting CLI (and a diverging copy in the widget) Volumes computed sitk.ResampleImageFilter , linear or nearest neighbor CLI Landmarks computed T⁻¹.TransformPoint per point CLI Mirror matrix computed fixed .tfm downloaded, diag(-1,1,1) GitHub release No neural network anywhere."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#environnement","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"11. Environment","x":"Nothing to install: SimpleITK , numpy , vtk , qt are already in Slicer. No conda, no CUDA. The only network access is the mirror matrix download, and only if the Mirror checkbox is checked. Confirmed bug Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . The legacy mechanism, for the record, is emit_event(PATIENT_DONE) ( Automatrix_CLI.py:27,287@3c67957 ), defined in ADT/ADTLib/progress_protocol.py : the pulse 0, 2, 0 with a PULSE_PAUSE of 0.2 s ( :41,59 ), read as 200 by isProgress ( Progress.py:40-47@3c67957 ). In the port, progress.report(index, total, \"patient\") appends one JSON line to the…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#pieges","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"12. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Summary Legacy (3c67957); status in VISOR , bullet by bullet. Still true: no composition, coordinateSystem never read, non-defined landmarks left in the old frame ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pipeline.py:95-96 , run on 2026-11-09: an undefined point stayed at [7,7,7] ), .npy and meshes unsupported (no longer advertised). Not true: .txt with a raw 4x4 matrix (accepted by a fallback), overwriting when the name option is off, truncation by .split(extension_scan)[0] (replaced by Path and relpath , SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/__init__.py:323-342 ). Legacy only: OnEndProcess , CheckGoodEntre…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#litterature","p":"AutoMatrix","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"13. Literature","x":"No paper covers AutoMatrix: it's a batch-application utility written for the lab (Gaëlle Leroux, UoM/DCBIA). The scientific content lives entirely in the modules that produce the matrices, AREG, ASO , MRI2CBCT , FlexReg , and is covered in their respective pages. For the transformation convention, the up-to-date reference is ITK / Slicer documentation rather than a paper: Transforms: Slicer user guide (fixed→moving direction, Slicer's RAS vs. ITK's LPS) Coordinate systems: Slicer developer guide SimpleITK: Transforms and Resampling SlicerAutomatedDentalTools DCBIA_Apply_matrix : the original…"},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html","p":"AutoMatrix: sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoMatrix's bibliography: search done, negative result, no work describes or cites the tool. Unpublished, and nothing cites it. No paper, abstract, poster, thesis or Project Week report covers AutoMatrix, and none names it either. It's a batch-application utility written for the lab (Gaëlle Leroux, UoM/DCBIA): the scientific content is entirely in the modules that produce the matrices, AREG, ASO , MRI2CBCT , FlexReg , and is covered in their respective pages."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"AutoMatrix: sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"Nothing. There is nothing to retrieve."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"AutoMatrix: sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Nothing."},{"u":"AutoMatrix/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"AutoMatrix: sources","t":"AutoMatrix","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Neighborhood","x":"For the transformation convention, the up-to-date reference is documentation, not a paper. None of these resources describe AutoMatrix . Transforms, Slicer user guide , fixed→moving direction, Slicer's RAS versus ITK's LPS . It's the one thing a successor should have read before touching this module. Coordinate systems: Slicer developer guide SimpleITK: Transforms and Resampling DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools : the module's repository. GaelleLeroux/DCBIA_Apply_matrix : the original repository, which still hosts the mirror matrix and the test sets cited by the README. Searched for…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"Queue that drives nnUNetv2_predict over a batch of CT/CBCT scans, with four interchangeable weight bundles, DentalSegmentator and its three variants, plus all the guard-rail, geometry-restoration, and export work around them. Batch dento-maxillofacial segmentation of CT/CBCT scans. The module is not a network: it is a queue and an orchestrator around nnUNetv2_predict , with four interchangeable weight bundles. All the learning happens outside this repository; what is written here is the model choice, the memory guard-rails, the geometry restoration, and the exports. Two code bases Everything…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#situation","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"No other module in the repository calls BatchDentalSeg , and BatchDentalSeg does not call any other module in the repository. It is a terminal tool. It does, however, depend on an external Slicer extension , NNUNet ( SlicerNNUNetLib ), from which it uses three classes. Called Origin Role SlicerNNUNetLib.InstallLogic NNUNet extension setupPythonRequirements() : installs nnunetv2 in Slicer's Python SlicerNNUNetLib.Parameter NNUNet extension validates the model directory tree and builds the command line SlicerNNUNetLib.SegmentationLogic NNUNet extension writes the volume as NIfTI into a…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#fichiers","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Size BATCHDENTALSEG.py wraps ScriptedLoadableModule , only instantiates the widget 88 lines SegmentationWidget.py everything : UI, queue, model choice, memory guard, crop, labels, exports 3037 lines Queue.py persistent queue, QueueItem / SegmentationQueue 204 lines PythonDependencyChecker.py download and freshness check for the DentalSegmentator weights 192 lines Utils.py , Signal.py , IconPath.py Qt helpers Resources/ML/ weight root; the repository ships Dataset111_453CT/.../dataset.json and plans.json Everything runs inside Slicer's Python . nnUNetv2_predict is launched as a…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#entrees","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"3. Inputs, folder traversal, naming","x":"3.1 What is accepted listVolumes() ( Queue.py:28 ) does a non-recursive glob over four patterns: *.nii , *.nii.gz , *.gipl , *.gipl.gz . Subfolders are not visited, and NRRD is not recognized : Slicer's default format, and a format the repository's other modules commonly produce. volumeStem() strips the compound suffix: case01.nii.gz → case01 (a Path.stem would leave case01.nii ). 3.2 The queue One entry = one scan , not one folder ( QueueItem , Queue.py:52 ): inputPath , outputDir , model , device , status , error , durationSec , autoCrop . Several folders with different models can…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#modeles","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"4. The four models","x":"4.1 What the combo box selects onApplyClickedForVolume() ( SegmentationWidget.py:1827 ) is an if/elif with four near-identical branches. Each builds the same object: Parameter(folds=\"0\", modelPath=basePath, device=self.deviceComboBox.currentText) The combo box's four entries all go through the same code; only the dataset folder and the label table change. Name in the combo box Folder under Resources/ML Weight origin DentalSegmentator Dataset111_453CT/nnUNetTrainer__nnUNetPlans__3d_fullres zip of the latest release of gaudot/SlicerDentalSegmentator , resolved via the GitHub API…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#coeur","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"5. The non-learned core","x":"The only learning happens in a single step; everything else, preprocessing, reconstruction, the replay loop, is computed. 5.1 Label map reconstruction and geometry restoration _buildLabelArray() ( SegmentationWidget.py:2010 ): a single ExportSegmentsToLabelmapNode , with the explicit list of segmentId (which pins down the correspondence \"exported value i +1 ↔ segIds[i] \"), then a numpy LUT that remaps to the official values in one pass ( lut[exported] ). The earlier version rasterized the full extent of the volume once per segment: 55 rasterizations of a full-mouth CBCT for UniversalLab. 5.2…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#sorties","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"6. Outputs","x":"onInferenceFinished always writes, regardless of which formats are checked, <outputFolder>/<stem>_Segmentation.nii.gz : a label map with the values from _get_active_label_map , on the geometry (spacing, origin, IJKToRAS ) of the original volume, uncropped if a crop took place. This is the file expectedOutputPath() looks for to decide a scan has already been processed. Additional formats, checkboxes, all written into the same folder: Format Function Naming Details STL, OBJ ExportSegmentsClosedSurfaceRepresentationToFiles one file per segment smoothing 1.0, no merging VTK (per label)…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#predit","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"7. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Preprocessing (transposition, resampling, CTNormalization ) computed deterministic, driven by plans.json nnUNetv2_predict , child process Segmentation predicted PlainConvUNet 3D network, 1 fold, no TTA same Automatic crop computed geometric (air threshold + bounding box) widget, numpy Restoration onto the original grid computed index offset, no interpolation widget, numpy Label map reconstruction computed single export + LUT widget, VTK/numpy Mirror correction computed geometric (sagittal plane on the incisors) widget, numpy Meshes computed marching cubes + windowed-sinc +…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#environnement","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"8. Environment","x":"Installed once per session , on the first Apply , by _runSetupThenStartQueue() ( SegmentationWidget.py:1749 ): pip install light-the-torch , then python -m light_the_torch install torch torchvision : ltt picks the CUDA wheel matching the detected driver, no version is pinned ; pip install numexpr>=2.10.2 ; pip install numpy<2.0 numexpr>=2.10.2 psutil , via PipRunner , a QProcess that returns control to the UI; SlicerNNUNetLib.InstallLogic().setupPythonRequirements() : nnunetv2 , outside this repository; PythonDependencyChecker.downloadWeightsIfNeeded() : DentalSegmentator weights only.…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#pieges","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug: NasoMaxillaDentSeg (legacy only) Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR. VISOR renames no segment: the table is Upper Skull 1, Mandible 2, Maxilla 3, Upper Teeth 4, Lower Teeth 5, Mandibular canal 6 ( SADT-VISOR sadt_batchdentalseg/catalogs.py:35-42 ) and is what names the per-label files ( pipeline.py:199-206 ). The legacy analysis follows. The segment names and the label table diverge for NasoMaxillaDentSeg. _get_active_label_map ( SegmentationWidget.py:1977-1987 ) gives Maxilla = 3 , Upper Teeth = 4 , Lower Teeth = 5 , Mandibular canal = 6 , while _updateSegmentationDisplay…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#litterature","p":"BatchDentalSeg","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"10. Literature","x":"DentalSegmentator is published: Dot G., Chaurasia A., Dubois G., Savoldelli C., Haghighat S., Azimian S., Rahbar Taramsari A., Sivaramakrishnan G., Issa J., Dubey A., Schouman T., Gajny L. DentalSegmentator: robust open source deep learning-based CT and CBCT image segmentation. Journal of Dentistry 147:105130, 2024. doi:10.1016/j.jdent.2024.105130 , PMID 38878813 nnU-Net v2 (v2.2 per the Zenodo notice for the weights), 470 CT and CBCT scans from 7 institutions for training, 5 structures, tested hold-out on 256 scans (133 internal, 123 external): DSC 92.2 ± 6.3% internal, 94.2 ± 7.4%…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg: sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"What is published, what is not, and what has been retrieved in this folder. The module is partially published : of the four models it bundles, only DentalSegmentator has a paper, and on that model the code and the paper agree. PediatricDentalSegmentator, UniversalLabDentalSegmentator, and NasoMaxillaDentSegmentator are not attached to any publication : a search was done, and nothing turned up (details at the end of this file). The module itself (queuing, memory management, batch UI) is described nowhere."},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"BatchDentalSeg: sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Dot_DentalSegmentator_JDent.pdf Dot G., Chaurasia A., Dubois G., Savoldelli C., Haghighat S., Azimian S., Rahbar Taramsari A., Sivaramakrishnan G., Issa J., Dubey A., Schouman T., Gajny L. DentalSegmentator: Robust open source deep learning-based CT and CBCT image segmentation. Journal of Dentistry 147:105130, 2024. Author's accepted version deposited on HAL ( hal-05454422 , 17 p., author layout; the manuscript carries the preprint's title, \"robust deep learning-based CBCT image segmentation,\" and the HAL notice mistakenly names the journal International…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"BatchDentalSeg: sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"To consult online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Isensee F., Jaeger P.F., Kohl S.A.A., Petersen J., Maier-Hein K.H. nnU-Net : a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical image segmentation. Nature Methods 18(2):203-211, 2021. Reference version to cite (the one cited by the repository's README); only the arXiv preprint is freely redistributable. nature.com 10.1038/s41592-020-01008-z Mattos C.T., Mota Júnior S.L., Teodoro A.B., Lorenzoni D.C., Cury-Saramago A.A., Cevidanes L. 705, Open-Source AI for Efficient 3D Orthodontic Workflows: Pediatric and Adult Dental Segmentation in Daily Practice. Journal of…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#variantes-non-publiees","p":"BatchDentalSeg: sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"The three variants: nothing published","x":"No publication. What was searched, with no result: Europe PMC, queries PediatricDentalSegmentator , UniversalLabDentalSegmentator , NasoMaxillaDentSegmentator , pediatric dental segmentator mixed dentition CBCT nnU-Net : 0 results every time. OpenAlex on the same names: nothing, other than the EJWF conference abstract above. Web search on each model name and on \"Cevidanes / DCBIA + pediatric CBCT + primary teeth + 513\": the only hits were the repository's own README and work from other, unrelated teams (e.g. Baraka et al., Multi-Architecture deep learning for CBCT segmentation of dental hard…"},{"u":"BatchDentalSeg/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"BatchDentalSeg: sources","t":"BatchDentalSeg","c":"Segmentation","s":"Adjacent work","x":"These works do not describe this module ; they evaluate DentalSegmentator from the outside, or provide its foundation. nnU-Net (Isensee et al. 2021): the foundation of all four models. Describes the self-configuring method, not these models or their weights. Sinard et al. 2025 (file 2025_Sinard_DL_impacted_teeth_CBCT.xml ): compares four segmentation methods, including DentalSegmentator, on CBCT scans of patients with multiple impacted teeth. An external evaluation of the original model; says nothing about the three variants nor about the batch module. The corresponding thesis is open access…"},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"NA-MIC Project Week 43 project page (Montreal, 2025): it takes CLIC as an existing building block and prototypes an extension to classify the severity of root resorption in adjacent teeth. Note NA-MIC project page reproduced here as-is (formatting only): the only primary source found on CLIC, see SOURCES.md . This is not a publication: a project in progress, presented by its authors. Field Value layout pw43-project permalink /:path/ project_title Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption category Segmentation /…"},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html#description","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Project Description","x":"The CLIC module uses a Mask R-CNN segmentation model to locate and classify impacted canines in CBCT scans. This project extends CLIC by developing a supervised model to automatically classify the severity of root resorption in teeth adjacent to impacted canines. Objective Segment impacted canines using the existing CLIC module. Extract adjacent teeth volumes for analysis. Assemble an annotated dataset of adjacent teeth with clinician‐provided resorption severity scores. Train a classification model to predict root resorption severity from segmented volumes. Integrate and visualize…"},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html#illustrations","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Illustrations","x":"Figure 1: Impacted canine segmentation results from CLIC. Image hosted on GitHub, not embedded here so the page stays readable offline: view the image on GitHub ."},{"u":"CLIC/2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.html#references","p":"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Background and References","x":"No response"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"CLIC segments a CBCT slice by slice with a Mask R-CNN and classifies each detected instance as a buccal, bicortical, or palatal impacted canine: classification and localization come out of the same network pass. Classification and Localization of Impacted Canines. 2D slice-by-slice instance segmentation of a CBCT by a torchvision Mask R-CNN , whose predicted class encodes the intraosseous position of the impacted canine: buccal, bicortical, palatal . The module's name promises two tasks; the code has only one network . There is no separate classification network, no separate localization…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#situation","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Standalone in both directions: no module in the repository calls CLIC, and CLIC calls no other module in the repository. It calls nothing but its own runner: Called When How CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py once per scan CondaSetUpCall.condaRunFilePython(runner, [\"--params_json=…\"], \"clic_env\") release CLIC_model/final_model.pth Download Model button requests.get , written to ~/Documents/CLIC_Models It starts from the raw CBCT. No prior segmentation, no landmarks, no orientation: the module reads a .nii / .nii.gz and outputs a label map. It does not depend on AMASSS , ASO , or ALI in any way. CLIC…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#fichiers","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs CLIC/CLIC.py widget, 622 lines, of which ~230 are a dark stylesheet and ~50 are conda patches Slicer Python CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py all the processing , 110 lines clic_env conda env, GPU if available CLIC/Resources/UI/CLIC.ui the interface – No Slicer CLI, no .xml , no MedX_Method -like layer: the widget writes a parameters JSON to slicer.app.temporaryPath and launches the runner as a standalone Python script inside the conda environment. The widget imports CondaSetUp at module level ( CLIC/CLIC.py:18 ) without declaring it in parent.dependencies : without…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#entrees","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 Scan collection _collect_scans() ( CLIC/CLIC.py:449 ) accepts .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .mha , .mhd , and follows a two-step rule: if there are subfolders containing at least one valid file, it returns the list of these subfolders ; otherwise, the list of valid files in the folder. Case 1 produces directory Path s, passed as-is as input_path to the runner, which does nib.load(str(inp)) : immediate failure. The nominal case is therefore a flat folder of .nii.gz . Furthermore the runner only uses nibabel : .nrrd , .mha , .mhd are accepted by the widget's filter and unreadable by the runner.…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#modele","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The model","x":"The only algorithmic path in CLIC, replayed for every axial slice. The entirety of the clinical decision (buccal / bicortical / palatal) comes out of the \"network\" box: the rest is deterministic arithmetic. 4.1 Architecture _blank_model(nc) ( CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py:44 ), called with nc = 4 : m = maskrcnn_resnet50_fpn(weights=None) m.roi_heads.box_predictor = FastRCNNPredictor(in_f, nc) m.roi_heads.mask_predictor = MaskRCNNPredictor(in_fm, 256, nc) backbone : ResNet-50 + FPN ( _resnet_fpn_extractor , 5 levels); RPN then ROI heads : box head ( FastRCNNPredictor : cls_score 4 classes…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#coeur","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The algorithmic core","x":"There is no non-learned part, apart from thresholding: no ICP , no morphology, no component extraction, no measurement. The entire runner is: load the model, loop over Z, threshold, write. The entire decision is inside the Mask R-CNN ."},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#sorties","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"out_dir = out_root / inp.stem out_path = out_dir / f\"{inp.stem}_{suffix}.nii.gz\" nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(seg.astype(np.int16), nii.affine, nii.header), out_path) a subfolder per scan , named after the scan; suffix from suffixLineEdit , default \"seg\" ; Path.stem on X.nii.gz is X.nii : the folder is called X.nii and the file X.nii_seg.nii.gz . Ugly but without functional consequence; affine and header are taken from the input volume : the label map overlays without registration. The original header describes a floating-point data type while the data is int16 ; Nifti1Image adjusts the header's…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#predit-calcule","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Per-slice min-max normalization computed arithmetic, 2 lines clic_env 800/1333 resizing + ImageNet standardization computed deterministic, internal to torchvision clic_env Detection, classification, mask predicted network : Mask R-CNN ResNet50-FPN, 4 classes clic_env , GPU if available 0.7 / 0.5 thresholds and write-back into the volume computed arithmetic clic_env Colors and legend Slicer widget All the clinical content, is this an impacted canine, and on which side of the cortical bone , comes out of the network alone. There is no post-hoc verification."},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#environnement","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Conda env clic_env (specific to CLIC) CLIC/CLIC.py:124 Python 3.9 CLIC/CLIC.py:171 Creation numpy<2.0.0 , scipy , nibabel , requests same Then, via pip torch==2.2.0 , torchvision==0.17.0 , torchaudio==2.2.0 from the cu118 index CLIC/CLIC.py:182 Then pip install 'numpy<2.0' a second time CLIC/CLIC.py:222 GPU optional, automatic CPU fallback CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py:73 _ensure_env() ( CLIC/CLIC.py:162 ) is replayed on every click on Predict : both pip install calls run again, even when everything is already in place (pip returns quickly but still queries the network).…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#pieges","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug (legacy only) Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR: progress goes through a progress file named by SADT_PROGRESS_FILE and written by the tool (SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/src/sadt_clic/progress.py:21,60-103 , called at __init__.py:170 ), and the results come back as an archive that the client can load as label maps (Remote CLICRemote/CLICRemote.py:67-72 ; see CLIC on VISOR ). The runner emits its markers via logger.info , with the formatter '%(name)s - %(levelname)s - (%(filename)s:%(lineno)d) - %(message)s' ( CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py:39 ). The received lines look like…"},{"u":"CLIC/CLIC.html#litterature","p":"CLIC","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Literature","x":"No paper covers CLIC. The corresponding work is announced under the title Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption (Tulissi, Cevidanes, Prieto), presented at NA-MIC Project Week 43 (Montreal, 2025) : a project in progress, not a publication. The original code comes from the personal repository ashmoy/maskRcnn , which is also where the test scans cited by the README come from ( MN138.nii , UM06.nii ). Not to be confused with the neighboring work below, which did not produce this code: buccal/palatal localization of…"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html","p":"CLIC: sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"No publication describes CLIC: the only primary source is a NA-MIC Project Week 43 project page, the rest is bibliographic neighborhood on a related problem. No publication describes CLIC. Checked: nothing under the module's name, nothing under its authors' names, no article, no preprint, no IADR/AADOCR abstract found. The only primary source is a NA-MIC Project Week 43 project page (Montreal, 2025), retrieved into this folder ( 2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.md ), which presents CLIC as an already-existing tool and describes ongoing work that extends it to root-resorption…"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"CLIC: sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Tulissi_NAMIC_PW43_impacted_canines.md Tulissi E, Cevidanes L, Prieto J, Bianchi J. \"Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption\". Project page, NA-MIC Project Week 43, Montreal, 2025. project report: only primary source project page 2017_He_Mask_RCNN.pdf He K, Gkioxari G, Dollár P, Girshick R. \"Mask R-CNN\". arXiv :1703.06870, 2017 (ICCV 2017). article: architecture used arXiv:1703.06870 .xml / .txt Kahraman EN, Güldiken İN, Tekin A, Kanbak T, Yılancı H. \"Buccal or palatal? AI-based…"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"CLIC: sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Galdi M, Cannatà D, Celentano F, Rizzo L, Rossi D, Bocchino T, Martina S. \"Development of a fully deep learning model to improve the reproducibility of sector classification systems for predicting unerupted maxillary canine likelihood of impaction\". arXiv :2511.20493, 2025. arXiv , freely downloadable, not included here: covers sector-based classification on panoramic radiographs , a different problem – Salmanpour F, Akpınar M. \"Performance of Chat Generative Pretrained Transformer-4.0 in determining labiolingual localization of maxillary impacted canines\". AJODO , 2025.…"},{"u":"CLIC/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"CLIC: sources","t":"CLIC","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighborhood","x":"This work does not describe CLIC's code . Buccal/palatal localization on panoramic radiographs. Kahraman 2026 ( BMC Medical Imaging ) and Helal 2026 ( Children ) train 2D image-classification CNNs on panoramic radiographs, using CBCT as ground truth. Only two classes, one image per patient. CLIC works directly on the CBCT volume, slice by slice, with three classes. 3D segmentation of impacted canines on CBCT. Swaity 2024 ( Scientific Reports ) and Ünal 2025 ( BMC Oral Health ) segment the impacted canine with a volumetric network ( nnU-Net / 3D CNN). They delineate the tooth; they do not…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"A language-model (LLM) inference module that reads free-text clinical notes and extracts key–value pairs from them: it is neither a geometric tool nor an image-processing one, despite its place in a suite of dental tools. Scope warning. Unlike AutoCrop3D and AutoMatrix , CNE is not a geometric utility: it touches no volume, no mesh, no coordinate. It is an LLM inference module that reads free-text clinical notes and writes key–value pairs. At the core of the module sits a quantized GGUF model loaded by llama-cpp-python inside Slicer's Python. Two code bases Everything numbered below…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#acronyme","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"0. The acronym","x":"The repository never spells out the acronym in any documentation: the README contains no CNE section, and no longer documents the MedX it replaced either, neither name appears in it. The module's help text ( CNE.py:157 ) says only \"This tool helps to create summaries of clinical notes\". Two log lines, and only those, give the expansion: logger.info(\"CNE (Clinical Notes Extraction)\") ( CNE.py:342 and CNE.py:663 ), and the CLI publishes the same name to the Slicer supervisor: <filter-name>Clinical Notes Extraction</filter-name> ( CNE_CLI.py:93 ). CNE = Clinical Notes Extraction , attested by…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#situation","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"1. Place in the pipeline","x":"Terminal module. Nothing calls it, and it calls no other module in the repository. Declared in CMakeLists.txt:50-52 ( CNE , CNE_CLI , CNE_Dashboard ). It replaces the \"Summarization\" half of MedX , which was disabled from the build in March 2026 with the comment \"Replaced by CNE\". Corrected since this page was written (legacy; Dashboard not in VISOR) Legacy (3c67957). The MedX removal also holds for VISOR; the Dashboard does not exist there: the string \"dashboard\" occurs neither in SADT-VISOR tools/CNE nor in Remote CNERemote/ (searched, not run; see CNE on VISOR ). This survey, dated…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#fichiers","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs CNE.py widget: dependencies, model download, launching the CLI, and the Dashboard button Slicer's Python CNE_CLI.py text extraction + llama.cpp inference Slicer's Python ( SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI ) CNE_Dashboard/CNE_Dashboard.py scripted CLI that draws the dashboard from the extractions (§8.1) Slicer's Python CNE_CLI/CNE_CLI_utils/ dashboard_utils.py (400 lines: one aggregation function per panel) and display_figure.py (725 lines: reading, parsing, matplotlib figure) Slicer's Python CNE.ui two ctkPathLineEdit widgets in Dirs mode, a Dashboard button, two TMJ…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#dependances","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"3. Dependencies, installed on demand","x":"check_dependencies() ( CNE.py:83 ) is called on clicking Apply , not on module load. It tests five imports ( CNE.py:93-110 ) and, if any are missing, asks for confirmation before slicer.util.pip_install : Import tested Package installed Use llama_cpp llama-cpp-python --extra-index-url https://abetlen.github.io/llama-cpp-python/whl/cpu inference fitz pymupdf PDF text extraction docx python-docx Word text extraction matplotlib matplotlib Dashboard figure ( CNE.py:102-107 ) pandas pandas Dashboard table ( CNE.py:109-110 ) Two points worth knowing: Gotcha (legacy only) Legacy (3c67957); not…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#modeles","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"4. The models","x":"No weights ship in the repository. getModelPath() ( CNE.py:513 ) downloads the GGUF on demand, via urllib.request.urlretrieve with a QProgressDialog , into <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/CNE/model/ . UI mode repo_id File requested Local name n_ctx TMJ DCBIA-OrthoLab/Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct-TMJ qwen-ft-q4_k_m.gguf Qwen-2.5-7B-TMJ.gguf 6144 Ortho DCBIA-OrthoLab/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Ortho model-q4_k_m.gguf Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Ortho.gguf 2048 Constructed URL: https://huggingface.co/{repo_id}/resolve/main/{fileName} . Corrected (legacy only) Legacy (3c67957) download code. In VISOR nothing is downloaded…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#entrees","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"5. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"SUPPORTED_EXTENSIONS = (\".txt\", \".pdf\", \".docx\") . The scan is non-recursive : glob.glob(os.path.join(notesFolder_input, f\"*{ext}\")) ( CNE_CLI.py:135 ), unlike every other module in the repository, which uses iglob(**, recursive=True) . A folder made of per-patient subfolders will yield nothing. Text extraction ( CNE_CLI.py:75 ): Format Extractor Detail .pdf fitz.open then \"\\n\".join(page.get_text() for page in doc) no OCR: a scanned PDF comes out empty .docx docx.Document , paragraph.text tables are lost ( doc.paragraphs does not iterate over them) .txt open(..., encoding=\"utf-8\") a file in…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#inference","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"6. Inference","x":"A single pass per file, with no chunking ( CNE_CLI.py:194-203 ): messages = [] if notesType.upper() == \"TMJ\": messages.append({\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": INSTRUCTION_TMJ}) messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": clinical_text}) output = llm.create_chat_completion(messages=messages, max_tokens=500, temperature=0.1) The two modes diverge before the call to the model (system instruction, context size) and then converge in the same llama.cpp call. The Ortho path is highlighted: it is the mode with no system instruction and the tighter context (§6). Worth noting: Gotcha (also true in VISOR)…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#post-traitement","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"7. Post-processing the response","x":"The model is expected to answer in JSON; the code does not enforce this (no response_format , no GBNF grammar) and simply fishes out the JSON from the text ( CNE_CLI.py:209-214 ): start_idx = ai_response.find('{') end_idx = ai_response.rfind('}') + 1 data = json.loads(ai_response[start_idx:end_idx]) if \"extraction\" in data: data = data[\"extraction\"] for key, value in data.items(): formatted_response += f\"{key} : {value}\\n\" So: first opening brace through last closing brace, unwrapping of a possible extraction level, then flattening into key : value lines. If the parse fails or there is no…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#sorties","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"8. Outputs","x":"One text file per input note, in the output folder: Extraction_<note name without extension>.txt Content: key : value lines, or the model's raw response if the parse failed. No CSV, no json and no per-patient aggregation from the extraction itself: the table and the figure come from the Dashboard button (§8.1), which reads these files afterwards. The output folder is created by os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True) on the widget side ( CNE.py:613 ). 8.1 The Dashboard The \"Dashboard\" half of MedX was ported on 2026-10-12, so the earlier statement of this page that it \"was not carried over\" no…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#predit-calcule","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"9. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where PDF/Word text extraction computed PyMuPDF / python-docx CLI Typographic normalization computed fixed replacement table, 7 entries CLI Key–value pair extraction predicted LLM, Qwen-2.5-7B-TMJ or Llama-3.1-8B-Ortho, GGUF Q4 CLI, in-process llama_cpp Formatting computed find('{') / json.loads / flattening loop CLI All the clinical content of the output comes from the model. There is no rule, no dictionary of terms, no schema validation: whatever the model writes is what gets saved."},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#environnement","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"10. Environment","x":"Element Value Source Interpreter Slicer's Python, no conda SlicerMacroBuildScriptedCLI , CMakeLists.txt Packages installed on the fly llama-cpp-python (CPU index), pymupdf , python-docx , matplotlib , pandas CNE.py:93-110 GPU requested ( n_gpu_layers=-1 ) but the install forces a CPU build §3 Weights ~4.4 GB (TMJ) / ~4.9 GB (Ortho), <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/CNE/model/ CNE.py:535 RAM not constrained in the code; a 7–8 B Q4_K_M model needs roughly 5–6 GB resident on top of Slicer inference"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#pieges","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"11. Gotchas and fragile points","x":"The Dashboard expects the TMJ schema (§8.1). The test files' output folder. copyTestFiles() ( CNE.py:454 ) used to put a path that did not exist into the output field. It now creates the output folder ( CNE.py:508 ), copies the notes again on every call without erasing the folder ( dirs_exist_ok=True , CNE.py:505 ), and leaves an output folder already chosen by the user alone ( CNE.py:328-330 ). Resources/testfiles/ still holds only the two input_ folders. Non-recursive scan of the input folder (§5). No length limit before the call: a note that is too long fails instead of being chunked…"},{"u":"CNE/CNE.html#litterature","p":"CNE","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"12. Literature","x":"No publication is cited in the repository for CNE, and the two DCBIA-OrthoLab/* Hugging Face model cards cite no paper (they point to a code repository, ortho-cde-llm-extraction , which was not read). The annotation corpus is not released and no evaluation is reported: both are not determinable from the repository . What is verifiable: The MedX → CNE lineage, documented only by the acknowledgement line of CNE_Dashboard.xml (\"Ported from MedX_Dashboard, which CNE replaced without taking this half with it\") and the comments in display_figure.py:35 and :675 . The root CMakeLists.txt no longer…"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html","p":"CNE: sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Unpublished tool: what the literature search looked for, did not find, and the neighborhood gathered instead. Unpublished tool. After checking, no publication describes CNE, nor the two fine-tuned models it downloads. The acronym Clinical Notes Extraction itself appears nowhere outside the repository. What was searched for, and turned up nothing: Search Result Europe PMC \"Clinical Notes Extraction\" 6 articles, none from the group, none about this code Europe PMC (AUTH:\"Cevidanes L\" OR AUTH:\"Prieto JC\" OR AUTH:\"Bianchi J\") AND (\"large language model\" OR LLM OR Llama OR Qwen OR GPT) 3…"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"CNE: sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"Nothing that describes CNE. The files below fall under the neighborhood (next section) and are listed here because they were downloaded. File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Wiest_privacy_preserving_LLM.xml / .txt Wiest IC, Ferber D, Zhu J, van Treeck M, Meyer SK, Juglan R, Carrero ZI, Paech D, Kleesiek J, Ebert MP, Truhn D, Kather JN. Privacy-preserving large language models for structured medical information retrieval. npj Digit Med 2024;7:257. Article (open access) 10.1038/s41746-024-01233-2 : PMC11415382 2026_Panchal_LLM_deidentification_EHR.xml / .txt Panchal O, Chang NW, Zhao ZR, Ramesh…"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#a-consulter","p":"CNE: sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"No reference of this kind: there is no paywalled article to flag either."},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#modeles-depots","p":"CNE: sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Models, repositories, engine","x":"Item Where Base models meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct , Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct Fine-tuned repositories (under the DCBIA-OrthoLab organization since ADT PR #274 and #275) DCBIA-OrthoLab/Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct-TMJ , DCBIA-OrthoLab/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-Ortho Inference engine, GGUF format, quantization Q4_K_M llama-cpp-python , llama.cpp The module SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"CNE/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"CNE: sources","t":"CNE","c":"Texte","s":"Neighborhood","x":"None of these works is about CNE. They share neither the authors, nor the models, nor the corpus. They are gathered here because they describe the same technical gesture: running an open-weight LLM locally , without sending the patient's text to a third-party service, to extract structured fields from it, and they make it possible to assess what CNE does, in the absence of any text about CNE itself. Wiest 2024 (file above): the work closest to CNE's gesture. Open-weight LLMs deployed locally extract structured data from clinical reports, with the privacy argument used to justify the local…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"Classification of .vtk meshes by a two-branch network (multi-head-attention point cloud + multi-view EfficientNet-B0 rendering), systematically followed by a Grad-CAM pass written as a per-point scalar on the mesh. Dental Oral and Craniofacial Shape Analysis eXplainability and Interpretability. A single user action: pick a data type, an input folder, an output folder. Everything else, which checkpoint, which architecture, how many classes, is inferred from the data type. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#situation","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Place in the pipeline","x":"Standalone: nothing in the repository calls DOCShapeAXI. The input meshes are assumed to be already segmented and extracted elsewhere ( AMASSS for condyles, airway segmentation, etc.); the module does not produce its own surfaces . Overview: DOCShapeAXI performs neither segmentation nor surface extraction, it classifies meshes that are already ready. Called When How DOCShapeAXI_CLI on every task conda run -n shapeaxi python -m DOCShapeAXI_CLI <8 args> model_path.json on raw.githubusercontent.com at CLI startup, if the .ckpt is missing requests.get then urlretrieve shapeaxi pip package at all…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#fichiers","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.py widget: conda, model choice, progress bar, ~180 lines of dark-theme styling Slicer's Python DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py prediction + explainability , 307 lines shapeaxi conda env, GPU if available model_path.json 5 checkpoints and their URLs – install_pytorch.py picks the pytorch3d wheel based on the installed torch, then shapeaxi>=2.0.2 conda env Most of the actual work lives in the shapeaxi pip package, outside the repository. The architecture details below were read from the real installation ( shapeaxi/saxi_nets_lightning.py , saxi_layers.py…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#entrees","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 The CSV The CLI flat-lists args.input_dir , keeps the .vtk files, and writes <output_dir>/files_<data_type>.csv with the header surf and one file name per line ( csv_edit , DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:105 ). Paths are relative; SaxiDataset joins them with mount_point = input_dir . data_type contains spaces (\"Mandibular Condyle\"), so the csv is named files_Mandibular Condyle.csv . The csv is regenerated only if it does not already exist ( DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:285 ): adding meshes to the input folder without emptying the output folder will not get them processed. 3.2 Geometric normalization…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#modeles","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The models","x":"4.1 The decision table find_model_name() / find_nn_type() ( DOCShapeAXI.py:804 ), the keyword tested is a token of the combo label ( self.data_type.split(' ') ): Data type (combo) Token Task model nn num_classes Mandibular Condyle Condyle severity condyles_4_class SaxiMHAFBClassification 4 Nasopharynx Airway Obstruction Airway binary airways_2_class SaxiMHAFBClassification 2 Nasopharynx Airway Obstruction Airway severity airways_4_class SaxiMHAFBClassification 4 Nasopharynx Airway Obstruction Airway regression airways_4_regress SaxiMHAFBRegression 1 Alveolar Bone Defect in Cleft Cleft…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#explicabilite","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The explainability mechanism","x":"Note (legacy only) Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR: explain: bool = True is an argument that can be turned off (SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/src/sadt_docshapeaxi/__init__.py:22,93-95 ; see DOCShapeAXI on VISOR ). This is the module's most distinctive part, and it runs systematically after prediction: neither an option nor a checkbox ( saxi_gradcam , DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:129 ). From logit to per-point scalar: saliency is computed on the rendered views then reprojected onto the mesh. a) The targeted layer. target_layer = getattr(model.convnet.module, '_blocks') mv_cam =…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#sorties","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"File Content <output>/<model>.ckpt the downloaded checkpoint, left in place <output>/files_<data_type>.csv the list of .vtk files <output>/files_<data_type>_prediction.csv the input csv plus one <task>_prediction column per task <output>/explainability/<task>/<name>.vtk a copy of the mesh plus N grad_cam_* arrays <log_path> \"<task>,<predict|explainability>,<index>,<num_classes>\" The column accumulation is intentional ( DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:243 ): if the prediction csv already exists, it is read back in and used as a base, which is why an airway dataset ends up with binary_prediction…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#predit-calcule","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Unit-sphere normalization computed geometric (bbox + scale) shapeaxi Color-encoded normals computed geometric (VTK) shapeaxi 4096-point sampling computed stochastic (pytorch3d) GPU 42-view 224×224 RGB+z render computed pytorch3d rasterization GPU Class / ratio predicted MHAEncoder + EfficientNet-B0 + attention GPU Grad-CAM map predicted derived from the network (Captum, multi-view branch only) GPU Face → vertex reprojection, cross-view max computed geometric GPU/CPU Median filter on the mesh computed geometric, topological neighborhood CPU Pitfall (also true in VISOR) Also…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#environnement","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Conda env shapeaxi , shared with ALI /AREG/ASO/FlexReg DOCShapeAXI.py:804 Python 3.12 DOCShapeAXI.py:666 Env creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK same pytorch3d wheel chosen from torch.__version__ + CUDA, from https://ImageMindAnalytics.github.io/pytorch3d-wheels/simple/ install_pytorch.py:10 shapeaxi shapeaxi>=2.0.2 , installed after pytorch3d install_pytorch.py:186 Patch patch_dentalmodelseg() rewrites saxi_nets.DentalModelSeg → saxi_nets_lightning.DentalModelSeg in the installed package install_pytorch.py:210 GPU not mandatory ( torch.device('cuda' if …) )…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#pieges","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug (legacy; corrected in VISOR) Legacy (3c67957): the installer still asks for shapeaxi>=2.0.2 ( ADT/ADTLib/env/install_pytorch.py:385 ). Corrected in VISOR: the tool pins shapeaxi==1.1.1 , and its comment says 2.x loads none of the five checkpoints (SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/pyproject.toml:15-30 ); the prediction calls model(points, views) ( engine.py:57 ; see DOCShapeAXI on VISOR ). The CLI is written for the shapeaxi 1.0.x API, the environment installs 2.0.2. This is the first thing to check if the module stops working: shapeaxi 1.0.10 shapeaxi 2.0.2 signature forward(self…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#litterature","p":"DOCShapeAXI","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Literature","x":"Three references, not to be confused with one another: 1. The framework. ShapeAXI: Shape Analysis Explainability and Interpretability , Prieto, Miranda, Gurgel, Anchling, Hutin, Barone, Al Turkestani, Aliaga, Yatabe, Bianchi, Cevidanes, Proc. SPIE Medical Imaging, April 2024 , DOI 10.1117/12.3007053 . It reports 79.78% on condyles and 81.58% on cleft severity: precisely two of the three datasets supported here. Code / paper divergence (also true in VISOR) Also true in VISOR: the tool loads the same SaxiMHAFBClassification and SaxiMHAFBRegression networks (SADT-VISOR catalog.py:24-25,50-71…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI: sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for the tool: what is deposited in this folder, what remains to be checked online, and the neighborhood of publications that do not describe the module itself. A partially published tool, and published in separate pieces: no paper describes the Slicer module, but each of the three embedded datasets has its own clinical publication, and the shapeaxi framework has its own as well. Code / paper divergence The code diverges from its reference paper. The 2024 SPIE paper ( 2024_Prieto_ShapeAXI_SPIE ) describes a multi-view render over an icosahedron subdivision, with the images…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"DOCShapeAXI: sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2024_Prieto_ShapeAXI_SPIE.xml / .txt Prieto JC, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Anchling L, Hutin N, Barone S, Al Turkestani N, Aliaga A, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L. ShapeAXI: Shape Analysis Explainability and Interpretability. Proc. SPIE 12931, Medical Imaging 2024: Imaging Informatics for Healthcare, Research, and Applications, 1293116, April 2024. Proceedings (full JATS text, NIHMS1990711 author manuscript) 10.1117/12.3007053 : PMC11085013, PMID 38736903 2025_Mattos_adenoid_airway_obstruction.xml / .txt Mattos CT, Dole L, Mota-Júnior SL, Cury-Saramago AA, Bianchi…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"DOCShapeAXI: sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Dole L, Mattos CT, Bianchi J, Oh H, Evangelista K, Valladares Neto J, Mota-Júnior SL, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. Enhancing airway obstruction diagnosis with multimodal 3D shape analysis. Int J Comput Assist Radiol Surg 2026;21(3):529–538 (online 14 Oct. 2025). The closest reference to the module , by the author of the original lucieDLE/DOC-ShapeAXI repository: it explicitly presents an \"open-source, automated deep learning tool,\" combines multi-view and point-cloud, and works on 1215 CBCTs from four centers. Paywalled; likely to reach PMC under NIH Public Access. Springer ·…"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#depots","p":"DOCShapeAXI: sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Repositories and software objects","x":"Object Where shapeaxi framework (the pip package actually run) DCBIA-OrthoLab/ShapeAXI Original repository of the module lucieDLE/DOC-ShapeAXI Zenodo software archive for ShapeAXI 10.5281/zenodo.22118309 Captum: LayerGradCam , used in §5 of the module captum.ai"},{"u":"DOCShapeAXI/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"DOCShapeAXI: sources","t":"DOCShapeAXI","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighborhood","x":"These works do not describe DOCShapeAXI . They are listed here because they set the condyle severity scale that the condyles_4_class dataset reuses without any text documenting it for this checkpoint. Tubau Ribera 2019 and de Dumast 2018 (SPIE + CMIG) , above: the Shape Variation Analyzer lineage, the direct ancestor of the group's condyle classifier. They grade condylar degeneration into 5, 6, or 7 groups depending on the version (normal, near-normal, overgrowth, Degeneration 1–4), on geometric descriptors + heat kernel signature , not on multi-view rendering. None of the three produces 4…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Patient-by-patient registration of Intra Oral Scans, with a registration patch built by hand (palatal butterfly, free curve, or mucogingival band) instead of being predicted by a network: the interactive counterpart to AREG_IOS. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs the same tool on a server, is described on its own page, FlexReg on VISOR . The VISOR port keeps the patch preview in Slicer and does the rest on the server; it does not carry the free curve, the patch deletion…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#situation","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Nothing in the repository calls FlexReg: it is a terminal tool, driven by the operator. It does, however, call two pretrained networks, and it produces the input to AREG_IOS : both tools read exactly the same point array. Called When Why dentalmodelseg (ShapeAXI / DentalModelSeg ) if the mesh has no Universal_ID array segment and number the crowns ALI_IOS (weights Lower_MG_*.pth ) lower-arch mode only place the 13 mucogingival landmarks Downstream, AREG_IOS.py:364 does vtkMeshTeeth(list_teeth=[1], property=\"Butterfly\") and AREG_IOS.py:378 the same on MGL_ARRAY_NAME : a patch built in FlexReg…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#fichiers","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Note (legacy only) Part of this module now lives in the shared layer. ADTLib centralises what several modules carried in copies: from here, geometry.py , io/landmarks.py , labels.py and the contract of method.py . Its page records how many copies each file replaced. Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR: the VISOR tool imports nothing from ADTLib; its utilities are local copies ( SADT-VISOR tools/FlexReg/src/sadt_flexreg/util.py , transformation.py ). File Role Where it runs FlexReg/FlexReg.py Slicer widget, 4400 lines: UI, joysticks, previews, full MGL path Slicer's Python…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#entrees","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"Pitfall: file conversion (legacy only) ensureVtkInput() ( FlexReg.py:3199 ) converts a .stl to .vtk before any operation. This is not cosmetic: dental_model_seg.py does os.remove(args.stl) and would destroy the source file if it were handed the .stl directly ( FlexReg.py:3409 ). Legacy (3c67957); not present in VISOR: read_surface reads .vtk , .vtp and .stl directly, by extension, and refuses an empty mesh ( SADT-VISOR sadt_flexreg/pipeline.py:50-78 ); no segmentation is run, so no source file is deleted. LPS ↔ RAS flip. The CLI reads the .vtk (LPS), applies Scale(-1,-1,1) to work in RAS…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#modeles","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The models","x":"4.1 Crown segmentation: DentalModelSeg predicted checkSegmentation() ( FlexReg.py:3402 ) does not test for the array's presence: it attempts the orientation above and reads the exception. ToothNoExist → the mesh is segmented but one of the 4 reference teeth is missing → message, stop. NoSegmentationSurf → no segmentation array → shapeaxi_conda() . conda run -n shapeaxi python -m CrownSegmentationcli \\ <surf> None <out> 1 latest 0 Universal_ID 0 None <vtk_folder> <dentalmodelseg_path> Arguments in order: surface, input csv, output folder, overwrite=1 , latest model, crownsegmentation=0…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#coeur","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"5. The algorithmic core","x":"5.1 Path A: the Butterfly patch (upper arch) computed make_butterfly.py . No network, purely geometric. a) Four centroids. The operator picks 4 teeth (anterior L/R, posterior L/R); vtkMeanTeeth averages the vertices carrying that Universal_ID . b) Three families of parameters applied to these centroids: Parameter Effect Detail shift_lr , shift_ap translates the whole patch the same vector is added to all 4 centroids; since the landmarks are affine combinations with weights summing to 1, the shift carries through the interpolation and the shape is unchanged adjust (mm) lengthens / shortens…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#sorties","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"The mesh. Patch type → rewritten into the input file . ICP type → written into path_output as <T2 name without extension><suffix>.vtk , after flipping back to LPS. The transform , the delicate step ( FlexReg_CLI.py:100-106 ): flip = np.diag([-1, -1, 1, 1]) composed = flip @ matrix_array @ flip # RAS -> LPS by conjugation composed_inv = np.linalg.inv(composed) # ITK convention: fixed -> moving sitk_tfm = sitk.AffineTransform(3) sitk_tfm.SetMatrix(composed_inv[:3, :3].flatten()) sitk_tfm.SetTranslation(composed_inv[:3, 3]) File <T2 name><suffix>.tfm , reusable by the other modules in the…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#predit-calcule","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"7. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Crown segmentation predicted multi-view network ( DentalModelSeg ) conda run -n shapeaxi Canonical orientation computed geometric, 4 centroids CLI Butterfly / curve computed geometric, Bézier + geodesic flood fill CLI, GPU required Butterfly preview computed geometric, point-in-polygon widget, CPU MG landmarks predicted multi-view network ( ALI_IOS , Lower_MG_*.pth ) conda run -n shapeaxi MGL band computed geometric, spline + Dijkstra widget, CPU Registration computed classic rigid ICP (VTK) CLI No part of FlexReg learns anything: the two networks it calls are pretrained…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#environnement","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Environment","x":"ensureBooted() ( FlexReg.py:192 ) centralizes the check, run once , at the moment an action needs it, not when a scan is displayed. The probes cost a measured 7.6 s: 2.2 s for conda --version , 0.6 s for the environment test, 4.8 s for importing the install module inside it. Item Value Source conda env shapeaxi , shared with ALI , AREG, ASO , DOCShapeAXI FlexReg.py:705 Python 3.12 (forced: prebuilt pytorch3d wheels only exist for cp310–cp313; shapeaxi ≥ 2.0.2 lifted the grpcio pin that used to force 3.9) same Packages at creation torch>=2.8,<2.13 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK install_shapeaxi…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#pieges","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"The CLI requires CUDA (also true in VISOR, for the patch step only) Dilation , drawPatch , and the final merge of the arrays all call .cuda() with no CPU fallback. The MGL patch, on the other hand, is computed on the widget side and does without it, hence the while args.type != \"icp_mgl\" that skips the merge for the lower arch ( FlexReg_CLI.py:267-292 , .cuda() at 272 and 286). Also true in VISOR for the patch: Dilation calls .cuda() with no fallback ( SADT-VISOR sadt_flexreg/propagation.py:31, 60, 66-68 ), called from make_butterfly.py:161 . No longer true for the rest: the merge of the…"},{"u":"FlexReg/FlexReg.html#litterature","p":"FlexReg","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Literature","x":"The published foundation is AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOral Scans (Hutin, Anchling, Cevidanes et al., Lecture Notes in Computer Science, 2023): crown segmentation by DentalModelSeg , initial alignment by shared centroids, palatal ROI segmented by multi-view 3D shape analysis, then ICP. FlexReg is its \"the operator draws the ROI instead of having it predicted\" counterpart. No paper deals specifically with FlexReg or with the MGL variant: the latter appears to be an in-house development that remains unpublished to date. The figures quoted in the code (4.2 mm vs 1.2 mm on 364…"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html","p":"FlexReg: sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"FlexReg's bibliography: what is deposited in this folder, what remains to be read online, and the neighboring work that does not describe this code. FlexReg is not described by any publication. Verification done: nothing in Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, arXiv, HAL, nor in the forty-three weeks of the NA-MIC project. The name appears publicly only in the README of SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. The MGL variant (mucogingival line) is not published either, and the figures quoted in its code (4.2 mm vs 1.2 mm on 364 predictions) are not sourced anywhere outside the comments. The published…"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#depose","p":"FlexReg: sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2021_Boubolo_FlyBy_CNN_surface_segmentation.xml / .txt Boubolo L, Dumont M, Brosset S, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel ML, Massaro C, Aliaga-Del Castillo A, Ioshida M, Yatabe MS, Benavides E, Rios H, Soki F, Neiva G, Paniagua B, Cevidanes L, Styner M, Prieto JC. « FlyBy CNN: a 3D surface segmentation framework ». Proc. SPIE 11596, Medical Imaging 2021: Image Processing , 115962B, 2021. SPIE proceedings, full text on PMC (author manuscript) 10.1117/12.2582205 : PMC7983301 2023_Leclercq_DentalModelSeg_intraoral_surfaces.xml / .txt Leclercq M, Ruellas A, Gurgel M…"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"FlexReg: sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Hutin N, Anchling L, Cevidanes L, Miranda F, Curado D, Gurgel M, Barone S, Bianchi J, Al Turkestani N, Ruellas A, Eason M, Mavani K, Prieto JC, Aliaga A. « AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans ». In: Shape in Medical Imaging, ShapeMI 2023, held jointly with MICCAI 2023, Vancouver, October 8, 2023, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14350, Springer, 2023, p. 223–235. link.springer.com : paywalled, no author's version deposited (Unpaywall closed , nothing in PMC/HAL) 10.1007/978-3-031-46914-5_18 Ioshida M, Muñoz BA, Rios H, Cevidanes L, Aristizabal JF, Rey…"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"FlexReg: sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighboring work","x":"Related work that does not describe this code . NA-MIC Project Week 39 (Montreal, 2023), « Automatic Registration Intra Oral Scan » (Hutin, Anchling, Cevidanes, Prieto et al.): project page . Describes AREG_IOS (network on the palate + ICP ), never the hand-drawn patch. NA-MIC Project Week 36 (2022), « ALIIOS, Automatic Landmarks Identification for Intra Oral Scans » (Baquero, Gillot, Cevidanes, Prieto): project page . This is the network that supplies the 13 mucogingival landmarks for FlexReg's MGL path. ALI_IOS has no publication of its own ; this page is its only public description and…"},{"u":"FlexReg/SOURCES.html#rien-trouve","p":"FlexReg: sources","t":"FlexReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Searched for, found nothing","x":"Searched the name « FlexReg » alone and combined (Slicer, Cevidanes, DCBIA, intraoral, registration) in Crossref, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, arXiv, HAL/DUMAS, and on the web; went through the complete listing of NA-MIC Project Weeks 36 → 43; searched for IADR/AADOCR abstracts on IOS registration by the mucogingival line. Nothing. The only public trace of FlexReg is the GitHub repository."},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Affine/rigid registration of T1/T2 CBCT scans by driving ITK-SNAP's greedy binary as a subprocess, with no network in the main path and no dedicated conda environment. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The VISOR port, which runs part of this tool on a server, is described in VISOR: how this tool runs now . It is a partial port: only the folder-to-folder Greedy registration exists there. The manual mode and the Distant (landmark) mode of sections 4.1 and 4.3 have no counterpart in the server tool.…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#situation","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Nothing in the repository calls GreedyReg. It is a terminal tool, driven by the operator, and it feeds the input of no other module. Called How When GreedyReg_CLI slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.greedyreg_cli, …) every automatic registration, single-case or batch greedy binary subprocess.run from the CLI two invocations per case (registration then resampling) ALI_CBCT slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.ali_cbct, …) \"Distant Registration\" mode only The widget assembles the parameters, then delegates: the CLI launches greedy , and ALI_CBCT is only called on in Distant mode. Verified Legacy only…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#fichiers","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs GreedyReg/GreedyReg.py widget, 1642 lines, about 700 of which are Qt UI (rotation wheel, slice overlay, style sheets) Slicer's Python GreedyReg_Method/Logic.py binary download, CLI parameters, pairing, Kabsch , ALI parsing Slicer's Python GreedyReg_CLI/GreedyReg_CLI.py loops over the pairs, builds and launches the two greedy commands python-real , i.e. Slicer's Python GreedyReg_CLI/bin/<platform>/greedy the executable, absent from the repository , downloaded on demand native Note Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . The server tool has its own venv, Python…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#binaire","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"3. The greedy binary: where it comes from","x":"greedyBinaryPath() looks for GreedyReg_CLI/bin/{linux|mac|windows}/greedy[.exe] . If it is missing, a red banner offers downloadGreedyBinary() ( Logic.py:109 , URLs at :123-127,179 ), which fetches an ITK-SNAP 4.2.2 archive from SourceForge and extracts only bin/greedy from it: Platform Source Extraction Linux itksnap-4.2.2-20241202-Linux-x86_64.tar.gz tarfile , member …/bin/greedy , chmod 755 macOS (arm64) itksnap-4.2.2-20241202-MacOS-arm64.tar.gz same Windows itksnap-4.2.2-20241202-win64-AMD64.exe (NSIS installer) 7-Zip if present, otherwise a silent install followed by uninstall The…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#modes","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The three modes, and what actually sets them apart","x":"Mode What is computed Who computes it Output Manual Alignment nothing: the operator moves a vtkMRMLLinearTransformNode widget matrix in the scene, used as -ia Automatic Registration affine or rigid, by optimizing an intensity metric computed greedy binary, via the CLI registered .nii.gz + .mat Distant Registration closed-form rigid ( SVD /Kabsch) on 3-5 predicted landmarks predicted computed widget + ALI_CBCT only a matrix, to be refined afterward Vocabulary trap Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . The server tool has no Distant mode ( SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:38-52 ). The…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#commande","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"5. The greedy command line","x":"buildRegistrationCommand() ( GreedyReg_CLI.py:104-125 ) always produces exactly this, with no option exposed beyond the metric and the number of degrees of freedom: greedy -d 3 -a \\ -m NMI | -m NCC 4x4x4 | -m SSD \\ -i <T1.nii.gz> <T2.nii.gz> \\ -o <ID>_warp.mat \\ -n 100x100x50x25 \\ -e 0.5 \\ -search 100 10 20 \\ -dof 6|12 \\ -ia <init.mat> \\ [-gm <binarized mask>] Flag Value Meaning (greedy docs) -d 3 fixed image dimension -a fixed affine/rigid mode . Greedy's diffeomorphic mode, which is its default and its reason for existing, is never used here -m NMI index 0 (UI default) normalized mutual…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#entrees","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Inputs, pairing, masks, initialization","x":"Trap Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . The port pairs by the shared rule of sadt_areg_common.pairing : recursive, case kept, timepoint tokens dropped, earlier outputs excluded ( SADT-VISOR AREG/common/src/sadt_areg_common/pairing.py:175-276 ). Run on 2026-11-09: 01_T1.nii.gz gives 01 , Patient-01.nii.gz gives Patient_01 . Patients present on one side only are reported under unmatched , and no pair at all is a ValueError ( SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:102-122 ). Legacy: GreedyReg_CLI.py:41,44-53@3c67957 . Pairing. ID_PATTERN = ^([A-Za-z]+\\d+) applied to the file name…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#batch","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Batch mode","x":"Two independent batches, which do not do the same thing. Automatic batch ( GreedyReg.py:1477-1544 ). The widget does not loop: it launches a single GreedyReg_CLI run over the folders, and it is the CLI that iterates ( main() , GreedyReg_CLI.py:145-194 ), emitting <filter-progress> / <filter-comment> tags read by Slicer's CLI infrastructure. The output folder is the T2 folder itself . No mask is binarized upstream, no init is passed ( initFolder=\"\" ): the batch always starts from the nudged identity. First case that fails → sys.exit(1) → the whole batch stops ( GreedyReg_CLI.py:186-188 ).…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#sorties","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Outputs","x":"File Content Where <ID>_registered.nii.gz T2 resampled into the T1 grid single case: temporary folder; batch: T2 folder <ID>_warp.mat 4×4 greedy affine matrix same <ID>_t2_aligned.nii.gz T2 voxels unchanged, affine corrected T2 folder (distant batch) Transform format, and the conversion that does not exist. <ID>_warp.mat is a plain four-line text file, in greedy's own convention: a 4×4 matrix that maps fixed-volume RAS coordinates to moving-volume RAS coordinates ( [ras(moving);1] = M · [ras(fixed);1] , greedy docs, \"Affine mode\" section). The \"Save Transform Matrix\" button does a plain…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#convention","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Initialization convention: very likely inverted","x":"Manual mode writes the Slicer transform node's matrix, as-is, into <ID>_init.mat ( Logic.py:279-286 ). This matrix is a MatrixTransformToParent applied to the moving volume: it maps the moving volume's own coordinates to world coordinates, i.e. moving → fixed . -ia expects a matrix of the same nature as the one produced by -o , i.e. fixed → moving . Likely trap, unconfirmed Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . The manual mode is absent. An initial_transforms file is handed to -ia unchanged ( SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/pipeline.py:91 , SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:261-265 ); this page…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#predit-calcule","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"10. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Manual pre-alignment interaction, no inference widget Centering T2 on T1 computed geometric (image centers) widget Rigid/affine registration computed intensity optimization (NMI/NCC/SSD, LBFGS inside greedy) native binary, CPU Resampling computed linear interpolation native binary Landmarks in distant mode predicted network, reinforcement learning ( ALI_CBCT , DNet ) slicer.cli.run(ali_cbct) , GPU if available Rigid from landmarks computed geometric, SVD/Kabsch widget (numpy) Mask binarization computed > 0 thresholding CLI (nibabel)"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#environnement","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Interpreter Slicer's Python, for the widget and the CLI ( #!/usr/bin/env python-real ) GreedyReg_CLI.py:1 CLI dependency nibabel alone, installed by ensureNibabelInstalled() after confirmation Logic.py:85 Distant mode dependencies itk , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 , monai ( 1.3.2 if Python ≥ 3.10, otherwise 0.7.0 ) Logic.py:337-339 torch assumed already present via the extension's NNUNet/PyTorch chain, never installed here comment at Logic.py:332-333 GPU irrelevant for greedy; useful for ALI ( torch.cuda.empty_cache() on the ALI side) – conda / WSL none , unlike…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#pieges","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Traps and fragile points","x":"Confirmed bug Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . The port writes into a separate output_dir ( SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:93-94,254-255 ) and, when pairing, sets aside the outputs of an earlier run, using one only for a patient who has nothing else ( pairing.is_previous_output , SADT-VISOR AREG/common/src/sadt_areg_common/pairing.py:214-229,262-266 ). Legacy: GreedyReg.py:1637@3c67957 writes _t2_aligned into the T2 folder. The batch writes its outputs into the T2 input folder. A01_registered.nii.gz and A01_t2_aligned.nii.gz both map to the identifier A01 under ID_PATTERN . On…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#litterature","p":"GreedyReg","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"13. Literature","x":"The engine is published, the module is not. greedy : a tool by Paul Yushkevich (PICSL, UPenn), bundled with ITK-SNAP. The usual reference for citing it is Venet, Pati, Yushkevich, Bakas, Accurate and Robust Alignment of Variable-Stained Histologic Images Using a General-Purpose Greedy Diffeomorphic Registration Tool (arXiv:1904.11929) . Documentation: greedy.readthedocs.io , code: pyushkevich/greedy . Note: the paper and the tool are about diffeomorphic registration; GreedyReg only uses the affine/rigid mode ( -a ), which is merely its initialization step. ALI_CBCT : Gillot et al., Automatic…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html","p":"GreedyReg: sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"GreedyReg's bibliography: what is deposited in this folder, what is available to read online, and its neighborhood, work that describes the engine or the borrowed components, never this module itself. The module itself is not published anywhere. No paper, no abstract, no Project Week report is about GreedyReg. What is published is the engine it drives, greedy , the tool by Paul Yushkevich (PICSL, University of Pennsylvania, shipped with ITK-SNAP), and the network it borrows for \"Distant Registration\" mode, ALI_CBCT. Both of these works are neighborhood material: they describe components, not…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"GreedyReg: sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2019_Venet_greedy_diffeomorphic_registration.pdf Venet L., Pati S., Yushkevich P., Bakas S. Accurate and Robust Alignment of Variable-Stained Histologic Images Using a General-Purpose Greedy Diffeomorphic Registration Tool. arXiv:1904.11929, 2019, 3 p. arXiv preprint arXiv:1904.11929 2023_Gillot_ALI_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M., Miranda F., Baquero B., Ruellas A., Gurgel M., Al Turkestani N., Anchling L., Hutin N., Biggs E., Yatabe M., Paniagua B., Fillion-Robin J.-C., Allemang D., Bianchi J., Cevidanes L., Prieto J.C. Automatic landmark…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"GreedyReg: sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Yushkevich P.A., Pluta J., Wang H., Wisse L.E.M., Das S., Wolk D. IC-P-174: Fast Automatic Segmentation of Hippocampal Subfields and Medial Temporal Lobe Subregions in 3 Tesla and 7 Tesla T2-Weighted MRI. Alzheimer's & Dementia 12(7S_Part_2):P126-P127, 2016. This is the citation the official greedy website asks to be used ( sites.google.com/view/greedyreg/cite ). Conference abstract, paywalled. alz-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com 10.1016/j.jalz.2016.06.205 Yushkevich P.A., Piven J., Hazlett H.C., Smith R.G., Ho S., Gee J.C., Gerig G. User-guided 3D active contour…"},{"u":"GreedyReg/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"GreedyReg: sources","t":"GreedyReg","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighborhood","x":"None of the works below describe GreedyReg . They describe the engine or the borrowed components. The greedy paper (Venet 2019, retrieved) describes what GreedyReg does not use. It presents a two-stage diffeomorphic registration: an initializing affine step, followed by estimating a deformation field. GreedyReg only ever calls the first one: greedy -a with -dof 6 or -dof 12 , and greedy -rf/-rm for resampling. The deformation field, the \"greedy\" in the tool's name, the diffeomorphic NCC metric: none of that is ever executed. Citing this paper to describe GreedyReg would amount to describing…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Report from the 43 rd NA-MIC Project Week (Montreal, 2025): the first version of the pipeline, coarse alignment by TorchReg followed by fine registration by Elastix on cropped volumes. Investigators Name Affiliation Country Alban Gaydamour University of Michigan USA Lucia Cevidanes University of Michigan USA Gaelle Leroux CPE Lyon France Juan Prieto University of North Carolina USA Steve Pieper Isomics USA Enzo Tulissi University of Michigan USA"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#description","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Project Description","x":"Accurate registration between CBCT and MRI scans offers complementary visualization of osseous and soft tissue structures around the temporomandibular joint ( TMJ ). This project aims to automate the registration process to support more effective diagnosis of temporomandibular degenerative joint disease."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#objective","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Objective","x":"Develop a robust multi-step registration pipeline to align MRI and CBCT images in the TMJ region. Enhance diagnostic accuracy by integrating soft tissue and osseous imaging modalities. Move toward a fully automated workflow that eliminates manual intervention."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#approach","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Perform initial coarse alignment using TorchReg to approximate global positioning of the volumes. Automatically crop both CBCT and MRI volumes to isolate the TMJ region of interest. Apply Elastix-based deformable registration to the cropped images for fine alignment. Evaluate registration quality through both visual assessment and quantitative metrics. Plan to implement automated TMJ region localization to eliminate manual cropping. Note This step 3 plan describes deformable registration by Elastix on the cropped images; the MRI2CBCT page describes, in the current code, no elastix at all: a…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#progress","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Implemented automated global registration using TorchReg. Established an automated cropping protocol focused on the TMJ region to reduce failure modes in deformable registration. Completed fine registration using Elastix on cropped image pairs. Next steps include developing automated tools to segment MRIs and adding an articular disc label to improve interpretability."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#illustrations","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Illustrations","x":"Four figures accompany the original report, hosted on GitHub (not embedded here so this page stays readable offline): Figure 1: Input MRI (grey) and CBCT (beige), view the image on GitHub Figure 2: Patient after first registration, view the image on GitHub Figure 3: Cropping of the left TMJ, view the image on GitHub Figure 4: Patient after second registration, view the image on GitHub"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2025_NAMIC_PW43_automated_cbct_mri_registration.html#references","p":"Automated CBCT-MRI Registration Advances Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease Diagnosis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Background and References","x":"TorchReg Elastix Github: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"Report from the 45 th NA-MIC Project Week (Boston, 2026): fixes to the existing pipeline and new manual registration tools designed for clinicians. Investigators Name Affiliation Country Eduardo Duarte Caleme University of North Carolina USA Lucia Cevidanes University of North Carolina USA Paul Dumont University of North Carolina USA Alex Buisson University of North Carolina USA Steve Pieper Isomics USA Gaelle Leroux CPE Lyon France Juan Prieto University of North Carolina USA Alban Gaydamour CPE Lyon France Andras Lasso Queen's University Canada"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#repo","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Github Repository","x":"Slicer"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#funding","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Funding Source(s)","x":"(to be added)"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#description","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Project Description","x":"MRI-to-CBCT registration remains a pressing challenge in medical imaging. Simultaneous visualization of hard and soft tissue structures benefits both clinicians and patients in the diagnosis of temporomandibular degenerative joint disease. Challenges remain in accurately registering these two modalities due to differences in intensity distributions that complicate mutual information optimization, as well as the necessity for initial manual alignment, which can prove unintuitive and challenging for clinicians using current 3D Slicer tools."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#objective","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Objective","x":"Fix issues with the current registration pipeline that impede it from producing appropriate outputs. Implement clinician-friendly manual registration tools. Expand options in both fully automated and clinician-in-the-loop workflows for multi modal registration between MRI and CBCT."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#approach","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Present the restored MR2CBCT pipeline and validated results to the community. Explore improved Elastix parameter strategies to increase the capture range beyond the current limits. Present, test, and optimize the new clinician-friendly manual registration tools. Discuss validation framework design."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#progress","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Fixed a critical bug in AREG_MRI.py where process_images() was never called. Fixed naming convention restrictions throughout the pipeline that proved unintuitive for clinicians. Fixed a mask size mismatch crash in apply_mask.py and a left/right label swap in TMJ crop side detection. Implemented clinician-friendly manual approximation tools, featuring options to freely translate the volume by dragging in slice views, rotate by dragging the circle edge, set a custom center of rotation, and center the MRI at the CBCT center of mass for extremely distant cases. This makes registration…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#illustrations","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Illustrations","x":"The original report includes a screenshot, a video, and four images, hosted on GitHub (not embedded here so this page stays readable offline): Screenshot: Screenshot01.jpg (file attached to the report, not redistributed here) Demo video: video on GitHub Initial images: image on GitHub Center MRI on CBCT function: image on GitHub Clinician manual registration: image on GitHub Elastix Refinement: image on GitHub"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/2026_NAMIC_PW45_MR2CBCT_restoring_extending.html#references","p":"MR2CBCT: Restoring and Extending Automated CBCT-MRI Registration for TMJ Analysis","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Background and References","x":"The original report lists no separate bibliography section beyond the Github repository cited above and the link to the Slicer pull request cited in \"Progress and Next Steps\"."},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"One-button MRI → CBCT registration of the TMJ. The module pairs an MRI folder with a CBCT folder, orients each CBCT, predicts the cranial base on both scans with two networks, then registers the two predicted surfaces by FPFH + RANSAC + ICP (Open3D) and climbs a recovery ladder when the result comes out flipped. The images themselves are never compared. Since PRs #277 and #278 the module is a single panel ( MRI2CBCT.py , 753 lines) over a Slicer-free package, MRI2CBCT_core/ . Seven steps are chained by one Run button: three CLIs of its own ( MRI2CBCT_PREPARE , MRI2CBCT_SEGMENT_MRI…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#situation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Nothing calls the three new CLIs but the module's own panel: a search for MRI2CBCT_PREPARE , MRI2CBCT_SEGMENT_MRI and MRI2CBCT_REGISTER across the whole extension finds them in plan.py and in their own folders only. Agent 's manifest still knows a single MRI2CBCT CLI, mri2cbct_resample ( Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml:491 ), and none of the three. The module declares dependencies = [] ( MRI2CBCT.py:49 ): it no longer depends on SlicerNNUNet, nnU-Net comes from pip . Called How When MRI2CBCT_PREPARE , MRI2CBCT_SEGMENT_MRI , MRI2CBCT_REGISTER slicer.cli.run , one step at a time ( MRI2CBCT.py:386…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#fichiers","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.py the panel ( MRI2CBCTWidget ), first-run setup and the pause views ( MRI2CBCTLogic ), one smoke test MRI2CBCT_core/plan.py the seven steps: which CLI, which arguments, which pause; model locations MRI2CBCT_core/pairing.py pairs the two folders by patient ID; detects swapped folders MRI2CBCT_core/mri_prep.py resample to 0.3 mm; left/right split; side from the header MRI2CBCT_core/seg516.py Dataset516 inference through nnU-Net's Python API MRI2CBCT_core/ladder.py one joint: surfaces, side cut, worker pool, the FLIP ladder MRI2CBCT_core/reg/ pipeline.py (constants…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#pipeline","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"3. Pipeline order, and what is actually mandatory","x":"pair → [resample] [split L/R] → orient CBCT (ASO) → cranial base on CBCT → cranial base on MRI → register The data flow as two lanes. The numbers are the order in which the panel runs the seven steps; the three ASO CLIs share one box (2 to 4). Both networks (shaded) only produce a cranial-base mask; the registration reads those two masks and nothing else. The panel counts seven steps ( plan.py:69-98 ): PREPARE, PRE_ASO_CBCT, ALI_CBCT, SEMI_ASO_CBCT, AMASSS_CLI, SEGMENT_MRI, REGISTER. The figure groups the three orientation CLIs in one box. # Step Status Why 1 MRI2CBCT_PREPARE mandatory pairs…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#preparation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Pairing and MRI preparation","x":"4.1 Pairing by patient ID pair_folders ( pairing.py:81 ). The modality comes from the folder the clinician chose, never from the file name ( pairing.py:3-4 ). The ID is the leading letters followed by a number, ^([A-Za-z]+)[_\\-\\s]*0*(\\d+) ( pairing.py:12 ): P01 , B038 , A3_scan . Leading zeros do not count, so P1 and P01 are the same patient, and case does not either ( patient_key , :27-32 ). Accepted extensions are .nii.gz , .nii and .nrrd ( :11 ), searched recursively ( list_images , :41-47 ). A file or patient is left out, with a message, when: no ID can be read, there is an MRI but no…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#orientation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"5. Orienting the CBCT (ASO)","x":"Three CLIs borrowed from ASO and ALI , called \"exactly as their own modules call them\" ( plan.py:3-5 ): PRE_ASO_CBCT centres the scan: a translation by minus the physical position of the image centre, then a resample. It receives a model_folder argument ( plan.py:73-76 ) that the CLI parses and never reads ( PRE_ASO_CBCT.py:272 is the only use), which is why MRI2CBCT's downloads do not include the PreASO models. ALI_CBCT places seven landmarks: Ba , LPo , N , RPo , S , LOr , ROr ( plan.py:14 ), with the arguments spacing [1,0.3] , speed_per_scale [1,1] , agent_FOV [64,64,64] , spawn_radius…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#segmentation","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"6. The two networks","x":"Neither network registers anything. Each produces one binary cranial-base mask; the registration works on the surfaces of those two masks. Element CBCT: AMASSS MRI: Dataset516 Nature predicted nnU-Net, one binary model for the CB structure predicted nnU-Net 3d_fullres , trainer nnUNetTrainer_250epochs__nnUNetPlans__3d_fullres ( env.py:20 ) Called as AMASSS_CLI through slicer.cli.run : skullStructure = CB , merge = MERGE , no VTK ( plan.py:87-92 ) nnU-Net's Python API in process, not the nnUNetv2_predict script, whose shebang \"cannot find libpython outside Slicer's launcher\" ( seg516.py:1-5 )…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#recalage","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Registration","x":"MRI2CBCT_REGISTER loads, per patient, the CBCT cranial-base mask once and the ASO transform ( MRI2CBCT_REGISTER.py:50-51 ), then registers each joint with ladder.register_joint ( :70-73 ). The images are not read at this point : the only inputs are two masks. 7.1 Surfaces MRI mask ( mri_surface , ladder.py:131-147 ): threshold at 0.5, refuse a mask under 50 voxels, keep the largest connected component (6-connectivity), sample the surface. The component filter is called mandatory in surface.py:35-41 , because normal orientation is propagated across a connectivity graph and detached islands…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#sortie","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Outputs, and the frame they are in","x":"Everything is written in the original CBCT's frame , not the ASO-oriented one: T = inv(T_aso) @ T_register ( MRI2CBCT_REGISTER.py:93 ). Per patient, in <output>/<PID>/ ( :4-8 , :34-36 ): File Content <PID>_<S>_MRI_REG<TAG>.nii.gz the MRI, registered onto the CBCT <PID>_<S>_MRI_MASK<TAG>.nii.gz its Dataset516 cranial base, registered <PID>_<S>_MRI_transform<TAG>.tfm the transform, MRI → CBCT <PID>_CBCT_MASK.nii.gz once per patient: the AMASSS cranial base, brought back to the original CBCT summary.csv one row per joint: patient, side, status, qc, tag, fitness, rotation_deg, mask_cm3…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#interface","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"9. The panel","x":"MRI2CBCTWidget . Top to bottom: One-time setup ( MRI2CBCT.py:529 , :544 ): a line that reads \"One-time setup needed (packages and models, about 5 GB)\" until everything is present, with a button; Run does it too. quick_status only looks at installed packages and download markers, with no torch import and no GPU query ( :529-542 ). If PyTorch had to be installed or replaced, the panel asks for a Slicer restart and stops ( :576-581 ). Inputs : the MRI, CBCT and output folders, and Use test files ( onTestFiles , :274 ): downloads a sample patient (one CBCT, two MRIs) to…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#predit-calcule","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"10. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Pairing, side from the header, left/right split, 0.3 mm resample computed file names, header geometry, SimpleITK PREPARE CBCT centring computed translation to the image centre PRE_ASO_CBCT Seven landmarks predicted ALI landmark models, one per landmark ALI_CBCT Frankfurt / midsagittal orientation computed transform from the landmarks to the reference SEMI_ASO_CBCT Cranial base on the CBCT predicted AMASSS, structure CB AMASSS_CLI Cranial base on the MRI predicted nnU-Net Dataset516, 5 folds or 1 SEGMENT_MRI Surfaces, side cut, FPFH, RANSAC, ICP computed numpy, SciPy…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#pieges","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"11. Traps and fragile spots","x":"Trap · not in VISOR Legacy only (3c67957); not present in VISOR . MRI2CBCT is not a tool in SADT-VISOR ( SADT-VISOR PROVENANCE.md:46-47 ) and the client modules are not built ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote CMakeLists.txt:31-32 ), so no VISOR code exists to check this against. A weak registration keeps a clean file name. The ladder only starts on a FLIP ( ladder.py:215 ). A pose that is not a flip but has fitness under 0.75 is accepted as it is, tagged FLAG in qc , and written without a suffix, because the _FLAGGED name is reserved for unresolved flips ( MRI2CBCT_REGISTER.py:92 , ladder.py:270…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#environnement","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"12. Environment","x":"Element Value Interpreter Slicer's Python everywhere; the three CLIs use #!/usr/bin/env python-real Conda none GPU optional for the MRI network (CPU fallback, seg516.py:19 ); the torch build is chosen from the GPU by ADTLib.env.cuda.torch_install_arguments and installed first ( requirements.py:11-12 , MRI2CBCT.py:605 ) Packages itk , pytorch_lightning , dicom2nifti>=2.6.2 , pydicom==3.0.2 , monai==1.3.2 , blosc2 , einops , nibabel , scipy , nnunetv2>=2.8.0 ( requirements.py:15-20 ): the union of what ASO, ALI, AMASSS and nnU-Net import, with the pins those modules already use numpy restored…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#litterature","p":"MRI2CBCT","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"13. Literature","x":"The paper linked from this tool is Novel CBCT-MRI Registration Approach for Enhanced Analysis of Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease , Leroux G. et al. , Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2024), LNCS vol. 15196, Springer. As this folder's bibliography reads its abstract, it describes the earlier sequence: automatic orientation, resampling, MRI inversion, normalization, then rigid registration by mutual information. That is not what the code does any more. The current registration is a global search on two predicted surfaces and uses no intensity. The same goes for the two NA-MIC…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html","p":"MRI2CBCT: sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What the module owes to the literature, what was retrieved in full text, and what remains paywalled or untraceable. Partially published, and published out of step with the code. Two texts do bear on this module: the method paper (Leroux et al. , CLIP 2024, paywalled) and a clinical case series that documents its use (Caleme et al. , Seminars in Orthodontics 2025, retrieved here). Neither describes the version that runs today: the CLIP 2024 paper and the 2025 case series cover the earlier sequence (orientation, resampling, inversion, normalization, rigid elastix on mutual information, with a…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"MRI2CBCT: sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Caleme_MR2CBCT_TMJ_case_series.xml / .txt Caleme E.D., Cevidanes L., Mattos C., Miranda F., Gurgel M., Barone S., Gaydamour A., Tulissi E., Claret J., Leroux G., Moro A., Gonçalves J., Ruellas A., Zupelari-Gonçalves P., Morettin-Zupelari M., Hsu N., Wolford L., Prieto J., Bianchi J. Aligning MRI and CBCT for Advanced TMJ Diagnostics: Case Series Using AI-Powered Registration in Dentistry and Orthodontics. Seminars in Orthodontics, July 2025. Journal article: NIHMS author manuscript , freely readable on PMC (retrieved via the NCBI E-utilities; Europe PMC's…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"MRI2CBCT: sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Leroux G., Mattos C.T., Claret J., Caleme E., Barone S., Gurgel M., Miranda F., Gonçalves J.R., Zupelari-Gonçalves P., Morettin-Zupelari M., Wolford L.M., Hsu N.S., Ruellas A.C.O., Bianchi J., Prieto J.C., Cevidanes L. Novel CBCT-MRI Registration Approach for Enhanced Analysis of Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease. In: Drechsler K. et al. (eds), Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2024, MICCAI workshop), Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 15196, Springer, 2024, p. 63-72. This is the module's method paper. No deposit, no author version: Unpaywall and…"},{"u":"MRI2CBCT/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"MRI2CBCT: sources","t":"MRI2CBCT","c":"Recalage","s":"Neighboring work","x":"Related works that do not describe this module . Al-Saleh 2016 and 2017 (retrieved above): a team from the University of Alberta, unaffiliated with DCBIA. They frame the problem, MRI/CBCT registration of the TMJ, its pitfalls, the state of the art before this module, and serve as a point of comparison, not a description of the code. elastix (Klein 2010, retrieved): the registration library of the earlier version of the module, through the ITKElastix wrapper with the default rigid parameter map. The current code does not use it. Second usual citation, not retrieved here: Shamonin D.P. et al.…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Extraction of TMD clinical indicators from free-text notes by a locally run language model (a fine-tuned BART-large-CNN), then aggregation of the cohort into a single figure panel: text from end to end; no medical image ever enters this module, whatever its name might suggest. Two code bases MedX is no longer in the legacy tree either. It was removed on 2026-09-17 (commit 34d496f , \"Drop MedX and SurgMovPred, kept on archive branches\"), so SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957 (2026-10-26) has no MedX/ folder. Everything numbered below describes the code kept on the branch archive/medx…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#situation","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"1. Where it sits in the chain","x":"MedX is self-contained in both directions : nothing in the repository calls it, and it calls no other module in the repository. Grepping for MedX outside MedX/ and MedX_CLI/ turns up only the table of contents in README.md and the root CMakeLists.txt . Two independent paths, two tabs, two CLIs: Path Button Executable called Where Summarization SummarizeButton python -m MedX_Summarize conda run -n summaries Dashboard DashboardButton slicer.modules.medx_dashboard Slicer's Python, via slicer.cli.run The Dashboard path consumes the output of the Summarization path (a folder of .txt files), but…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#fichiers","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs MedX/MedX.py widget, 1552 lines including ~200 of dark stylesheet; conda management, progress bar, download Slicer's Python MedX_Method/summarize.py , dashboard.py path validation, building the parameters dict same MedX_Method/Method.py , Progress.py abstract base classes (copied from ASO /AREG) same MedX_Summarize.py BART inference loop conda env summaries MedX_CLI_utils/utils.py pdf/docx reading, chunking, model loading same MedX_Dashboard.py dashboard entry point Slicer's Python display_figure.py summary parsing → DataFrame → matplotlib figure (689 lines) same…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#entrees","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"3.1 Grouping by patient process_notes() ( MedX_Summarize.py:67 ) walks the folder non-recursively ( os.listdir , no recursion), keeps only .pdf , .docx , .txt , and groups by file_name.split(\"_\")[0] . Everything before the first underscore is the patient identifier; several notes from the same patient are concatenated into a single text before inference. The progress bar's count, however, uses Method.search() , which is recursive ( glob.iglob(..., recursive=True) , Method.py:115 ). On a tree with subfolders, the denominator counts more files than the CLI actually processes. Pitfall ·…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#modele","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"4. The model","x":"4.1 What actually gets loaded load_model_and_tokenizer() ( utils.py:22 ): tokenizer = BartTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_path) model = BartForConditionalGeneration.from_pretrained(model_path) Nothing else. No peft , no quantization , despite peft and bitsandbytes being listed among the packages installed in the environment ( MedX.py:849 ): those two, plus evaluate and scikit-learn , are leftovers from training. 4.2 The weights URL: https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools/releases/download/MedX/BART.zip ( summarize.py:43 ), 1.51 GB. The archive contains a standard…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#dashboard","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"5. The algorithmic core: the dashboard","x":"No learning here, just parsing and matplotlib. 5.1 Text summary → DataFrame read_summaries() ( display_figure.py:33 ) only reads files ending in _summary.txt , key = split(\"_\")[0] . initialize_key_value_summary() ( dashboard_utils.py:343 ) creates a dict of 56 keys , all typed str , all initialized to \"\" : patient_age , headache_intensity , disc_displacement , muscle_pain_score , sleep_disorder_type , cpap_used , pain_onset_date … This is the module's target schema , and the only place in the repository where it's written down. update_dictionary() ( display_figure.py:69 ) walks the summary…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#sorties","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"6. Post-processing and outputs","x":"Path File Content Summarize <output>/<patient_id>_summary.txt free text, chunks separated by 100 dashes Summarize <log_path> an integer: the number of patients processed Dashboard <output>/dashboard.png the 22×14-inch panel Dashboard <output>/patient_data.csv the 56-column DataFrame Dashboard <output>/dashboard_full_dataframe.csv the same DataFrame , written a second time from generate_dashboard_figure The log_path is <tempDirectory>/process.log ( MedX.py:384 ); the widget watches its mtime and reads the first line ( onCondaProcessUpdate , MedX.py:1125 ). If the Load in 3D Slicer box is…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#predit-calcule","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"7. What's predicted vs what's computed","x":"Block Nature Where pdf/docx/txt extraction computed deterministic (PyMuPDF, python-docx) env summaries Chunking computed deterministic, regex + section heuristics env summaries Extraction of the 56 indicators predicted network: fine-tuned BART-large-CNN, free-form generation env summaries , GPU if available key: value parsing + merging computed deterministic Slicer's Python Cohort aggregates computed deterministic, regex + pandas Slicer's Python Figure computed matplotlib Slicer's Python Only one network in the whole module, and it's generative : it's the one deciding the output format. The…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#environnement","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Conda env summaries (specific to MedX, not shapeaxi ) MedX.py:1364 Python 3.12 MedX.py:1388 Packages in the env transformers , torch , pymupdf , python-docx , evaluate , scikit-learn , peft , bitsandbytes , matplotlib MedX.py:849 Inside Slicer (dashboard) numpy<2.0.0 , pandas , matplotlib MedX.py:903 Windows conda via WSL ( CondaSetUpCallWsl ), checks libxrender1 , libgl1 / libgl1-mesa-glx , libglx-mesa0 check_lib_wsl check_cli_script() ( MedX.py:1457 ) tests import MedX_Summarize in the env and, if that fails, pushes Slicer's PYTHONPATH into it ( conda env config vars set…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#pieges","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"Pitfall · archived code Archived code ( archive/medx at fbfa0fb, removed from the legacy tree on 2026-09-17); not present in VISOR . No VISOR code exists for MedX to compare with: nothing under SADT-VISOR tools/ and no module in AutomatedDentalToolsRemote . The Download button downloads nothing. setup() creates <Documents>/SlicerDownloads/MedX ( MedX.py:394 ), then downloadModel calls DownloadUnzip(directory=SlicerDownloadPath, folder_name=SlicerDownloadPath) . os.path.join with an absolute path makes that path absolute, so out_path = the folder already created, and the if not…"},{"u":"MedX/MedX.html#litterature","p":"MedX","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"10. Literature","x":"The corresponding paper is AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment (2025, Springer LNCS). It describes fine-tuning BART and DeepSeek-R1 on 1,813 annotated segments drawn from 500 TMD records , to extract 56 clinical indicators , the exact number of keys in initialize_key_value_summary() , and feed a patient and cohort dashboard. The paper concludes that BART beats DeepSeek on field-extraction accuracy despite a slightly lower ROUGE score; that's consistent with the module shipping a BART. Gaps between the paper and the repository's code: the…"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html","p":"MedX: sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for MedX: what's deposited in this folder, what's left to consult online, and the neighborhood, work that does not describe this text-summarization module. A tool published once, and only partially : a MICCAI 2025 workshop chapter describes the fine-tuning of the summarization model that MedX ships, but the module in the repository implements only a fraction of what the paper presents, the paper works within a multimodal framework (registered MRI and CBCT), MedX only reads text. The chapter is paywalled and not in PMC: nothing specific to MedX's own work could be retrieved here.…"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"MedX: sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved into this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2019_Lewis_BART_denoising_pretraining.pdf Lewis M, Liu Y, Goyal N, Ghazvininejad M, Mohamed A, Levy O, Stoyanov V, Zettlemoyer L. BART: Denoising Sequence-to-Sequence Pre-training for Natural Language Generation, Translation, and Comprehension. arXiv:1910.13461, Oct 29, 2019 (ACL 2020, pp. 7871–7880). Preprint, arXiv arXiv:1910.13461 2025_Caleme_MRI_CBCT_TMJ_registration.xml / .txt Caleme ED, Cevidanes L, Mattos C, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Barone S, Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Claret J, Leroux G, Moro A, Gonçalves J, Ruellas A, Morettin Zupelari M, Zupelari-Gonçalves P…"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"MedX: sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Mattos C, Teixeira R, Shin M, Hershey A, Kwon A, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Barone S, Aliaga A, Yatabe M, Zupelari P, Zupelari M, Hanauer D, Hsu N, Pieper S, Caleme E, Bianchi J, Goncalves J, Goncalves D, Wolford L, Ruellas A, Prieto J, Li T, Zhu H, Dai R, Styner M, Al Turkestani N, DaSilva AF, Cevidanes L. AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment. In: Clinical Image-Based Procedures, 14th International Workshop, CLIP 2025, held in conjunction with MICCAI 2025, Daejeon, South Korea, September 23, 2025.…"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#modeles","p":"MedX: sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Models and repositories","x":"Item Where facebook/bart-large-cnn , the fine-tuned upstream model Hugging Face The module SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"MedX/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"MedX: sources","t":"MedX","c":"Texte","s":"Neighborhood","x":"This work does not describe MedX and has no bearing on the module's automatic summarization code. It is included here to situate the clinical problem and the rest of the system that MedX was meant to be a building block of. Caleme 2025 (file above): the imaging half of the CLIP 2025 paper, by a team that overlaps almost exactly with the chapter's, Gaydamour and Tulissi are co-authors on it. It covers the MRI/CBCT registration that the chapter describes as the multimodal side, and nothing about clinical text or summarization. It is the only article indexed in Europe PMC carrying Gaydamour's…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"Detection and replacement of sensitive entities in text documents (DOCX, TXT, PDF, CSV, XML, ODT) via Microsoft Presidio , the module never touches a medical image: this is not a DICOM anonymiser . Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). VISOR has no server tool for it. The VISOR client extension ships an older copy of the module that runs inside Slicer and sends nothing, and it is not the de-identification of the files VISOR uploads (that one is specified and not built, see De-identification in VISOR ).…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#pas-dicom","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"0. What the module is not","x":"The name suggests a DICOM anonymiser. It is nothing of the sort, and it can be checked with a single command: $ grep -rin \"dicom\\|pydicom\\|deface\" Medical_Data_Anonymizer_Module/ (no result) $ grep -rlni \"deface\" --include=\"*.py\" --include=\"*.md\" . (no result anywhere in the repository) Direct consequences: no DICOM tag is ever erased or replaced : not PatientName (0010,0010), not PatientID (0010,0020), not PatientBirthDate , not StudyDate , not InstitutionName , not the UIDs, not the private tags , not BurnedInAnnotation ; no defacing : the reconstructible face in a CBCT or scanner is never…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#situation","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"An isolated tool, without a CLI. Nothing calls it, and it calls nothing else in the repository. Everything lives in a single 767-line file, ~350 of which are Qt stylesheets: Medical_Data_Anonymizer_Module.py . Each document is reduced to a text string, analysed by Presidio (spaCy NER + rules), then rebuilt after replacement; the mapping table file_mappings.csv is written separately, in the same output folder. Medical_Data_Anonymizer_ModuleLogic and …Test are empty classes ( pass ): all the logic lives in the widget, hence on Slicer's main thread , the interface freezes during processing…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#entrees","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"2. Inputs: text extraction, format by format","x":"Recursive walk of the input folder ( os.walk ); files starting with ~$ (Word lock files) are ignored. Each file is reduced to a single character string before analysis: Format Extraction What is lost along the way .docx python-docx , \"\\n\".join(p.text for p in doc.paragraphs) tables , headers/footers, footnotes, text boxes, comments, file metadata .txt raw UTF-8 read – .pdf pdfplumber , page.extract_text() ; on exception, a second attempt via extract_tables() images, scanned PDFs (no OCR) → empty text .csv csv.reader , each row flattened by \" \".join(row) the column structure .xml ElementTree…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#moteur","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"3. The detection engine","x":"No model in the repository, no weights downloaded by the module itself apart from the spaCy model. Two Presidio engines are instantiated on every click of \"Anonymize Files\" ( …py:493 ), with stderr short-circuited by a SuppressStderr class: analyzer = AnalyzerEngine() # default config anonymizer = AnonymizerEngine() AnalyzerEngine() with no argument uses Presidio's default NLP configuration, that is spaCy en_core_web_lg : the only model that install_dependencies() downloads ( spacy.cli.download , …py:411 ). The two families of Presidio recognizers combine before score filtering: spaCy NER…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#entites-methodes","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"4. Entities and replacement methods","x":"Twelve checkboxes, seven checked by default ( …py:90 ): Presidio entity UI label Default PERSON predicted Names (patients, doctors) ✔ PHONE_NUMBER computed Phone Numbers ✔ EMAIL_ADDRESS computed Email Addresses ✔ DATE_TIME predicted Dates and Times ✔ LOCATION predicted Addresses and Locations ✔ US_SSN computed Social Security Numbers ✔ MEDICAL_LICENSE computed Medical License Numbers ✔ US_DRIVER_LICENSE , CREDIT_CARD , US_BANK_NUMBER , IP_ADDRESS , URL computed – ✘ Four methods, in \"Advanced Options\" ( …py:720 ): Method Implementation Result replace (default) operators = {} → passed as None…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#sorties","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"5. Outputs and the mapping table","x":"Written flat into the output folder: the input's subfolder hierarchy is not reproduced. Input Output Note x.docx x_anonymized.docx rebuilt document, one paragraph per line, no styling x.txt x_anonymized.txt – x.pdf x_anonymized.pdf regenerated by reportlab , a single Preformatted block x.csv x_anonymized.csv see below x.xml x_anonymized.xml plain text under an .xml extension : no tags remain, and the code comment admits it (\"XML anonymization is complex\") x.odt x_anonymized.txt extension change The CSV case is the most destructive: the file was read by flattening each row with spaces, then…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#predit-calcule","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"6. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Text extraction computed deterministic, by format library widget, UI thread Detection of PERSON / LOCATION / DATE_TIME predicted network: spaCy NER en_core_web_lg (CNN + embeddings), via Presidio widget, CPU Detection of numbers (SSN, phone, card, license…) computed regular expressions + checksums and context keywords same Replacement computed deterministic ( replace / redact / SHA-256 / mask) presidio-anonymizer Document reconstruction computed deterministic, by format widget No model is trained or bundled in the repository; en_core_web_lg is pulled by spacy.cli.download…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#limites","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"7. Limits: what is NOT removed","x":"The most useful section for deciding whether a dataset can be shared. Out of scope by construction All images. DICOM, NIfTI, NRRD, meshes: never read, never modified. No header tag is cleaned, no face is erased ( §0 ). File and folder names. Dupont_Jean_2019.docx becomes Dupont_Jean_2019_anonymized.docx . The patient's name survives intact in the file name, and it is copied verbatim into file_mappings.csv . The input hierarchy, often just as identifying ( .../Patients/Dupont_Jean/ ), is flattened, and if two subfolders each contain a file with the same name, the second silently overwrites…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#environnement","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"8. Environment","x":"Everything runs in Slicer's Python, installed by the \"Install Dependencies\" button ( …py:392 ), with no confirmation and no pinned versions: presidio-analyzer, presidio-anonymizer, pandas, python-docx, pdfplumber, odfpy, lxml, langdetect, reportlab then spacy.cli.download(\"en_core_web_lg\") Notes: spacy is not in the pip_install list: it is only imported ( …py:409 ). It works because presidio-analyzer pulls it in as a dependency; if that import fails, the whole block falls into the except and shows \"Error installing dependencies.\" A Slicer restart is requested after installation (dialog box)…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#pieges","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"9. Traps and fragile points","x":"Silent failure returning the original text ( §7.9 ). Flattened output folder : name collisions, overwriting with no message. CSV restructured by line.split() , XML degraded to plain text, ODT renamed to .txt . UUID generated but never applied : the \"mapping table\" allows no controlled re-identification. file.replace(\".docx\", \"_anonymized.docx\") : str.replace replaces every occurrence; a name containing the extension twice produces an unexpected result. The output PDF has no pagination (a single Preformatted block): a long document can overflow the frame or raise a reportlab exception, in…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#references","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"10. Literature and references","x":"No paper covers this module; nor is there an evaluation (recall, precision) in the repository, on any corpus. The building blocks used are documented: Microsoft Presidio : the detection and anonymisation engine, with the exact list of its predefined recognizers and operators ( replace , redact , hash , mask , encrypt , the latter, reversible with a key, is not offered by the module). spaCy en_core_web_lg : the English NER model used by default. HIPAA Safe Harbor, 45 CFR §164.514(b)(2) : the 18 categories of identifiers to remove. Useful as a reading grid for §7 : file names, medical record…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"No article describes this module: only its two technical building blocks ( Presidio , spaCy ) and the clinical text de-identification literature (i2b2/n2c2) make up the relevant context. Unpublished No article, abstract, poster, or Project Week report covers this module, and the repository contains no evaluation: no recall, no precision, on any corpus. The only real references are technical: Presidio (Microsoft) and spaCy , its two building blocks, neither of which has a review article. Add to that a neighboring body of work that must be named, since the page establishes that this module…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2025_Alrazihi_presidio_philter_evaluation.xml / .txt Alrazihi L.A., Biswas S., George J. Evaluating the accuracy of automated and semi-automated anonymization tools for unstructured health records. Surgical Neurology International 16:313, 2025. Evaluation of Microsoft Presidio and Philter on 200 unstructured clinical documents: the performance measurement this module does not provide. Review article, full JATS text (Europe PMC, CC BY-NC-SA) 10.25259/SNI_459_2025 : PMCID PMC12477974"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"The overviews of the clinical text de-identification campaigns are author manuscripts deposited in PMC: free to read, but outside the freely redistributable subset. Links and DOIs, then, rather than files. Reference Where DOI Uzuner Ö., Luo Y., Szolovits P. Evaluating the state-of-the-art in automatic de-identification. JAMIA 14(5):550-563, 2007. The first i2b2 de-identification challenge. PMC1975792 10.1197/jamia.M2444 Stubbs A., Uzuner Ö. Annotating longitudinal clinical narratives for de-identification: The 2014 i2b2/UTHealth corpus. Journal of Biomedical Informatics 58(Suppl):S20-S29…"},{"u":"MedicalDataAnonymizer/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: sources","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer","c":"Texte","s":"Neighboring work","x":"None of these works describes this module . Microsoft Presidio : the detection and anonymisation engine. No paper: the documentation is the authoritative reference, with the exact list of predefined recognizers and operators ( replace , redact , hash , mask , encrypt , the latter, reversible with a key, is not offered by the module). microsoft.github.io/presidio · github.com/microsoft/presidio spaCy : the English NER en_core_web_lg used by default. No paper either; cite the software record: Honnibal M., Montani I., Van Landeghem S., Boyd A. spaCy: Industrial-strength Natural Language…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"NA-MIC Project Week 45 project page (Boston, 2026): the only primary report found on SurgMovPred, a progress report, not a peer-reviewed publication. Investigator Affiliation Country Alexandre Buisson University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Paul Dumont University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Juan Carlos Prieto University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Lucia Cevidanes University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill USA Steve Pieper Isomics USA Mauro I. Dominguez (I added me if that's ok) Independent Argentina Presentation location ( presenter_location ): not provided in the…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#github","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Github Repository","x":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#financement","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Funding Source(s)","x":"R01DE024450"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#description","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Project Description","x":"Predicting surgical movements and bone displacement vectors in virtual surgical planning software remains an expert-intensive task, requiring surgeons to simulate osteotomies and manually adjust bone segments. Although statistical shape models and deep learning regression networks have been explored to automate this phase, they output dense deformation fields that lack the geometric interpretability needed to guide clinical or surgical decisions. This project introduces a dedicated 3D Slicer module driven by a Machine Learning Stacking model, trained on a robust dataset of 1,496 patients.…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#objectif","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Objective","x":"Embed a pre-trained Stacking ML model into the 3D Slicer environment to leverage multi-patient surgical data. Enable seamless parsing of input files (Excel/CSV) to extract patient-specific features. Generate and display precise bone movement recommendations directly within the Slicer interface based on the model's inference."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#approche","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Approach and Plan","x":"Develop the input file parser (Excel/CSV) to clean and prepare patient data for the model. Integrate the pre-trained Stacking ML model into a Python-based 3D Slicer scripted module. Design a user-friendly Slicer UI that allows clinicians to load patient files, trigger the prediction, and visualize the recommended bone displacements."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#progres","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"The core Stacking ML model has been successfully trained and validated using a dataset of 1,496 patient cases. During the project week we'll build an interactive UI and backend pipeline within 3D Slicer to handle file inputs and run the model's prediction pipeline. Also, we'll verify the accuracy of the outputs within the Slicer environment and explore intuitive ways to display the predicted movements to the user."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#illustrations","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Illustrations","x":"Pitfall This project page's illustrations are hosted on GitHub ( github.com/user-attachments/... ) and are therefore not reproducible offline; they are left here as links rather than as broken images. Two of the three screenshots actually show other modules (IOS-CBCT Registration, VFACE ), not SurgMovPred itself: the same copy-paste drift noted in SurgMovPred.py (see the tool page, \"Position in the pipeline\" section). Screenshot (1728×695, alt: \"Screenshot 2026-06-25 at 10 29 58 AM\"), overview of the tool. Tool Demo: demo video . IOS-CBCT Registration tool: screenshot (670×794, alt…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.html#references","p":"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Background and References","x":"Github link : https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools This work was supported by the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR) of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01DE024450 ."},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html","p":"SurgMovPred: sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"SurgMovPred bibliography: no article describes the tool, only a NA-MIC project page constitutes a primary source. No article describes SurgMovPred. But the page can be filled out on one point: there is a NA-MIC Project Week 45 project page (Boston, 2026) devoted exactly to this module, New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery , retrieved in this folder. It names the authors (Alexandre Buisson, Paul Dumont, Juan Carlos Prieto, Lucia Cevidanes, Steve Pieper, UNC Chapel Hill; Mauro I. Dominguez), confirms the model ( Machine Learning Stacking model , Excel/CSV…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"SurgMovPred: sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Retrieved in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2026_Buisson_NAMIC_PW45_surgery_movement.md Buisson A, Dumont P, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L, Pieper S, Dominguez MI. \"New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery\". Project page, NA-MIC Project Week 45, Boston, 2026. project report: only primary source project page 2023_Cheng_orthognathic_plan_deep_learning.xml / .txt Cheng M, Zhang X, Wang J, Yang Y, Li M, Zhao H, Huang J, Zhang C, Qian D, Yu H. \"Prediction of orthognathic surgery plan from 3D cephalometric analysis via deep learning\". BMC Oral Health , 2023, 23(1), 161. neighboring…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"SurgMovPred: sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"To read online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Ma Q, Kobayashi E, Fan B, Hara K, Nakagawa K, Masamune K, Sakuma I, Suenaga H. \"Machine-learning-based approach for predicting postoperative skeletal changes for orthognathic surgical planning\". Int J Med Robot Comput Assist Surg , 2022, 18(3), e2379. publisher : paywalled ( oa_status: closed ) 10.1002/rcs.2379 de Oliveira PHJ, Li T, Li H, Gonçalves JR, Santos-Pinto A, Gandini Junior LG, et al. , Cevidanes L, Bianchi J. \"Artificial intelligence as a prediction tool for orthognathic surgery assessment\". Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research , 2024, 27(5), 785-794.…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"SurgMovPred: sources","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighboring resources","x":"These works do not describe the SurgMovPred code nor the 112 model packages shipped with it. The exact problem, another team. Ma 2022 ( IJMRCAS ) predicts post-operative skeletal changes from pre-operative measurements, using tabular machine learning. This is the closest formulation to SurgMovPred's, but nothing links this work to the embedded weights. Same goal, deep learning. Cheng 2023 ( BMC Oral Health ) predicts the surgical plan from a 3D cephalometric analysis; Kim 2025 ( Nature Communications ) goes further and synthesizes the post-operative lateral cephalogram via a graph network +…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"A spreadsheet of cephalometric measurements goes in, a spreadsheet of predicted bone displacements comes out: 112 stacked scikit-learn regressors, one per target, no neural network, no image, and no mesh. Prediction of the surgical movements of an orthognathic surgery from a spreadsheet of cephalometric measurements . No neural network, no image, no mesh: 112 stacked scikit-learn regressors, one per value to predict, applied row by row to an Excel/CSV file. Two code bases Everything numbered below describes the legacy Slicer module (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957, 2026-10-26). The…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#situation","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Position in the pipeline","x":"Isolated tool. Nothing in the repository calls it, and it calls nothing in the repository: it reads no volume, no mesh, no landmarks. Its input is a folder of spreadsheets, its output another spreadsheet. The complete pipeline: deterministic cleanup, then 112 independent regressions, one per target. No return trip into Slicer's 3D scene. Called How SurgMovPred_CLI slicer.cli.run(slicer.modules.surgmovpred_cli, …) ( SurgMovPred.py:847 ) The module isn't wired into the MRML scene: no vtkMRMLNode is created, read or transformed. It's a launch interface sitting on top of a pandas script. Build…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#fichiers","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Role Where it runs SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.py widget: 3 folder selectors, downloading of models and test files, dependency installation, ~230 lines of QSS Slicer's Python SurgMovPred_CLI/SurgMovPred_CLI.py all of the processing: reading, column-name cleanup, predict , writing Slicer's Python (scripted CLI) SurgMovPred/Resources/UI/SurgMovPred.ui 3 fields ( inputFolderLineEdit , modelFolderLineEdit , outputFolderLineEdit ) + 4 buttons – Only three positional parameters ( inputFolder , modelPath , outputFolder , SurgMovPred_CLI.xml ): nothing is configurable, no threshold, no target…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#entree","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Input and preprocessing","x":"Accepted format. load_data_from_directory() ( SurgMovPred_CLI.py:143 ) collects every *.csv , *.xlsx , *.ods file in the folder (extension in lowercase and uppercase), reads them ( read_csv , read_excel , read_excel(engine='odf') ) and concatenates them ( pd.concat(..., ignore_index=True) ). No recursion into subfolders. For an Excel workbook, only the first sheet is read: the official test file contains seven ( all_data , demographics , Invivo_ceph , surgical_mov , dolp_ceph_T0 , dolp_ceph_T1 , dolp_ceph_total ), and it's all_data that sits in first position. Column-name normalization…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#modeles","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The models","x":"The weights are not in the repository . onDownloadDefaultModel() ( SurgMovPred.py:782 ) downloads and unzips: https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools/releases/download/SurgMovPred/all_models.zip -> Documents/SlicerDownloads/SurgMovPred/Models/all_models/ 706 MB , 112 folders, one stacking_package.pkl of ~13 MB each. load_all_model_packages() does a recursive Path.glob(\"**/stacking_package.pkl\") and indexes by package['target_name'] : pointing the selector at all_models/ loads all 112 models, pointing at a subfolder loads only one. The CLI's help text and a comment…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#traitement","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The core processing","x":"predict_all_targets() ( SurgMovPred_CLI.py:196 ), in full: for target_name, pack in packages.items(): X_target = df_cleaned[[feature_source[f] for f in pack['features_names']]] X_target.columns = pack['features_names'] X_scaled = pack['scaler'].transform(X_target) predictions_by_target[target_name] = pack['model'].predict( pd.DataFrame(X_scaled, columns=..., index=df_cleaned.index)) No post-correction, no bounding, no inter-target consistency, no confidence interval: the GaussianProcessRegressor can produce a standard deviation, it isn't asked to. Each target is handled independently, with a…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#sorties","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Outputs","x":"save_results() writes two files, same content, into the output folder: File Content predictions_outputs.xlsx one row per input patient, IDPatient column then one column per predicted target predictions_outputs.csv same index=True in both cases: the pandas index (0, 1, 2…) becomes an unnamed column. Column names are the packages' raw target_name , so with / and + ( Mx_ALL_APoint_P-/A+_Pred ). Pitfall There is no reapplication of the result whatsoever. Nothing builds a matrix, moves a mesh, creates a Slicer node, or opens a view: the prediction stops at the spreadsheet. The question \"how is…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#predit-vs-calcule","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Column-name cleanup, ID detection computed deterministic (regex) CLI Standardization computed learned StandardScaler CLI Prediction of the 112 displacements predicted stacking ElasticNetCV + LightGBM + GP, meta LinearRegression CLI, CPU Consolidation into a single segment transform nonexistent – The dashed boxes do not exist in the code: nothing guarantees a consistent rigid-body movement for an entire bone segment. Pitfall The maxilla's 10 points are predicted by 40 independent models. Nothing guarantees that the predicted displacements are compatible with a rigid-body…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#environnement","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Environment","x":"Everything runs inside Slicer's Python. check_dependencies() ( SurgMovPred.py:153 ) is called on every click of Apply: Item Value Packages pandas , joblib , openpyxl , scikit-learn , lightgbm Missing from the list odfpy , yet needed by read_excel(engine='odf') for the advertised .ods files numpy slicer.util.pip_install(\"numpy==2.4.0\") unconditional , on every Apply macOS ensure_mac_openmp() downloads libomp.dylib from mac.r-project.org and drops it into Slicer's cli-modules folder, then loads it with RTLD_GLOBAL : LightGBM needs it Compilation CC=gcc / CXX=g++ forced during pip_install…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#pieges","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Pitfalls and fragile points","x":"pip_install(\"numpy==2.4.0\") on every run. Replacing numpy under a live Slicer session is exactly what breaks scipy's import elsewhere in the extension (Slicer's scipy is compiled against its numpy). By far the most dangerous point in the module, and it's triggered by the main button. Inoperative \"test files\" button : DownloadTestFiles() ( SurgMovPred.py:763 ) does download TestFiles.zip , but only fills in the input/output fields if os.path.exists(.../V_FACE/DefaultList) , a copy-paste leftover from the VFACE module. Without VFACE 's test data, the archive is downloaded and then ignored.…"},{"u":"SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#litterature","p":"SurgMovPred","t":"SurgMovPred","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Literature","x":"No paper covers SurgMovPred. Neither the repository, nor the release, nor the README cites any reference, and a search on the module's name returns nothing. The module isn't even listed in the extension's README. The published context, not to be confused with a description of this code: Machine-learning-based approach for predicting postoperative skeletal changes for orthognathic surgical planning (PubMed 35132764) : the problem posed is the same (predicting post-operative skeletal displacements from pre-operative measurements), but nothing indicates this is the work behind these weights.…"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"NA-MIC Project Week 39 project report (Montreal, 2023) describing AQ3DC, the Slicer extension for automatic measurement whose logic ( functionaq3dc.py ) VFACE reuses to compute distances and angles. Field Value Project title Automatic Quantification 3D Components Category Quantification and Computation Presentation In person Project link github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/Q3DCExtension Key investigators Nathan Hutin (University of Michigan) · Luc Anchling (University of Michigan, France) · Baptiste Baquero (University of Michigan, France) · Maxime Gillot (University of Michigan, France) · Lucia…"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#description","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Project Description","x":"The Automatic Quantification 3D Components(AQ3DC) was developed during Namic-project 37 is now available in Slicer Q3DC extension. The Automatic Quantification 3D Components(AQ3DC) aims to provide a user-friendly automated tool that decrease user time for extraction of quantitative image analysis features. AQ3DC is a Slicer extension to automatically compute lists of measurements selected by users for a single case or a whole study sample, at one or more time points. The current implementation is aimed at automatic computation of 3D components like distances (AP, RL and SI) between points…"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#objectives","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Objectives","x":"Renaming function and variable Create Documentation for developer Find bugs"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#progress","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Progress and Next Steps","x":"Progress Resolve few issues Ranaming most of the function Next Steps Improve readability of the code Create Documentation for developer"},{"u":"VFACE/2023_Hutin_NAMIC_PW39_AQ3DC.html#illustrations","p":"Automatic Quantification 3D Components (AQ3DC)","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Illustrations","x":"The six original screenshots are hosted on GitHub ( user-images.githubusercontent.com ); they are not brought over here to keep the site readable offline, but the links are kept. 1. Slicer Interface Screenshot from 2022-06-30 18-31-37 (external link) 2. List of measurements exported. Screenshot from 2022-06-30 18-29-01 (external link) 3. Results of the computation for all the list of measurement for a sample of patient. Screenshot from 2022-06-30 19-01-23 (external link) 4. Skeletal measurements signs meaning. skeletal_measurement (external link) 5. Linear measurements signs meaning.…"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html","p":"VFACE: sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"Bibliography for VFACE: what is published, what is left to check online, and the neighboring work that sheds light on the problem without describing this code. VFACE is partially published , and what is published does not describe what the module actually runs. The SPIE Medical Imaging 2026 proceedings paper (doi:10.1117/12.3087010) describes the same upstream chain, standardized preprocessing, mirroring, voxel registration, extraction of anatomical measurements, but stops at an unsupervised clustering step (dimensionality reduction PCA / t-SNE / UMAP, then Spectral Clustering with kNN or…"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#recupere","p":"VFACE: sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Archived in this folder","x":"File Reference Type DOI / ID 2026_Peng_3D_facial_asymmetry_review.xml / .txt Peng J, Qu W, Zhang S, Lin Y. \"Automated assessment of 3D facial asymmetry: a systematic review\". European Journal of Orthodontics , 2026, 48(3), art. cjag012. systematic review (neighboring work) 10.1093/ejo/cjag012 : PMC13207581 2023_Gillot_ALI_landmark_identification.xml / .txt Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. \"Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam…"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#en-ligne","p":"VFACE: sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"To check online (not freely downloadable)","x":"Reference Where DOI Sanna A, Ng J, Ruellas A, Aliaga A, Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Huang Y, Prieto JC, Al Turkestani N, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Pieper S, Cevidanes L, Li T. \"Automated classification of skeletal facial asymmetry in CBCT using a reproducible 3D Slicer workflow for patient-specific decision support\". In: Gimi BS (ed.), Medical Imaging 2026: Clinical and Biomedical Imaging , Proc. SPIE vol. 13929, art. 139292J. SPIE, 2026. Conference held in Vancouver (Canada), February 15-19, 2026; ISBN 9781510697959. SPIE Digital Library : paywalled ( oa_status: closed at Unpaywall and OpenAlex)…"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#voisinage","p":"VFACE: sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Neighboring work","x":"These works do not describe VFACE's code . They shed light on the problem. The mirror method, within this same group. AlHadidi 2011 (DMFR) directly compares the two strategies: mirroring across a mid-sagittal plane defined by landmarks, versus mirroring across an arbitrary plane followed by a voxel registration on the cranial base. Cevidanes 2011 (OOOOE) validates both on simulated data. AlHadidi 2012 adds SPHARM-PDM, Paniagua 2011 adds tensor-based morphometry. It is the second strategy, mirror then voxel registration, that VFACE implements ( AutoMatrix applies Matrix_mirror.tfm , AREG-CBCT…"},{"u":"VFACE/SOURCES.html#complement","p":"VFACE: sources","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Addendum: three upstream texts retrieved afterward","x":"NIH author manuscripts return an empty fullTextXML at Europe PMC, which is what had them classified as \"to check\". The efetch route serves them: curl -sL \"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=pmc&id=<number without PMC>\" Three of them are now in this folder, in .xml (JATS) and .txt : File Reference PMCID 2011_Cevidanes_3D_quantification_mandibular_asymmetry Cevidanes L. et al. , Oral Surg Oral Med Oral Pathol Oral Radiol Endod , 2011 PMC3095695 2011_Paniagua_mandibular_asymmetry_tensor_morphometry Paniagua B. et al. , Proc IEEE ISBI , 2011 PMC3892912…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"An orchestrator that learns and segments nothing itself: it chains six other modules to register a CBCT onto its own mirror image, measures the gap, and classifies skeletal facial asymmetry with three LightGBM models. The flow as the lab presents it: ASO orients the scan, ALI_CBCT places the landmarks, AutoMatrix builds the reflection, BatchDentalSeg segments, AReg registers the patient onto their mirror, and the classification comes out at the end. The modules the code actually calls are listed in the rest of this page: they do not match this diagram exactly. Lab diagram: high-resolution…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#situation","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Where it sits in the pipeline","x":"Nothing in the repository calls VFACE: CMakeLists.txt:54 adds it as a terminal module. It does, however, call eight processes, seven of which are other modules of the extension. Everything is built in a single pass by CreateListProcess() ( createlistprocess.py:120 ), which returns a list of dictionaries {Process, Parameter, Module, Display, Review*} ; the widget unwinds it one item at a time. Called Slicer module Role in VFACE MRI2CBCT resample mri2cbct_resample_cbct_mri 0.3 mm isotropic resampling + recentering ASO PRE pre_aso_cbct geometric centering, writes a .tfm ALI-CBCT ali_cbct…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#fichiers","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File Lines Role VFACE/VFACE.py 2977 widget: UI, review pauses, batch navigation, sequential execution, cleanup createlistprocess.py 2636 the pipeline plan , plus derive_landmarks , reorganizeStat , postprocess , heatmaps functionaq3dc.py 2484 vendored copy of the AQ3DC module; only AQ3DCLogic is used segmentation_logic.py 1051 drives nnU-Net via SlicerNNUNetLib , VTK exports Measure.py 956 the formulas : Distance , Angle , Diff2Measure review_steps.py 249 catalogue of pauses, and restrictStepToPatients _batch_worker.py 235 one subprocess per mesh pair for the heatmap VFACE_CLI/VFACE_CLI.py…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#entrees","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"3. Inputs and preprocessing","x":"Input : a folder of T1 CBCTs, one per patient ( .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz ). No segmentation, no landmark: VFACE starts from the raw volume. checkInputFolder() ( VFACE.py:1221 ) refuses to start on an empty folder. Patient identity : patientIdFromFileName ( functionaq3dc.py:1370 ) cuts the name on _Scan , _Or , _MAND , _MD , _MAX , _MX , _CB , _lm , _T2 , _T1 , _Cl , . . The same chain is duplicated three times in createlistprocess (marked TIMEPOINT-SUFFIX ): only _T1 / _T2 are recognized , a _T3 silently breaks the pairing. Resampling : spacing [0.3, 0.3, 0.3]…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#modeles","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"4. The models called","x":"VFACE contains only one model of its own, the LightGBMs. The others belong to the upstream modules, downloaded by DownloadAllFiles() ( VFACE.py:950 ). Model Origin Nature ALI-CBCT release v0.1-v2.0_models (7 zips: Cranial Base, Lower Bones 1/2, Upper Bones v2, teeth) RL agents, DNet = monai DenseNet ( brain.py:39 ) AMASSS AMASSS_CBCT/AMASSS_Models.zip 3D UNet, label map output DentalSegmentator Dataset111_453CT_v100.zip (SlicerDentalSegmentator v1.0.0-alpha) nnU-Net 3d_fullres, fold 0 sym_asymm.txt , mand_asym.txt , max_asym.txt release VFACE/V_FACE_Models.zip LightGBM , joblib pickles 4.1…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#mesures","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"5. The algorithmic core: the measurements","x":"5.1 The eight measurement types All instantiated by AQ3DCLogic.createMeasurement ( functionaq3dc.py:1995 ), from the Type of measurement column of the xlsx: matched by exact string equality . Positions are in LPS ( x = left, y = posterior, z = superior). Type (exact string) Inputs Formula ( Measure.py ) Distance between 2 points T1 / T2 P1, P2 at the same time point d = p2 − p1 ; (lr, ap, si) = (−dx, −dy, dz) ; 3D = ‖d‖ ; rounded to 3 decimals Distance between 2 points T1 T2 P1 @T1, P2 @T2 identical, but crossed between the two point sets: this is the asymmetry measurement Distance point…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#classifieur","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"6. The classifier","x":"VFACE_CLI loads three joblib pickles ( VFACE_CLI.py:96 ). These are lightgbm.sklearn.LGBMClassifier models, not a network, not a hand-written rule tree. Verified The hyperparameters and the number of trees kept below were confirmed by unpickling the three .txt files. VISOR loads three files of the same names, sym_asymm.txt , mand_asym.txt and max_asym.txt ( VISOR:SADT-VISOR tools/VFACE/src/sadt_vface/classify.py:28-32 ). Model Hyperparameters Features Trees kept sym_asymm.txt gbdt , objective=binary , num_leaves=31 , learning_rate=0.03 , n_estimators=5000 , class_weight=balanced…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#revue","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"7. Manual review and batch navigation","x":"7.1 The pauses Added by commit 2609bc4 . A step in the plan can carry Review* keys; the widget stops at the end of the step, loads the results into the scene, and waits. The catalogue lives in review_steps.py , kept separate from the pipeline so the panel can list the pauses before CreateListProcess has even created a single folder. Id Label Nature Available if t1_landmarks_orientation_max maxillary orientation landmarks editable Full pipeline t1_oriented_max maxillary orientation view only Full pipeline t1_landmarks_orientation_cb cranial base orientation landmarks editable Full pipeline…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#sorties","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Post-processing and outputs","x":"Folder under OutputFolder Contents Survives cleanup T1 Resample/CBCT 0.3 mm centered volumes no Centered T1 Scans/{CB,MAX} centered volumes + centering .tfm no Oriented T1 Scans/{CB,MAX} <pat>_{CB,MAX}_Or.nii.gz , _transform.tfm , _lm_*.mrk.json no T1 Masks <base>_seg_{CB,MAND,MAX}MASK.nii.gz no T2_Scan/{CB,MAX} or T2 Centered the mirror (or the real T2 in longitudinal mode) no T2_Masks mirrored masks, unused (CMFReg) no Registered Scan/<region>/<pat>_OutReg/ registered scan + _Reg_matrix.tfm no T1 Landmarks/{CB,MAX} ALI .mrk.json and derivatives no Mirrored Landmarks/{CB,MAX} _mir suffix no…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#predit-calcule","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"9. What is predicted vs what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Resampling, centering computed geometric MRI2CBCT / ASO Landmarks (orientation and measurements) predicted RL agents, monai DenseNet ALI-CBCT Orientation onto gold standard computed rigid VTK ICP on landmarks ASO SEMI Bone masks predicted 3D UNet AMASSS AMASSS Mirror computed fixed matrix diag(-1,1,1) AutoMatrix Registration computed rigid Elastix, MI, 3 resolutions AREG MAX landmarks computed rigid composition of two .tfm Python, widget Bone surfaces predicted nnU-Net DentalSegmentator Python, widget, CUDA Linear / angular measurements computed geometric, numpy Python…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#pieges","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"10. Traps and fragile spots","x":"derive_landmarks cannot work outside \"Full pipeline\". In \"File already Oriented\" / \"already Registered\" mode, SplitOriented ( createlistprocess.py:1877 ) only copies the .nii* / .nrrd* files into Oriented T1 Scans/ , not the .tfm files . The \"Deriving T1 Landmarks (MAX)\" step then logs missing an orientation transform for every patient, T1 Landmarks/MAX stays empty, so does Measurements_MAX.xlsx , and the classification is missing five features out of fourteen. CMFReg does not change the registration , it only adds a T2_Masks folder that nothing reads (§1). Hardcoded _T1 / _T2 suffixes…"},{"u":"VFACE/VFACE.html#litterature","p":"VFACE","t":"VFACE","c":"Analyse","s":"11. Literature","x":"The corresponding published work is \"Automated classification of skeletal facial asymmetry in CBCT using a reproducible 3D Slicer workflow for patient-specific decision support\" , SPIE Medical Imaging 2026, vol. 13929, art. 139292J, doi:10.1117/12.3087010 ( SPIE page ). The public abstract describes 170 Class III patients, and the same upstream chain: standardized preprocessing, mirroring , voxel registration, extraction of anatomical measurements. Code / paper divergence The code diverges from the paper at the last step. The abstract describes an unsupervised approach: dimensionality…"},{"u":"glossary.html","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"47 terms the tool pages assume you already know. Defined the way they're used in this repository , not in the abstract. Reference frames and geometry · Registration · Networks and learning · Text and language models · Anatomy and modalities"},{"u":"glossary.html#reperes-et-geometrie","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Reference frames and geometry","x":"direction 3×3 matrix giving the orientation of the voxel grid's axes in physical space. Together with the origin and the spacing, it forms the index-to-millimetre transform. isosurface Surface connecting every point of a volume that shares the same intensity value. AREG_IOSCBCT extracts one at threshold 400 to give its ICP a geometric target. LPS Left–Posterior–Superior: the three axes point toward the patient's left, the back, and up. This is the frame used by DICOM, ITK, and this repository's .vtk files. ALI_CBCT and ALI_IOS both write \"coordinateSystem\": \"LPS\" in their JSON. mesh A…"},{"u":"glossary.html#recalage","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Registration","x":"affine 12 degrees of freedom: rigid, plus scale and shear. Straight lines stay straight and parallel; distances don't. elastix Image-registration library driven by parameter files. AREG_CBCT calls it through itk-elastix for its voxel-to-voxel registration (MRI2CBCT did before it moved to surface registration). ICP Iterative Closest Point : for each moving point, find its nearest neighbour in the target, estimate the rigid transform that brings them closer, and repeat. Converges well only if the starting point is already close, hence the systematic landmark pre-alignment. Kabsch The algorithm…"},{"u":"glossary.html#reseaux-et-apprentissage","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Networks and learning","x":"reinforcement-learning agents ALI_CBCT doesn't segment: it moves agents through the volume step by step, each one learning to get closer to its landmark. The search stops when an agent starts oscillating, then resumes at a finer resolution. checkpoint A file of trained weights. Reading its hyperparameters is often the only reliable way to know what a model actually does: several code/paper discrepancies in this repository were established that way. cross-encoder A model that takes the query and the document together and outputs a relevance score, unlike embeddings, which encode them…"},{"u":"glossary.html#texte-et-modeles-de-langage","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Text and language models","x":"BART Pretrained encoder–decoder model for text generation. MedX uses a large-cnn variant fine-tuned for summarization. GGUF llama.cpp's weight format, designed for CPU inference and quantisation. This is the format both Ollama and CNE require. llama.cpp C++ inference engine for language models, optimised for CPU. Runs under Ollama, and directly under CNE. Ollama Local server that downloads, caches, and serves language models over an HTTP API on port 11434. Decides GPU usage on its own, hence the trap with the snap install. Presidio Microsoft library for detecting and replacing personal data…"},{"u":"glossary.html#anatomie-et-modalites","p":"Glossary","t":"","c":"","s":"Anatomy and modalities","x":"arch The full set of teeth on one jaw. Upper and lower are treated as two separate inputs by nearly every module. CBCT Cone Beam Computed Tomography : a cone-beam scanner, producing a 3D volume. Low dose, high spatial resolution, but intensities are often not calibrated in Hounsfield units, which makes any hardcoded threshold fragile. condyle The head of the mandible, articulating in the temporal fossa. A central structure in asymmetry analyses. FOV Field Of View : the physical extent covered by the acquisition. A \"small FOV\" captures only one region, which changes which models apply. IOS…"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"ALI drops anatomical landmarks onto your scans for you. It works just as well on a CBCT (a volume) as on an intraoral scan (a surface), using two different engines behind a single window. On video ALICBCT 3 min 49 · DCBIA Videos channel ALIIOS 2 min 52 · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see ALI with VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#quand","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Any time you need landmarks and placing them by hand would take too long: a dozen points on one patient is manageable; on a series of forty, it isn't anymore. ALI also supplies the landmarks for several other modules: if you run an automatic orientation or registration, it may already be running behind the scenes without you having to open it. Two engines, one window The module picks the right engine on its own based on your data: CBCT for a volume, IOS for an intraoral scan. All you do is say what type your files are and which points you want."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#besoin","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of scans: CBCTs as .nii.gz , or intraoral scans as .vtk The group of points you want, checked in the list The trained models, downloaded once What comes out One landmark file per scan and per group, in Slicer's format ( .mrk.json ) Opens straight into the scene, points draggable with the mouse Your original scans are left untouched CBCT, a volume Intraoral scan, a surface Basion Ba Sella S Nasion N Right porion RPo Left porion LPo Right frontozygomatic RFZyg Left frontozygomatic LFZyg C2 vertebra C2 Place the landmarks again landmarks placed A CBCT and an intraoral…"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#marche","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the models. The \"Download latest models\" button fetches the trained weights. Only needed once, but essential: without them the module has nothing to predict from. Choose the data type. CBCT or intraoral scan. This decides which engine gets used. Point it to the input folder. ALI processes the whole folder in one go; there's no need to run it patient by patient. Check the points you want. They're grouped by region. Only check what you actually need: every point adds computing time. Set an output folder , then launch. A progress bar tracks the patients as they go through. The…"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#temps","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"Expect around 15 seconds per point per patient with a graphics card , and up to a minute without one . Over ten points and thirty patients, that gap stops being trivial: roughly an hour versus five. The module runs fine without a GPU, but plan for an overnight run on a large series."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#coince","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"\"Nothing comes out, or the points look wrong\" First check that the models were actually downloaded, then that the data type you checked matches your files. A CBCT processed as an intraoral scan won't produce anything usable. Intraoral scans: teeth need to be identified first On an intraoral scan, the engine aims its measurements tooth by tooth. If your file doesn't have crown identification yet, ALI runs it automatically along the way: expect extra time on the first attempt. DICOM import Use .nii.gz files instead. The module's DICOM path doesn't work in automatic mode. Disk space The…"},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#apres","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What's next","x":"The landmarks you get are a starting point, not ground truth: open them in Slicer and check them before using them for a measurement or a registration. They can be dragged with the mouse like any other markup."},{"u":"guide/ALI.html#citer","p":"ALI: place landmarks automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used ALI in your research, cite: Gillot M, Miranda F, Baquero B, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Anchling L, Hutin N, Biggs E, Yatabe M, Paniagua B, Fillion-Robin JC, Allemang D, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC. Automatic landmark identification in cone-beam computed tomography. Orthodontics & Craniofacial Research, 2023, 26(4), 560-567. doi:10.1111/ocr.12642 . The paper behind the CBCT path: agents that walk through the volume to the landmark. Reports the measured error, landmark by landmark. Baquero B, Gillot M, Cevidanes L, Al Turkestani N, Gurgel M, Leclercq M, Bianchi J…"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AMASSS automatically segments the anatomical structures of a craniofacial CBCT, mandible, maxilla, cranial base, upper airway, cervical vertebra, skin, each predicted by a network trained for that structure alone. You don't tune contrast or windowing by hand: the module handles that itself. On video AMASSS - Automatic Multi-Anatomical Skull Structure Segmentation 4 min 11 · DCBIA Videos channel AMASSS RC Seg tutorial 5 min 08 · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy module on this page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#quand","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you need bone, airway, or skin segmentations from a CBCT: for a single patient or for an entire batch at once. AMASSS also runs behind the scenes in two other tools: AREG_CBCT uses it to register two CBCT scans of the same patient, and VFACE uses it for its asymmetry analysis. If you use either of those, part of AMASSS's work is already being done for you, without opening this module."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#structures","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it can segment","x":"Six structures have a trained model available in the published bundle: Reset view Raw CBCT: threshold only Drag to rotate · scroll to zoom · click a structure Mandible MAND label 1 The lower jaw, and the only bone of the skull that moves. Carries the lower teeth. Maxilla MAX label 4 The upper jaw, fused to the rest of the skull. Carries the upper teeth. Cranial base CB label 2 The floor of the braincase. It stops changing early, which is why registration uses it as the stable reference. Upper airway UAW label 3 Not bone: the column of air from the nasal cavity down to the pharynx. Segmenting…"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#besoin","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A CBCT, or a folder of several CBCT scans, in .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , or .nrrd.gz The trained models, to download once The structures you want, checked in the list What comes out One binary mask per structure, or a single merged map where each structure carries its own value If requested, 3D surface(s) ( .vtk ) colored by structure, ready to display Your original scans are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#marche","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Step by step","x":"Download the models. The \"Download latest models\" button downloads the model archive (about 2 GB) once and fills in the model folder for you. You can also point the module at a folder where the archive is already unpacked. Point it at the input. A single CBCT or an entire folder; AMASSS finds scans even inside subfolders. Check the structures you want from the list. Choose the output format. Separate segmentations, merged, or both; also check surface generation if you want .vtk meshes ready to display in the scene. Set an output folder and run with the \"Predict\" button. This is when…"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#temps","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"AMASSS runs without a graphics card, but that's more theoretical than practical: on a batch of scans, the image-resampling step alone (without a GPU) costs roughly seven times the cost of the network itself. With a GPU, expect a reasonable computation per structure and per scan; without one, a batch of several patients quickly becomes impractical. One thing works in your favor: models are loaded once per structure, not once per scan, processing an entire folder at once costs little more than processing a single CBCT, structure by structure."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#coince","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's stuck","x":"\"Small FOV\" changes nothing about the computation (legacy module only) The checkbox that switches to a \"high definition\" list (teeth, root canal, and mandibular canal included) only changes which checkboxes are shown on screen. It does not reduce the field of view or the resolution of the computation: don't expect it to run faster or produce a finer result. The VISOR panel has no such checkbox. Root canal is checked by default, with no model (legacy module only) \"Root canal\" is checked by default in the structure list, but the published model bundle has no model for it. Checking it produces…"},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#apres","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"No automatic cleanup is applied to the segmentations produced, no hole filling, no keeping only the largest connected component: what you get is the network's raw output. Check it before using it for a measurement or a registration. For a single-file input with surface generation checked, the .vtk meshes are loaded directly into the scene once the computation finishes."},{"u":"guide/AMASSS.html#citer","p":"AMASSS: segmenting structures from a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"The paper below describes the UNETR version of the module. What runs today is a switch to nnU-Net that nobody has published: citing it to describe AMASSS as it runs means describing an architecture that is no longer there. If you used AMASSS in your research, cite: Gillot M, Baquero B, Le C, Deleat-Besson R, Bianchi J, Ruellas A, Gurgel M, Yatabe M, Al Turkestani N, Najarian K, Soroushmehr R, Pieper S, Kikinis R, Paniagua B, Gryak J, Ioshida M, Massaro C, Gomes L, Oh H, Evangelista K, Chaves Junior CM, Garib D, Costa F, Benavides E, Soki F, Fillion-Robin JC, Joshi H, Cevidanes L, Prieto JC.…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AREG_CBCT superimposes two CBCT scans of the same patient taken at two different times (T1 and T2), anchored on a bony region that's assumed not to have changed: the cranial base, the mandible, or the maxilla. The registration itself is a classic geometric computation, no neural network involved: reproducible, but entirely dependent on picking a good anchor. On video Automated Registration for CBCT (ARegCBCT) - Tutorial 13 min 59 · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy module and VISOR This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#quand","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you need to compare two CBCT scans of the same patient, before/after treatment, growth follow-up, post-surgical check, and they need to share the same spatial frame to be overlaid or measured. AREG_CBCT only does this voxel-to-voxel registration on a stable bony structure: your two scans must already belong to the same patient and represent two distinct time points."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#modes","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Three modes, one registration","x":"All three modes end up computing the same registration; they only differ in what happens beforehand. Semi-Automated : you supply the reference-region masks on T1 yourself. The most direct route if that work is already done. Fully-Automated : AREG_CBCT computes those masks for you. Only reliable when your T1 scans are already oriented consistently with each other. Orientation & Registration : AREG_CBCT starts by centering T1, placing landmarks on it and orienting it to a common reference, before computing masks and registering. The most complete mode, for T1 scans with no consistent…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#besoin","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"Cranial base Mandible Maxilla Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again the registration will only look inside, mean surface gap inside the mask: Two time points of the same patient, superimposed. Pick the structure you want to superimpose on: the green region is the only thing the registration looks at. Notice that the three give different results: the translation along one axis is 5.08 mm on the cranial base but 1.33 on the mandible. That is the whole point: you superimpose on something stable in order to measure how much the rest has moved. What to provide A baseline CBCT…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#marche","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Pick a mode. Semi-Automated, Fully-Automated, or Orientation & Registration: see above. Point it to the T1 and T2 folders. Check that each pair of files shares the same patient id, followed by _T1 or _T2 . In Semi-Automated, set the T1 masks folder. Without it, there's nothing to register against. Check the reference region(s). Cranial Base, Mandible, Maxilla : each one triggers its own registration. Download the models the first time you run it , then, if you want, check under \"Review and manual adjustment\" the steps you'd like to inspect before the run continues. Set an output folder and…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#temps","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"The registration itself runs on CPU, no graphics card needed, and on a single thread to stay reproducible from one run to the next: a choice that favors stability of the result over speed. The steps around it (automatic mask segmentation, orientation) are much faster with a GPU. The paper describing the method reports under five minutes per patient for the whole chain; without a GPU for the upstream steps, budget more time on a large series."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#coince","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"A patient goes missing, with no error message T1/T2 pairing only works with the _T1 and _T2 suffixes in the file name. Any other convention ( _T3 , _baseline , a date…) keeps AREG_CBCT from recognizing the pair, and that patient is silently dropped: nothing is shown to flag it. If a patient is missing from the output, the first thing to check is that patient's file names. Avoid mask keywords in a scan's file name AREG_CBCT recognizes a mask file by keywords in its name ( seg , mask , pred , or the region's abbreviation: cb , mand , max ). The first file that matches is used as-is, with no…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#apres","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What's next","x":"The registered CBCT and its matrix are directly reusable: in AutoMatrix, in the chain's analysis tools, or for a simple visual overlay in Slicer. As with any automatic registration, a quick visual check before using it for a measurement remains good practice. Also note that the matrix doesn't account for any centering applied upstream to T2: to bring the follow-up scan all the way back to its original frame, you need to chain that step together with the AREG_CBCT matrix, not use one without the other."},{"u":"guide/AREG_CBCT.html#citer","p":"AREG_CBCT: register a follow-up CBCT onto the baseline","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used AREG_CBCT in your research, cite: Anchling L, Hutin N, Huang Y, Barone S, Roberts S, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Tinawi S, Bianchi J, Yatabe M, Ruellas A, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L. Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans. In: Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2023), held with MICCAI 2023, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14242, Springer Cham, 2023, p. 43-58. doi:10.1007/978-3-031-45249-9_5 . The module's paper: orientation then voxel-based registration on a region of reference, with the error measured on a patient series.…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AREG_IOS superimposes two intraoral scans of the same patient taken at two different times, before and after treatment, say, without you having to draw the stable region by hand. It processes a whole batch of patients at once, not one scan at a time. Legacy module and VISOR This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#quand","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you want to compare T1 and T2 of the same arch, measuring tooth movement, an arch change under treatment, and drawing the stable region by hand, scan after scan, would take too long across your series. If you only have one patient and would rather keep control over the registration region yourself, FlexReg is the interactive tool for that; AREG_IOS is its automatic, batch counterpart. Two modes, one per arch Butterfly (upper arch): the module predicts the stable palatal patch itself, with a network, and uses it as the registration anchor. MGL (lower arch): there's no palate down…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#besoin","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Two folders of scans, T1 and T2, in .vtk , .vtp , .stl or .obj Filenames that literally contain T1 or T2 , plus an arch marker (Upper/Lower, U/L, Maxilla/Mandible): matching is done on these words The palatal patch model, downloaded once, for Butterfly mode For MGL: a band width in mm, or your own mucogingival landmarks if you already have them What comes out Per patient: the T1 scan with the registration region marked on it, and the T2 scan registered onto T1 In Butterfly, if the lower arch is provided as a pair, it automatically follows the upper arch's registration A transform…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#mode","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Automatic or semi-automatic","x":"Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again the two time points, still apart the Butterfly patch, predicted on the palate, rigid ICP, on the patch alone registered, mean gap on the patch Two time points of the same arch. The green area is the only thing the registration uses: a patch on the palate, chosen because it changes little between visits. Everything else follows along. The motion here is small (4.66°) because ASO has already brought both scans into a common frame upstream; AREG_IOS is the fine pass, not the coarse one. This is the second choice to make, independent of…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#marche","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Step by step","x":"Pick the regime. Auto for raw scans, semi-auto if they're already segmented and oriented. Pick the arch. Butterfly for the upper arch, MGL for the lower. Point to the two input folders , T1 and T2. AREG_IOS pairs up patients on its own from the filenames. For MGL , set the band width (0 to 20 mm), or point to a folder of mucogingival landmarks if you already have them: otherwise they're placed automatically. For Butterfly , download the palatal patch model if you haven't already. Point to an output folder that already exists , then run. A progress bar tracks patients as they complete."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#temps","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Time and hardware","x":"Butterfly mode needs a compatible graphics card : the palatal patch prediction has no CPU fallback, unlike some other tools in the suite. Without a GPU, this mode simply won't run: use MGL on machines that don't have one. MGL mode doesn't call that network at all; it relies on landmark placement and a geometric band construction instead, both lighter. Either way, processing runs folder by folder, so total time mostly scales with how many patients are in the batch."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#coince","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"No compatible graphics card In Butterfly mode, the palatal patch inference requires a CUDA GPU, full stop. On a machine without a compatible GPU, this mode fails outright. Use MGL on those machines, or process the upper arch elsewhere. File naming: order matters Put the arch marker before the timepoint marker in the filename ( P001_Upper_T1.vtk ), not after ( P001_T1_Upper.vtk ): in the wrong order, one of the output matrices won't be found, with no blocking error, the run finishes but an expected file is simply missing. Also avoid the same words (Upper, Lower, U, L, Mx, Md…) in parent…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#apres","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens next","x":"The registered T2 scan overlays directly onto T1 in the scene: that's your basis for measuring change between the two timepoints. The marked region on T1 (palatal patch or mucogingival band) is only a registration aid, not a segmentation to interpret on its own, and as noted above, check it before trusting the result."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOS.html#citer","p":"AREG_IOS: register two scans of the same patient","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used AREG_IOS in your research, cite: Hutin N, Anchling L, Cevidanes L, Miranda F, Curado D, Gurgel M, Barone S, Bianchi J, Al Turkestani N, Ruellas A, Eason M, Mavani K, Prieto JC, Aliaga A. AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans. In: Shape in Medical Imaging (ShapeMI 2023), held with MICCAI 2023, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14350, Springer, 2023, p. 223-235. doi:10.1007/978-3-031-46914-5_18 . The module's paper: a network isolates the stable palatal region, then an ICP registers the two scans on it. Background, not a citation for the tool Vasilakos et al.…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AREG_IOSCBCT registers an intraoral scan (IOS) onto the CBCT of the same patient, taken at the same visit. It has nothing to do with AREG_IOS, which compares two scans from two different visits: here the two inputs come from different sensors, optical and volumetric, that have to be brought into agreement. On video Automated registration of intra oral scan with CBCT using an accurate and quick workflow 45 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy module and VISOR This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#quand","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you want the surface detail of an intraoral scan, crowns, contact points , and the bone anatomy of a CBCT in the same frame: for a visual check, a combined measurement, or simply to overlay both images from one visit. The module does the alignment; there is nothing to draw or register by hand. Three ways to get there Depending on what you already have, nothing, raw scans, or landmarks already placed, the module offers three modes that don't ask for the same input files. See \"Three modes\" below before you start."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#besoin","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Intraoral scans in .vtk only: no other format is read CBCT in .nii.gz only: same rule File names carrying the patient id, a timepoint ( T0 , T1 or T2 ), and the arch ( U for upper, L for lower) as standalone tokens, not buried inside another word Depending on the mode: landmarks already computed, or nothing beyond the raw scans What comes out One registered scan per arch and per patient, in .vtk One landmark file per arch, in Slicer's format ( .mrk.json ), carrying the registered scan's coordinates Normalized output names: they may no longer look exactly like your input names"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#modes","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Three modes, which one to pick","x":"Lower arch Upper arch Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Register again the CBCT surface; this demo contours it at the fallback level, ALI places the same six occlusal points on each modality paired by label, never by position, rigid pre-alignment, vtkLandmarkTransform then ICP, inside a 1 mm window, not replayed here Your intraoral scan and your CBCT start in unrelated positions. ALI places the same six occlusal points on each, they are matched up by name, and the scan is brought onto the CBCT. The numbers are real: the average mismatch drops from 24.94 mm to 2.88 mm on the lower…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#marche","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Pick the mode that matches what you already have: see the section above. Check your file names before launching anything. A file named P001_T1_U_IOS.vtk is recognized; a timepoint outside T0–T2, or an arch letter glued to the rest of the name, is not. Point the module to the folders the chosen mode asks for: IOS, CBCT, and where relevant the landmark folders. Set an output folder , then launch. Open the log when it's done if the number of registered patients is lower than expected: that is where skipped patients are reported."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#coince","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's wrong","x":"No matrix is written Registration produces surfaces that are already transformed, not a matrix file. Unlike AREG_IOS or AREG_CBCT, you cannot reopen the result and nudge it with the mouse: if the alignment is off, the fix is to rerun, with different landmarks or a different setting, not to correct it by hand. One scanned arch means the patient is dropped Every patient must provide both arches, scan and landmarks included. If even one is missing, say, only one arch was ever scanned, the patient disappears from the result with no visible error, only a line in the log. Only when no patient at…"},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#apres","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens next","x":"The registered scans open directly in Slicer, next to the original CBCT. Since no matrix is kept, the only check available is visual: inspect the overlay before using it for a measurement. Also check the log for any line saying the pre-alignment was skipped: that would mean a batch came out without the first registration step, and nothing else would have told you."},{"u":"guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#citer","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT: place an intraoral scan into a CBCT's frame","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"No publication describes this module's multimodal path. The AReg IOS paper covers registering one intra-oral scan onto another over time, not matching a mesh with a CBCT. Nothing describes the module itself, so cite the extension as software: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. DCBIA-OrthoLab. https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools Add the date you accessed it if your journal's style asks for one. Background, not a citation for the tool Zheng et al., Clinical Oral Investigations 2025 : Automatic multimodal registration of cone-beam computed tomography and intraoral scans: a…"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"ASO rotates a scan to bring it into the frame of a reference patient: it does not compare two time points against each other, and it does not segment anything. It handles both a CBCT (a volume) and an intraoral scan (a surface), with two different engines behind a single window. On video Automated Standardized Orientation (ASOCBCT) - Tutorial 7 min 09 · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see ASO with VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#quand","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Before most registrations, really. A registration assumes its two inputs already start in a reasonably consistent orientation; without that, it has no correct starting point. AREG_IOS, for instance, looks directly for the transform file that ASO produces to build its own matrix: if your scans have never been oriented by ASO, there is nothing for it to find. Run ASO whenever you are preparing scans for a registration, for superimposing a longitudinal series, or simply to bring a whole cohort into the same frame."},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#mode","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Choosing a mode","x":"Two data types, two ways of working You first tell ASO what kind of files you have: CBCT for a volume, IOS for an intraoral scan. For each, two modes are available. Fully-Automated asks only for your raw scans: ASO places the landmarks it needs itself (via a network, for a CBCT) or segments the teeth (for a scan) before orienting. Semi-Automated skips that prediction step and works directly on landmarks or teeth you have already identified: faster, but it assumes that preparatory work already exists. CBCT semi-automatic: don't run it on its own On a CBCT, the semi-automatic mode needs a…"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#besoin","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide CBCT : the volume as .nii.gz (DICOM is accepted but less reliable, prefer .nii.gz when you can). In semi-automatic mode, also your own landmarks already placed and the transform file described above Intraoral scan (IOS) : the mesh, in a common format (converted to .vtk along the way). In semi-automatic mode, the 3 or 4 reference teeth to check on the arch; in automatic mode, nothing more: tooth segmentation runs on its own if it hasn't already been done The reference patient (the \"gold\"): a dataset downloaded once, which every orientation targets What comes out The reoriented…"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#marche","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Step by step","x":"Download the reference patient and the models. A one-time step: this is the target every one of your scans will orient itself to. Choose the data type and the mode. CBCT or IOS, then Fully-Automated or Semi-Automated depending on what you already have on hand. Point to the input folder. ASO processes the whole folder in one run. For an intraoral scan in semi-automatic mode, check the reference teeth (3 or 4) that the orientation will line up on. For a CBCT in semi-automatic mode, make sure your landmarks cover at least 4 points shared with the gold: the interface accepts 3 checked, but…"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#coince","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"Your CBCT landmark files are gone In semi-automatic mode, ASO starts by merging every landmark file it finds in your input folder into a single file, then deletes the originals . Keep a copy of your .mrk.json files elsewhere before running a job, especially if the input folder is also where you keep your only copy of those landmarks. Occlusion: name your files Upper / Lower unambiguously Occlusion mode pairs the upper and lower arches by file name ( Upper / _U_ or Lower / _L_ ). A file whose name carries none of these markers is silently treated as a lower arch: with no error message. Check…"},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#apres","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"A scan oriented by ASO is not guaranteed to be anatomically perfect: the orientation is a geometric estimate, not ground truth. Open the result in Slicer and compare it visually against the reference patient before feeding it into a registration. That downstream use is exactly why the transform file is produced: the next modules use it to rebuild the full matrix, from the raw scan through to the registered result."},{"u":"guide/ASO.html#citer","p":"ASO: orient your scans automatically","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used ASO in your research, cite: Anchling L, Hutin N, Huang Y, Barone S, Roberts S, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Al Turkestani N, Tinawi S, Bianchi J, Yatabe M, Ruellas A, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L. Automated Orientation and Registration of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography Scans. In: Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2023), held with MICCAI 2023, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 14242, Springer Cham, 2023, p. 43-58. doi:10.1007/978-3-031-45249-9_5 . The ASO/AREG family paper: how the automatic orientation is obtained, and how far it departs from a manual one. free full text . Background…"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"Agent is a conversational assistant that runs entirely on your machine. You describe what you want to do, in plain language, and it proposes a tool from the extension already filled in with its parameters, or it just answers you, depending on the mode you pick. On video AI Agent directly in 3D Slicer to help you learn dental and cranofacial imaging 30 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy module page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. The VISOR extension ships a copy of this panel that still runs on your computer, and the VISOR server has a separate Agent tool that no panel…"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#quand","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Use it when you don't want to hunt down which module in the extension matches what you need, or build a command line by hand: you describe the task, \"orient these CBCTs\", \"segment these scans\", and Agent proposes a tool with the parameters it understood from your request. It also works as a plain consultant, in \"Ask\" mode, for questions that don't need anything run."},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#installer","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What to install first","x":"Agent relies on a language-model server running locally on your machine, Ollama . If it isn't already present, the module offers to download and start it for you. Either way, expect the first launch to download several gigabytes , the language model alone is a bit over 5 GB, before you can ask your first question. Nothing to install by hand on the Slicer side The required Python packages install themselves automatically on first launch, inside Slicer's own Python."},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#besoin","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A request written in plain language, in English or French The mode you want: Agent (Automated) to actually run something, Ask (Interactive) to just talk If relevant, the folders involved, dropped into the drop zone What comes out In Agent mode: a chosen tool, its parameters, a confirmation dialog, then execution if you approve In Ask mode: a free-text reply, nothing is launched If the tool fails, up to two automatic repair attempts, each with its own confirmation"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#marche","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Make sure Ollama is running. If needed, let Agent install and start it for you, and wait out the model download on first launch. Pick a mode. \"Agent (Automated)\" to actually launch a tool, \"Ask (Interactive)\" to only get an answer. Describe what you want , in plain language, the way you'd explain it to a colleague. Drop the relevant folders into the drop zone if your request involves specific data: Agent will try to guess which is which. Send the message. In Agent mode, a tool and its parameters are proposed; in Ask mode, the answer comes straight back."},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#confirmation","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Reading the confirmation dialog","x":"In Agent mode, nothing runs without a \"Yes\" from you, in a dialog that lists every parameter it settled on, as key=value pairs. This is the only point where you can catch a wrongly guessed folder or a value that doesn't match what you meant, before the tool actually starts. Read every line, carefully For most tools, the mapping between what Agent believes it's passing and what the tool actually expects is off. A run can therefore start with wrong values without any error warning you beforehand: the confirmation dialog is your only real check. Once you approve, Slicer's interface freezes…"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#coince","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"\"Nothing launches, or the error makes no sense\" As the repository stands, Agent can't find the scripts it wants to run on its own: unless you tell it yourself where they are, execution fails before it even starts. The automatic repair step that follows can't fix this: the problem isn't in the parameters, it's in the script's path. A run starting with wrong values See above: always check the confirmation dialog before approving. A parameter mismatch usually shows up only by eye, in that dialog, not in an error message. Everything is slow without a graphics card If the model server is running…"},{"u":"guide/Agent.html#citer","p":"Agent: let a local assistant pick the tool","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"No publication: the module is work in progress, described by a single NA-MIC project report. If you used Agent in your research, cite: Dumont P, Buisson A, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L, Pieper S. AI-Agent for SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. 45th NA-MIC Project Week, Boston, 2026. projectweek.na-mic.org . The project report: what the agent is meant to do, and how far the work got. Background, not a citation for the tool Qwen Team, arXiv 2025 : Qwen3 Technical Report . The model that decides which tool to send you to and extracts its parameters. Reimers & Gurevych, EMNLP 2019 : Sentence-BERT: Sentence…"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoCrop3D batch-crops a folder of volumes to a shared region of interest, or to a separate region per patient. No network, no model to download: it's a geometric crop, near-instant, that never touches your voxel values. Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#quand","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Any time a series is too large or too inconsistent in field of view to process as is: run it before a segmenter (AMASSS, BatchDentalSeg) to shrink what needs processing, or after one to keep only the useful region. AutoCrop3D can also generate a VTK mesh from a segmentation you already produced, for a quick preview."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#roi","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Preparing the region of interest","x":"The region to crop comes from a single Slicer ROI file ( .mrk.json ) and nothing else: place a Markups > ROI box in the scene on a representative patient, adjust it, and save it. Two ways to use it: one ROI applied to every volume in the folder, or one ROI per patient, in that case, name each ROI file with the same prefix as its matching volumes, so the pairing works (see below). The ROI must be an axis-aligned box, saved as-is Legacy module (3c67957). Not quite the same with VISOR. The server reads the coordinate system, so a ROI saved in RAS is converted. A genuinely rotated box is still…"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#besoin","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of volumes: .nii.gz , .nii , .nrrd.gz , .nrrd , .gipl.gz , or .gipl An ROI file ( .mrk.json ), or a folder with one ROI per patient An output suffix (\"cropped\" by default) What comes out One cropped volume per input, mirroring the input folder structure Optional: a VTK mesh per cropped segmentation No transform file: the geometry lives entirely in the volume itself"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#marche","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Place and save your ROI. One box for the whole series, or one per patient, named to match its volumes. Point it to the input volume folder. AutoCrop3D processes the whole folder in one go. Set the ROI file or folder. A single file gets applied to everyone; several get matched per patient. Choose an output suffix , and if you need it, \"Keep the same size as input\" to preserve the full original geometry, with everything outside the ROI set to zero. Rotated ROI only: check \"Use module Crop Volume for tilted images\", read the warning below before you do. Set an output folder , then run it."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#preserve","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What's preserved","x":"This is the whole point of the tool: the default crop path never resamples. It just extracts a block of voxels and recomputes the volume's origin; spacing, orientation and voxel values stay bit-for-bit identical to the original. In practice, a registration matrix computed on the full volume stays valid, unchanged, on the cropped volume, because the physical coordinates never moved: you can crop after registering without recomputing anything."},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#coince","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"A folder with exactly one ROI file → error Legacy module (3c67957). Not the case with VISOR : a folder holding exactly one ROI is applied to every scan. If you're using per-patient ROIs but the folder holds only a single .mrk.json file, the module errors out instead of applying it to every volume. In that case, point it directly at that file as a single shared ROI rather than at the folder containing it. Per-patient ROI: matching breaks on an underscore Legacy module (3c67957). Not the case with VISOR : scans and ROIs go through one naming rule, so Patient_01_Scan.nii.gz and…"},{"u":"guide/AutoCrop3D.html#citer","p":"AutoCrop3D: crop a series of volumes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"A utility module: it has no publication of its own and needs none. It applies in batch the cropping Slicer already knows how to do. Nothing describes the module itself, so cite the extension as software: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. DCBIA-OrthoLab. https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools Add the date you accessed it if your journal's style asks for one. Background, not a citation for the tool Documentation 3D Slicer : Crop Volume . The underlying Slicer module: what the interpolation, sampling factor and isotropy you see here actually mean. Barone et al., Orthod Craniofac…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"AutoMatrix applies one or more ITK transformation matrices to an entire folder of volumes and landmark files in a single pass. No neural network, no GPU: it's a batch tool, not a computation step, the matrix itself comes from a registration you ran somewhere else. Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#quand","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"You ran a registration module (AREG, ASO, FlexReg…) and got a transformation matrix ( .tfm ) out of it. Now you want to apply that same matrix to other files for the same patient, a segmentation, a second volume, a set of landmarks, without opening each one in Slicer by hand. Or you ran that registration on a whole series of patients and want to process the entire folder in one go. That's what AutoMatrix is for: it doesn't register anything itself, it replays a transformation you already computed. One matrix, one motion Also true with VISOR . The server moves landmark points through the…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#appariement","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How files and matrices get matched","x":"AutoMatrix never asks you to point out by hand which matrix goes with which patient. It guesses the pairing by comparing file names: it strips a list of known keywords ( _Seg , _Or , _MAND , _CB , a timestamp like _T1 , _T2 …) from the scan's name, and a slightly different list from the matrix's name, until it's left with a patient identifier on each side. If the two identifiers match, the file and the matrix are paired. The catch: those two keyword lists aren't the same. The rule that works reliably: start every file name with the patient identifier, then an underscore, then whatever else…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#besoin","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of volumes as .nii , .nii.gz or .nrrd , and/or Slicer landmark files ( .mrk.json ) An ITK matrix ( .tfm , .h5 or .mat ), or a folder of matrices named following the same rule as the scans Check this if your volumes are segmentations: the dedicated box, so labels don't get smoothed out by interpolation Optional: a reference file to force the output grid (size, orientation) What comes out A transformed copy of each matched file, laid out in the same folder structure as the input Your original files are never modified In a landmark file, only the points marked as…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#marche","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Point it to the input folder. Scans and/or landmark files to transform; the whole folder is processed in one go. Point it to the matrix, or a folder of matrices. Check that the names follow the patient + suffix rule above, or point straight at a single file to apply it to everyone. Check the segmentation box if your volumes are labelmaps. Give it a reference file if you want to force the output grid; otherwise each file keeps its own input volume's grid, and anything pushed outside the field of view by the transform is lost. Pick an output suffix , and decide whether the matrix name should…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#miroir","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Flipping left/right without hunting for a matrix","x":"If all you need is a left/right mirror, there's no need to build your own matrix: the Mirror mode downloads a ready-made reflection matrix and fills it into the matrix field for you, with its own output suffix ( _mir ). The mirror isn't centered on your patient Legacy module (3c67957). Different with VISOR. The mirror matrix is the server's hosted file, applied through the box Use the mirror matrix . The panel does not offer a reference volume, and without one the VISOR output grid follows the transform; for a reflection about the world origin this can leave the result empty. See With VISOR…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#coince","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Some advertised formats no longer work Legacy module (3c67957). Partly changed with VISOR . The VISOR panel no longer offers meshes or .npy matrices, and nothing counts them. A .txt file holding a plain 4x4 matrix is accepted there. The interface still offers meshes ( .vtk , .vtp , .stl , .off , .obj ) as input scans for intraoral surfaces, and matrices as .npy , both get counted in the progress bar, but neither produces any output: each file of that kind is simply skipped, with a line in the error log. A .txt matrix only works if it's an actual ITK transform file, not a plain 4×4 array. A…"},{"u":"guide/AutoMatrix.html#citer","p":"AutoMatrix: apply a matrix across a whole batch of files","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"No publication, and nothing to publish: the module applies transformation matrices to files, in batch. What matters is knowing what a matrix is in Slicer and which coordinate frame it is in. Nothing describes the module itself, so cite the extension as software: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. DCBIA-OrthoLab. https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools Add the date you accessed it if your journal's style asks for one. Background, not a citation for the tool Documentation 3D Slicer : Transforms . What a linear transform is in Slicer, how it is applied and how it is inverted.…"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"BatchDentalSeg segments your dento-maxillo-facial CT and CBCT volumes assembly-line style: point it at a folder, and it applies the same set of labels to every scan, one at a time. The choice that matters here isn't a network setting: it's the model . Each one segments different structures. Legacy module on this page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#quand","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you have several CT or CBCT scans that need the same anatomical structures segmented: skull, mandible, teeth, mandibular canal, or a tooth-by-tooth breakdown. BatchDentalSeg is a standalone tool: no other module in the repository calls it, and it doesn't prepare anything for a downstream module. It's an endpoint, not a step in a pipeline. One extension to install first (legacy module only) BatchDentalSeg relies on the external Slicer extension NNUnet to run the computation. It doesn't install itself: get it through the Extensions Manager, then restart Slicer. Without it, the module…"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#modele","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Choosing a model","x":"Four models are offered in the dropdown. They share the same engine and the same window, but they don't segment the same thing: Model What it segments Built for DentalSegmentator 5 structures: upper skull (maxilla included), mandible, upper teeth, lower teeth, mandibular canal Permanent dentition (adult) PediatricDentalsegmentator The same 5 structures, same breakdown Mixed dentition (child): only the training data differs NasoMaxillaDentSeg 6 structures: the maxilla is detached from the skull and output as its own structure When you need the maxilla isolated from the rest of the facial mass…"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#besoin","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A folder of scans in .nii , .nii.gz , .gipl , or .gipl.gz : no NRRD, and subfolders aren't scanned The model you want, picked from the menu An output folder What comes out Always: <name>_Segmentation.nii.gz , the label map on the original scan's geometry Optionally: STL/OBJ meshes, VTK per structure or merged, binary NIfTI per structure, glTF Your original scans are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#marche","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Install the NNUnet extension if you haven't already, and restart Slicer. Pick a model from the dropdown, based on which structures you need (table above). The first time you use a given model, its weights are downloaded automatically. Point it at the input folder. BatchDentalSeg processes the whole folder at once; scans that already have a segmentation can be skipped automatically (checked by default). Check the output formats you need beyond the label map, which is always written. Set the output folder and the device (GPU or CPU), then run. A queue works through the scans one at a time with…"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#memoire","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Memory and time","x":"A full-mouth CBCT can demand a lot of RAM during inference: the module's own notes cite a real case of a scan that filled 114 of a machine's 125 GB and got killed by the system. Three lines of defense are in place: before starting, a scan whose estimated memory need exceeds your budget (85% of free RAM by default) is marked failed without ever starting ; during the run, a confirmed overrun on two consecutive checks stops the scan cleanly instead of letting the system lock up; a Free memory button lets you reclaim memory by hand if any processes are left hanging after an abrupt stop. If a…"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#coince","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"Switching models later can wipe the others' weights (legacy module only) Re-downloading or updating DentalSegmentator's weights clears the entire models folder, including anything already downloaded for PediatricDentalsegmentator, NasoMaxillaDentSeg, and UniversalLabDentalsegmentator. If you switch between models, expect to re-download them. With VISOR, models are staged on the server and nothing is downloaded when you run. Your files aren't being picked up (legacy module only) Only .nii , .nii.gz , .gipl , and .gipl.gz are recognized, and only at the top level of the chosen folder: a scan…"},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#resultat","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"The label map is always written, whatever other formats you checked: open it in Slicer to check the segmentation before using it for a measurement or an export. With UniversalLabDentalsegmentator, the network sometimes mixes up left and right; a dedicated button corrects the laterality using the central incisors, without touching the original result. If a mesh export fails, the scan is still marked done: the label map is what counts."},{"u":"guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#citer","p":"BatchDentalSeg: segment CT/CBCT scans in bulk","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"The original model is published and documented. The three variants added later, pediatric, universal labelling , naso-maxillary, are not: no training set, no evaluation. If you used BatchDentalSeg in your research, cite: Dot G, Chaurasia A, Dubois G, Savoldelli C, Haghighat S, Azimian S, Rahbar Taramsari A, Sivaramakrishnan G, Issa J, Dubey A, Schouman T, Gajny L. DentalSegmentator: Robust open source deep learning-based CT and CBCT image segmentation. Journal of Dentistry, 2024, 147, 105130. doi:10.1016/j.jdent.2024.105130 . The model this module runs: the five structures segmented, the…"},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"CLIC scans a CBCT slice by slice and flags impacted canines, telling you for each one where it sits relative to the bone: buccal , bicortical , or palatal . You start from the raw volume: no prior segmentation or landmarking needed. Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see CLIC with VISOR and, for the general workflow, Using VISOR . The progress bar doesn't move: that's expected While the tool runs, the progress bar stays at zero and nothing shows up in the Slicer scene, even after processing has finished. This is not a crash: the…"},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#quand","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"When you have a CBCT with one or more impacted canines and want to know, without inspecting each one by eye, which side of the cortical bone it sits on. CLIC has no dependency on other modules: you don't need to have run AMASSS, ASO, or ALI first."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#besoin","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"Provide A folder of CBCT scans in .nii or .nii.gz The trained model, downloaded once An output folder, chosen explicitly You get One subfolder per scan, holding a .nii.gz label map Three possible voxel values: buccal, bicortical, palatal Directly overlayable on the source scan, no registration needed Accepted formats that don't actually work The file picker also lists .nrrd , .mha , and .mhd . Don't use them: processing can only read .nii / .nii.gz , and fails on the other formats regardless of what the picker allowed. Linux only The module has no working path on Windows, whatever the README…"},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#marche","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the model. The dedicated button fetches the trained network. Do this once. Point it at the input folder. A flat folder of .nii.gz files, not a folder of subfolders. Choose an output folder. This step is mandatory: without one, processing fails before it even starts. Run. The first run also sets up the computing environment, so it takes longer than later ones. Wait it out , even if nothing moves on screen. Processing goes scan by scan with no visual feedback in between."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#resultat","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Where to find the result","x":"Once processing is done, open the output folder you chose: it holds one subfolder per processed scan, and inside each, a .nii.gz file carrying the suffix shown in the interface ( seg by default). Load that file into Slicer yourself as a segmentation volume: it's already aligned to the source scan, no registration required. Values 1, 2, and 3 correspond to buccal, bicortical, and palatal respectively."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#coince","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If it gets stuck","x":"Cancel does nothing It stops neither the running computation nor the underlying process. If you genuinely need to stop, close Slicer. The first run needs internet access Building the network downloads generic pretrained weights before the trained model is applied on top. Without a connection on the first attempt, processing fails. Two model files in the folder With more than one model file in the models folder, whichever name sorts first alphabetically gets used, with no warning. Keep a single .pth file in that folder to avoid any ambiguity."},{"u":"guide/CLIC.html#citer","p":"CLIC: find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"The module has no publication yet: its only public description is a NA-MIC project page. If you used CLIC in your research, cite: Tulissi E, Cevidanes L, Prieto J, Bianchi J. Interpretable Deep Learning for the Detection and Classification of Impacted Canines and severity of root resorption. Project page, NA-MIC Project Week 43, Montreal, 2025. projectweek.na-mic.org . The project report: the goal, the data, and where the work stood at that point. Background, not a citation for the tool He et al., ICCV 2017 : Mask R-CNN . The architecture the module runs: detect objects and produce their…"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"The name doesn't say it: CNE doesn't touch any image , volume, or mesh. It's a reading tool: it takes free-text clinical notes and pulls structured information out of them, as key/value pairs, using a language model (LLM) that runs locally inside Slicer. On video CNE (Clinical notes extraction) to summarize your notes and extract common data elements 34 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see CNE with VISOR and, for the general workflow, Using VISOR . Note that with a VISOR server your notes are…"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#quand","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Any time you have a batch of free-text notes, TMJ or Ortho visits, and want the key facts (symptoms, diagnoses) pulled out quickly instead of reading each one by hand. CNE processes a whole folder of notes at once, using a different model depending on whether the notes are TMJ or Ortho. Nothing leaves your machine (legacy module only) This holds for the legacy Slicer module only, not for VISOR. In the legacy module the model runs inside Slicer's own Python, on your computer. Notes aren't sent to any online service during extraction: the only step that touches the network is the one-time…"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#besoin","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of notes, placed directly inside it (no subfolders): .txt , .pdf , or .docx The note type: TMJ or Ortho , each with its own model The matching model, downloaded once (a few GB) What comes out One text file per note, Extraction_<note name>.txt , in the output folder key : value lines summarizing the note No summary table from the extraction itself: one file per note, to be checked one at a time. The Dashboard button (below) builds a table and a figure afterwards"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#marche","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Choose the note type. TMJ or Ortho: this decides which model gets used and how long a note it can take in one go. Download the matching model. On the first click on \"Apply\", the module installs whatever is missing (after you confirm), then fetches the model from Hugging Face. Only needed once per note type. Point it to the notes folder. Files must sit directly at the root of the chosen folder, not sorted into per-patient subfolders. Set an output folder. Launch, then wait. Notes are processed one after another; expect several tens of seconds per note."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#tableau-de-bord","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"The Dashboard button","x":"Beside Run, the module has a Dashboard button. It draws one summary picture ( dashboard.png ) and writes the table it was drawn from ( patient_data.csv ) from the Extraction_*.txt files already sitting in your output folder. It extracts nothing and needs no model: run the extraction first, then press Dashboard with the same output folder selected. Percentages and averages are computed over all the notes of the folder, for things such as age groups, pain scores, mouth opening, headache locations and disc displacement. The Dashboard is built for TMJ notes It looks for the fields the TMJ model…"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#temps","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes, how much memory","x":"Even though the module mentions using a graphics card, the install only sets up CPU packages: in practice, extraction always runs on CPU . Expect roughly a minute per note depending on its length; on a batch of several dozen notes, it's better to start the run and come back later. The loaded model needs about 5 to 6 GB of RAM on top of what Slicer already uses, and its download is several gigabytes, done once per note type."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#relire","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Check the result","x":"All the clinical content in the output file comes from the model: there's no rule, no term dictionary, no schema check behind it. The module expects a JSON reply but doesn't enforce it technically: it just looks for the first opening brace and the last closing brace in the response. If the model's output isn't well formed, its raw text lands in the file as-is, with no indication that formatting failed. Check every file it produces before relying on it."},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#coince","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Notes sorted into per-patient subfolders CNE only looks at files sitting directly in the folder you chose, not at anything inside subfolders. A folder organized with one subfolder per patient will produce no output at all, with no error message. Scanned PDF PDF text extraction doesn't do OCR: if your PDF is actually a scanned image, the extracted text is empty and the note can't produce anything. Tables inside a .docx Only paragraph text is read from a Word document; table content is skipped. If your key information sits in a table, it never reaches the model. Note too long There's no…"},{"u":"guide/CNE.html#citer","p":"CNE: extract information from clinical notes","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"The module has no publication, and the models it runs are lab fine-tunes uploaded without a model card: no described training set, no evaluation. The readings below give what is generally known about how reliable this kind of extraction is. Nothing describes the module itself, so cite the extension as software: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. DCBIA-OrthoLab. https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools Add the date you accessed it if your journal's style asks for one. Background, not a citation for the tool Wiest et al., npj Digital Medicine 2024 : Privacy-preserving large…"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"DOCShapeAXI takes already-segmented meshes and sorts them into a category: a severity stage, a presence or absence of obstruction, or a continuous ratio depending on the case. For every shape it classifies, it also writes a map directly onto the mesh showing where the network looked most closely to reach its decision. Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see DOCShapeAXI with VISOR and, for the general workflow, Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#quand","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Reach for it whenever you have 3D shapes already extracted, condyles, airways, cleft bone defects, and want an automatic classification instead of a case-by-case visual read, with a map thrown in that gives you a lead on the model's reasoning. DOCShapeAXI does not segment anything itself: the .vtk meshes you feed it must already exist, produced by another tool (AMASSS for condyles, an airway segmentation, and so on). Three shape families, one window The data type you pick is the only thing that decides which model runs and which categories come out: Mandibular Condyle : a severity stage out…"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#besoin","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A folder of already-segmented .vtk meshes, all of the same shape type The data type picked from the list: condyle, airway, or cleft An output folder, created ahead of time before you launch What comes out A csv listing every mesh with its prediction, one column per task A copy of each mesh, per task, carrying the explainability map as a per-point scalar Your original meshes are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#marche","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Pick the data type. Mandibular condyle, airway obstruction, or cleft bone defect. This choice alone decides which model gets downloaded and which categories come back. Point to the input folder. It should hold only already-segmented .vtk meshes of the type you just picked. Create and point to an output folder. On the first run against that folder, the matching trained model is downloaded automatically (roughly 70 MB). Launch. For airway, three passes chain automatically: obstruction, severity, ratio; for the other two types, a single pass is enough. Every mesh then gets its explainability…"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#lire","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Reading the result","x":"The prediction csv adds one column per task to your list of meshes: a class (an integer) for a severity stage or a binary obstruction call, a number between 0 and 100 for the airway's continuous ratio. No confidence score comes with the class: DOCShapeAXI only reports the category it settled on, not how close the call was between two neighboring classes. For the explainability map, open the mesh produced in the explainability subfolder for that task. In Slicer's Models module, under the Scalars tab, set Active Scalar to the array named after the class you're interested in (one array per…"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#coince","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's stuck","x":"The output folder has to exist before you launch If the folder you point to doesn't exist yet, the model download is simply skipped, and the error only surfaces later, when the model tries to load. Create the output folder yourself before starting. Adding files mid-way has no effect The list of meshes to process is only rebuilt if it doesn't already exist in the output folder. If you drop more meshes into the input folder without changing the output folder, they simply won't be processed. Disk space The downloaded model isn't cached: every new output folder re-downloads it in full. Across…"},{"u":"guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#citer","p":"DOCShapeAXI: classify a 3D shape and see what drove it","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used DOCShapeAXI in your research, cite: Prieto JC, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Anchling L, Hutin N, Barone S, Al Turkestani N, Aliaga A, Yatabe M, Bianchi J, Cevidanes L. ShapeAXI: Shape Analysis Explainability and Interpretability. Proc. SPIE 12931, Medical Imaging 2024: Imaging Informatics for Healthcare, Research, and Applications, 1293116, 2024. doi:10.1117/12.3007053 . The framework this module drives: classify a 3D shape, then show which part of the surface drove the decision. Background, not a citation for the tool Mattos et al., Journal of Dentistry 2025 : Explainable artificial…"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"FlexReg registers two intraoral scans of the same patient taken at different times. Instead of letting a network guess which area hasn't moved, you mark it yourself, with the mouse, on the upper or lower arch. On video Discover the new features of FlexReg 1 min 52 · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy module on this page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR . The VISOR version does not offer the free-hand outline, the automatic lower-arch band or the automatic crown segmentation."},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#quand","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Reach for FlexReg when AREG_IOS, the automatic version, can't find a good registration area on its own: a partially edentulous arch, an unusual palate shape, or simply a case where you'd rather keep control over what counts as the fixed reference. FlexReg is the \"draw it yourself\" version of the same registration: both tools produce and read the same kind of landmark, so you can start in one and continue in the other. Two ways to build the stable area On an upper arch, the area is anchored on the palate: pick four reference teeth and FlexReg builds a butterfly-shaped patch around them, or…"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#besoin","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Two scans of the same patient, as .vtk or .stl ( .stl is converted automatically) A \"moving\" scan (the more recent one) and a \"fixed\" scan (the reference) Teeth identified crown by crown: if not, FlexReg runs the segmentation itself, at the cost of a wait on that first pass A graphics card (GPU): required to build the patch What comes out A registered scan in .vtk , saved next to the original A transformation matrix ( .tfm ) reusable by the other tools in the repository A preview shown in the scene before anything is saved"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#marche","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Load both scans. Mark which one is moving (the one being aligned) and which one is the fixed reference. Check the tooth identification. If the arch has no segmentation array yet, FlexReg computes one on its own the first time: expect extra time on that run only. Build the stable area. On an upper arch, pick four reference teeth and shape the butterfly patch with the sliders (position, length, width), or draw its outline freehand. On a lower arch, let FlexReg place the mucogingival landmarks, then adjust the band's height around them. Preview. The preview button runs the registration into a…"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#temps","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How long it takes","x":"Building the patch and running the registration take a few seconds per pair of scans on a GPU, once the area is drawn. The slow step is crown segmentation on the first pass over an unidentified arch: that wait happens once per scan and isn't repeated afterward."},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#coince","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If it gets stuck","x":"A graphics card is mandatory (with VISOR: on the server) Building the patch and running the registration only work with a compatible GPU. Without one, these steps fail outright: this isn't a performance nicety, it's a hard requirement of the module. With VISOR, the card is the server's, and only the patch step uses it: your computer needs none. \"The registration only looks at the teeth I checked\" FlexReg aligns only the area you marked out: never the whole arch. If the result looks off away from that area, that's expected: the rest of the mesh simply follows the motion computed on the patch…"},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#apres","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens next","x":"The registered scan and its transformation matrix are ready to use directly in AREG_IOS and in the other tools in the repository that overlay scans across time. As with any automatic registration, a quick visual check before using it for a measurement is still good practice."},{"u":"guide/FlexReg.html#citer","p":"FlexReg: register two scans by drawing the stable area yourself","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"No publication describes FlexReg: neither the region you draw by hand nor the registration that follows. The readings below explain the approach, not the module. Nothing describes the module itself, so cite the extension as software: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. DCBIA-OrthoLab. https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools Add the date you accessed it if your journal's style asks for one. Background, not a citation for the tool Hutin et al., ShapeMI 2023 : AReg IOS: Automatic Registration on IntraOralScans . The published half of the family: a network finds the stable palatal…"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"GreedyReg aligns two CBCT scans of the same patient (a T1 and a T2) by driving the ITK-SNAP greedy engine. No neural network in the main computation, nothing to install: the result comes from a classic intensity-based optimization, not a prediction. Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#quand","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"To register two CBCT scans of the same modality, a before/after follow-up, for instance, when you want fine control over the computation: which metric, how many degrees of freedom, optionally a mask to focus the optimization on one region. It is an end-of-chain tool: nothing else in SADT consumes its result. If you need the resulting transform to be reused elsewhere in Slicer, this is not the right module: its matrix stays specific to greedy and is not a Slicer transform file. Three modes, one real registration Legacy module (3c67957). Different with VISOR. The server tool has a single mode…"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#besoin","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide Two folders of CBCT scans in .nii.gz : one for the fixed volume (T1), one for the moving volume (T2) File names starting with letters then digits, for example A01_T1.nii.gz : that is what lets GreedyReg pair a T1 with its T2 for the same patient; any other naming is never paired, with no warning Optional: a mask (segmentation or labelmap), in the space of the fixed volume Optional: an initialization matrix, or a manual pre-alignment done inside the module What comes out The T2 resampled into the T1 grid, as .nii.gz , plus the affine matrix found In Distant mode: only a starting…"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#modes","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"The three modes, and which one to pick","x":"Manual Alignment. You move the moving volume by hand (sliders, or the \"Center T2 on T1\" button for a quick centering). Useful when the two CBCT scans are already close and only a rough adjustment is missing before running the actual computation. Automatic Registration. The only mode that actually registers: the greedy engine optimizes a rigid or affine transform from image intensities. This is where you pick the metric (NMI by default, for heterogeneous intensities; NCC for a local correlation; SSD if both volumes share the same modality) and the degrees of freedom (rigid or full affine).…"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#marche","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Split your volumes. One folder for the fixed CBCT scans (T1), one for the moving ones (T2), with the same patient identifier in each file name. Add a mask if needed. It must be in the space of the fixed volume: that is where the optimization will look for its gradient. Pre-align if the misalignment is large. By hand in Manual mode for a small gap, via Distant Registration for a large one. Switch to Automatic Registration. Pick the metric and the degrees of freedom (rigid or affine), then run. For a series , point directly at the full T1/T2 folders: batch processing chains the pairs matched…"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#coince","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"\"Save Registered Volume\" does nothing in Distant mode Legacy only. There is no Distant mode and no \"Save Registered Volume\" button with VISOR . This mode does not produce a registered volume, only a starting matrix: the button always answers \"No result to save!\". Use \"Save Transform Matrix\" instead, or go straight to Automatic Registration to get a volume worth saving. Misleading checkboxes in Distant mode Legacy only. There is no Distant mode with VISOR . Checking several structures (cranial base, mandible, maxilla) does not combine them. Only one region is actually used, chosen by a fixed…"},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#apres","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"And after","x":"Always check the result by eye, overlaying the registered volume on the fixed CBCT in Slicer, before using it for a measurement. And keep in mind that the exported matrix is a plain greedy-format file, not a Slicer or ITK transform: reusing it outside this module needs a conversion that GreedyReg does not perform itself."},{"u":"guide/GreedyReg.html#citer","p":"GreedyReg: register two CBCT scans with the ITK-SNAP engine","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used GreedyReg in your research, cite: Venet L, Pati S, Yushkevich P, Bakas S. Accurate and Robust Alignment of Variable-Stained Histologic Images Using a General-Purpose Greedy Diffeomorphic Registration Tool. arXiv:1904.11929, 2019. arXiv:1904.11929 . The paper for greedy , the engine this module merely drives. Short, and it is the citation its authors ask for. Background, not a citation for the tool Yushkevich et al., NeuroImage 2006 : User-guided 3D active contour segmentation of anatomical structures: Significantly improved efficiency and reliability . The ITK-SNAP reference…"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"MRI2CBCT registers an MRI onto a CBCT of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ), so you can look at both exams in the same frame. It is now one button: choose the folders, press Run, and the module prepares, orients, segments and registers for every patient. Legacy module page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. It is not available through a VISOR server: see MRI2CBCT and VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#quand","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you have, for the same patient, an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ and you want them superimposed: bone shows up better on the CBCT, soft tissue (disc, capsule) better on the MRI, and registering the two lets you look at both at once instead of comparing them from memory. You no longer go through tabs one by one: a single Run does the whole chain for every patient found in both folders. What it registers The module does not match the two images' brightness. It asks a network to find the cranial base on the CBCT and another to find it on the MRI, then fits the two surfaces together. So the…"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#besoin","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what it gives back","x":"To provide An MRI folder and a CBCT folder ( .nii.gz , .nii or .nrrd ) Files whose names start with the patient ID ( P01 , B038 …): this is how the two folders are paired. One CBCT per patient, one or two MRIs. An output folder A one-time setup: Python packages and models, about 5 GB, downloaded for you What comes out Per patient, a folder with each MRI joint registered onto the original CBCT, its cranial-base mask, and the transform file A summary.csv with a verdict for every joint: OK, SMALL, FLAG or FLIP Two volumes you can overlay in the same scene, not a merged file; the CBCT is never…"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#marche","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What happens when you press Run","x":"Preparing the MRIs. Pairs the patients and, by default, resamples each MRI to 0.3 mm and splits a volume that holds both joints into a left and a right half. A volume with a gap between two stacks is rebuilt using the \"acquisition slice spacing\" you set (3.0 mm by default). The side of a single-joint MRI is read from where it sits in the file. Orienting the CBCT. Three automatic steps (the ones of the ASO module) put the head in the standard Frankfurt and midsagittal orientation. Cranial base on the CBCT. The AMASSS network outlines it. Cranial base on the MRI. A second network, Dataset516…"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#options","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Options and checks","x":"Use test files downloads a sample patient (one CBCT, two MRIs), fills the three folders, and keeps the expected result in a reference folder so you can compare. Resample and Separate left and right are on by default. Turn separation off if each of your MRIs is already a single joint or a whole head. Advanced: put the registered MRI on the CBCT's voxel grid (off by default: the MRI keeps its own voxels and only its position changes), keep the intermediate files, set the number of registration workers. Review: tick \"Pause during the run\" and choose where. At each pause the images open in…"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#coince","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If it gets stuck","x":"Restart after the first setup If PyTorch had to be installed or replaced for your graphics card, the panel asks you to restart Slicer and press Run again. On Linux, the registration library needs a system with glibc 2.31 or newer. A patient is skipped A file with no ID at the start of its name, an MRI without a CBCT (or the reverse), or a patient with more than one CBCT is left out, and the panel lists them before it starts. Keep your output folder outside the MRI and CBCT folders: the folders are read recursively, so an earlier run's results could be picked up as new scans. Read the…"},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#apres","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Afterward","x":"The original CBCT is never modified. Open the registered MRI and the CBCT together in Slicer to check the overlay before using it for any measurement. If you need the transform for something else, load the original MRI and apply the .tfm to it: nothing is interpolated that way. A registration the verdict marks as flawed is better rerun with a review pause on the two outlines than adjusted by hand."},{"u":"guide/MRI2CBCT.html#citer","p":"MRI2CBCT: align an MRI and a CBCT of the TMJ","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used MRI2CBCT in your research, cite: Leroux G, Mattos CT, Claret J, Caleme E, Barone S, Gurgel M, Miranda F, Gonçalves JR, Zupelari-Gonçalves P, Morettin-Zupelari M, Wolford LM, Hsu NS, Ruellas ACO, Bianchi J, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L. Novel CBCT-MRI Registration Approach for Enhanced Analysis of Temporomandibular Degenerative Joint Disease. In: Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2024), held with MICCAI 2024, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol. 15196, Springer, 2024, p. 63-72. doi:10.1007/978-3-031-73083-2_7 . The paper of the earlier, elastix-based version of the module; the…"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"Despite the name, MedX never looks at an image: it reads written clinical reports ( .pdf , .docx , .txt ), has a local language model turn each one into a structured summary, and can then roll a whole cohort of those summaries into a single page of figures. Two distinct steps, worth knowing before you start. Legacy module page This page describes the module as it was before it was removed from the legacy extension on 2026-09-17; it is no longer in the current version. It was never available through a VISOR server: see MedX and VISOR and Using VISOR ."},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#quand","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"When you have a pile of free-text reports, clinical exam, radiographic exam, consultation notes, and want the clinical indicators out of them (age, pain, sleep, mouth opening, and so on) without reading each one by hand. Once you have those summaries, the second half of MedX lets you see the whole cohort at a glance: age breakdowns, associated disorders, average scores. Two independent paths MedX has two tabs, two buttons, and nothing chains them together automatically. Summarize reads your documents and writes one summary file per patient. Dashboard reads those summaries back and produces…"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#besoin","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"Summarize path What to provide A flat folder of reports as .pdf , .docx , or .txt (no subfolders) File names where everything before the first \"_\" is the patient ID: several files sharing that ID are grouped and summarized together The summarization model, downloaded once What comes out One <patient-id>_summary.txt file per patient, in free text Your source documents are left untouched Dashboard path What to provide The summary folder produced by the Summarize path (files must end in _summary.txt ) What comes out A dashboard.png image: age, sleep, joint and muscle pain, mouth opening, across…"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#marche","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Summarize the reports Download the model. Only needed once; without it, the Summarize path has nothing to work from. Point it to the folder of reports , named with a patient ID before the first \"_\". Set an output folder , then launch. A progress bar tracks patients as they're processed. Read through the summaries you get before relying on them: see below. Build the dashboard Switch to the Dashboard tab. This doesn't happen on its own after summarization. Set the summary folder from the previous step as input. Set an output folder , then launch. On the first run, Slicer may ask to install a…"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#relecture","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Reading the result","x":"Each report's summary comes from a generative model: it writes free text, not a guaranteed form. The key: value format the dashboard expects is a habit the model learned, not an enforced rule: a malformed line is simply dropped, and silently missing from the dashboard. The model also samples part of its output: running the summary again on the same document doesn't produce the exact same text twice. For both reasons, a summary MedX generates is a starting point to read through, not a validated report: don't use it as-is for a clinical decision."},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#coince","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"The model download button does nothing Clicking the download button creates the expected folder but never puts a file in it: the field stays empty and a warning message appears. Get the model archive yourself from the GitHub releases page of the SlicerAutomatedDentalTools repository (the BART.zip file, under the \"MedX\" release), unzip it, then point the module at that folder. The dashboard progress bar never moves Unlike the Summarize path, the Dashboard progress bar stays still for the whole run. That isn't a sign it's stuck: let it run and wait for dashboard.png to show up in the output…"},{"u":"guide/MedX.html#citer","p":"MedX: summarize reports, then build a dashboard","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used MedX in your research, cite: Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Mattos CT, Teixeira RHF, Shin MY, Hershey A, Kwon A, Miranda F, Gurgel M, Barone S, Aliaga A, Yatabe M, Zupelari-Gonçalves P, Morettin-Zupelari M, Hanauer DI, Hsu NS, Pieper S, Caleme E, Bianchi J, Gonçalves JR, Gonçalves D, Wolford L, Ruellas ACO, Prieto JC, Li T, Zhu H, Dai R, Styner M, Al Turkestani N, DaSilva AF, Cevidanes L. AI-Driven Multimodal TMJ Patient Modeling: From Unstructured Notes to Precision Treatment. In: Clinical Image-Based Procedures (CLIP 2025), held with MICCAI 2025, Lecture Notes in Computer Science vol.…"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"MedicalDataAnonymizer finds and replaces personal data, names, dates, addresses, ID numbers, inside text documents ( .docx , .txt , .pdf , .csv , .xml , .odt ). It is not a DICOM anonymiser : it never reads, modifies, or erases a single medical image. On video Medical Data Anonymizer: Your AI model who anonymize all your medical text files 41 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Legacy module page This page describes the legacy Slicer module. The VISOR extension ships a copy of it that runs on your computer, never on the server, and it does not de-identify the files other panels upload: see…"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#quand","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"For a first pass over a batch of English-language reports, letters, or notes that need the most visible mentions stripped before you share or export them, not to declare a dataset de-identified in one step. The output still needs a read-through, and it doesn't substitute for renaming files or handling the images separately."},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#besoin","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of text documents: .docx , .txt , .pdf , .csv , .xml , or .odt The dependencies installed once (a dedicated button), with Slicer restarted afterward The categories of data to detect, checked in the list: seven are checked by default (names, phone numbers, emails, dates, addresses, US social security numbers, medical license numbers) What comes out A copy of each document, suffixed _anonymized , in the output folder A file_mappings.csv file listing original and output file names: don't hand this out together with the documents Your original files are left untouched…"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#marche","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Install the dependencies (once), using the dedicated button. The module downloads Presidio and an English text-analysis model; Slicer then asks to be restarted. Point it to the source folder. It's walked in full, subfolders included; there's no need to process documents one at a time. Check the categories to detect. Only add what you actually need beyond the seven checked by default (credit card, IP address, URL, US driver's license…). Pick a replacement method in \"Advanced Options\": tag replacement ( <PERSON> , the default), redaction, masking with asterisks, or hashing. A hash stays the…"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#verifier","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Check the result","x":"Before sharing anything, reopen every _anonymized file and compare it against the original: that's the only way to catch a silent failure (see above). Then rename the files yourself, the patient's name survives in the output file name, and don't attach file_mappings.csv to anything sent outside your team: it lists original file names, not a usable re-identification key."},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#limites","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What the detection misses","x":"The language is fixed to English. A folder in French or any other language runs through the same model, with no warning, and recall drops sharply. The default confidence threshold drops the least certain hits: typically rare or non-Anglo-Saxon names. Hospital identifiers , medical record number, accession number, study ID, bed number, aren't searched for by any of the categories on offer. Non-US ID numbers , a French social security number, an NHS number, a passport, aren't covered; only US formats are recognised. Content that couldn't be extracted from a file (tables in a .docx , attributes…"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#coince","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Dependency installation fails It needs internet access to download Presidio and the language model. If installation errors out, check the connection and try again; Slicer will need restarting once it succeeds either way. Two files with the same name, only one comes out The output folder is written flat: the input's subfolder structure isn't reproduced, and if two subfolders each hold a file with the same name, the second one silently overwrites the first. Rename ahead of time if that's a risk. A .csv comes out unreadable It's the most fragile format here: columns aren't preserved as-is. For…"},{"u":"guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#citer","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: de-identify a text document","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"No publication: the module assembles existing pieces (Presidio, spaCy). What is worth reading is what those pieces let through: automatic anonymisation is never complete. Nothing describes the module itself, so cite the extension as software: SlicerAutomatedDentalTools. DCBIA-OrthoLab. https://github.com/DCBIA-OrthoLab/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools Add the date you accessed it if your journal's style asks for one. Background, not a citation for the tool Alrazihi et al., Surgical Neurology International 2025 : Evaluating the accuracy of automated and semi-automated anonymization tools for…"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"SurgMovPred doesn't look at your volumes or your meshes: it takes a spreadsheet of cephalometric measurements and turns it into a numeric estimate of the bone displacements expected after orthognathic surgery, how far a point such as Pogonion or the posterior nasal spine should move, along the three axes of space. No imaging, no neural network: 112 small, independent statistical models, one per predicted value, applied row by row to your table. On video Surgical Movement Prediction: Machine learning model to help surgery preparation 28 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Two ways to run it…"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#quand","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Use it once you already have, per patient, a set of cephalometric measurements, the pre-surgical state and the intended occlusal outcome, and you want a quick numeric estimate of the corresponding bone displacements, across a whole batch of patients at once rather than one at a time. It's a numeric complement to your own surgical planning, not a 3D visualization tool. A spreadsheet-only tool SurgMovPred doesn't read any volume, surface, or Slicer landmark, and it doesn't touch your 3D scene: nothing is created or displayed there. Everything happens on disk: a folder of spreadsheets in, a…"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#format","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"The input spreadsheet format","x":"This is where most people get stuck with this module: what it expects on input is quite specific, and nothing warns you clearly when it isn't met. A folder, not a single file. Every .csv , .xlsx , and .ods file inside is read and stacked into one big table, with no check that their columns actually match. Subfolders aren't scanned. Only one sheet per workbook. For an Excel or .ods file, only the first sheet is read. If your workbook has several sheets (demographics, raw measurements…), make sure the one with per-patient measurements comes first. Column headers get lightly cleaned up…"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#besoin","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What it needs, what you get","x":"What to provide A folder of spreadsheets ( .csv , .xlsx , or .ods ) in the format described above The trained models, downloaded once (dedicated button, about 700 MB) An output folder What comes out Two identical spreadsheets ( .xlsx and .csv ) in the output folder One row per input patient, one column per predicted displacement value Your input spreadsheets and your Slicer scene are left untouched"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#marche","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the models. Dedicated button, about 700 MB, only needed once but required before the first run. Prepare your input folder following the format above: one consolidated spreadsheet or several files, either way, as long as the expected columns are there. Set the three folders : input, downloaded models, output. Run it. There's nothing to tune: no threshold, no target selection, no skeletal-class option. All 112 values are predicted at once, for every row of the assembled table. Collect the two output spreadsheets from the folder you chose."},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#lire","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to read the result, and not read too much into it","x":"Each output column is a predicted displacement value for a given point on the maxilla, the mandible, or the genioplasty segment, expressed along three components of the cephalometric frame (front/back, left/right, up/down), plus a fourth \"Total\" column that's most likely an overall magnitude. The unit is written nowhere in the tool; it's probably millimeters, but that's an inference, not a stated certainty. Each point is predicted on its own, not the whole segment The 112 output values come from 112 independent models, one per value. Nothing guarantees that the ten points predicted for the…"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#coince","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something's off","x":"Clicking Apply reinstalls a dependency every time (legacy module) Clicking Apply always forces a reinstall of numpy, even when everything was already working. That makes the run slower, and swapping out a core numerical library inside an already-running Slicer session can unsettle other tools that depend on it. If another module starts misbehaving after you've used SurgMovPred, restart Slicer before looking any further. A missing column in the output isn't a crash If one of the expected values is missing from the result, nothing errors out visibly: the model behind it was simply skipped…"},{"u":"guide/SurgMovPred.html#citer","p":"SurgMovPred: estimate the expected bone movement after surgery","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"No publication describes the module. Its only public description is a NA-MIC project report, and the models it runs are evaluated nowhere. If you used SurgMovPred in your research, cite: Buisson A, Dumont P, Prieto JC, Cevidanes L, Pieper S, Dominguez MI. New 3D Slicer Module to predict surgery movement for maxillofacial surgery. Project page, NA-MIC Project Week 45, Boston, 2026. projectweek.na-mic.org . The project report: the intent, the starting data, and the state of the work. Background, not a citation for the tool de Oliveira et al., Orthod Craniofac Res 2024 : Artificial intelligence…"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html","p":"VFACE: classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"VFACE measures and classifies skeletal facial asymmetry from a single CBCT per patient. You don't need a second scan for comparison: the module builds its own \"mirror\" of the patient and compares the patient against that reflection. On video Classify facial Asymmetry and longitudinal studies, here is VFACE the new tool who do both 33 s (overview) · DCBIA Videos channel Two ways to run it This page describes the legacy Slicer module. Using it through a VISOR server: see With VISOR and Using VISOR . The flow as the lab presents it: ASO orients the scan, ALI_CBCT places the landmarks…"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#quand","p":"VFACE: classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to use it","x":"Whenever you want to objectify a facial asymmetry on a CBCT, quantify it rather than eyeball it, and possibly find out whether it involves the mandible, the maxilla, or both. VFACE starts from a raw volume: no segmentation or landmarks to prepare beforehand, it handles all of that itself. The heaviest module in the extension VFACE is not a standalone tool: it's an orchestrator that chains, one after another, six other modules of the extension (resampling, orientation, bone segmentation, registration, dental surface segmentation, classification). On a batch of patients, expect the full run to…"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#besoin","p":"VFACE: classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What you need, what you get","x":"To provide A folder of raw T1 CBCTs, one per patient ( .nii , .nii.gz , .nrrd , .nrrd.gz , .gipl , .gipl.gz ) No prior segmentation or landmarks: the module starts from the volume alone The trained models (ALI, AMASSS, DentalSegmentator, the VFACE classifiers), to download once An output folder, on a drive with room to spare What comes out A per-patient table of measurements (skeletal distances and angles) A final classification file stating whether the patient is symmetric or asymmetric, and whether the asymmetry involves the mandible and/or the maxilla Optionally, bone surfaces and color…"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#marche","p":"VFACE: classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Steps","x":"Download the models. A dedicated button fetches all the weights needed by the chained modules. Only needed once. Point to the input folder containing your T1 CBCTs, and to an output folder. Choose the analysis mode. For an asymmetry study (the common case), VFACE builds the patient's mirror and produces the classification. A \"longitudinal studies\" mode exists to compare two real time points of the same patient, but it doesn't produce a classification in that case. Choose what you want as output. Measurements and classification (quantitative mode), surfaces and distance maps (visualization…"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#lire","p":"VFACE: classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to read the result","x":"Before the table, the picture. This is the measurement the classifier is fed from: the patient against their own mirror image, and the gap that is left after superimposing on one structure. Cranial base Maxilla Mandible Drag to rotate · pinch or scroll to zoom Mirror again −35 mm +35 mm 0. t1_oriented_cb : semi_aso_cbct turns the patient 2.67° onto the plane x = 0 1. t1_landmarks : the 26 points ALI predicted here 2. t1_masks , AMASSS: the bone surfaces the maps are measured on 3. mirror_scans : diag(-1, 1, 1) , a reflection about x = 0 4. registration_cb / _max / _mand : on one structure…"},{"u":"guide/VFACE.html#citer","p":"VFACE: classify facial asymmetry","t":"guide","c":"","s":"How to cite","x":"If you used VFACE in your research, cite: Buisson A. Automated Classification of Facial Asymmetry, Identification of Regional Asymmetry Patterns. Poster, 104th IADR General Session / 55th AADOCR / 50th CADR, San Diego, March 25-28, 2026. dentistry.unc.edu . The poster presented at the 104th IADR General Session, San Diego. The UNC Adams School of Dentistry round-up gives its title and author; the poster itself is not deposited anywhere public, and the IADR archives are not freely indexed. Sanna A, Ng J, Ruellas A, Aliaga A, Gaydamour A, Tulissi E, Huang Y, Prieto JC, Al Turkestani N, Yatabe…"},{"u":"guide/index.html","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"What each tool does, what to feed it, what it returns, without going into the code. For the internal mechanisms, switch to the Atlas."},{"u":"guide/index.html#pour-qui","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Who this guide is for","x":"Anyone who opens SlicerAutomatedDentalTools in 3D Slicer and wants to use it: clinicians, researchers, students. It assumes no ability to read code and no idea what a neural network is. If what you want is the inner workings, which model is called, when, on what data, you want the Atlas instead: both sections cover the same 20 modules, each from the opposite end."},{"u":"guide/index.html#pourquoi","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Why it exists","x":"The extension gathers 20 modules written by different teams at different times. Their documentation amounts, depending on the case, to a few lines of README, a project page, or nothing at all. Several behave differently from what their publication describes: the paper documents a version the code no longer runs. This guide was written by reading each module's code, not its documentation. So it says what the tool actually does when you click, including when that is inconvenient: 89 warnings and 4 bugs are flagged at the exact point where you are likely to hit them. Nothing is fixed here: the…"},{"u":"guide/index.html#plan","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What every page contains","x":"They all follow the same plan, so you know where to look without rereading: When to use it , which problem the tool answers, and the cases where it is of no help. What you need, what you get : the expected inputs, their format, and what comes out at the end. Step by step : the steps in order, field by field. When it goes wrong : the common failures and what causes them. How to cite : the reference to cite in a paper where the tool has one, and the pointer to the Atlas page."},{"u":"guide/index.html#commencer","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Where to start","x":"If you know which tool you are after, take it from the list below. Otherwise start from what you have to do: Compare two scans of the same patient over time → Registration Isolate structures in a CBCT → Segmentation Place anatomical landmarks, or put a scan back upright → Landmarks & orientation Measure, classify or predict from a shape → Analysis Crop or apply a matrix, in batch → Utilities Extract or anonymise clinical text → Text & language"},{"u":"guide/index.html#visor","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"VISOR: run the tools on a server","x":"The tool pages below describe the legacy Slicer modules, and each ends with a With VISOR section. This page covers the new way of running them. Using VISOR Connect Slicer to a VISOR server, run a tool, review and download the results"},{"u":"guide/index.html#registration","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Registration","x":"FlexReg Register two intraoral scans by picking the stable area yourself AREG_IOS Register two scans of the same patient, with nothing to draw AREG_CBCT Superimpose two CBCT scans of the same patient taken at two dates AREG_IOSCBCT Place an intraoral scan into the frame of a CBCT GreedyReg Register two CBCT scans using the ITK-SNAP engine MRI2CBCT Align an MRI and a CBCT of the temporomandibular joint in one run"},{"u":"guide/index.html#segmentation","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Segmentation","x":"AMASSS Segment mandible, maxilla and other structures from a CBCT BatchDentalSeg Segment teeth and bone across a series of scans in one run"},{"u":"guide/index.html#landmarks","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Landmarks & orientation","x":"ALI Place anatomical landmarks automatically ASO Bring scans into a shared orientation"},{"u":"guide/index.html#analysis","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Analysis","x":"VFACE Measure and classify a patient's facial asymmetry DOCShapeAXI Classify 3D shapes and see what drove the decision CLIC Find and classify impacted canines on a CBCT SurgMovPred Estimate the expected bone movement after surgery"},{"u":"guide/index.html#utilities","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Utilities","x":"AutoCrop3D Crop a series of volumes to the same region AutoMatrix Apply registration matrices across a whole series"},{"u":"guide/index.html#text","p":"User guide","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Text & language","x":"CNE Pull structured information out of clinical notes MedX Summarise clinical reports and build a dashboard from them MedicalDataAnonymizer Strip personal data from a text document Agent Ask in plain language which tool to run"},{"u":"guide/visor.html","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"With VISOR, the computation no longer runs on your computer. You pick a tool in Slicer, the files you select are sent to a server, the server computes, and the result comes back into your scene. This page covers what you have to set up, what you provide, and what to check before sending patient data. How VISOR is built is in the VISOR Atlas . Before using patient data The VISOR client sends your files as they are on disk. Nothing is de-identified. See What leaves your machine ."},{"u":"guide/visor.html#what","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What changes for you","x":"The rest of this site describes the legacy extension, SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: each module runs on your own computer. VISOR is a different Slicer extension, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote . Its modules are thin panels over a remote server. The calculation leaves your computer. A laptop that cannot run a segmentation can still ask for one. Nothing to install into Slicer's Python , and no model to download: the extension declares no dependency on other extensions, and the models live on the server. The form comes from the server. A tool's panel is built when you open it, from the description…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#install","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Install the extension","x":"The README and the build file say the following, and nothing more is assumed here. The extension is AutomatedDentalToolsRemote . It works with the stable and the nightly versions of 3D Slicer, and its modules appear under the category Automated Dental Tools Remote . It lists no extension dependency ( EXTENSION_DEPENDS \"NA\" ): you do not need SlicerConda, SlicerDentalModelSeg or NNUNet for it. The README states it is listed in the Extensions Catalog during the transition. Do not install it next to the legacy extension. The README asks you to use one or the other, because many modules share…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#connect","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Connect Slicer to a server","x":"Open Server Tools Settings (category Automated Dental Tools Remote.Advanced ). Until you save something here, the address is a placeholder ( https://set-your-server.invalid ) that reaches nothing, so no file leaves your machine. To provide Server URL: an https:// address whose certificate is signed by a public authority. API key: the server's API token (the field is masked). The README says to use the API token, never the admin token. Timeout: 1 to 3600 seconds, 600 by default. What you cannot change No http:// , not even http://localhost . The certificate is always checked: the panel shows…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#run","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Run a tool","x":"Choose a tool in the module list. It builds its form from the server on opening. If the server cannot be reached, the panel says it could not load the tool and offers a Retry button. Fill the form. What you provide depends on the tool; the field names come from the server, so read each tool's own Guide page . Press Apply. The panel checks the form first (unknown argument, missing file, missing required field) before anything is sent. Watch the progress and cancel if you need to. What you can give as input A file is sent as it is, same name and same bytes. A folder is packed into an archive…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#results","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Get the results","x":"When the run ends, the panel downloads the result (in parallel ranges), checks the archive, and unpacks it into the output folder. By default that folder is \"Slicer Output\" in your Documents. An archive is refused before anything is written if it holds absolute paths, .. , links, more than 100 000 entries or more than 20 GB. The check box \"Load the results into the scene when done\" imports what the tool produced: volumes, label maps, models or landmarks, depending on the tool. At most 12 items are loaded by default; beyond that the panel tells you there are too many to load at once. Some…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#review","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Quality review with VISU","x":"Some tools can stop after a step so that you check the intermediate result. The form then shows a \"Quality control\" section with a multiple choice stop_after ; nothing is ticked by default. Tools that call other tools offer it, for example ALI for intraoral scans or VFACE. Stop. The server pauses after the step you ticked. The panel shows \"Stopped after <step>, waiting for your review\", downloads what was produced so far, and opens VISU on it. Review. VISU shows one patient at a time: scan, masks, surfaces and landmarks together. The arrow keys go to the previous or next case. Use Flag to…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#privacy","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"What leaves your machine","x":"Nothing is de-identified No de-identification tool exists in VISOR today. What you select is sent to the server as it sits on disk: file names, DICOM headers and every identifier they carry. A de-identification pipeline is specified in the client repository but its README states it is not implemented, and a search of the code finds no implementation. The notice. After you save a server address that is not this computer, Slicer shows once per server: \"Files are uploaded unmodified. This client does not de-identify anything... De-identify your data before selecting it, or confine use to data…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#legacy","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"When to keep the legacy modules","x":"Legacy (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools) VISOR (AutomatedDentalToolsRemote) Where it computes On your computer On a server Extension dependencies SlicerDentalModelSeg, NNUNet, SlicerConda None Your files Stay on your machine Sent to the server, not de-identified You need Enough local hardware A server, a public HTTPS address and a key MRI2CBCT Present Not shipped MedX Documented on this site No module; CNE is its successor according to the README Medical Data Anonymizer, Agent Present Present, still local Quality review Review pauses inside some tools (AREG_CBCT) \"Stop after\" and VISU Keep the…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#coince","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"If something goes wrong","x":"For most failures, Slicer shows the server's own message. Here is what each means. \"The server URL must start with https://\" You typed an http:// address, or Slicer found one from an older version. Use the public https:// address; there is no way around the rule, including for a server on your own computer. 401: authentication failed The key is wrong or missing. The panel can still say \"Server: online\" because the health page needs no key. Re-enter the key in Server Tools Settings and Save. 503 during a maintenance window While the server is being updated, it refuses new work with a 503 and…"},{"u":"guide/visor.html#tools","p":"Using VISOR: run the tools on a server from 3D Slicer","t":"guide","c":"","s":"Tools available through VISOR","x":"Each tool has a VISOR Guide page, listed below, on top of its legacy Guide page. The Legacy | VISOR selector at the top of the sidebar switches from one to the other. A legacy page describes the legacy interface, which is not the one you see in VISOR: the form is generated by the server, so fields and layout can differ. AMASSS CBCT segmentation BatchDentalSeg Teeth and jaw segmentation ALI Landmarks, one VISOR panel for CBCT and IOS ASO Orientation AREG_CBCT Registration of two CBCT (in VISOR, the AREG panel) AREG_IOS Registration of two intraoral scans (AREG panel) AREG_IOSCBCT Intraoral…"},{"u":"index.html","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"One page per tool, organised around the pipeline : what is computed, what is predicted by a network, when the model gets called, what it actually does, what the preprocessing is, and what comes out. This is not a user manual. Code links point to the ~/Projects/UNC/slicer/SlicerAutomatedDentalTools repository. Two code bases: legacy and VISOR The tool pages listed below describe the legacy Slicer modules ( SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957 , 2026-10-26). The team is moving the tools to VISOR : a Slicer client that sends the work to an inference server, where each tool runs in its own…"},{"u":"index.html#visor","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"VISOR","x":"Overview What VISOR is, legacy against VISOR, which tools are ported, and the commits this survey was read at Architecture Client and server, the tool registry and schemas, facades, the call graph A run, step by step Upload, admission, execution, progress, results, quality-control stops Packaging and deployment The image, one virtualenv per tool, data, configuration, benchmarks Security and privacy What protects a run today, what is only specified, and what exists only as a prototype Tests and provenance How tools are tested, named and traced back to their legacy code Crown_Seg Tooth…"},{"u":"index.html#lecture","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Where to start","x":"From the bottom of the chain to the top. The first two steps are the ones everything else depends on. # Page Why this one first 1 FlexReg The simplest, everything is explicit. Introduces the LPS / RAS conventions, crown segmentation, and patch-based ICP . 2 ALI and AMASSS The two landmark providers everything else depends on. 3 ASO Orientation, a prerequisite for most registrations. 4 AREG_IOS then AREG_CBCT The registrations themselves. 5 VFACE Highest in the chain: it stacks all the others."},{"u":"index.html#briques","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"The three shared building blocks","x":"Almost every module rests on the same three components. Understanding them once means not re-reading the same mechanics across five pages. Who calls what, found by grepping slicer.modules.* . A filled dot means \"actually launches\". ALI sits in the path of all six modules : it's the building block to understand first. Details in ALI and AMASSS ."},{"u":"index.html#fiches","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"All the pages","x":"Registration FlexReg IOS registration with a hand-drawn patch AREG_IOS IOS registration with a predicted patch: the automatic FlexReg AREG_CBCT T1/T2 CBCT registration AREG_IOSCBCT multimodal IOS ↔ CBCT registration GreedyReg ITK-SNAP-style CBCT registration, Greedy engine MRI2CBCT multimodal MRI ↔ CBCT registration Segmentation AMASSS craniofacial structure segmentation on CBCT BatchDentalSeg DentalSegmentator and variants, in batch Landmarks and orientation ALI automatic landmark placement, CBCT and IOS ASO automatic scan orientation, CBCT and IOS Analysis and classification VFACE facial…"},{"u":"index.html#carte","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Call map","x":"Found by grepping slicer.modules.* and conda run -m <module> invocations. An arrow means \"actually launches\". Checked again on 2026-10-12: MedX is no longer there, having left the tree with its two CLIs for the archive/medx branch, its page is kept here as the dated record it is. Agent ──────────────► Agent_CLI ──► (routes to any module) VFACE ──────────────► AMASSS_CLI ├────────────► ALI_CBCT ├────────────► AREG_CBCT ├────────────► PRE_ASO_CBCT / SEMI_ASO_CBCT ├────────────► mri2cbct_resample_cbct_mri ├────────────► Automatrix_CLI └────────────► VFACE_CLI AREG ───────────────► AREG_CBCT /…"},{"u":"index.html#divergences","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"When the paper doesn't describe the code","x":"A frequent case here, flagged at the top of the relevant SOURCES pages. Tool The paper describes The code does AMASSS UNETR MONAI , 128³ crop nnU-Net v2 PlainConvUNet VFACE unsupervised UMAP + spectral clustering 3 supervised binary LightGBM models DOCShapeAXI ResNet-18, condyles as binary dual-branch 4096 points + 42 views, 4-class checkpoint AREG_CBCT two passes, SimpleElastix one pass, itk-elastix MRI2CBCT CLIP 2024: orientation, resampling, MRI inversion, normalization, rigid registration by mutual information FPFH + RANSAC + ICP on two network-predicted cranial-base surfaces, no intensity"},{"u":"index.html#constats","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"What the reading turned up","x":"Found while documenting, not while hunting for bugs. The \"verified\" column distinguishes what was reproduced or confirmed directly from the code from what a page reports and still needs checking. Where Symptom Verified MedicalDataAnonymizer anonymize_text_presidio does return text on any exception: the _anonymized file holds the untouched text, and the UI shows \"Complete!\" yes, read line by line CLIC the [SEG] / [PROGRESS] markers are emitted through logger.info with a prefix, the reader tests startswith : the segmentation is never loaded into Slicer yes BatchDentalSeg NasoMaxillaDentSeg…"},{"u":"index.html#conda","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Shared conda environment","x":"Coupling worth knowing ALI , AREG , ASO , DOCShapeAXI and FlexReg share the same conda environment shapeaxi , on Python 3.12 . A change to the Python version breaks all of them at once. Details in FlexReg , Environment section."},{"u":"index.html#organisation","p":"SlicerAutomatedDentalTools: how each tool works","t":"Accueil","c":"","s":"Folder layout","x":"One folder per tool. Each one holds: <Tool>.html : the pipeline, read from the code; SOURCES.html , the bibliography: what's deposited here, what's meant to be read online, and the \"neighbourhood\" (related work that does not describe the code); the retrieved documents, as PDF where the publisher allows it, otherwise as .xml (JATS, the full text) with a readable .txt alongside it. The original .md files are kept next to the HTML pages. 29 PDFs and 54 full texts, all verified: the PDFs are paginated, the XML files carry a text body. Nothing was obtained by working around a paywall: arXiv…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"How ALI runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does. The legacy module is described on its own page . VISOR runs the dental tools on a server: Slicer keeps only a panel that sends the data and loads the results (see Architecture ). This page describes the VISOR port of ALI, read in the code of the repositories named below and compared with the legacy Slicer module at 3c67957, which has its own page . \"Run for this page\" marks what was executed while checking it; everything else was read in the code. Abbreviations used for…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-status","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"1. Status and position in the chain","x":"implemented and tested The port is two server tools, not one: ALI_CBCT and ALI_IOS , split from a single ALI tool on 2026-08-17 (SADT-VISOR commit 2ac7d9d). The tool a user sees is the facade ALI : it has no code of its own, only dispatch = { \"CBCT\" = \"ALI_CBCT\", \"IOS\" = \"ALI_IOS\" } (VISOR-serve deployment.toml:205-210 ). At startup the server builds its form from the two targets: a required mode choice first, then every argument of both, each carrying a visible_when on mode if only one target has it (VISOR-serve registry/facade.py:98 , :155-166 , :236-245 ). The Slicer panel is the module…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-files","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78 ( wc -l , run for this page). The two engines share nothing but the small sadt-ali-common package, which has no dependency and installs in both venvs ( common/pyproject.toml ). File Lines Role ALI_CBCT/src/sadt_ali_cbct/__init__.py 173 run() , the published signature; PREDICTION_ID = \"Pred\" , OUTPUT_SUFFIXES CB/dispatch.py 274 detect() (what the input holds, DICOM conversion), identify() (work dir, engine call, run_report.json ) CB/engine.py 922 weight discovery, landmark selection, per-scan preprocessing, process pool, markups writing, report CB/agent.py…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-cbct","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"3. ALI_CBCT: agents inside the volume","x":"ALI_CBCT on VISOR. Grey boxes are computed, the violet one is the network, the accent box is what the tool writes. One agent per landmark , its networks rebuilt for each (scan, landmark) pair, all scans of the batch in one run. Drag to rotate · scroll to zoom Basion Ba The anterior margin of the foramen magnum, on the skull base. Sella S The centre of the sella turcica, the hollow that holds the pituitary. Nasion N The bridge of the nose, where the frontal and nasal bones meet. Replay the search walking, one voxel per step circled: arrived. Scale now steps to reach the landmark The geometry…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-ios","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"4. ALI_IOS: rendering, one tooth at a time","x":"ALI_IOS on VISOR. The violet boxes run a network: the UNet of this tool, and Crown_Seg , a separate server tool called only for meshes that carry no tooth labels. Drag to rotate · click a tooth Run the camera pass tooth labels first, from the mesh or else from Crown_Seg: camera placed on tooth UNet segmenting the render of tooth landmarks placed, one camera pass per tooth The geometry comes from the published test arch (the ASO_IOS test file, a lower arch cut by its own PredictedID array; no patient data). The order is VISOR’s: the label array comes first, because each tooth’s centroid, and…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-args","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"5. Arguments and schema","x":"Signatures: CB/__init__.py:62-104 and IO/__init__.py:64-120 . \"Hidden\" means the argument exists in the published schema but the panel does not draw it; the facade keeps the first target's description of an argument both tools share, so input keeps the CBCT wording (\"Scan or Folder\") in IOS mode too (VISOR-serve registry/facade.py:146-147 , by reading). Argument Type, default Tool In the panel mode choice, required, first mode ticked facade shown, first field input path, required both file or folder picker ( FILE_INPUTS = {\"input\": \"file_or_folder\"} , Remote ALIRemote.py:101 ); the form also…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-predicted","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"6. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Input detection, DICOM conversion computed GDCM, SimpleITK CB/dispatch.py Histogram clamp, resampling computed SimpleITK, itk CB/preprocess.py Agent's move direction predicted DenseNet3D + MLP, 6 classes CB/brain.py Convergence, change of scale, Focus , respawn computed memory of 10 positions, averaging, seeded generator CB/agent.py Voxel to millimetres computed abs(origin / spacing) CB/environment.py Crown segmentation (unlabelled meshes) predicted by another tool, Crown_Seg IO/dispatch.py → Crown_Seg Unit sphere, camera placement, MG frame, arch fit computed geometry…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-environment","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"7. Environment","x":"Item ALI_CBCT ALI_IOS Python >=3.11,<3.12 ( ALI_CBCT/pyproject.toml:5 ) >=3.11,<3.12 ( ALI_IOS/pyproject.toml:5 ) torch 2.8.0 , cu128 index ( :16 , :46-59 ) 2.11.0 , cu128 index, with torchvision 0.26.0 ( :17 , :23 , :80-81 ) Key libraries monai 1.6.0 , itk 5.4.7 , SimpleITK 2.5.6 , numpy 2.3.2 ( :17-20 ) pytorch3d 0.7.9+pt2110cu128 (prebuilt wheel, :24 , :63-82 ), monai 1.6.0 , vtk 9.6.2 , numpy 2.3.2 ( :28-30 ) pytorch3d none, which is why torch is not moved to 2.11 ( :6-9 ) imported lazily; without it the schema still publishes and an IOS run fails with ToolUnavailableError…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-changes","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"8. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Each row names both sides. Legacy lines are at 3c67957. Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR Orientation of the CBCT volume reoriented to LPS on load: sitk.DICOMOrient(img, \"LPS\") , ALI_CBCT_utils/environment.py:72 (commit e073746, 2026-09-02) not done: the image is read as stored ( CB/environment.py:63-79 ); the resampler keeps the image's own direction ( CB/preprocess.py:120 ) Search budget 1000 steps ( ALI_CBCT_utils/agent.py:27 , ALI_SEARCH_MAX_STEPS ) plus a 900 s guard rail ( :33 , :375-389 ) plus a stop after 64 steps on known ground, Cycling ( :43 , :471-491 ) 900 steps by default, or…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-findings","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"9. Findings in VISOR","x":"Confirmed bug · a DICOM series folder gives a hidden output file Select the folder of one DICOM series as the input (the case the panel invites) and the scan key becomes \".\" : _dicom_directories includes the root itself ( CB/dispatch.py:70-77 ), scan_key is os.path.relpath(root, root) ( CM/discovery.py:43 ), the converted file is named ..nii.gz ( CB/dispatch.py:156 , :164 ) and scan_stem(\"..nii.gz\") is \".\" ( CB/engine.py:152-158 ). The output is ._lm_Pred.mrk.json , a hidden file. Run for this page: scan_key and scan_stem return exactly those values. A complete run on a real series was not…"},{"u":"visor/ALI/ALI.html#visor-literature","p":"ALI on VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"10. Literature","x":"The networks are the published ones: ALI_CBCT follows Gillot et al. 2023 (agents on a multi-scale volume, scales 1 mm and 0.3 mm, CB/catalog.py:69 ), ALI_IOS the CLIP 2022 paper for the O model (unit sphere, five views per tooth, patch segmentation, IO/surface.py:72 , IO/render.py:39-54 ). The Mucogingival path is unpublished, as on the legacy side. The bibliography is the tool's own: ALI sources ; the legacy page discusses each paper against the code in its section 8 ."},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"How AMASSS runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does: one binary nnU-Net v2 network per anatomical structure, now predicted side by side on a server. The legacy module is described on its own page . VISOR runs AMASSS on a server: Slicer only sends the scans and receives the segmentations. This page follows a run from the panel to the report: what runs where, which files do what, each step of the pipeline with the code that performs it, the arguments the server publishes, and how the port differs from the legacy module.…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-status","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"1. Status and position in the chain","x":"Item Value Reference State Ported and implemented: the pipeline, a report and a test suite exist in SADT-VISOR. Not ported: DICOM input, small FOV , teeth, root canal and mandibular canal models, the save_in_folder option. SADT-VISOR catalog.py:13-18 API name AMASSS SADT-VISOR tools/AMASSS/pyproject.toml:33-35 Tool code package sadt_amasss , own virtual environment (Python 3.11, torch 2.8.0, nnunetv2 2.8.1, SimpleITK 2.5.6, numpy 2.3.2, vtk 9.6.2) tools/AMASSS/pyproject.toml:7-23 Provenance Ported from AMASSS_CLI/ of SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at commit 21a62a8 (2026-05-22), by way of an…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-files","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"2. Files involved","x":"The tool is a plain Python package with no knowledge of the server: run() receives paths and returns a path, and the failures a caller can act on are its own exception classes ( errors.py:1-14 ). Line counts at 6ba2a78: File Lines Role __init__.py 109 the public run() : its signature is the published schema; it converts the lists into codes and calls segment() pipeline.py 657 the whole run: argument checks, scan discovery, model resolution, conversion, parallel inference, per-scan assembly, report, cleanup nnunet_runner.py 245 nnU-Net v2 behind four functions: device, model folder lookup…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-pipeline","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"3. The pipeline at a glance","x":"run() ( __init__.py:20-109 ) turns the two list arguments into codes and calls segment() ( pipeline.py:280-376 ), which checks the arguments, finds the scans and the models, then hands over to _run() ( pipeline.py:379-558 ) for three phases that share the progress bar: reading (0 to 10 %), predicting (10 to 90 %), writing (90 to 100 %) ( pipeline.py:391-395, 471, 492 ). A run, step by step. Purple: the only learned step. Accent: what the caller receives besides the masks. Steps 1 to 3 fail fast, before any inference; from step 4 on a failure is recorded per scan or per structure and the run…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-inputs","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"4. Inputs: checks, discovery and conversion","x":"4.1 From the panel's lists to codes structures and merge are published as lists of fixed options ( list[Literal[...]] , __init__.py:28-31 ), which the schema generator turns into a multi-select ( SADT-VISOR scripts/describe.py:240 ). catalog._codes_from() ( catalog.py:108-135 ) accepts four shapes: None (the defaults), a single string, an iterable of codes or display names, and the old {name: bool} dictionary. The string branch exists because a bare string would otherwise be iterated character by character, so that no code matches; the comment records that merge once shipped that way and…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-models","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"5. Models and inference","x":"5.1 The bundle and its lookup The server hands the hidden model argument the whole DATA/AMASSS/models/ folder ( layout.py:26-32 ). resolve_models() descends into it when it holds a single wrapping folder (for instance AMASSS_Models/ ) and none of the code folders directly ( pipeline.py:143-153 ), then calls find_model_folder() for each requested code ( nnunet_runner.py:50-72 ): <bundle>/<CODE>/**/<Dataset...>__nnUNetPlans__3d_fullres/fold_0/checkpoint_final.pth A candidate is accepted only once its fold_0/checkpoint_final.pth exists, so a half-copied bundle makes a structure unavailable…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-outputs","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"6. Assembly, outputs and the report","x":"6.1 Per-scan assembly Once every structure has run, each readable scan is assembled in turn ( pipeline.py:490-522 ) by _assemble_scan_outputs() ( pipeline.py:561-657 ): read pred_<CODE>/p_NNN.nii.gz for each predicted structure (a missing file is logged by case id and skipped, :581-588 ), binarise with array > 0 ( :590 ), then write: SEPARATE when requested, or whenever only one structure was produced: one binary file per structure ( :599-606 ); MERGED when requested and at least two structures exist: painted in MERGING_ORDER , where a later structure overwrites an earlier one on shared…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-io","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"7. Arguments and schema","x":"Argument Type Default In the panel scans path required yes: a volume or a folder (walked recursively); also hosted test files, if the server has any model path (bundle name) required hidden, filled by the server ( layout.py:32 ) output_dir path required not published, filled by the server ( VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:36 ) structures list of MAND, MAX, CB, CV, UAW, SKIN, CBMASK, MANDMASK, MAXMASK MAND, MAX, CB, CV, UAW yes, as chips grouped Bones / Soft tissue / Masks, with the full name as help; at least one ( layout.py:33-48 ) merge list of MERGED, SEPARATE MERGED yes, two…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-predicted","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"8. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where Argument checks, scan discovery, model lookup computed file system and string rules pipeline.py:299-336 Conversion to NIfTI computed SimpleITK read and write pipeline.py:177-194 Preprocessing (resampling, CTNormalization ) computed deterministic, driven by each model's plans.json ; resampler substituted on GPU nnU-Net, in the tool process Segmentation of each structure predicted nnU-Net v2 3D network, one binary model per structure, fold 0 nnU-Net, nnunet_runner.py:190-236 Sliding window, Gaussian weighting, step 0.5 computed deterministic nnU-Net Binarization, label merge…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-env","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"9. Environment","x":"The tool has its own virtual environment, built by uv from its lockfile; nothing is installed into Slicer. The pins are those of the stack the server already ran, not upstream's torch 2.2.0 / nnunetv2 2.8.0, because no AMASSS result had been produced on the latter ( tools/AMASSS/pyproject.toml:5-10 ; README.md:73-85 ). Package Version Note Python >=3.11,<3.12 the deployment image's interpreter ( pyproject.toml:5-7 ) torch 2.8.0 from the explicit pytorch-cu128 index ( :49-52, 66 ) nnunetv2 2.8.1 inference backend SimpleITK 2.5.6 reading, conversion, writing numpy 2.3.2 the legacy pins 1.26.4…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-changes","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"10. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR Prediction engine nnU-Net v2 through its Python API, fold 0, no mirroring, tile step 0.5 ( AMASSS_CLI.py:112-137, 204-238 ) Same settings ( nnunet_runner.py:75-117, 190-236 ), plus cudnn.benchmark = False ( nnunet_runner.py:116 ), which the legacy CLI never sets GPU memory failure Automatic retry of the structure with scipy resampling ( AMASSS_CLI.py:240-251 ) No retry: the structure is recorded as failed ( nnunet_runner.py has no handler; pipeline.py:475-478 ) CPU path predict_from_files_sequential in every case ( AMASSS_CLI.py:232-237 ) CPU or…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-findings","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"11. Findings in VISOR","x":"Scans are skipped silently when their name looks like an output When scans is a folder, a file is left out if its name (without extension) contains _<prediction_ID>_ , ends with _MERGED , ends with one of the nine structure codes ( _MAND , _MAX , _CB , _CV , _UAW , _SKIN , _CBMASK , _MANDMASK , _MAXMASK ) or ends with _Seg or _SegOut ( SADT-VISOR pipeline.py:69-85 , applied at pipeline.py:111-113 ). A patient file called P01_CB.nii.gz or P01_Seg.nii.gz is therefore ignored. Only a count is logged, never the names ( pipeline.py:119-123 ); if every file is ignored, the run stops with \"No scan…"},{"u":"visor/AMASSS/AMASSS.html#visor-literature","p":"AMASSS on VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"12. Literature","x":"The bibliography is the tool's, unchanged by the port: the 2022 PLOS ONE paper describes a MONAI UNETR , while both the legacy and the VISOR code run nnU-Net v2 checkpoints ( nnunet_runner.py:33-34, 208-212 ; no MONAI in tools/AMASSS/pyproject.toml:11-23 ). The divergence is discussed on the legacy page, legacy §10 , and the references are in SOURCES.html ."},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"How AREG_CBCT runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does, step by step. The legacy module is described on its own page . AREG_CBCT registers a follow-up CBCT (T2) onto the baseline CBCT (T1) of the same patient with a rigid elastix registration, confined to a stable region of T1. In VISOR the whole computation runs on a server; Slicer only shows the form, sends the folders and opens what comes back. This page describes that port, read at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78 (branch tool/vface ), VISOR-serve e9a7e5d and…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-status","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"A client never names AREG_CBCT . The Slicer module sends AREG , a facade declared in the server's deployment file, with mode = \"CBCT\" ; the server swaps the facade for its target and runs this engine. The engine itself computes only the pairing and the registration: the masks and the orientation come from two other tools it calls through the supervisor ( sup.run ), each in its own environment. Item State API name AREG_CBCT , declared by [tool.sadt] name ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/AREG_CBCT/pyproject.toml:19-21 ). A client does not send that name: it sends AREG with mode = \"CBCT\" . Facade…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#files","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78. The engine is split into a schema adapter, a dispatcher that owns every cross-argument rule, and three computing modules; what the three AREG engines share lives in a package with no dependency at all. File Lines Role cbct/__init__.py 120 run() : the published signature. Its type hints and defaults are the schema; it forwards everything to dispatch.main ( cbct/__init__.py:33-120 ) cbct/layout.py 100 presentation only: sections, labels, visible_when per mode, hidden fields; merged into the schema by describe.py ( cbct/layout.py:1-12 ) cbct/dispatch.py 528…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-where","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"3. What runs where, and the pipeline","x":"Step Server Client (Slicer) Form publishes the schema of the facade, mode “ CBCT ” fields shown through visible_when builds the panel; hosted test cohorts CBCT_T1 and CBCT_T2 are offered above the pickers ( server/deployment.toml:45 ) Inputs unpacks the archives; a single file is linked into a folder; DICOM series are converted in a scratch folder ( cbct/dispatch.py:360-378 , cbct/dicom.py:39 ) sends the folders Orientation Oriented mode only: sup.run(\"ASO\", ...) on T1 ( cbct/dispatch.py:229-236 , cbct/tools.py:80-107 ) nothing Masks Fully-Automated and Oriented: one sup.run(\"AMASSS\", ...)…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-args","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Types and defaults below are those of the published schema (observed: scripts/describe.py in the tool's environment); layout comes from cbct/layout.py . Reached through the facade, the panel also shows the facade's own mode drop-down first; the engine's drop-down is labelled “Automation” and not “Mode” precisely to avoid two drop-downs with the same word ( cbct/layout.py:57 and the comment above it). keep_intermediate and stop_after are injected by the server and hidden ( cbct/layout.py:38,45 ). Argument Published type Default Widget and rule t1 , t2 path, required – pickers “T1 (baseline)”…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#predicted","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where it runs Orientation landmarks (ALI_CBCT, through ASO) predicted network, see ALI on VISOR separate tool, Oriented mode Orientation of T1 (ASO) computed from those landmarks onto the reference, see ASO on VISOR separate tool, Oriented mode Region masks and returned structures (AMASSS) predicted network, see AMASSS on VISOR separate tool, automatic modes Pairing, mask lookup computed from file names engine T1/T2 registration computed rigid elastix , mutual information engine, CPU Resampling, writing computed linear interpolation, Int16 engine As in the legacy module, the…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#environment","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Python requires-python = \">=3.11,<3.12\" ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/AREG_CBCT/pyproject.toml:5 ) Dependencies sadt-areg-common , torch==2.8.0 , itk==5.4.7 , itk-elastix==0.23.0 , SimpleITK==2.5.6 , numpy==2.3.2 , dicom2nifti==2.6.2 ( pyproject.toml:9-17 ), locked in uv.lock ; torch comes from the pytorch-cu128 index ( pyproject.toml:33-41 ). The comment above the list explains that the split from AREG_IOS freed this engine from the torch 2.11 pin pytorch3d imposes there ( pyproject.toml:6-8 ) Shared package sadt_areg_common , installed from ../common , which declares no dependency…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-changes","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR (6ba2a78) Where it runs Slicer CLI areg_cbct , launched by the AREG widget, packages pip-installed into Slicer ( AREG/AREG.py ) server engine in a locked environment, reached through the facade AREG Chaining the widget builds a list of CLI steps per mode and runs them one after another ( AREG/AREG_Method/CBCT.py ) sup.run calls from inside the engine ( cbct/tools.py:80-139 ) Patient id string split on a fixed list of markers ( ADT/ADTLib/naming.py:34-47 ) whole-token suffixes, timepoint tokens t1 t2 t0 only ( common/pairing.py:175 , common/catalogs.py:148 ).…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-findings","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"Elastix transform now exact The centre of rotation is read and applied, and the unit tests recover a known rigid transform on a phantom ( cbct/elastix.py:212-240 , SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/AREG_CBCT/tests/test_elastix.py:50 ). Observed: pytest in the tool's environment, 101 passed. Unpaired subjects are reported A subject present at one time point only is listed under unmatched in AREG_report.json instead of vanishing ( cbct/dispatch.py:259-260 ). Read in code. Oriented mode with the Occlusal reference The legacy code derived the landmark list from the chosen reference…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_CBCT/AREG_CBCT.html#literature","p":"AREG_CBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Literature","x":"The method is the one published for the legacy module (voxel-based rigid registration on a masked stable region); the bibliography of the tool, with the points where the code departs from the paper, is on its sources page and in section 12 of the legacy page . The port adds no publication of its own."},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"How AREG_IOS runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does, step by step. The legacy module is described on its own page . AREG_IOS registers a follow-up intraoral scan (T2) onto the baseline scan (T1) of the same patient by a rigid ICP on a patch that treatment does not move: the palate on the upper arch, or a band along the mucogingival line on the lower arch. In VISOR the whole computation runs on a server, including the network that finds the palatal patch; Slicer only shows the form, sends the folders and opens what…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#visor-status","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"A client never names AREG_IOS : the Slicer module sends AREG with mode = \"IOS\" , and the server swaps the facade for this engine. The engine owns the pairing, the palatal network, the mucogingival band and the ICP ; tooth labels, orientation and the mucogingival landmarks come from three other tools it calls through the supervisor. Item State API name AREG_IOS ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/AREG_IOS/pyproject.toml:25-27 ). A client sends AREG with mode = \"IOS\" ( VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:186-193 ; the facade is composed in server/registry/facade.py:98 and swapped for its target in…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#files","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78. Every heavy import (torch, monai, pytorch3d) sits inside a function, so the tool can be imported to publish its schema on a machine that lacks them ( ios/net.py:16-19 ). File Lines Role ios/__init__.py 90 run() : the published signature ( ios/__init__.py:23-41 ) ios/layout.py 96 sections, labels, visible_when , hidden model fields ios/dispatch.py 479 main resolves the hosted bundles and checks every rule ( :416-479 , _check_ios :100-139 ); register ( :348-413 ); _run_ios , the pipeline ( :142-254 ); _collect_transforms ( :257-279 ) ios/tools.py 169…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#visor-where","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"3. What runs where, and the pipeline","x":"Step Server Client (Slicer) Form publishes the schema; the fields of the other modes stay hidden by visible_when builds the panel; hosted cohorts IOS_T1 and IOS_T2 are offered ( server/deployment.toml:68 ) Pairing one folder per time point, searched recursively, the jaw read from the name ( ios/pipeline.py:38-116 ) sends the folders Labels and orientation Fully-Automated: sup.run(\"Crown_Seg\") on T1, then T2, then sup.run(\"ASO\") on T1, then T2 ( ios/dispatch.py:172-181 ) nothing Patch palate: network in ios/butterfly.py ; mucogingival: landmarks sent, or sup.run(\"ALI_IOS\") asked for the…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#visor-args","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Types and defaults are those of the published schema (observed: scripts/describe.py ); layout comes from ios/layout.py . Five arguments opt out of the hosted-model convention ( server_selectable ... = \"none\" , VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:47-68 ): left to that convention they would receive the whole models/ folder, sixty checkpoints , instead of one bundle. keep_intermediate and stop_after are injected by the server and hidden ( ios/layout.py:33,40 ). Argument Published type Default Widget and rule t1 , t2 path, required – pickers “T1 (baseline)”, “T2 (follow-up)” ( ios/layout.py:42-43…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#predicted","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where it runs Tooth labels (Crown_Seg) predicted network, see Crown_Seg on VISOR separate tool, Fully-Automated Orientation (ASO, IOS ) computed from tooth centroids onto the reference, see ASO on VISOR separate tool, Fully-Automated Canonical frame for rendering computed four tooth centroids engine Palatal patch predicted 2D UNet on seven renderings, voted back onto the mesh engine, GPU if present Patch cleaning computed islands, dilation, erosion engine Mucogingival landmarks (ALI_IOS) predicted network, see ALI on VISOR separate tool, unless sent Mucogingival band computed…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#environment","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Python requires-python = \">=3.11,<3.12\" ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/AREG_IOS/pyproject.toml:5 ) Dependencies sadt-areg-common , torch==2.11.0 , torchvision==0.26.0 , pytorch3d==0.7.9+pt2110cu128 , monai==1.6.0 , SimpleITK==2.5.6 , numpy==2.3.2 , vtk==9.6.2 ( pyproject.toml:10-23 ), locked in uv.lock . torch and torchvision come from the pytorch-cu128 index, pytorch3d from a prebuilt wheel index ( pyproject.toml:39-54 ) Data the deployment holds DATA/AREG/models/AREG_model (released checkpoint , VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:295-296 ), DATA/AREG/models/IOS_Gold_files…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#visor-changes","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR (6ba2a78) Where it runs a conda environment launched by the AREG widget with python -m server engine in a locked environment, reached through the facade AREG Pairing Sort : replace(\"T1\",\"\") , jaw tested on the whole path, case-sensitive; an unnamed jaw counts as lower ( AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS_utils/dataset.py:89-165,209-282 ) patient stem with the jaw tokens dropped, jaw read on whole tokens of the base name, meshes without a jaw reported ( ios/pipeline.py:38-116 , ios/surfaces.py:113-130 ) Teeth kept out of the band Universal ids range(17, 33) , that is 17 to 32…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#visor-findings","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"The default patch height is 0, which means no band run() publishes mgl_patch_height: float = 0.0 and documents it as “0 uses the engine's default” ( SADT-VISOR ios/__init__.py:36,65-66 ). The code does the opposite: only None is replaced by the 5.0 mm default ( ios/dispatch.py:395-397 ), and a height of 0 builds no curve and no band, only the snapped landmarks , which the ICP then uses alone ( ios/mgl.py:196-211 ; the check says “0 registers on the landmarks alone, without any band”, ios/dispatch.py:119-123 ). Observed: calling run() with the mucogingival patch and no height hands the engine…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOS/AREG_IOS.html#literature","p":"AREG_IOS on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Literature","x":"The method (a palatal patch predicted by a multi-view network, then ICP ) is the one published for the legacy module; the bibliography of the tool is on its sources page and in section 12 of the legacy page . The port adds no publication of its own."},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"How AREG_IOSCBCT runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does, step by step. The legacy module is described on its own page . AREG_IOSCBCT places an intraoral scan in the frame of a CBCT of the same patient, taken at the same visit: a rigid fit on landmarks both sides share, then an ICP of the mesh against a surface contoured from the CBCT. In VISOR the whole computation runs on a server, and the landmarks, when you do not send them, come from other tools the server calls; Slicer only shows the form, sends the folders and…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-status","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"A client sends AREG with mode = \"IOS to CBCT\" , and the server swaps the facade for this engine. The engine has no network of its own and imports neither torch nor pytorch3d ( ioscbct/__init__.py:9-14 ): tooth labels, landmarks and orientation are all asked of other tools, and what is left in the engine is geometry. Item State API name AREG_IOSCBCT . A client sends AREG with mode = \"IOS to CBCT\" ( VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:186-193 ); the name of the mode is the one the Slicer module offered, and it travels on the wire ( server/deployment.toml:187-192 ). The facade is composed in…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#files","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78. File Lines Role ioscbct/__init__.py 100 run() , the published signature, and ACCEPTS , the extensions of each path argument, derived from the pipeline's own tables ( ioscbct/__init__.py:34-60 ) ioscbct/layout.py 86 sections Inputs, Landmarks , Models, Outputs; visible_when per mode ( ioscbct/layout.py:5-15 ) ioscbct/dispatch.py 365 main : mode check, supervised calls, try/finally around the run ( :277-365 ); register , the per-patient, per-mesh loop ( :202-274 ); landmark-file matching ( :87-175 ); the CBCT surface once per patient ( :57-84 )…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-where","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"3. What runs where, and the pipeline","x":"Step Server Client (Slicer) Form publishes the schema; the landmark pickers only appear in Registration mode, the model fields only in the two other modes ( ioscbct/layout.py:13-15 ) builds the panel; hosted cohorts for the four inputs are offered ( VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:85 ) CBCT orientation Fully-Automated: sup.run(\"ASO\", ...) , reference Frankfurt resolved by the tool ( ioscbct/dispatch.py:317-332 , ioscbct/tools.py:159-177 ) nothing Labels and landmarks Semi and Fully: Crown_Seg on the meshes, then ALI_IOS (network Occlusal alone) unless ios_landmarks is sent, then ALI_CBCT…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-args","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Types and defaults are those of the published schema (observed: scripts/describe.py ); layout comes from ioscbct/layout.py . The four model arguments opt out of the hosted-model convention, which would hand them the whole models/ folder; each callee resolves its own weights ( VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:74-85 ). keep_intermediate and stop_after are injected by the server and hidden ( ioscbct/layout.py:25,32 ). Argument Published type Default Widget and rule ios path, required, .stl .vtk – “Intraoral scans”, one folder searched recursively ( ioscbct/layout.py:34 ) cbct path, required…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#predicted","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where it runs CBCT orientation (ASO, through ALI_CBCT) predicted landmarks , computed fit onto the reference, see ASO on VISOR separate tools, Fully-Automated Crown labels (Crown_Seg) predicted network, see Crown_Seg on VISOR separate tool Intraoral landmarks (ALI_IOS) predicted network, see ALI on VISOR separate tool, unless sent CBCT landmarks (ALI_CBCT) predicted network separate tool, unless sent Surface level and contour computed medians of the scan, marching cubes engine Landmark fit computed closed-form rigid fit, trimming engine ICP computed point-to-plane, KD-tree…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#environment","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Python requires-python = \">=3.11,<3.12\" ( SADT-VISOR tools/AREG/AREG_IOSCBCT/pyproject.toml:5 ) Dependencies sadt-areg-common , pyvista>=0.45 , SimpleITK==2.5.6 , numpy==2.3.2 , scipy==1.16.2 , vtk==9.6.2 ( pyproject.toml:16-26 ), locked in uv.lock . pyvista is raised from the 0.44.2 the legacy carried, because 0.44 pins an older vtk ( pyproject.toml:18-21 ). No torch: the comment in ioscbct/__init__.py:12-14 explains that holding both callee stacks would need torch 2.8 and 2.11 in one environment Data the deployment holds the Frankfurt orientation reference under…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-changes","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR (6ba2a78) Where it runs Slicer CLI areg_ioscbct , chained by the AREG widget server engine reached through the facade AREG Inputs four flat folders ( os.listdir ), .vtk and .nii.gz only, one landmark file per jaw ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:1051-1171 ) recursive and sorted, .vtk .stl , every scan format ( ioscbct/pipeline.py:55-87 ) Patient key letters and digits normalised, plus a timepoint [Tt][0-2] ; a T3 file is skipped ( AREG_IOSCBCT.py:1061-1113 ) the digits of all tokens except t0 t1 t2 , leading zeros removed; the timepoint no longer enters the key…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-findings","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"Patients and time points that differ only by letters are merged patient_key keeps the digits and nothing else ( ioscbct/pipeline.py:30-52 ). Observed on 6ba2a78: A12_T1_Upper.vtk , B12_T1_Upper.vtk and P1_visit2_U.vtk all give '12' , while P001_T2_U.vtk and P_0001_T2.nii.gz both give '1' , which is intended. With four meshes P001_T1_{U,L}.vtk and P001_T2_{U,L}.vtk beside three CBCTs P_0001_T1.nii.gz , P_0001_T2.nii.gz and Q001_T1.nii.gz , discover returned a single patient '1' holding the four meshes and the first CBCT in sort order, and an empty unpaired : the other two CBCTs disappeared…"},{"u":"visor/AREG_IOSCBCT/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#literature","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT on VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Literature","x":"No paper covers AREG_IOSCBCT as implemented in either code base; the methodological neighbourhood and the group's related publications are listed on its sources page and in section 8 of the legacy page ."},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"How ASO runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does. The legacy module is described on its own page . VISOR runs the dental tools on a server: Slicer keeps only a panel that sends the data and loads the results (see Architecture ). This page describes the VISOR port of ASO, read in the code of the repositories named below and compared with the legacy Slicer module at 3c67957, which has its own page . \"Run for this page\" marks what was executed while checking it; everything else was read in the code. Abbreviations: ASO/ =…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-status","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"1. Status and position in the chain","x":"implemented and tested ASO is one server tool, ASO , in SADT-VISOR tools/ASO : a single run() ( ASO/__init__.py:24-72 ) replaces the legacy widget and its four command-line modules ( PRE_ASO_CBCT , SEMI_ASO_CBCT , PRE_ASO_IOS , SEMI_ASO_IOS ). It has no facade. The Slicer panel is ASORemote (122 lines), which draws the form from the schema the server publishes and adds only the loading of results (Remote ASORemote/ASORemote.py:80-122 ). The tool has its own environment, Python 3.11 and no torch ( tools/ASO/pyproject.toml:8-21 ). Tests run for this page: 205 passed, 5 skipped ( pytest in…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-files","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78 ( wc -l , run for this page). File Lines Role ASO/__init__.py 153 run() , the published signature; CASE_INPUT = \"input\" ASO/dispatch.py 942 orient() : cross-argument checks, work dir, reference choice, the CBCT and IOS runs, the call to ALI_CBCT, ASO_report.json ASO/geometry.py 391 the coarse alignment (translation, two rotations) and the deterministic triplet search, shared by both modalities ASO/cbct/pipeline.py 384 patient discovery and naming, reference loading, recentering, per-patient orientation and writing ASO/cbct/icp.py 298 outlier pruning…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-modes","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"3. The principle, the modes and the call to ALI_CBCT","x":"The target is not an analytic frame but a reference case : an already-oriented patient's landmarks for a CBCT , two labelled meshes and their landmark files for an intraoral scan. The two published CBCT references carry disjoint landmark sets, \"Frankfurt Horizontal + Midsagittal has Ba/S/N/RPo/LPo/ROr/LOr, Occlusal + Midsagittal has ANS/IF/PNS/UL6O/UR1O/UR6O\" (comment, ASO/dispatch.py:309-312 ), so the landmarks asked for decide the reference: choose_reference keeps the only bundle carrying all of them, and refuses with the list of what each bundle carries if none or several fit ( :305-371…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-cbct","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"4. CBCT, step by step","x":"ASO in CBCT on VISOR. Grey boxes are computed; the violet box is the only network, in another tool; the accent box is what the tool writes. In the legacy Semi mode the recentering did not happen; here it always does. 4.1 Input, DICOM and patients A single file is linked into a folder of its own; a folder is used as is; a .zip has already been extracted by the server ( _as_directory , ASO/dispatch.py:902-926 ). DICOM is detected ( dicom.holds_a_series , ASO/cbct/dicom.py:34-57 ) or forced by the hidden dicom_input ; the series are converted under .aso_work/dicom_nifti , GDCM first and…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-ios","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"5. Intraoral scans, step by step","x":"ASO on intraoral meshes in VISOR. Everything is computed; no box runs a network. The grey box on the left is a refusal, not a step. 5.1 Patients and jaws discover ( ASO/ios/pipeline.py:122-180 ) pairs each mesh with an optional landmark file by patient and jaw, both read from the file name with the shared vocabulary sadt_naming.JAW_TOKENS ( :38 , patient_and_jaw :47-110 ). A jaw token may come first ( Upper_gold.vtk ). A file with no jaw token raises JawError , is skipped and counted in a warning ( :43 , :147-152 ): the legacy defaulted to Lower . Meshes are read once per run through a…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-args","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"6. Arguments and I/O","x":"Signature ASO/__init__.py:24-72 ; 16 published arguments, sup excluded, returns \"path\" , calls [\"ALI_CBCT\"] , case axis input ( ASO/__init__.py:21 ). Run for this page: scripts/describe.py in the ASO environment publishes exactly these 16 arguments. The panel rules below come from ASO/layout.py and the choice counts from ASO/catalogs.py . Every cross-argument rule is checked before any file is read ( ASO/dispatch.py:126-148 ). Argument Type, default In the panel input path, required \"Scan / Landmark Folder\" ( ASO/layout.py:46 ) reference hosted model name hidden ( layout.py:53 ); the server…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-predicted","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"7. What is predicted vs. what is computed","x":"Block Nature Where DICOM conversion, recentering computed GDCM / dicom2nifti, SimpleITK ASO/cbct/dicom.py , ASO/cbct/pipeline.py CBCT landmarks , when not supplied predicted by another tool, ALI_CBCT (DenseNet3D agents) ASO/dispatch.py → ALI on VISOR Reference choice, outlier pruning, triplet search computed set inclusion, distance and angle tables, exhaustive or seeded search ASO/dispatch.py , ASO/cbct/icp.py , ASO/geometry.py Coarse alignment, ICP , composition computed Rodrigues rotations, VTK rigid ICP, SimpleITK composites ASO/geometry.py , ASO/cbct/icp.py , ASO/ios/icp.py Tooth labels…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-environment","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"8. Environment","x":"Item Value Source Python >=3.11,<3.12 , the deployment image's interpreter tools/ASO/pyproject.toml:5-8 Libraries sadt-naming (local path), SimpleITK 2.5.6 , vtk 9.6.2 , numpy 2.3.2 , dicom2nifti 2.6.2 ; uv.lock alongside pyproject.toml:10-21 torch, pytorch3d, conda none pyproject.toml:8-21 Data reference bundles in DATA/ASO/models/ (one folder per bundle); test files in DATA/ASO/testfiles/ ( CBCT_FullyAuto , CBCT_FullyAuto_DCM , CBCT_SemiAuto , CBCT_SemiAuto_DCM , IOS_FullyAuto , IOS_SemiAuto ); the models folder is absent from the copy read here VISOR-serve DATA/ASO ALI weights never…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-changes","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"9. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Legacy lines are at 3c67957. Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR Structure widget plus four command-line modules through slicer.cli.run ( ASO/ASO_Method/CBCT.py:387 , :536-538 ) one run() , no progress XML, no *Error.txt files ( ASO/dispatch.py:15-18 ) PreASO network a PreASOModels checkpoint is still asked for and passed to PRE_ASO_CBCT , which only recentres ( ASO_Method/CBCT.py:76-80 , PRE_ASO_CBCT/PRE_ASO_CBCT.py:97-113 ) argument and model removed; recenter is the same operation ( ASO/cbct/pipeline.py:276-299 ) Recentering in Semi CBCT none: Semi_CBCT.Process launches semi_aso_cbct alone…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-findings","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"10. Findings in VISOR","x":"Pitfall · a misnamed landmark file is silently replaced by a prediction With a supervisor (which the server supplies to a tool that asks for one), a patient with no markups file matched to its scan is sent to prediction, even in Semi-Automated : _needs_prediction returns not patients[key][\"markups\"] ( ASO/dispatch.py:651-661 ), the orphan list is emptied once any patient is predicted ( :684-685 ), and the report says landmark_source is ALI_CBCT or mixed . Run for this page with the helpers of tools/ASO/tests/test_run.py : a scan P1_CBCT.nii.gz with a file P1.mrk.json (a name that does not…"},{"u":"visor/ASO/ASO.html#visor-literature","p":"ASO on VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"11. Literature","x":"The method is the one the legacy page discusses against the papers in its section 10 ; the VISOR port keeps the same geometry (reference case, triplet initialisation, rigid ICP ) and removes only the unused PreASO network. Bibliography: ASO sources ."},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"In VISOR there are two unrelated things called Agent. On the server, Agent is a new tool: it reads the server's own catalogue of tools, asks a language model served by Ollama which tool fits a free-text request and with which arguments, checks the answer against the real schema, and runs the chosen tool only when asked to. In Slicer, AgentRemote is a copy of the legacy module that still runs everything on the workstation and never contacts the server. This page describes both. The legacy module is described on its own page . State at the reference commits: SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78, VISOR-serve…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-status","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Server tool Agent Client module AgentRemote State Implemented, 206 test functions in tests/ (those needing Ollama deselected by default, pyproject.toml addopts ; none run for this page). No run on a deployed server is documented here: Ollama has to be supplied separately. Shipped and built, but a copy of the legacy widget, not a server client. Where SADT-VISOR tools/Agent/src/sadt_agent/ ( __init__, catalog, llm, ranking, routing, prompts, validation, execution, conversation, errors, layout ); served because the folder carries [tool.sadt] and the Dockerfile loops over /tools/*/ ( VISOR-serve…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-files","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Server tool, line counts at 6ba2a78. Legacy was Agent/Agent.py (1375 lines), Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI.py (234), Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI_utils/utils.py (404) and Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml (699 lines, 16 entries). File Lines Role __init__.py 271 run() , the Agent and Ask branches, routing.json and Agent_report.json catalog.py 271 reading the catalogue (file or GET /tools ), checking its shape, name lookup, fillable and missing arguments ranking.py 239 BM25 over each tool's published text, with a vocabulary synonym table llm.py 181 the endpoint, one /api/chat POST, JSON extraction from the answer…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-pipeline","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"3. The server tool's pipeline","x":"The Agent tool, SADT-VISOR tools/Agent/src/sadt_agent/ . The violet boxes are calls to the language model; every answer is checked by code before it is used. Nothing runs unless execute is true and the three checks pass. 3.1 The catalogue, read from GET /tools The legacy agent carried its own hand-written manifest of tools and parameters, which drifted from the tools. The server tool takes the catalogue as an input ( catalog.py:1-23,52-83 ): catalog_file , a JSON file holding what GET /tools returns, if given; otherwise <SADT_API>/tools , read with urllib and no token ( catalog.py:86-100 ).…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-args","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature of the server tool, SADT-VISOR tools/Agent/src/sadt_agent/__init__.py:35-51 . sup is injected by the runner and never sent by a client; output_dir is removed from the published schema and filled by the server with the job's output/ ( VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:36 ). The tool's layout file hides history , endpoint , temperature , seed and timeout_seconds , and shows execute only in Agent mode ( layout.py:19-50 ). Since no client panel calls this tool, that layout is not shown anywhere today. Argument Type Default Panel (layout) Role prompt text, required none \"What…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-predicted","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Catalogue, normalisation, BM25 ranking computed deterministic Agent venv Which tool, and the confidence predicted language model (router call) the Ollama host Name check against the candidates computed Agent venv Argument values predicted language model (extractor call) the Ollama host Type, choice and required checks, execution gate computed Agent venv Ask-mode advice predicted language model , free text the Ollama host The confidence numbers are the model's own self-report; the 0.5 gate is a floor against a model that says it is guessing, not a measure of quality…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-env","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Where Python >=3.11,<3.12 pyproject.toml Dependencies none ( dependencies = [] ); HTTP through urllib pyproject.toml , llm.py:15-18 Language model an Ollama server with qwen3:8b (or the tag given) already pulled; not in the image, not in VISOR-serve llm.py:1-23,33-39 Hosted data none: no DATA/Agent entry is needed – Resources and timeout none declared; 300 s per model call VISOR-serve server/config.py:76-77 ; __init__.py:48 The legacy module downloaded and started an Ollama binary itself and pulled the model on a 404 (legacy Agent/Agent.py:101-207 ; legacy page, the language model…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-changed","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Point Legacy (3c67957) VISOR server tool Catalogue Hand-written manifest, 16 entries ( Agent_CLI/manifest.yaml ) Live registry ( catalog.py ) Ranking Cross-encoder, k=3 ( Agent_CLI/Agent_CLI.py:102,182 ; utils.py:53 ) BM25 ( ranking.py:32-33,214-215 ); a ranking with no signal keeps every tool ( :222-239 ) Ranking failure first three tools in file order ( utils.py:94 ) RankingError , never a silent prefix ( ranking.py:14-19 ) Mode test == \"Agent (Automated)\" , else consultant ( Agent_CLI.py:86 ) mode == MODE_ASK tested first, everything else is agent ( __init__.py:161 ) Folders Joined and…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-findings","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"A server tool with no client, a client with no server tool The server tool is not reachable from any panel, and the client panel never contacts the server. Nothing in AutomatedDentalToolsRemote builds a request for Agent , and AgentRemote.py does not import ServerToolsCoreLib . The two share a name and a model tag, nothing else. Execution is off by default, with three refusals execute defaults to false ( __init__.py:43 ). When it is true, the run is still refused if no tool was chosen, if a required argument is missing, or if the confidence is below 0.5 ( routing.py:104-125 ). The source…"},{"u":"visor/Agent/Agent.html#visor-literature","p":"Agent on VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"9. Literature","x":"The method and the publications are those of the tool: see the Agent sources page ."},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"How AutoCrop3D runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does. The legacy module is described on its own page . In VISOR, AutoCrop3D is a Python package that runs on the server: the Slicer side only draws or picks the box, sends the files and loads what comes back. The crop itself is the legacy arithmetic, kept on purpose; what changed is everything around it, how scans are paired with ROIs, what is checked, what is reported. This page follows the port from the request to the files that come back: position, files, each step…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-status","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"AutoCrop3D is a utility at the head of a chain: it reduces a CBCT , or its segmentation, to the region a clinician drew, before another tool works on it. In VISOR it calls nothing and nothing calls it. The ROI is drawn in Slicer, which is why the client keeps that part. Item Value State Implemented and tested at tool level. pytest in SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D : 103 passed on 2026-11-09 (6ba2a78, clean tree). The 15 client tests of test_autocrop3d_client.py were not run (see the findings). API name AutoCrop3D ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote AutoCrop3DRemote/AutoCrop3DRemote.py:55 ) Server package…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-files","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File (SADT-VISOR tools/AutoCrop3D/ ) Lines Role src/sadt_autocrop3d/__init__.py 368 run() , discovery of scans and ROIs, the per-scan loop, the report and its error messages src/sadt_autocrop3d/pipeline.py 585 patient key, ROI reading, crop bounds, crop, re-padding, output names, the surface (padding, marching cubes , smoothing, patient space, colours) tests/test_run.py 1441 103 tests (passed on 2026-11-09), one per corrected defect among them README.md – provenance ( b1ae78c ), the thirteen corrections and their tests ( :107-125 ), what is not ported ( :159-180 ) pyproject.toml 40 pinned…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-where","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"3. What runs where","x":"Step Server Client (Slicer) Draw the box – The ROI is a Markups ROI node already in the scene, picked from the ROI row ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote ServerToolsCore/ServerToolsCoreLib/formgen.py:354 ); or a .mrk.json file or folder. Send Receives scans and ROI; default upload limit 500 MB ( VISOR-serve server/config.py:240 ). Scans and ROI are two required path arguments, so the tool cannot be split by patient and a folder goes up in one request ( VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:158-190 ). Uploads, or names a hosted test file. Crop Reads with SimpleITK, maps the box to indices…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-pipeline","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"4. The pipeline, step by step","x":"One call of run() crops every scan it finds ( SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D/__init__.py:76-167 ). The server pipeline, from __init__.py:76-262 and pipeline.py:158-344 . No step is a network; the accent marks the crop itself and what is written. 4.1 Finding the scans and the ROIs computed _discover_scans takes a file, or walks a folder recursively, keeps .nii.gz .nrrd.gz .gipl.gz .nii .nrrd .gipl ( pipeline.py:26 ), skips hidden files, and sets aside files that look like this run's earlier output ( pairing.is_previous_output , a whole trailing token, so Cropped_01 is not excluded); those are used…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-args","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"5. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature at SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D/__init__.py:32-47 ; everything after output_dir is keyword-only. Panel sections come from the server's naming conventions, there is no layout.py for this tool, and no argument is hidden ( VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:41-56,90-108 ). Argument Type Default Panel Role scans path required Inputs A volume or a folder, searched recursively: .nii.gz .nii .nrrd .gipl.gz .gipl ( pipeline.py:26 ). A .nrrd.gz is found but refused, file by file, because ITK has no reader for a gzipped NRRD ( pipeline.py:37 , __init__.py:210-214 ). Hosted test file…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-predicted","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"6. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where The box drawn by hand in Slicer client Pairing, ROI frame, index bounds, crop, re-padding computed geometry tool Surface computed marching cubes and smoothing tool Nothing in AutoCrop3D is predicted, in VISOR as in the legacy module."},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-env","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"7. Environment","x":"Item Value Reference Python >=3.11,<3.12 SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D/pyproject.toml:5 Libraries SimpleITK==2.5.6 , numpy==2.3.2 , vtk==9.6.2 , sadt-areg-common (local path ../AREG/common ) pyproject.toml:6-21, 37-38 Models none – Hosted test files MG_test_scan.nii.gz (98 611 496 bytes), ROI_box VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:790-806 Deployment section none VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml (grep) Time limit, upload cap none ( TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 0 ), 500 MB server/config.py:77, 240 GPU admission no device argument, so the server assumes the GPU server/execution/dispatch.py:95-116…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-changes","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"8. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Each row gives both sides. Legacy lines are at 3c67957, VISOR lines at 6ba2a78. The legacy module is on its own page ( how the box is applied , the surface ). Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR (6ba2a78) Patient key Scans: fifteen chained .split() ( AutoCrop3D_CLI.py:52 ). ROI: split('_')[0] ( FilesType.py:77 ). Two rules. One function for both sides ( pipeline.py:60-113 ). Patient_01_Scan and Patient_01_ROI both give Patient_01 (run on 2026-11-09). Two ROIs, one patient The last one silently wins ( FilesType.py:78 ). ValueError naming the files ( __init__.py:345-367 ). A folder holding one ROI…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-findings","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"9. Findings in VISOR","x":"Server paths in the report When a surface is written, the report names it with an absolute server path: entry[\"surface\"] = str(surface_path) and the same string appended to produced ( SADT-VISOR AutoCrop3D/__init__.py:253-255 ), while the comment just above says the report must stay relative to output_dir because it travels to the client ( :231-232 ). The volume entry is relative ( :236 ). The only test of the rule checks produced[0] ( tests/test_run.py:1424-1441 ), which is the volume. Reproduced on 2026-11-09 by calling run() on a synthetic label map named P1_Seg.nii.gz : produced held…"},{"u":"visor/AutoCrop3D/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-literature","p":"AutoCrop3D on VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"10. Literature","x":"AutoCrop3D is geometry with no method of its own to cite; the tool's bibliography is on AutoCrop3D sources . For the VISOR mechanics shared by every tool, see Architecture and A run, step by step ; for using the panel, the VISOR guide ."},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"How AutoMatrix runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does. The legacy module is described on its own page . In VISOR, AutoMatrix is a Python package that runs on the server: Slicer sends the files and the transforms, the server pairs them by patient, resamples the volumes, moves the landmark points and sends the results back. The transform convention is the legacy one (the volume is resampled through the transform, points move through its inverse); what changed is the pairing rule, the output grid when no reference is…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-status","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"AutoMatrix is the only tool of this group that another VISOR tool calls: VFACE drives it through the supervisor, once to mirror the scans and once to move landmarks by each patient's registration transform. It calls nothing itself. Its inputs are typically the outputs of a registration (AREG, GreedyReg, FlexReg) or the hosted mirror matrix. Item Value State Implemented and tested at tool level. pytest in SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix : 234 passed on 2026-11-09 (6ba2a78, clean tree). The 25 client tests of test_automatrix_client.py are registered with ctest ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-files","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File (SADT-VISOR tools/AutoMatrix/ ) Lines Role src/sadt_automatrix/__init__.py 358 run() , discovery, the legacy-name fallback pairing, the per-file loop, output names, the report src/sadt_automatrix/pipeline.py 296 patient key, formats, landmarks through the inverse, resampling, transform reading with the 4×4 text fallback, legacy cut lists, scan or segmentation detection src/sadt_automatrix/layout.py 59 sections and labels; hides reference and content src/sadt_automatrix/progress.py 103 \"patient i of n\" events, a copy of the shared progress writer tests/ ( conftest.py and six test files)…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-where","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"3. What runs where","x":"Step Server Client (Slicer) Choose files and transforms Receives them. Each argument is staged in its own sub-folder so the argument name cannot leak into the patient key ( VISOR-serve server/main.py:1146-1166 ). Pickers take a file or a folder. Hosted test files can be chosen in the dropdowns ( AutoMatrixRelease1 to 4 , Mirror ). Pair, read, resample, move points All of it: discovery and pairing, transform reading with the 4x4 text fallback, ResampleImageFilter , landmark points through the inverse, scan or segmentation detection, report ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pipeline.py:71-180,264-296…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-pipeline","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"4. The pipeline, step by step","x":"One call of run() applies every patient's transforms to every file of that patient ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/__init__.py:68-185 ). The server pipeline, from __init__.py:68-358 and pipeline.py . No step is a network; the accent marks the two ways a file is moved and the report. 4.1 Discovery and the patient key computed _walk takes a file as itself or walks a folder recursively, skips hidden files, and keys every file with the same function ( __init__.py:267-298 ). Accepted: images .nii.gz .nrrd.gz .gipl.gz .nii .nrrd .gipl , landmarks .mrk.json , transforms .tfm .mat .h5 .hdf5 .txt…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-args","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"5. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature at SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/__init__.py:30-39 ; unlike AutoCrop3D, no argument is keyword-only. Sections, labels and the two hidden arguments come from SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/layout.py . A hidden argument is neither shown nor sent: the server applies its default ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote ServerToolsCore/ServerToolsCoreLib/formgen.py:2272-2277,2813-2820 ). Argument Type Default Panel Role files path required Inputs · \"Files to transform\" ( layout.py:8 ) A scan, a segmentation, a landmark .mrk.json , or a folder searched recursively. transforms path required Inputs · \"Transforms\"…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-predicted","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"6. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Pairing computed file-name rules tool Scan or segmentation computed three value tests and a count of distinct values tool Resampling, landmark points computed SimpleITK tool The transforms themselves computed upstream, by a registration (AREG, GreedyReg, FlexReg), or the fixed mirror matrix before the request Nothing in AutoMatrix is predicted."},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-env","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"7. Environment","x":"Item Value Reference Python >=3.11,<3.12 SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/pyproject.toml:5 Libraries SimpleITK==2.5.6 , numpy==2.3.2 , sadt-areg-common pyproject.toml:6-17 Models none (the tool's README speaks of 1.4 GB of models, see the findings) – Hosted test files AutoMatrixRelease1 to 4 (247 961 348, 247 962 104, 254 256 972 and 787 127 578 bytes), Mirror (468 bytes) VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:731-751 Deployment section [tools.AutoMatrix] : server_selectable only VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:224-230 Time limit, upload cap none ( TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 0 ), 500 MB, no split of a…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-changes","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"8. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Legacy lines are at 3c67957 ( Automatrix_CLI.py ), VISOR lines at 6ba2a78. The legacy module is on its own page ( the transformation convention , resampling and reference , matching ). Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR (6ba2a78) Patient key, files Sixteen chained .split , a .split('.') , and a _T0 to _T49 loop ( :77-79 ). pairing.patient_stem(…, also_drop=PATIENT_TOKENS_TO_DROP) , both sides ( pipeline.py:27-42 ). Patient key, transforms Another token list ( :130-133 ). Same function; C_0001_CB_Reg_transform.tfm gives C_0001 (run on 2026-11-09). Second chance None. Substring cuts like the…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-findings","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"9. Findings in VISOR","x":"The status line always says \"0 file(s) written\" AutoMatrixRemote._summarize reads report.get(\"written\") and report.get(\"skipped\") ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote AutoMatrixRemote/AutoMatrixRemote.py:227-235 ). The report written by the tool has neither key: it carries summary.files_written and the lists without_a_transform and transforms_without_a_file ( SADT-VISOR AutoMatrix/__init__.py:105-106,158-161 ). Run on 2026-11-09: the report of a run that wrote one file held summary.files_written = 1 and no written key, so, reading the code of _summarize , the message is AutoMatrix: 0 file(s)…"},{"u":"visor/AutoMatrix/AutoMatrix.html#visor-literature","p":"AutoMatrix on VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"10. Literature","x":"AutoMatrix applies transforms and has no method of its own to cite; the tool's bibliography is on AutoMatrix sources . For the VISOR mechanics shared by every tool, see Architecture and A run, step by step ; for using the panel, the VISOR guide ."},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"How BatchDentalSeg runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does, step by step. The legacy module is described on its own page . VISOR runs BatchDentalSeg on a server: Slicer sends the scans and the name of a hosted model, and receives label volumes, per-label files or meshes. One nnU-Net v2 network, chosen among four bundles, labels every structure of a scan in a single pass, and one call covers the whole batch. This page says what runs where, follows the pipeline from the input file to the run report, lists the arguments…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-status","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Batch_Dental_Seg is a leaf: it calls no other tool, and one tool calls it. Its run() takes no supervisor argument at all ( SADT-VISOR __init__.py:13-26 ), so it cannot reach another tool even by mistake. Item Value Reference State Ported and implemented as a server tool; a test file exists in SADT-VISOR (55 test functions, read in code, not run here). Not ported on purpose: auto-crop, memory guard, mirroring correction, persistent queue, glTF export. SADT-VISOR pipeline.py:4-24 ; tools/Batch_Dental_Seg/tests/test_run.py API name Batch_Dental_Seg , with underscores; this site and the Slicer…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-files","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File (SADT-VISOR tools/Batch_Dental_Seg/ ) Lines Role src/sadt_batchdentalseg/__init__.py 123 the only public function, run() : signature, defaults and docstring the server publishes as the schema; delegates to pipeline.segment src/sadt_batchdentalseg/pipeline.py 398 the run: discovery, model resolution, conversion, one inference call, writing, report, cleanup src/sadt_batchdentalseg/catalogs.py 163 the four models and their label tables, keyed by bundle folder name src/sadt_batchdentalseg/nnunet_runner.py 241 device fallback, bundle lookup, nnUNetPredictor construction, GPU resampling…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-pipeline","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"3. The pipeline","x":"segment() ( SADT-VISOR pipeline.py:216-398 ) is a straight line. The bundle is checked before any scan is read, every scan is converted into one folder, nnU-Net runs once over that folder, then each scan is written back on its own grid. Only the network step is learned. Purple is the only learned step. One model bundle, one nnU-Net call over the whole batch, then a per-scan loop that puts the labels back on the scan's grid and writes what export_formats asks for. Sources: pipeline.py:216-398 , nnunet_runner.py:192-232 . 3.1 Inputs and discovery discover_scans() ( pipeline.py:87-107 ) takes…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-io","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Argument Type Default In the panel scans path required yes: a file or a folder (walked recursively); hosted test files if the server has any model path (bundle name) required yes: a menu of the bundles the server hosts; the tool accepts four names, DentalSegmentator , PediatricDentalSeg , NasoMaxillaDentSeg , UniversalLab output_dir path required no, filled by the server separate_segments boolean false yes prediction_ID text Seg yes export_formats list of NIFTI, STL, OBJ, VTK, VTK (merged) NIFTI yes, as chips in the \"Export format\" section, at least one device , gpu_resampling…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-predicted","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Discovery, conversion to NIfTI computed SimpleITK read and write pipeline.py:87-149 Preprocessing (transposition, resampling, CT normalisation) computed deterministic, driven by the bundle's plans.json nnU-Net , in the tool's process or its workers Segmentation of every structure predicted one 3D multi-label nnU-Net network per bundle, fold 0 nnunet_runner.py:192-232 Sliding window, gaussian weighting, step 0.5 computed nnU-Net Output resampling and argmax computed torch per class with a running argmax (GPU path) or scipy and nnU-Net's argmax nnunet_runner.py:124-189…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-env","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"6. Environment","x":"The tool runs in its own virtual environment, built from pyproject.toml and uv.lock ; nothing is installed in Slicer. The pins follow the server image rather than upstream's declared versions ( pyproject.toml:5-13 ). Package Pinned Locked Role Python >=3.11,<3.12 – the image's interpreter ( pyproject.toml:5-7 ) torch 2.8.0 2.8.0+cu128 , from the PyTorch cu128 index inference and GPU resampling nnunetv2 2.8.1 2.8.1 predictor, plans, resamplers SimpleITK 2.5.6 2.5.6 reading, conversion, geometry, writing numpy 2.3.2 2.3.2 labels, per-label split vtk 9.6.2 9.6.2 surfaces ( pyproject.toml:9-13 )…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-changes","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR Where and how it runs Inside Slicer through a Qt process, one scan at a time ( SegmentationWidget.py:96-123 ) On the server, one nnU-Net call for the whole batch ( pipeline.py:256-309 ) Inference engine nnUNetv2_predict command line through the NNUNet extension, three nnU-Net environment variables pointed at the model ( legacy §4.2 ) nnUNetPredictor Python API, explicit model path, same fold, step and no mirroring ( nnunet_runner.py:76-102, 192-232 ) Inputs .nii .nii.gz .gipl .gipl.gz , top level only, no NRRD ( Queue.py:17, 28-34 ) Adds NRRD; recursive…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-findings","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"VFACE asks for surfaces and gets label volumes VFACE's heat-map path calls this tool with three arguments only, scans , model and prediction_ID=\"Seg\" ( SADT-VISOR tools/VFACE/src/sadt_vface/tools.py:359-364 ), so export_formats keeps its default [\"NIFTI\"] ( SADT-VISOR __init__.py:22-24 ) and no mesh is written ( pipeline.py:342-354 ; mesh_export.py:187-189 ). VFACE then looks for .vtk or .vtp files only ( heatmap.py:29, 194-204 ), finds none, and ends with \"No heat map could be drawn\" ( heatmap.py:246-251 ). Read in code, not executed. The deployment read here publishes no surface_model for…"},{"u":"visor/BatchDentalSeg/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-literature","p":"BatchDentalSeg on VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"9. Literature","x":"The networks are the DentalSegmentator family; the bibliography is the tool's own and is not repeated here: BatchDentalSeg sources , and the literature section of the legacy page . The manifest points at the same release URLs the legacy module downloads from: the three DCBIA-OrthoLab releases ( VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:542-581 ; legacy ADT/ADTLib/model_registry.py:59-64 ) and, for DentalSegmentator, the archive recorded in the legacy Resources/ML/download_info.json ( data-manifest.yml:582-586 ). Whether the files behind those URLs are byte for byte the ones a legacy install…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"In VISOR, CLIC runs entirely on the server: the Slicer panel uploads CBCT scans and gets back one label map per scan plus a JSON report. The network and the slice-by-slice method are the legacy ones; how scans are found, how the checkpoint is chosen, how outputs are named and how a failed scan is handled have changed. The legacy Slicer module is described on its own page . References on this page name a repository, then a path inside it, then a line: SADT-VISOR is the tool catalogue (commit 6ba2a78), VISOR-serve the server (e9a7e5d), Remote the Slicer client (AutomatedDentalToolsRemote…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-status","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"State: ported, implemented in code and covered by tests in the tool (SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/tests/ , not run for this page). Nothing here says a given server is running it. API name CLIC , declared by [tool.sadt] tool = true, name = \"CLIC\" (SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/pyproject.toml:24-26 ). The server discovers every tool folder that carries this table; there is no hard-coded list ( Architecture ). Client panel CLICRemote , a thin panel generated from the server's tool description (Remote CLICRemote/CLICRemote.py:44,53 ), built with the extension (Remote CMakeLists.txt:35 ). Calls / called by…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-files","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"The tool is the 110 computing lines of the legacy runner, rewritten and split; the Qt panel and the conda installer stayed behind (SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/README.md:24-29 ). Line counts are at 6ba2a78. File Lines Role src/sadt_clic/__init__.py 250 run() : scan discovery call, checkpoint resolution, the per-scan loop, output naming, the report src/sadt_clic/pipeline.py 184 the computing part: discover_scans , resolve_device , class_count , build_model , normalise , segment_volume src/sadt_clic/catalogs.py 67 the label table Buccal 1, Bicortical 2, Palatal 3 and its colours…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-pipeline","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"3. The pipeline","x":"The whole of CLIC on the server. One box is a network (violet outline); the clinical decision, buccal, bicortical or palatal, comes out of it alone. Every other box is deterministic code in SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/src/sadt_clic/ . The per-slice row is the legacy runner's loop, unchanged. 3.1 Finding the scans scans is a single file or a folder. A folder is walked recursively with os.walk , hidden files are skipped, and only names ending in .nii or .nii.gz (case-insensitive) are kept, as file paths, sorted ( pipeline.py:58-79 ). The legacy collector also advertised .nrrd , .mha and .mhd , which…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-args","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature of run() : SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/src/sadt_clic/__init__.py:76-88 ; published form checked by running scripts/describe.py on the tool. The server then applies its conventions (VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py ). Argument Published type Default Shown to the user Meaning scans path, required – yes one .nii / .nii.gz , or a folder searched recursively; may be picked from the server's DATA/CLIC/testfiles output_dir path filled by the server no removed from the published schema and set to the job's output/ (VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:36 ) model path \"\" no…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-predicted","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Scan discovery, checkpoint choice, class count computed file system and state-dict shape server, CLIC venv Per-slice min-max normalisation, 3-channel replication computed arithmetic server Resizing and ImageNet standardisation computed deterministic, inside torchvision server Detection, class, mask per slice predicted network : Mask R-CNN ResNet50-FPN, 4 classes server, GPU if visible Score and mask thresholds, painting, NIfTI writing computed arithmetic server Class names and colours computed fixed table in catalogs.py , written to the report server Display of the label…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-env","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Where Python >=3.11,<3.12 , \"the deployment image's interpreter\" pyproject.toml:5-6 torch, torchvision 2.11.0 , 0.26.0 , from the explicit pytorch-cu128 index pyproject.toml:13-14,43-51 nibabel, numpy 5.3.2 , 2.3.2 pyproject.toml:18-19 Lock file uv.lock , 529 lines tools/CLIC/uv.lock Weights expected DATA/CLIC/models/final_model.pth , 176 269 352 bytes, staged by scripts/setup-models.sh --tool CLIC VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:630-634 ; README :112-114 Resources and timeout width_from = false ; no timeout (server default 0) VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:254-255…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-changes","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"The network, the per-slice normalisation, the 3-channel replication, the slice axis and the thresholds are the same. The rest differs. Legacy lines are at 3c67957 ( CLIC/runner/clic_runner.py , 101 lines; CLIC/CLIC.py , 453 lines). Point Legacy VISOR Where it runs runner launched by the widget in the clic_env conda environment, one process per scan one run() on the server in the tool's venv, all scans in one process ( __init__.py:167-186 ) Scan discovery accepts .nrrd/.mha/.mhd ; returns sub-folders when any holds a scan, then nib.load fails (legacy page, §3.1 ) NIfTI only, recursive, files…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-findings","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"The backbone download is still reachable The tool builds the network with maskrcnn_resnet50_fpn(weights=None) (SADT-VISOR tools/CLIC/src/sadt_clic/pipeline.py:136 ), as legacy did. In the pinned torchvision 0.26.0 ( pyproject.toml:14 ), weights_backbone defaults to ResNet50_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1 ( torchvision/models/detection/mask_rcnn.py:416 ) and resnet50(weights=weights_backbone) is called ( :490,502 ), which fetches the file through load_state_dict_from_url ( torchvision/models/_api.py:91 ); the checkpoint is loaded over it afterwards ( pipeline.py:142 ). The test that forbids network…"},{"u":"visor/CLIC/CLIC.html#visor-literature","p":"CLIC on VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Literature","x":"The method and its publications are those of the tool, whatever runs it: see the CLIC sources page . VISOR adds no new model."},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"In VISOR, CNE runs on the server: the Slicer panel uploads the clinical notes, the server reads them and runs the language model, and the extractions come back as files. The clinical text therefore leaves the workstation, which the legacy module never did. The prompts and context windows are the legacy ones; reading the notes, parsing the answer and naming the outputs have changed. The legacy Slicer module is described on its own page . References on this page name a repository, then a path inside it, then a line: SADT-VISOR is the tool catalogue (commit 6ba2a78), VISOR-serve the server…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-status","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"State: ported, implemented in code, with tests in the tool (SADT-VISOR tools/CNE/tests/test_run.py , 130 test functions, not run for this page). The Dashboard is not ported. Only the TMJ model is staged on the server's data manifest. API name CNE (SADT-VISOR tools/CNE/pyproject.toml:35-37 ), code in __init__.py , extraction.py , readers.py , dependencies.py , layout.py . Client panel CNERemote , a thin panel (Remote CNERemote/CNERemote.py:57,61-62 ; built, CMakeLists.txt:36 ). It reads CNE_report.json after a run and shows a one-line summary in the status bar ( :75-103 ). Calls / called by…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-files","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at 6ba2a78. The legacy module was CNE/CNE.py (678 lines, the panel, the pip installs and the model download) and CNE_CLI/CNE_CLI.py (279 lines, the inference), plus the Dashboard; only the inference was ported. File Lines Role src/sadt_cne/__init__.py 324 run() : folder checks, output planning, warnings, the per-note loop, the report src/sadt_cne/extraction.py 349 the model: prompts and windows ( :38-51 ), resolve_model_file , load_model , complete , parse_extraction , render_extraction src/sadt_cne/readers.py 264 note discovery and the three readers, character normalisation…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-pipeline","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"3. The pipeline","x":"CNE on the server, SADT-VISOR tools/CNE/src/sadt_cne/ . One box is a model that predicts (violet outline): the quantised language model. Everything around it decides what the model is allowed to see and which of its answers are written. 3.1 Checking the folders and finding the notes A missing notes path is a FileNotFoundError ( __init__.py:97-100 ). If notes is a folder and output_dir is the same folder (symlinks resolved), the run is refused, because the extractions would be read back as notes by the next run ( :106-110,320-324 ). The tool then creates output_dir itself ( :116 ); legacy…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-args","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature: SADT-VISOR tools/CNE/src/sadt_cne/__init__.py:46-56 ; published form checked by running scripts/describe.py on the tool. Sections and labels come from layout.py:12-24 . Argument Published type Default Shown to the user Meaning notes path, required – yes, \"Clinical notes\" one note or a folder, searched recursively, extension matched without case ( readers.py:92-123 ) notes_type \" TMJ \" or \"Ortho\", required – yes, \"Note type\" chooses the system prompt and the context window together ( extraction.py:49-51 ) model path, required – yes, \"Model\" (a list of hosted names, no \"automatic\"…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-predicted","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Discovery, reading, character normalisation, empty-text refusal computed deterministic server, CNE venv Prompt and window from notes_type computed fixed table server The extraction itself (which fields, which values) predicted language model : Qwen 2.5 7B TMJ fine-tune, q4_k_m GGUF (the hosted one) server, CPU build of llama.cpp Truncation check, JSON or key-value parsing, file writing computed deterministic server At temperature 0 with a fixed seed the same note should give the same answer; nothing checks the answer against the note."},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-env","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Where Python >=3.11,<3.12 pyproject.toml:5-6 llama-cpp-python 0.3.35 from the explicit CPU wheel index abetlen.github.io/llama-cpp-python/whl/cpu pyproject.toml:13,60-67 CUDA runtime wheels nvidia-cuda-runtime-cu12==12.8.90 , nvidia-cublas-cu12==12.8.4.1 , preloaded for a CUDA build that is not the one pinned pyproject.toml:24-25 Readers pymupdf==1.28.2 , python-docx==1.2.0 pyproject.toml:29-30 Model expected DATA/CNE/models/<bundle>/ , one .gguf per folder; the manifest stages TMJ/qwen-ft-q4_k_m.gguf VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:687-692 Resources and timeout batch =…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-changes","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"The system prompt of TMJ mode, the absence of one in Ortho mode, and the two default windows are unchanged. Legacy lines are CNE_CLI/CNE_CLI.py at 3c67957. Point Legacy VISOR Where it runs Slicer CLI module on the workstation, engine in Slicer's Python the server, tool venv ( section 1.1 ) Finding the notes glob(folder/*ext) , flat, case-sensitive ( :134-135 ) recursive, case-insensitive, skips ~$ , dotfiles and __MACOSX ( readers.py:92-123 ) Nothing found warning, exit status 0 ( :137-141 ) 422 with the supported extensions ( __init__.py:119-126 ) Output folder must exist, created by the…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-findings","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"The text of the notes is sent to the server The legacy statement that CNE keeps clinical text on the machine (\"no network exfiltration\", legacy page §11 , and the legacy Guide's \"Nothing leaves your machine\" ) does not hold for VISOR . The VISOR panel uploads the notes you select to a tool server where the model runs (Remote README.md:24-35 ; CNERemote/CNERemote.py:57 and the server-side notes argument, Arguments and schema ). The Remote README says in so many words that there is no de-identification in the current client ( README.md:32 ), and the VISOR security page lists the…"},{"u":"visor/CNE/CNE.html#visor-literature","p":"CNE on VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"9. Literature","x":"The fine-tuned models and their publications are the tool's: see the CNE sources page . VISOR adds no new model."},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"Crown_Seg writes the dental number of every tooth of an intraoral surface scan into a per-vertex array, so that the landmark and registration tools that follow can find the teeth. It has no page of its own in the legacy atlas because the legacy repository has no such tool: the work was done by an external Slicer command-line module, called from four modules. VISOR makes it a tool of its own, called by three other tools. No legacy module The legacy repository (SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957) contains no Crown_Seg module and no file with “crown” or “dentalmodelseg” in its path ( git…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#status","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"1. Status and where it lives","x":"Item State API name Crown_Seg , declared by [tool.sadt] tool = true, name = \"Crown_Seg\" ( SADT-VISOR tools/Crown_Seg/pyproject.toml:33-35 ). Environment Python 3.11, its own environment: torch==2.11.0 , vtk==9.6.2 , numpy==2.3.2 ( pyproject.toml:7,16-18 ). The segmentation engine is an optional extra, segmentation : shapeaxi>=2.0.2 , ocnn==2.2.1 , SimpleITK , pytorch3d==0.7.9+pt2110cu128 , torchvision==0.26.0 ( pyproject.toml:37-48 ), so that the package can be imported, and its schema published, without the GPU stack. Engine Not ported: shapeaxi.dental_model_seg.main is called directly with…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#callers","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"2. Who calls it","x":"The three callers of Crown_Seg ( VISOR-serve docs/TOOL_GRAPH.md:11-18 and the calls list of ALI_IOS, observed). ASO refuses a mesh without labels instead of segmenting it ( SADT-VISOR tools/ASO/src/sadt_aso/dispatch.py:882-895 ). Caller When Call ALI_IOS any mesh of the batch with no label array, before the landmarks sup.run(\"Crown_Seg\", meshes=<staged folder>, model=<the models folder it was handed>, device=<the deployment's device>) , then reads run_report.json and renames each result to the input name; a mesh that fails is dropped alone ( ali_ios/dispatch.py:58,193-300 ) AREG_IOS…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#files","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"3. Files involved","x":"Line counts at 6ba2a78. There is no legacy file to compare with: the legacy work lived in an external extension (see Legacy counterpart ). File Lines Role cs/__init__.py 113 run() : argument defaults, _own_models , the array name from the numbering, the call to segment_crowns ( cs/__init__.py:18-29,30-113 ) cs/pipeline.py 613 everything else: constants, the shapeaxi import and its repair, device, checkpoint search, discovery, the two branches, the call to shapeaxi, the report cs/layout.py 36 hides model and array_name , labels numbering \"Tooth numbering\" cs/errors.py 20 ToolInputError (422)…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#pipeline","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"4. The pipeline","x":"Crown_Seg on the server. The first two rows are the tool's own code ( cs/pipeline.py:384-551 ); the third is shapeaxi's dental_model_seg.main , unchanged, read in an installed copy of shapeaxi 2.0.2, the version the lock file resolves. One box is a network (violet outline). A mesh that already carries labels takes the left branch and never reaches the network. 4.1 What the tool does around the engine Checkpoint first : find_checkpoint accepts a .pth file, or a folder searched recursively for a file whose name carries val-loss ; none or two is a ToolInputError naming them…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#args","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"5. Arguments and I/O","x":"Types and defaults are those of the published schema (observed: scripts/describe.py run on the tool with the interpreter of AREG_IOS, which has vtk; the tool's own environment is absent here). The schema declares no extensions for meshes . Argument Published type Default Widget and rule meshes path, required – a file or a folder; .vtk and .stl only, folders searched recursively ( cs/pipeline.py:51,292-320 ) output_dir path, required – not shown; filled by the server model path empty hidden ( cs/layout.py:5-15 ). Empty means DATA/Crown_Seg/models ( cs/__init__.py:18-29,104 ) array_name str…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#predicted","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"6. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Checkpoint search, discovery, sorting on array names, copy branch computed file system and VTK server, Crown_Seg venv Label per face, 34 classes predicted network : shapeaxi DentalModelSeg , checkpoint 07-21-22_val-loss0.169.pth server, GPU when visible Faces to points, island removal, closing computed inside shapeaxi server Universal to FDI computed look-up table server Report, statuses, output paths computed server Every tooth number comes from the network; nothing checks it against the anatomy afterwards. The README's reproducibility note ( Findings ) concerns the…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#legacy","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"7. Legacy counterpart","x":"There is no legacy Crown_Seg to compare line by line. Four places of the legacy repository at 3c67957 do the work, and the table says which part of Crown_Seg each one corresponds to. ADTLib has no part in it: the shared layer does not mention the segmentation, except for one repair of the installed library ( ADT/ADTLib/env/install_pytorch.py:404-444 ). Legacy piece (3c67957) What it does VISOR counterpart The CrownSegmentationcli module of the extension SlicerDentalModelSeg, not in the repository; ALI, ASO, AREG and FlexReg call it ( ALI/ALI.py:1188 , ASO/ASO.py:1472…"},{"u":"visor/Crown_Seg/Crown_Seg.html#findings","p":"Crown_Seg: tooth numbering, VISOR only","t":"Crown_Seg (VISOR)","c":"","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"FDI numbering over a mesh that is already labelled With numbering = \"FDI\" and skip_segmented on, a mesh that already carries a label array is copied unchanged ( cs/pipeline.py:480-487 ; _write_as_vtk converts the file format, not the labels), while the report states array_name = \"FDI_ID\" and numbering = \"FDI\" ( cs/pipeline.py:556-561 ). The copy keeps its Universal integers under their Universal name, so the report announces a numbering the file does not hold. Observed: a synthetic mesh carrying a Universal_ID array was passed through segment_crowns with fdi=True and array_name=\"FDI_ID\"…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"In VISOR, DOCShapeAXI runs on the server: the Slicer panel uploads .vtk surfaces and a checkpoint name, and gets back a JSON report of the predictions and, optionally, the surfaces carrying the Grad-CAM maps. The networks and the Grad-CAM method are the legacy ones; the checkpoint now decides anatomy, task and class count by itself, there is no CSV any more, and explainability can be turned off. The legacy Slicer module is described on its own page . References on this page name a repository, then a path inside it, then a line: SADT-VISOR is the tool catalogue (commit 6ba2a78), VISOR-serve…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-status","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"State: ported, implemented in code, with tests in the tool (SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/tests/test_run.py , 91 test functions, not run for this page). A server set up only with the VISOR-serve staging scripts cannot run it : the EfficientNet backbone it requires is not staged by any of them (see Findings in VISOR ). API name DOCShapeAXI (SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/pyproject.toml:57-59 ); modules __init__.py , catalog.py , engine.py , pipeline.py . Client panel DOCShapeAXIRemote , a thin panel (Remote DOCShapeAXIRemote/DOCShapeAXIRemote.py:63,67,69 ; built, CMakeLists.txt:34 ). It loads…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-files","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at 6ba2a78. Legacy was DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.py (830 lines, panel, conda and WSL handling) and DOCShapeAXI_CLI/DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py (310 lines, the computation); only the computation was ported. File Lines Role src/sadt_docshapeaxi/__init__.py 133 run() : catalogue lookup, discovery, the two passes, renaming, the report src/sadt_docshapeaxi/catalog.py 88 the five checkpoints and what each implies ( Analysis , ANALYSES , analysis_for ) src/sadt_docshapeaxi/engine.py 129 the dataset, predict and explain src/sadt_docshapeaxi/pipeline.py 199 discovery, device, backbone check, pickle…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-pipeline","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"3. The pipeline","x":"DOCShapeAXI on the server. The network (violet outline) runs in both passes: once without gradients to predict, then once per class through Grad-CAM to explain. Everything else is deterministic code in SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/src/sadt_docshapeaxi/ or in shapeaxi. 3.1 One checkpoint name decides the analysis Legacy asked the caller for data_type , task , model , nn and num_classes as separate positional arguments (legacy DOCShapeAXI_CLI/DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py:299-307 ), so a two-class checkpoint could be run with a four-class scale. VISOR takes one argument, model , and looks its base name…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-args","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature: SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/src/sadt_docshapeaxi/__init__.py:18-25 ; published form checked by running scripts/describe.py on the tool: six arguments. The tool has no layout.py . Argument Published type Default Shown to the user Meaning surfaces path, required – yes one .vtk , or a folder searched recursively ( pipeline.py:22,40-60 ) model path, required – yes (hosted names) the checkpoint ; its file name decides anatomy, task, class count and network ( catalog.py:50-71 ); an unknown name is a ValueError listing the known ones ( :74-88 ) output_dir path filled by the server no…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-predicted","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Checkpoint to anatomy, task, classes, network computed fixed table server, DOCShapeAXI venv Mesh creation, point sampling, multi-view rendering computed shapeaxi and pytorch3d; point sampling is random, unseeded server, GPU in practice Class or severity score predicted network : SaxiMHAFBClassification or SaxiMHAFBRegression , EfficientNet-B0 views branch plus point branch server Argmax computed server Grad-CAM maps computed from the network's gradients; describes the network, does not predict anything new server Resize, clipping, projection, median filter computed server"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-env","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Where Python >=3.11,<3.12 , \"the only one the pytorch3d wheel is built for there\" pyproject.toml:5-7 torch, torchvision, pytorch3d 2.11.0 , 0.26.0 (cu128 index), 0.7.9+pt2110cu128 (prebuilt wheels index) pyproject.toml:15-17,76-99 shapeaxi 1.1.1 , pinned because all five checkpoints carry 1.x hyperparameters that 2.x's MHAEncoder rejects pyproject.toml:18-30 numpy, captum, others numpy==1.26.4 , captum==0.8.0 , ocnn==2.2.6 , vtk==9.6.2 , pandas==2.3.3 , tqdm==4.67.1 ; no OpenCV pyproject.toml:31-52 Weights expected DATA/DOCShapeAXI/models/<name>.ckpt , five files VISOR-serve…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-changes","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"The network architecture, the point-cloud and multi-view inputs, the Grad-CAM layer and its percentile clipping are the same. Legacy lines are DOCShapeAXI_CLI/DOCShapeAXI_CLI.py at 3c67957. Point Legacy VISOR What the caller says eight positional arguments, data_type , task , model , nn , num_classes ... ( :299-307 ) one model ; the catalogue gives the rest ( catalog.py:50-71 ) List of surfaces os.listdir , CSV opened with mode 'a' in the output folder ( :111-118 ) recursive walk, in-memory table ( pipeline.py:40-60 , engine.py:11-34 ) Checkpoint downloaded into the output folder…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-findings","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"The EfficientNet backbone is a prerequisite nobody stages shapeaxi builds an EfficientNet-B0 around every checkpoint . The tool refuses to let it be downloaded in the middle of a request, and instead raises FileNotFoundError unless efficientnet-b0-355c32eb.pth is already under <torch hub dir>/checkpoints/ (SADT-VISOR tools/DOCShapeAXI/src/sadt_docshapeaxi/pipeline.py:80-105 , called from load_network at :146 ; README.md:112-121 ). Neither the data manifest nor any script of VISOR-serve mentions this file (searched for \"efficientnet\" in the three repositories: only the tool's own README.md…"},{"u":"visor/DOCShapeAXI/DOCShapeAXI.html#visor-literature","p":"DOCShapeAXI on VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Literature","x":"The networks and their publications are the tool's: see the DOCShapeAXI sources page . VISOR adds no new model."},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"How FlexReg runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does. The legacy module is described on its own page . VISOR splits FlexReg in two. The server builds the palatal patch and registers one arch onto another; the Slicer panel only previews the outline of the patch while you move its corners. This page follows the port from the request to the files that come back: who calls it, which files carry it, each step of the server pipeline with the code that does it, the published arguments, the environment, what changed from the…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-status","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"FlexReg calls no other tool and no tool calls it. run() takes no supervisor argument ( SADT-VISOR __init__.py:49-78 ), and the server hands a supervisor only to a tool that declares *, sup ( VISOR-serve server/execution/runner.py:6-9 ). The consequence is the main difference with the legacy chain: the legacy widget ran dentalmodelseg when the arch had no tooth labels, and ALI_IOS for the lower arch. In VISOR the arch must arrive already labelled (a per-point Universal_ID array), for example from a Crown_Seg run, and nothing computes the mucogingival patch any more. Item Value Reference State…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-files","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"The tool is a plain Python package; the geometric core ( make_butterfly.py , orientation.py , propagation.py , ICP.py , vtkSegTeeth.py , util.py ) is the legacy FlexReg_Method/ with renamed variables and a different logger, and the new code is the entry point and its pipeline. Line counts at 6ba2a78: File (SADT-VISOR tools/FlexReg/ ) Lines Role src/sadt_flexreg/__init__.py 202 run() : signature the server publishes, loop over the surfaces, report src/sadt_flexreg/pipeline.py 246 new: read and write surfaces (LPS/RAS flip), walk a folder, build and merge the patch, register, write the .tfm…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-where","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"3. What runs where","x":"Server Client (Slicer) Reading the surfaces (RAS), building the palatal patch (orientation, four tooth centroids, outline, geodesic fill), merging patch arrays, rigid ICP on the patch, writing the registered surface, the .tfm and the report ( SADT-VISOR __init__.py:49-202 ) The patch preview : while a joystick is dragged, the outline of the patch is recomputed in Slicer and drawn in the scene, with no round trip ( FlexRegRemote.py:156-193 ) The only GPU step: the geodesic fill of the patch ( propagation.py:31, 60, 66-68 ) The form, generated from the server's schema: vec2 arguments become…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-pipeline","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The pipeline, step by step","x":"One call of run() handles one surface or a folder of them. The steps below are those of SADT-VISOR __init__.py:108-202 , in order. The server pipeline for one surface, from __init__.py:108-202 . No step is a network (no purple box); the accent marks the GPU step and what is written. The reference enters only the registration, and it is never patched in this run. 4.1 Checks before reading computed The mode decides two flags: registering for Register and Patch and register , and the array to register on, Butterfly for the palate or Bottom_MGL for the mucogingival line ( __init__.py:111-112…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-io","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"5. Arguments and schema","x":"Argument Type Default Widget, section, visibility surfaces path required \"Arches\", Inputs: a labelled .vtk , .vtp or .stl , or a folder; hosted test files offered ( VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml:232-237 ) mode choice: Patch , Register , Patch and register Patch and register \"What to do\", Inputs patch choice: Palate (butterfly) , Mucogingival line Palate (butterfly) \"Register on\", Inputs reference path empty; required to register \"Register onto\", Inputs, visible only when mode registers; hosted test files offered; scene models offered ( FlexRegRemote.py:80 ) anterior_right…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-predicted","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"6. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Tooth labels ( Universal_ID ) predicted upstream, by whatever labelled the arch; not run by FlexReg before the request Canonical orientation, centroids, corners, outline computed geometric tool (and the preview, in the client) Patch fill computed geodesic flood fill tool, CUDA required Merge of patch arrays computed logical OR tool, numpy Registration computed rigid ICP (VTK), 1000 iterations tool, CPU Mucogingival patch not computed anywhere in VISOR – FlexReg on VISOR runs no network at all: the legacy module's two predicted inputs, the crown segmentation and the ALI_IOS…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-env","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Environment","x":"Item Value Reference Python >=3.11,<3.13 SADT-VISOR tools/FlexReg/pyproject.toml:8 Pinned libraries numpy==1.26.4 , vtk==9.3.1 , SimpleITK==2.4.0 , torch==2.8.0 pyproject.toml:13-18 torch build CUDA 12.8 wheels from an explicit index ( pytorch-cu128 ) pyproject.toml:45-51 Test marker gpu declared, used by no test pyproject.toml:32-34 Models none loaded by the tool; the manifest still lists a Fly-by-CNN checkpoint under FlexReg.models , a leftover of the legacy downloadModel() VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:511-517 Hosted test files Arches (from RegTestFiles.zip , 222 MB) and…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-changes","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"8. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"The legacy module is described on its own page ( algorithmic core , outputs ). Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR Geometry of the patch and the registration FlexReg_CLI/FlexReg_Method/make_butterfly.py , propagation.py , ICP.py Same code. A comparison with diff shows only renamed variables, a different logger, and the change on missing teeth below A required tooth is absent The error is logged and the function returns: the mesh is written with no patch and the run looks successful ( make_butterfly.py:77-79 ) The error is raised, turned into an input error naming the problem, and that surface is…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-findings","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Findings in VISOR","x":"\"Patch and register\" patches only the moving surface, not the reference The patch is built on surfaces only ( SADT-VISOR __init__.py:155-157 ); the reference is read once and used as is ( __init__.py:121 ). The ICP selects the points of the patch array in both meshes ( ICP.py:70-72 ; pipeline.py:209-210 ). A reference without the Butterfly array fails with NoSegmentationSurf ( vtkSegTeeth.py:59-61 ), and an all-zero array with ToothNoExist ( vtkSegTeeth.py:75 ); neither is an input error, which matters for a batch: see the next finding. In practice, the reference must already carry its…"},{"u":"visor/FlexReg/FlexReg.html#visor-literature","p":"FlexReg on VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Literature","x":"The method is the legacy one, so its bibliography is the tool's: FlexReg sources . For the VISOR mechanics shared by every tool, see Architecture and A run, step by step ; for using the panel, the VISOR guide ."},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"How GreedyReg runs in VISOR (client and server), what changed from the legacy Slicer module, and what the code of the port does. The legacy module is described on its own page . In VISOR, GreedyReg is a Python package that runs on the server: Slicer sends the two folders of scans, the server pairs them by patient, runs Greedy twice per pair (an affine search, then a resampling of T2 into T1) and sends the results back. Greedy is no longer a downloaded binary but the picsl_greedy package, and the command it receives is the legacy one, flag for flag. This page describes the port in SADT-VISOR…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-status","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"GreedyReg calls no tool and no tool calls it: there is no sup.run in the package and no other tool of SADT-VISOR names it (grep over tools/*/src ). Its natural upstream is a segmentation that gives the mask of a stable structure, typically the cranial base from AMASSS (the docstring says so, __init__.py:64-67 ), but the clinician brings that mask as a file: the tool does not fetch it. In the legacy module the Distant mode called ALI_CBCT for landmarks ; that path does not exist in VISOR (the calls: [\"ALI_CBCT\"] entry exists only in the client test fixture). Item Value State Implemented at…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-files","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File (SADT-VISOR tools/GreedyReg/ ) Lines Role src/sadt_greedyreg/__init__.py 286 run() , pairing, discovery of masks and transforms, the per-patient loop, the report src/sadt_greedyreg/pipeline.py 165 the Greedy commands, the nudged identity, mask binarisation, the child-process call with its timeout, choice checks src/sadt_greedyreg/layout.py 27 puts masks and initial_transforms in \"Advanced\" src/sadt_greedyreg/progress.py 103 \"patient i of n\" progress events tests/ ( conftest.py and six test files) 2014 pairing, commands, contract with the README, batch, run, pipeline; Greedy stubbed…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-where","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"3. What runs where","x":"Step Server Client (Slicer) Inputs Receives T1 and T2 (two required path arguments, so no batch axis and no split of a cohort, VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:158-190 ), optional masks and initial transforms. Upload limit 500 MB by default ( VISOR-serve server/config.py:240 ). Pickers for the folders; nothing is computed in Slicer. Pair and prepare Pairs by patient ( __init__.py:102 ), binarises the mask above zero and keeps the affine of the mask ( pipeline.py:117-126 ), or writes the nudged identity as the start ( pipeline.py:101-114 ). – Register Two Greedy calls per pair, each…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-pipeline","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"4. The pipeline, step by step","x":"One call of run() registers a whole cohort, patient by patient, in SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:88-190 . The server pipeline, from __init__.py:88-190 and pipeline.py . No step is a network; the accent marks the two Greedy calls, each bounded to 600 s in its own child process. 4.1 Pairing T1 with T2 computed The choices are checked first, so a bad metric is one error, not one per patient ( __init__.py:92 ; pipeline.py:47-55 ). Then pairing.pair(t1, t2, output_suffix) ( __init__.py:102 ). The shared rule walks both folders recursively, accepts .nii.gz .nrrd.gz .gipl.gz .nii .nrrd .gipl…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-args","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"5. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature at SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:38-52 . No argument is in the server's list of technical arguments ( VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:41-56 ), so none is hidden. Every path argument is published as a hosted-test-file picker by convention ( :236-240 ) because deployment.toml has no [tools.GreedyReg] section. Argument Type Default Panel Role t1 path required Inputs Folder of first-timepoint scans: the fixed images. t2 path required Inputs Folder of second-timepoint scans: the moving images that get resampled. output_dir path – not shown Filled by the server. masks path…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-predicted","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"6. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Pairing, masks, initial transforms computed file-name rules tool Affine search computed Greedy, NCC / NMI / SSD optimisation, 6 or 12 dof tool, child process, CPU Resampling computed Greedy -r tool, child process Mask of a stable structure predicted when it comes from a segmentation network such as AMASSS, outside this tool before the request GreedyReg on VISOR runs no network. The legacy Distant mode was its only predicted step (ALI_CBCT landmarks ), and it is not ported."},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-env","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"7. Environment","x":"Item Value Reference Python >=3.11,<3.12 , no torch SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/pyproject.toml:5 Libraries picsl_greedy==1.4.0.1 , nibabel==5.3.2 , numpy==2.3.2 , sadt-areg-common (local path) pyproject.toml:6-24 Models none – Hosted test file CBCT_T1_T2_pair (see the findings) VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:756-781 Deployment section none VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml (grep) Time limit server: none ( TOOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 0 ); tool: 600 s per Greedy call server/config.py:77 ; pipeline.py:136, 145 Upload cap 500 MB, no split of a cohort server/config.py:240…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-changes","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"8. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Legacy lines are at 3c67957, VISOR lines at 6ba2a78. The legacy module is on its own page . Subject Legacy (3c67957) VISOR (6ba2a78) Engine ITK-SNAP greedy binary, downloaded, path passed as an argument ( Logic.py:123-138,179-186 ). picsl_greedy==1.4.0.1 in a child interpreter ( pipeline.py:138-165 ). Registration command GreedyReg_CLI.py:104-125 . Same flags ( pipeline.py:74-94 ); resampling the same ( GreedyReg_CLI.py:136-139 and pipeline.py:97-98 ). Unknown metric Falls through to SSD ( GreedyReg_CLI.py:107-112 ). Refused by name ( pipeline.py:29-34, 58-71 ). Timeout 600 s per call…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-findings","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"9. Findings in VISOR","x":"The Slicer client describes a tool the server does not have AutomatedDentalToolsRemote GreedyRegRemote/GreedyRegRemote.py:11-24 (docstring) and :50-57 (help text) describe a mode argument with Greedy , Landmark and Landmark + Greedy , a landmark_model and a second tool reached through the supervisor. The server tool has none of it: its run() takes t1, t2, output_dir, masks, initial_transforms, metric, transform_type, output_suffix ( SADT-VISOR GreedyReg/__init__.py:38-52 ) and a search for mode or \" landmark \" in the package finds nothing. The comment at GreedyRegRemote.py:76-77 names an…"},{"u":"visor/GreedyReg/GreedyReg.html#visor-literature","p":"GreedyReg on VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"10. Literature","x":"The registration engine is Greedy, as in the legacy module, and the bibliography is the tool's: GreedyReg sources . For the VISOR mechanics shared by every tool, see Architecture and A run, step by step ; for using the panel, the VISOR guide ."},{"u":"visor/MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html","p":"MRI2CBCT: not ported to VISOR","t":"MRI2CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"MRI2CBCT does not run on a VISOR server. This page says exactly what exists of it in the VISOR repositories: no server tool, an older client module kept but not built, and model files listed for a server that has no tool to use them. The module itself is described on its legacy page . State at the reference commits: SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78, VISOR-serve e9a7e5d, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote 19b95e8; legacy is SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957. Everything below was read in the code and the manifests. The MRI2CBCT that the legacy page documents is the rewritten module of 3c67957 ( MRI2CBCT_core/…"},{"u":"visor/MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#visor-status","p":"MRI2CBCT: not ported to VISOR","t":"MRI2CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What exists, layer by layer","x":"Layer Exists? Evidence Server tool No No MRI2CBCT folder under SADT-VISOR tools/ ; PROVENANCE.md:46-47 and tools/VFACE/src/sadt_vface/resample.py:3-5 both say MRI2CBCT \"is not a tool\" there Server section No No MRI2CBCT entry in VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml Model and test files Listed, with no tool to use them VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:704-727 , under the heading \"NOT PORTED YET\": the TMJ crop network ( TMJCrop/checkpoint_final.pth , 1 132 326 645 bytes, with its dataset.json and plans.json ) and TestFile.zip (258 100 460 bytes) Client module Kept in the tree, not built…"},{"u":"visor/MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#visor-where","p":"MRI2CBCT: not ported to VISOR","t":"MRI2CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What the client copy is, and is not","x":"AutomatedDentalToolsRemote keeps MRI2CBCTRemote/MRI2CBCTRemote.py (2397 lines) and MRI2CBCT_CLIRemote/ , six command-line modules: APPROX , ORIENT_CENTER_MRI , RESAMPLE_CBCT_MRI , REG , LR_CROP , TMJ_CROP . This is the earlier module, the one the legacy rewrite replaced (see the legacy page, section 1 ). It is not a server client: it is a local widget that runs its command-line modules through slicer.cli.run and installs torch itself ( pip_install(\"torch==2.2.0 ...\") , MRI2CBCTRemote.py:117 ). Since it is not built, no user of the VISOR extension gets it. A future port would start from the…"},{"u":"visor/MRI2CBCT/MRI2CBCT.html#visor-reuse","p":"MRI2CBCT: not ported to VISOR","t":"MRI2CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What VISOR did reuse","x":"Only the resample step, and as a copy. SADT-VISOR tools/VFACE/src/sadt_vface/resample.py vendors the generic \"put the cohort on one voxel grid and recentre it\" behaviour that the legacy VFACE gets from MRI2CBCT_RESAMPLE_CBCT_MRI , with every MRI-specific branch switched off ( resample.py:1-34 ). SADT-VISOR PROVENANCE.md:53-56 records the cost: if MRI2CBCT is ever ported, the two copies can drift apart without anything failing. See VFACE on VISOR . None of the findings on the legacy page can be checked against VISOR code, since there is none: they all describe the legacy rewrite at 3c67957."},{"u":"visor/MedX/MedX.html","p":"MedX: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedX (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"MedX does not exist in VISOR, and it is no longer in the current legacy extension either. This page says exactly what is left of it in the VISOR repositories. The archived module is described on its legacy page . State at the reference commits: SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78, VISOR-serve e9a7e5d, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote 19b95e8; legacy is SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957. Everything below was read in the repositories; nothing was run."},{"u":"visor/MedX/MedX.html#visor-status","p":"MedX: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedX (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"What exists, layer by layer","x":"Layer Exists? Evidence Legacy module Archived Removed by commit 34d496f (2026-09-17, \"Drop MedX and SurgMovPred, kept on archive branches\"); kept on the branch archive/medx at fbfa0fb , with MedX/MedX.py , MedX/MedX_Method/ and MedX_CLI/ . Absent from 3c67957: no MedX file and no MedX line in CMakeLists.txt Server tool No No MedX folder under SADT-VISOR tools/ ; no section in VISOR-serve server/deployment.toml Model files Listed, with no tool to use them VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:644-651 : BART.zip (1 508 192 833 bytes), under the heading \"NOT PORTED YET\" Client module No No…"},{"u":"visor/MedX/MedX.html#visor-successor","p":"MedX: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedX (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"CNE is the successor in use, not in method","x":"The client README calls CNE the \"Successor of the former MedX module\" (AutomatedDentalToolsRemote README.md:208,601 ). That describes a use, not an equivalent algorithm. MedX ran a fine-tuned BART summarisation model to write one summary per patient, then built a figure panel from the cohort (legacy page, section 4 and section 5 ). The VISOR tool CNE runs a quantised GGUF language model through llama.cpp and writes, per note, the structured findings as JSON and the same findings as key : value text (SADT-VISOR tools/CNE/README.md:1-22 ); its README mentions no dashboard. The models and the…"},{"u":"visor/MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"There is no server-side Medical Data Anonymizer. The VISOR client extension ships an older copy of the legacy module, which runs inside Slicer on text documents, and which does not de-identify the files VISOR uploads. The module itself is described on its legacy page . State at the reference commits: SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78, VISOR-serve e9a7e5d, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote 19b95e8; legacy is SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957. MDA-Remote below stands for AutomatedDentalToolsRemote Medical_Data_Anonymizer_ModuleRemote/Medical_Data_Anonymizer_ModuleRemote.py . Everything was read in the code…"},{"u":"visor/MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#visor-status","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"What exists, layer by layer","x":"Layer Exists? Evidence Server tool No No folder under SADT-VISOR tools/ , no entry in VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml , no section in server/deployment.toml Client module Yes, built, local MDA-Remote (768 lines), added by AutomatedDentalToolsRemote CMakeLists.txt:37 . It does not inherit ServerToolWidgetBase , the base class of the panels that call the server Where it computes In Slicer The client README lists it among the modules that \"run on this machine and send nothing\" ( README.md:28-30 ) What it reads Text documents only DOCX, PDF, TXT, CSV, XML, ODT ( README.md:215 ); no image…"},{"u":"visor/MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#visor-where","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"What runs where","x":"Everything runs in the Slicer process, as in the legacy module: install_dependencies pip-installs presidio-analyzer, presidio-anonymizer, pandas, python-docx, pdfplumber, odfpy, lxml, langdetect and reportlab into Slicer's Python, then downloads the spaCy model en_core_web_lg ( MDA-Remote:392-441 ). Nothing is sent to a server and nothing is hosted."},{"u":"visor/MedicalDataAnonymizer/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html#visor-changed","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: not ported to VISOR","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"How the copy differs from the legacy module","x":"The shipped copy is an older revision of the module, not a port: 768 lines against 774 in the legacy file at 3c67957 ( Medical_Data_Anonymizer_Module/Medical_Data_Anonymizer_Module.py ). Since then the legacy file has switched to ADTLib.logging_setup instead of a local logger, given the widget a separate Logic object, and renamed variables. The copy's module category is \"Automated Dental Tools Remote\" instead of \"Automated Dental Tools\" ( MDA-Remote:41 ). The detection logic is the same in both: the findings of the legacy page about the fixed language, the mapping file and the silent failure…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"In VISOR, SurgMovPred runs on the server under the API name Surg_Mov_Pred : the Slicer panel uploads a table of pre-operative measurements and the surgical plan, and saves back two tables of predicted post-operative movements. The algorithm, 112 stacking regressors applied one target at a time, is the legacy one, unchanged; what changed is the ordering, the error handling and the environment. The legacy Slicer module is described on its own page . References on this page name a repository, then a path inside it, then a line: SADT-VISOR is the tool catalogue (commit 6ba2a78), VISOR-serve the…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-status","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Position in the chain","x":"Item Value API name Surg_Mov_Pred . The Slicer panel is called SurgMovPred. The tool was packaged as Surg_Mov_Pred , and the server accepts the old spelling in lookups (VISOR-serve server/registry/__init__.py:282-293 ). Tool folder SADT-VISOR tools/Surg_Mov_Pred : src/sadt_surgmovpred/{__init__,pipeline,layout}.py . Slicer module SurgMovPredRemote , a thin panel: TOOL_NAME = \"Surg_Mov_Pred\" (Remote SurgMovPredRemote/SurgMovPredRemote.py:33 ), RESULT_KIND = \"save_as\" ( :46 ), built by Remote CMakeLists.txt:45 . Calls, called by None, none. No sup argument ( __init__.py:37-46 ). It is a leaf…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-files","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"Line counts at 6ba2a78. Legacy was SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.py (878 lines, the panel, with a pip install at each Apply) and SurgMovPred_CLI/SurgMovPred_CLI.py (323 lines, the computation). File Lines Role src/sadt_surgmovpred/__init__.py 73 run() : resolves the models folder ( _own_models , :22-34 ) and calls predict src/sadt_surgmovpred/pipeline.py 319 everything else: clean_name , find_id_column , table and package loading, predict_all_targets , save_results , predict src/sadt_surgmovpred/layout.py 26 hides model , labels measurements \"Measurements and plan\" pyproject.toml , uv.lock 66, 306…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-pipeline","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"3. The pipeline","x":"Surg_Mov_Pred on the server, SADT-VISOR tools/Surg_Mov_Pred/src/sadt_surgmovpred/pipeline.py . Each of the 112 packages predicts one target on its own (violet outline); nothing ties the targets together afterwards. 3.1 Loading the model packages model is optional and hidden. When it is empty, run() uses <data_root>/Surg_Mov_Pred/models ( __init__.py:19,22-34,71 ). load_model_packages then takes every stacking_package.pkl below it, in sorted order, and loads each with joblib ( pipeline.py:162-195 ): package_files = sorted(model.glob(\"**/stacking_package.pkl\")) # legacy: list(...), filesystem…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-args","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Argument Type Default Panel measurements one CSV, XLSX or ODS table, or a folder of them (required) – visible, labelled \"Measurements and plan\" ( layout.py:25 ); may be picked from the server's test files model folder of model packages empty: every stacking_package.pkl found under the models folder of the tool's data hidden ( layout.py:14 ), still accepted by the API output_dir folder filled by the server not published (VISOR-serve server/registry/conventions.py:36 ) data_root injected by the runner – – Signature: __init__.py:37-46 . The output is a dictionary with two files…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-predicted","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Block Nature Where Reading, ID detection, column cleaning, feature resolution computed deterministic server, Surg_Mov_Pred venv Standardisation computed each package's own fitted scaler server Each post-operative movement predicted stacking regressor (scikit-learn, with LightGBM sub-estimators), one per target, 112 in the shipped set server, CPU Table assembly and writing computed server"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-env","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Where Python >=3.12,<3.13 (CLIC, CNE and DOCShapeAXI target 3.11) pyproject.toml:5-7 Libraries numpy==2.2.6 , pandas==2.3.3 , scikit-learn==1.7.2 , joblib==1.5.3 , lightgbm==4.7.0 , openpyxl==3.1.5 , odfpy==1.4.1 pyproject.toml:13-25 No GPU stack torch absent, asserted by a test README.md:106-107 Models expected DATA/Surg_Mov_Pred/models/ , from all_models.zip (740 183 656 bytes), extracted to one folder per target VISOR-serve scripts/data-manifest.yml:91-99 Resources and timeout none declared; no timeout; threads capped by the server's admission rule VISOR-serve…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-changes","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Legacy file: SurgMovPred_CLI/SurgMovPred_CLI.py ( cli ) at 3c67957. VISOR file: pipeline.py . The algorithm itself is the same ( README.md , \"Changes from upstream\"). Point Legacy VISOR Where it runs Slicer CLI module, Slicer's Python server, own venv, CPU Input form a folder only a file or a flat folder ( pipeline.py:132-159 ) Column name that cannot be cleaned returns 'f_unnamed' , so two bad columns collide ( cli:63,66 ) raises a ValueError naming the column ( pipeline.py:57 ) Order of the models list(base_path.glob(...)) , filesystem order ( cli:121 ) sorted(model.glob(...)) , same…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-findings","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"Unnamed index column in both tables Both result files start with a column holding 0, 1, 2 and so on, before IDPatient ( pipeline.py:283-284 ). The legacy CLI does the same (legacy SurgMovPred_CLI/SurgMovPred_CLI.py:268-269 ). Account for it when reading column positions. Stale comments in the client The client says output_kind \"is file\" (Remote SurgMovPredRemote/SurgMovPredRemote.py:44 ) and the base widget gives \"SurgMovPred's single predictions_outputs.xlsx\" as its example (Remote ServerToolsCore/ServerToolsCoreLib/base_widget.py:1390-1391 ). The tool returns two files, which the schema…"},{"u":"visor/SurgMovPred/SurgMovPred.html#visor-literature","p":"SurgMovPred on VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Literature","x":"The model and its sources are the tool's: see the SurgMovPred sources page . VISOR adds no new model."},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"An orchestrator on the server: VFACE drives six other VISOR tools to orient, segment, mirror, register and place landmarks on a CBCT cohort, then measures the result and classifies skeletal facial asymmetry with three LightGBM models. The legacy Slicer module is described on its own page . A port on two feature branches The VFACE tool exists only on the branch tool/vface of SADT-VISOR (6ba2a78, 22 commits ahead of main ), and its server section [tools.VFACE] only on the branch conf/vface-deployment of VISOR-serve (e9a7e5d). git ls-tree main tools/ on SADT-VISOR lists no VFACE folder, and the…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-status","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"1. Status and position in the chain","x":"Item Value API name VFACE ( pyproject.toml:30-32 ; signature SV:__init__.py:22-49 ) Tool folder SADT-VISOR tools/VFACE , on branch tool/vface (6ba2a78). Not on main . Server declaration [tools.VFACE] in VS:server/deployment.toml:290-292 , on branch conf/vface-deployment (e9a7e5d). Not on main . Slicer module VFACERemote , a thin panel generated from GET /tools : TOOL_NAME = \"VFACE\" , AUTO_UI = True ( R:VFACERemote/VFACERemote.py:115, 120 ), built by R:CMakeLists.txt:38 . The old local code VFACE_utils/ stays in the tree and is no longer wired ( R:VFACERemote/CMakeLists.txt:5-11 ). Calls Six…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-files","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"2. Files involved","x":"File ( tools/VFACE/ ) Lines Role src/sadt_vface/__init__.py 120 run() , the only public function; imports dispatch lazily so that schema generation needs no pandas, SimpleITK or vtk src/sadt_vface/dispatch.py 642 The chain: validation, bundle resolution, the three modes, the two studies, measurements, classification, heat maps , the report src/sadt_vface/tools.py 364 The seam with the six tools: one function per call, the up-front require , the lookup of AREG's region folder src/sadt_vface/catalogs.py 145 Modes, studies, outputs, the two frames and their landmarks , the three regions with…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-pipeline","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"3. The pipeline","x":"dispatch.py states the chain in its first lines ( SV:dispatch.py:5-6 ): resample -> orient (per frame) -> segment (per frame) -> the second scan -> register (per region) -> landmarks -> measure -> classify The order of dispatch._run ( SV:dispatch.py:415-540 ). The first row prepares the scans, the second turns landmarks into measurements, the third reads them. The heat-map branch starts again from the oriented scans and from AREG's output; it does not need the landmarks. Purple boxes hold a network: inside ASO (through ALI_CBCT), AMASSS, ALI_CBCT and Batch_Dental_Seg, plus the classifier…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-args","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"4. Arguments and schema","x":"Signature: SV:__init__.py:22-49 . Panel layout: SV:layout.py:25-105 . Opt-outs from the hosted-file menu: VS:server/deployment.toml:292 . Argument Type, choices Default Panel t1 path: folder of CBCT , searched recursively (required) – \"CBCT volumes\", section Inputs; folder picker zipped before upload, and the hosted Scans test cohort mode Full pipeline, File already Oriented, File already Registered Full pipeline \"Starting from\" study Asymmetry assessment, Longitudinal study Asymmetry assessment \"Study\" outputs Measurements and classification, Measurements and heat maps , Heat maps…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-predicted","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"5. What is predicted vs computed","x":"Step Nature Where Resample to 0.3 mm computed linear interpolation VFACE Orientation landmarks predicted ALI_CBCT, inside ASO callee Orientation transform computed fit onto the gold case ASO Registration masks predicted one nnU-Net network per structure AMASSS Mirror, and moving landmarks by a matrix computed applying a transform AutoMatrix Registration computed per region, on the mask AREG_CBCT Measurement landmarks predicted one agent per landmark ALI_CBCT Padding, derivation into the MAX frame computed geometry VFACE Measurements, signed features computed arithmetic and sign tables VFACE…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-env","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"6. Environment","x":"Item Value Reference Python >=3.11,<3.12 pyproject.toml:8 Libraries SimpleITK 2.5.6, numpy 2.3.2, scipy 1.16.2, vtk 9.6.2, pandas 2.3.3, openpyxl 3.1.5, lightgbm 4.6.0, joblib 1.5.2, scikit-learn 1.7.2 pyproject.toml:12-28 Why scikit-learn the three models are LightGBM's scikit-learn wrappers (they carry feature_name_ ), and unpickling one imports lightgbm.sklearn ; the legacy never installed it pyproject.toml:21-27 ; README.md:34-39 GPU, torch none: no torch, no nnU-Net ; every network belongs to a callee, each with its own virtual environment README.md:130, 160-162 Data folder…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-changes","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"7. What changed from the legacy logic","x":"Both sides are cited. Legacy files: clp ( VFACE/VFACE_utils/createlistprocess.py , 2,722 lines) and VFACE_CLI.py at 3c67957. The port is from the legacy at 84ef432 (2026-09-18) ( README.md:13-16 ). Step Legacy VISOR Orchestration CreateListProcess , about 40 CLI invocations chained by Qt callbacks ( clp:114 ) dispatch._run and sup.run ( SV:dispatch.py:415-540 ; README.md:26-28 ) Resample MRI2CBCT CLI call, spacing 0.3, 0.3, 0.3, centred ( clp:208-219 ) vendored code, same spacing ( SV:resample.py:48 ); folder tree kept ( README.md:40-43 ) Orientation separate PRE_ASO, ALI and SEMI_ASO steps…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-findings","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"8. Findings in VISOR","x":"Never run with its six real callees The README states that no supervised chain has been executed with the six tools: the calls are covered by a fake supervisor asserting the parameters, plus a test that reads the six published schemas. It also states that nothing has been compared with the legacy output on a patient, and that nothing is validated clinically ( README.md:184-193 ). The 174 passing tests ( README.md:183 ) are evidence for the arithmetic and the wiring, not for a patient's result. Heat maps : Batch_Dental_Seg is not asked for surfaces segment_surfaces sends Batch_Dental_Seg only…"},{"u":"visor/VFACE/VFACE.html#visor-literature","p":"VFACE on VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"9. Literature","x":"The bibliography is the tool's, on SOURCES ; the legacy page discusses it in its literature section . One point carries over unchanged: the published work describes an unsupervised clustering of the measurements, while the code, legacy and VISOR alike, reads them with three supervised LightGBM classifiers ( SV:classify.py:1-8 ). The VISOR README does not cite a publication."},{"u":"visor/architecture.html","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"How the pieces fit: what stays in Slicer, what the server does before a tool ever runs, and how a tool declares itself so that nobody writes its interface by hand. A run, from upload to result, is on its own page ; so are the image , security and tests . Citations read repository path:line and refer to the commits of the overview : VISOR-serve is e9a7e5d , SADT-VISOR is 6ba2a78 , Remote is AutomatedDentalToolsRemote 19b95e8 ."},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#shape","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"1. The shape of it","x":"Two machines, one boundary. On the clinician's side, Slicer holds a Remote module whose panel was built by formgen from the server's tool descriptor. On the server side, a single FastAPI process validates the request, finds the tool in its registry, waits for room in its resource budget, and starts the tool in a subprocess that uses the tool's own interpreter. The server process contains no torch ; the heavy libraries live only in the environments of the tools. One request, one subprocess. A tool that needs another does not make a new request: it asks the supervisor, which starts the other…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#rules","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"2. Three rules that decide everything else","x":"1. One tool, one folder, one venv A tool is a directory with its own interpreter and lockfile. The server never imports it. Only the two fixtures, Example_Tool and Test_Tool , are Python classes loaded in-process. 2. Discovery is declared A folder is a tool because its pyproject.toml says [tool.sadt] tool = true , optionally with name . The scan goes two levels deep, so group folders such as tools/ALI/ work. 3. Tools call tools through the supervisor Never by import. A tool names the other by string, the schema publishes that name under calls , and the server checks at startup that every…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#registry","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"3. The registry","x":"At startup _build_registry assembles the table of served tools, in this order: packaged tools, in-process fixtures, the check of deployment.toml against what loaded, transitive stop points, facades, the check of supervised calls, and finally the refusal to start if packaged tools exist on disk but none loaded ( VISOR-serve server/registry/__init__.py:521-605 ). Facades come after the stop points because a facade copies its targets' arguments. Step Rule Code Discovery Walk TOOLS_DIR to depth 2. Names starting with _ or . are skipped. A folder already recognised as a tool is not descended…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#schema","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"4. From the signature of run() to the schema","x":"A tool publishes no interface by hand. scripts/describe.py (1,197 lines) reads the signature of run() , its annotations and the Args: block of its docstring, plus the AST for supervised calls, and prints a .schema.json . It has to be run with the tool's own interpreter and stays compatible with Python 3.9 so that it can run in old environments. For anything it cannot represent it prints a message on stderr and exits with code 2 , so a schema that is \"almost right\" is never emitted ( SADT-VISOR scripts/describe.py:1-18, 1185-1187 ). The type vocabulary Annotation in run() Schema type Server…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#deployment-toml","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"5. What deployment.toml declares","x":"Everything specific to one deployment lives in server/deployment.toml , read once at startup. Per tool, the only keys accepted are server_selectable , max_upload_mb , data_dir , hidden , timeout_seconds , dispatch , batch and width_from ( VISOR-serve server/registry/deployment.py:69-70 ). A value of server_selectable is model , testfile or none , with an optional scope written testfile:Sub_folder ( deployment.py:53 ). The file is mounted into the container, so it can be newer than the server image. The file at e9a7e5d has 292 lines and 14 [tools.X] sections: Section What it declares…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#facades","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"6. Facades: ALI and AREG","x":"ALI and AREG each became several engines ( ALI_CBCT and ALI_IOS ; AREG_CBCT , AREG_IOS and AREG_IOSCBCT ), but the Slicer panel still asks for \"ALI\" or \"AREG\". A facade reconciles the two. It is not a folder and not an interpreter: folder is None and run() raises RuntimeError ( VISOR-serve server/registry/facade.py:68, 80-81 ). Its schema is composed at startup from the dispatch table of deployment.toml : a leading mode argument, then the union of the targets' arguments, each visible only in the modes that have it, and optional unless every target requires it ( facade.py:42, 98 ). If two…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#graph","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"7. The call graph that actually exists","x":"The calls field of each schema lists the tools named in sup.run(\"X\", …) . The matrix below is that field for the six tools that call another one, checked against the sup.run sites in SADT-VISOR tools/ (for example tools/ALI/ALI_IOS/src/sadt_ali_ios/dispatch.py:229 , tools/ASO/src/sadt_aso/dispatch.py:537 , tools/VFACE/src/sadt_vface/tools.py:145-359 ). Twelve tools call nobody; Agent calls tools too but declares none (see the callout below). Who calls whom (a dot is one tool starting another). The three outlined dots are the deepest chain: VFACE → AREG_CBCT → ASO → ALI_CBCT, four levels.…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#client","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"8. What stays on the client","x":"The client repository has 23 module folders. Fifteen of them front a server tool: AMASSS, ALI, ASO, AREG, AutoMatrix, AutoCrop3D, FlexReg, BATCHDENTALSEG, DOCShapeAXI, CLIC, CNE, VFACE, GreedyReg, SurgMovPred and Crown_Seg ( Remote CMakeLists.txt:24-48 ). Every one of them inherits ServerToolWidgetBase , which does the common work: The panel. At setup it calls GET /health then GET /tools , finds its own tool (exact name, then ignoring case and separators), and formgen turns the schema into widgets: scalars, choices, multichoices, vec2 joysticks, and file rows that accept a file or a folder.…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#supervisor","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"9. The supervisor: sup","x":"A tool whose run() declares a keyword-only sup (no annotation) receives a supervisor object, the class _Supervisor in runner.py ( VISOR-serve server/execution/runner.py:1140, 1685 ). It has eight members: Member What it is Line run(tool, **params) runs another tool, blocking, and returns what its run() returned runner.py:1808 out the job's output/ directory :1785 tmp scratch under the job, removed with it :1789 datapath the read-only DATA root, so a tool can name a neighbour's model folder; None if the deployment publishes none :1804 channels(wanted) how many parallel workers the budget…"},{"u":"visor/architecture.html#read-on","p":"VISOR architecture","t":"visor","c":"","s":"10. Where to read on","x":"What happens between a click on Apply and the result in the scene: A run, step by step . The image, the compose profiles, how the environments are built: Packaging and deployment . Which protections are implemented, specified or absent: Security and privacy . How a tool, its name and its provenance are tested: Tests and provenance . The overview, tool by tool: VISOR, state of each tool ."},{"u":"visor/deployment.html","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"How VISOR-serve is built into one container, where models and configuration live outside it, what every setting defaults to, which scripts stand a server up and keep it current, what the benchmarks published with the repository measured and what they did not, and what a dated inspection of a running instance found. The server is a Docker image with one Python 3.13 API environment that never loads torch, and one hardlinked virtual environment per tool. Note Citations are file:line in VISOR-serve at commit e9a7e5d , which is main ( e91557e ) plus one commit that touches only…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#image","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"1. The image","x":"There is one container for all tools. docker/Dockerfile (232 lines) builds it in stages. The runtime stage starts from python:3.13-slim ( :76 ), installs the one system library no lockfile can supply, libgomp1 ( :86 ), copies uv 0.12.3 ( :89 ) and builds the API environment at /opt/sadt/.venv from server/requirements-api.txt , which lists only fastapi , uvicorn[standard] , python-multipart and pydantic-settings ( :105-108 ). The server process therefore imports no torch, no nnU-Net and no MONAI . Each tool's own interpreter is installed by uv itself ( :75 ), so tools can sit on different…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#data","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"2. Models and test files","x":"Weights are never in the image and never uploaded from a laptop. They live in a DATA/ directory mounted read-only at /DATA : DATA/<slug>/models/<name> and DATA/<slug>/testfiles/<name> , optionally one level deeper as a scope ( DATA/AREG/testfiles/CBCT_T1 ). LocalDataStore reads it; a different backend can be plugged in through the abstract DataStore class ( DATA_BACKEND , config.py:31 ). What crosses the container boundary. Everything the server may write is either inside the container ( /jobs ) or in the named volume. Which folder. The slug is the tool's declared data_dir ; if none, the…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#config","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"3. Settings","x":"All settings are a pydantic Settings class in server/config.py . A value is read from the environment (names are the field names) or from a .env file in the working directory ( config.py:23 ); unknown keys are ignored. API_TOKEN is the one setting without a default: the server will not start without it ( :27 ). The defaults below are those of config.py ; the image overrides the ones listed at the end of section 1. Variable Default Line Meaning API_TOKEN none, required :27 The shared Bearer token. Removed from the environment given to tools. DEVICE cpu :28 cuda on the GPU server. Injected…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#deployment-toml","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"4. deployment.toml","x":"server/deployment.toml holds what is specific to one installation. It is read once at startup, mounted read-only into the container over the image's copy so that changing a line does not mean rebuilding the image ( docker-compose.yml:215-220 ). Each [tools.<name>] table may contain exactly these keys ( _TOOL_KEYS , registry/deployment.py:69 ): server_selectable , max_upload_mb , data_dir , hidden , timeout_seconds , dispatch , batch , width_from . server_selectable takes model , testfile or none , with a scope written testfile:Sub_folder ( :53 , SCOPE_SEPARATOR ). An unreadable file or an…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#scripts","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"5. Scripts and operation","x":"File What it does scripts/setup-server.sh From nothing, curl | sh : clones the repository ( main by default), checks that Docker works, writes a .env with mode 0600 and a generated token, starts the right compose service for the hardware and waits for /health . Options: --dir , --tool (also fetch that tool's data; nothing is downloaded by default), --device gpu|cpu , --bind (default 127.0.0.1 ), --port , --no-start . Safe to re-run: the token already in .env is kept. scripts/install-docker.sh · install-docker-gpu.sh Docker Engine and the compose plugin on Linux, root required; the second…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#packaging-ali-aso","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"6. Two historical documents: packaging ALI and ASO","x":"Two files at the root of VISOR-serve describe the port of ALI and ASO from in-process tools to packaged ones. Both are dated records of how the decision was made and are partly obsolete; the code and the live catalogue are the reference. What the document says Document and date What is true at e9a7e5d Four tools are packaged and were run end to end: AMASSS (21.8 s), Batch_Dental_Seg (71 s, 86 MB upload), Surg_Mov_Pred (8.6 s), Crown_Seg (0.95 s, “passed through untouched”). PACKAGING_ALI_AND_ASO.md , “where things stand (2026-08-13)” Snapshot of that morning. 19 packaged tools are served…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#live","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"7. What an inspection of a running instance found","x":"A dated observation, not a property of the code One running instance was inspected on 2026-10-20 (a report kept outside the repositories). What it found about that machine may no longer be true. This section keeps what the code explains; the details that identify the machine or its network are deliberately left out. The only check made while writing this page was an unauthenticated GET /health and GET /tools : they answered {\"status\":\"ok\"} and 23 entries. What the code predicts, it did. GET /health and GET /tools answered without a token, GET /status refused a request without one (401). The…"},{"u":"visor/deployment.html#benchmarks","p":"Packaging and deployment","t":"visor","c":"","s":"8. The benchmarks published with the server","x":"benchmarks/ is a re-runnable harness ( python -m benchmarks.run --campaign b1..b5 [--dry-run] ), configured by config.yaml . Three execution paths exist: local (the tool in its own interpreter, no HTTP, with four documented differences in NOTES-local-path.md ), loopback (the HTTP API on the same machine) and lan , which is never measured : server.lan_base_url is null ( config.yaml:22 ). The harness's HTTP client speaks the Slicer client's protocol: 8 MB parts, four concurrent transfers, a SHA-256 per part, gzip, X-Result-Delivery: reference , ranged downloads. Statistics are the median with…"},{"u":"visor/guide/ALI.html","p":"ALI with VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run ALI through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through a VISOR server the analysis runs on the server, not on your computer. The module in Slicer is called ALIRemote . It is a panel that sends your scans to the server and brings back the landmark files. How to connect and what the server does with your data is in Using VISOR ; this page is only what changes for ALI compared with the legacy module . It is written from the code of the server and of the panel, not from a session on a…"},{"u":"visor/guide/ALI.html#visor-change","p":"ALI with VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"One window, a choice at the top. The first field is the Mode , CBCT or IOS . It plays the role of the data type in the legacy window. The list of points that follows changes with it (119 points for a CBCT, 153 for an intraoral scan). No models to download. The trained weights are kept on the server. The model, the device and the search settings are not shown in the panel. The input. A file or a folder. A folder is zipped and sent for you. The panel can also offer the test files the server hosts. The points are picked in tabs , one tab per group. If you tick nothing, the CBCT run places all…"},{"u":"visor/guide/ALI.html#visor-same","p":"ALI with VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"What stays the same","x":"The landmark names, the two ways of working (a volume walked by an agent, a surface photographed from several sides), the .mrk.json format, and your original files left untouched. The points still arrive locked, as the legacy files did: unlock a point in the Markups module before moving it."},{"u":"visor/guide/ALI.html#visor-different","p":"ALI with VISOR","t":"ALI (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"What is different in the files and in the results","x":"One file per scan with all the groups together, named <scan>_lm_Pred.mrk.json , where the legacy module wrote one file per group ( CBCT ) or per jaw and per network ( IOS ). The folder layout of your input is reproduced in the result. A report comes with it : run_report.json lists, scan by scan, the points placed and the points that did not converge. Supported intraoral formats are .vtk and .stl . Meshes that already carry tooth numbers are processed first; a mesh without them goes through the server's crown segmentation ( Crown_Seg ), then follows. The landmark files show up when loaded.…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AMASSS.html","p":"AMASSS with VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run AMASSS through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through VISOR, the module is AMASSS Remote : the computation runs on a server, not on your computer. Using VISOR explains how to connect Slicer to a server; this page covers what is specific to AMASSS."},{"u":"visor/guide/AMASSS.html#visor-change","p":"AMASSS with VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Nothing to install, no model to download. The \"Download latest models\" button and the first-run installation of libraries are gone: the models are on the server, and you cannot pick or send your own. Input: one scan or a whole folder, as before. A folder is zipped and sent; a large cohort is cut into batches by the panel (by default at most 400 MB or 25 files per batch). If the server offers test files, you can pick one instead of your own scan. Structures: nine are offered, shown as chips. Mandible, maxilla, cranial base, cervical vertebra and upper airway are checked by default; skin and…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AMASSS.html#visor-same","p":"AMASSS with VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"What stays the same","x":"One network per structure, the same label values in the merged map (mandible 1, cranial base 2, upper airway 3, maxilla 4, cervical vertebra 5, skin 6, then the three masks 7 to 9), and no cleanup of the network's raw output."},{"u":"visor/guide/AMASSS.html#visor-diff","p":"AMASSS with VISOR","t":"AMASSS (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"What is different in the results","x":"Results come back as a zip that the panel unpacks into the output folder you pick. For each scan there is a folder <scan>_<ID>_SegOut , and at the root an AMASSS_report.json that says which structures were done, which failed, and what happened to each scan. File extensions can change: .nii comes back as .nii.gz , .gipl as .gipl.gz , and .nrrd.gz as .nrrd . Surfaces are written in binary and reduced by 90 % of their triangles by default, so they are much lighter than the legacy ones. At most 12 results are loaded into the scene; the rest stay in the output folder. Your scan is shown in 3D…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_CBCT.html","p":"AREG_CBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run AREG_CBCT through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . AREG_CBCT registers a follow-up CBCT (T2) onto the baseline CBCT (T1) of the same patient, on a region that has not changed between the two. With VISOR the registration is computed on a server: in Slicer you fill a form, send the folders and get the results back. What you do is the same in spirit, but the panel and a few details differ. This page was written from the VISOR code (SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78, VISOR-serve e9a7e5d…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-panel","p":"AREG_CBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Open the module AREG Remote (category “Automated Dental Tools Remote”). One module serves the three kinds of AREG registration: the first field, Mode , is where you pick CBCT . The other fields then follow the mode. Automation keeps the three choices (Semi-Automated, Fully-Automated, Oriented + Fully-Automated) and starts on Fully-Automated. Nothing to download: the segmentation and orientation models stay on the server, and the field for the segmentation model is not shown. The review pauses (“Review and manual adjustment”), the Merge Segmentations box and Include Approximation Step are not…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-provide","p":"AREG_CBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What you provide","x":"T1 and T2 : a folder each, or a hosted test cohort chosen from the list above the pickers. A single cohort folder holding T1/ and T2/ is accepted. Accepted formats: .nii.gz .nrrd.gz .gipl.gz .nii .nrrd .gipl . Tick Input is DICOM for DICOM series. Register on : the regions, as before. Each one gives its own registration and its own output folder. In Semi-Automated , the T1 masks , which are required. A mask is recognised when its name holds a mask word ( mask , seg , pred or segmentation ) and a region word as separate parts of the name. In Oriented + Fully-Automated , the Orientation…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-same","p":"AREG_CBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What stays the same","x":"A rigid elastix registration, confined to the region you chose on T1, with the T2 moved into the T1 frame. For each patient and region you get the registered T2 and its .tfm . Your inputs are not modified."},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_CBCT.html#visor-different","p":"AREG_CBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What is different in the results","x":"The results are in one folder per region code ( CB , MAND , MAX ), plus AREG_report.json at the root: for each patient and region, whether it worked, which mask was used, and a note when something was unusual. The written transform now includes the centre of rotation elastix used. The legacy module dropped it, so its transform could differ from the registration elastix had actually found, by an amount that grows with the distance of the scan from the origin; the Atlas page gives the figures. T2 is no longer centred beforehand, and in Oriented mode only T1 is oriented. Only T1 is segmented…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOS.html","p":"AREG_IOS with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run AREG_IOS through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . AREG_IOS registers a follow-up intraoral scan (T2) onto the baseline scan (T1) of the same patient, on a patch that treatment does not move: the palate, or a band along the mucogingival line. With VISOR the registration is computed on a server, including the network that predicts the palatal patch: in Slicer you fill a form, send the folders and get the results back. This page was written from the VISOR code (SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOS.html#visor-panel","p":"AREG_IOS with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Open AREG Remote (category “Automated Dental Tools Remote”) and pick IOS in the first field, Mode . Automation : Fully-Automated (the default) labels the teeth and orients the scans on the server; Semi-Automated expects scans that already carry tooth labels and a common orientation. Registration patch replaces the two modes of the legacy module: Palate (upper arch) predicts the palatal patch and registers the upper arch, the lower arch following; Mucogingival line (lower arch) registers the lower arch alone. No model to download and no graphics card needed on your machine. The patch model…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOS.html#visor-provide","p":"AREG_IOS with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What you provide","x":"T1 and T2 : one folder each, or the hosted test cohorts. Formats .vtk .vtp .stl .obj . Because the two time points are two folders, the file names no longer have to contain T1 or T2 . Each mesh must still say its arch in its name, as a separate word ( U , Upper , Maxilla , L , Lower , Mandible …). A mesh that does not is reported and left out, not guessed. The arch is read from the file name only, so the folder names no longer matter. The patient is recognised from the sub-folder path plus the rest of the name, which must match between T1 and T2."},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOS.html#visor-same","p":"AREG_IOS with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What stays the same","x":"A rigid ICP on a stable patch, the same two patches, and the other arch carried along by the palatal registration. Your inputs are not modified."},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOS.html#visor-different","p":"AREG_IOS with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOS (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What is different in the results","x":"A transform file ( .tfm ) is written for every patient, in both modes and for both patches. When the server oriented your scans first, the transform is composed with that orientation so that it refers to the scans you sent. The output keeps your folder structure. With the palate patch it also holds the lower arch of each time point, moved with the upper one; with the mucogingival patch only the lower arch comes back. AREG_report.json lists for each patient what was done. A failed canonical orientation of the palatal patch is written there as a note, instead of only in a log. The mucogingival…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run AREG_IOSCBCT through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . AREG_IOSCBCT places an intraoral scan in the frame of a CBCT of the same patient, taken at the same visit. With VISOR the registration is computed on a server: in Slicer you fill a form, send the folders and get the results back. The registration itself is pure geometry and needs no graphics card, but the two modes that predict the landmarks use tools that do. This page was written from the VISOR code (SADT-VISOR 6ba2a78…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-panel","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Open AREG Remote (category “Automated Dental Tools Remote”) and pick IOS to CBCT in the first field, Mode . Automation has three choices. Registration (the default here; the same name, but the last choice, in the legacy module) takes your two landmark folders and calls no other tool. Semi-Automated labels the crowns and predicts both landmark sets. Fully-Automated first orients the CBCT, then does the same. You send Intraoral scans and CBCT volumes as two folders, or the hosted test cohorts, plus, in Registration mode, the Intraoral landmarks and CBCT landmarks ( .json or .mrk.json ). The…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-provide","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What you provide","x":"Meshes in .vtk or .stl , and CBCTs in any of the usual volume formats, not only .nii.gz . Each mesh names its arch as a separate word of its name ( U , Upper , L or Lower ), and so does each intraoral landmark file. Both arches of a patient are no longer required: a mesh without landmarks fails alone and the others go on. The patient is matched between the two folders by the digits of its name. See the warning below."},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-same","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What stays the same","x":"The same two stages: a rigid fit on the landmarks both sides share, then an ICP against the CBCT isosurface . Each arch is registered on its own, so the occlusion between the two arches is not constrained. The level of the isosurface is read off the scan, as it already was in the legacy module at the commit this site describes."},{"u":"visor/guide/AREG_IOSCBCT.html#visor-different","p":"AREG_IOSCBCT with VISOR","t":"AREG_IOSCBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What is different in the results","x":"Each registered mesh comes with its composed 4x4 matrix as a numpy file ( _matrix.npy ). Still no .tfm , and the registered landmarks are no longer saved. The output is one folder per patient key (the digits of the identifier), holding files named after each mesh, with AREG_report.json at the root. For each mesh it says whether the landmark fit was accepted and why not, and how well the ICP matched. You no longer need to read the log for a skipped pre-alignment. A mesh whose ICP matches under 5% of the arch is refused rather than written. The level of the isosurface can no longer be…"},{"u":"visor/guide/ASO.html","p":"ASO with VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run ASO through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through a VISOR server the orientation is computed on the server and the Slicer module, ASORemote , only sends your files and loads what comes back. How to connect and what the server does with your data is in Using VISOR . This page is only what changes for ASO compared with the legacy module . It is written from the code of the server and of the panel, not from a session on a live server. The details, with the code references, are…"},{"u":"visor/guide/ASO.html#visor-change","p":"ASO with VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"You choose the data type, not the mode. The panel asks for the Input Type , CBCT or IOS . The Semi or Fully choice is not drawn: the server decides from your data. For a CBCT, a scan that already has a landmark file beside it is oriented on those landmarks; a scan without one gets its landmarks predicted by the server (ALI_CBCT). If you give a folder of landmark files ( Landmark folder , in the Advanced section), those are used first. Nothing to download. The reference patient (the \"gold\") and the models stay on the server and are chosen for you. CBCT landmarks: a list of 115 points in tabs…"},{"u":"visor/guide/ASO.html#visor-same","p":"ASO with VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"What stays the same","x":"The principle (a 3-landmark start, then a rigid ICP onto the reference), the reoriented scan, a transform file written beside it for the registration tools, and your scans left in place."},{"u":"visor/guide/ASO.html#visor-different","p":"ASO with VISOR","t":"ASO (VISOR)","c":"Landmarks","s":"What is different","x":"Semi-automatic CBCT needs no transform file , and the server always recentres every scan first, so a semi-automatic run on its own is fine. The legacy guide's warning against running it alone ( Choosing a mode ) does not apply here. Your landmark files are not deleted. The server merges them in memory and writes nothing in your input folder. Rerunning writes again. Each run goes to a fresh output folder on the server and every file is written, whether or not an earlier one existed. Intraoral file names must name the jaw (for example U , Upper , L , Lower , Max , Mand ). A file with no jaw…"},{"u":"visor/guide/Agent.html","p":"Agent with VISOR","t":"Agent (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"What the Agent panel of the VISOR extension is, and why it does not use the VISOR server. The legacy module has its own page . The VISOR extension includes an Agent panel, but it is the same local assistant , not a server tool. It still needs Ollama on your own computer, it still runs on your computer, and it sends nothing to the VISOR server ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote README.md:28-30 , commit 19b95e8). It does not ask the server to run the tools it picks. What is different from the legacy guide : nothing you see. The panel is a copy of the legacy one ( AgentRemote/AgentRemote.py ) and…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoCrop3D.html","p":"AutoCrop3D with VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run AutoCrop3D through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through a VISOR server the module is called AutoCrop3D Remote . You still draw the box in Slicer; the files are sent to the server, cropped there, and the results come back to a folder you choose. The panel is built from what the server publishes, in three groups: Inputs, Options and Outputs. Nothing is installed or downloaded on your computer, and the crop needs no model."},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-fournir","p":"AutoCrop3D with VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"What you provide","x":"The scans : one volume or a folder ( .nii.gz , .nii , .nrrd , .gipl.gz , .gipl ), uploaded to the server. A .nrrd.gz is refused with a message: decompress it to .nrrd first. A server can also host test files; the dropdown then offers MG_test_scan.nii.gz . The default upload limit is 500 MB, and a folder goes up as a single request because AutoCrop3D cannot be split by patient. The region of interest : pick the ROI you drew, straight from the scene (the ROI row lists the ROI nodes of the scene), or give a .mrk.json file or a folder of them. The hosted test box is called ROI_box . Options…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-identique","p":"AutoCrop3D with VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"What stays the same","x":"The crop is the same arithmetic: the box is mapped to voxel indices of the original grid and the block is extracted, with no resampling. The port keeps even the truncation toward zero, on purpose. One ROI for everybody, or one ROI per patient matched by name. Output files keep the input folder layout. Results are unpacked into the output folder you chose; up to 12 are loaded into the scene, shown with the CT-AAA volume rendering preset."},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoCrop3D.html#visor-different","p":"AutoCrop3D with VISOR","t":"AutoCrop3D (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"What is different","x":"No \"Crop Volume for tilted images\". A rotated ROI is cropped as its axis-aligned box and the report carries roi_orientation_ignored . A ROI saved in RAS is understood. The coordinate system declared in the file is read. Naming mistakes are named. The panel ends with a line such as \"N of M scan(s) cropped. No ROI for: …\". The run report lists the same scans. A box that misses the volume is an error (the ROI does not overlap this volume), and a box that only partly overlaps is cropped and flagged clamped_to_the_volume . Earlier results are not cropped again. If the scans folder also holds…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoMatrix.html","p":"AutoMatrix with VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run AutoMatrix through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through a VISOR server the module is called AutoMatrix Remote . You choose the files and the transforms in Slicer; they are sent to the server, which pairs them by patient, moves them, and sends the results back to a folder you choose. The panel is built from what the server publishes. Nothing is installed or downloaded on your computer."},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoMatrix.html#visor-fournir","p":"AutoMatrix with VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"What you provide","x":"Files to transform : a scan, a segmentation, a landmark file ( .mrk.json ) or a folder of them. A server can host test sets ( AutoMatrixRelease1 to 4 ) that appear in the dropdown. Transforms : .tfm , .mat , .h5 , .hdf5 or .txt , a file or a folder. A file holding a plain 4×4 matrix (four lines of four numbers, which is what GreedyReg writes as .mat ) is read too, as an affine matrix taken as written. Same transform for every patient : tick it when you give a single transform for the whole folder. Without it, a transform whose name matches no patient is used only if there is exactly one…"},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoMatrix.html#visor-identique","p":"AutoMatrix with VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"What stays the same","x":"Volumes are resampled with the transform; landmark points move through its inverse, so a scan and its landmarks keep the same motion. Several transforms for one patient give one output each, never a chain. The landmark coordinateSystem is still not read. The output keeps the input folder layout; up to 12 results are loaded into the scene."},{"u":"visor/guide/AutoMatrix.html#visor-different","p":"AutoMatrix with VISOR","t":"AutoMatrix (VISOR)","c":"Utilitaires","s":"What is different","x":"One naming rule for scans and matrices ( P1_scan.nii.gz pairs with P1_matrix.tfm ). Where it fails, a fallback imitates the legacy cuts, and the report says which patients were paired that way ( paired_by ). Unpaired files are reported in AutoMatrix_report.json . No meshes and no .npy ; the supported volumes are .nii.gz .nii .nrrd .gipl and their .gz forms ( .nrrd.gz is looked for but cannot be read). A transform that cannot be inverted (a deformation field) makes that patient's landmark file fail; the failure is in the report. The final status line is wrong The message shown in Slicer's…"},{"u":"visor/guide/BatchDentalSeg.html","p":"BatchDentalSeg with VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run BatchDentalSeg through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through VISOR, the module is BatchDentalSegmentator Remote : the segmentation runs on a server. Using VISOR explains how to connect Slicer to a server; this page covers what is specific to BatchDentalSeg."},{"u":"visor/guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-change","p":"BatchDentalSeg with VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"No extension to install, no weights to download , no memory settings: the computation and the memory are the server's. The auto-crop, the \"Free memory\" button, the UniversalLab left/right correction button and the saved queue do not exist in the remote module. The model is picked from the list of models hosted on the server. The names are DentalSegmentator , PediatricDentalSeg , NasoMaxillaDentSeg and UniversalLab ; you cannot send your own weights. Input: one scan or a folder, or a test file the server hosts. A folder is searched recursively and may contain NRRD, NIfTI or GIPL files. A big…"},{"u":"visor/guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-same","p":"BatchDentalSeg with VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"What stays the same","x":"The four models, their structures and their label numbers, a single fold with no ensemble, no morphological cleanup of the network's output, and the result placed back on the original grid of your scan."},{"u":"visor/guide/BatchDentalSeg.html#visor-diff","p":"BatchDentalSeg with VISOR","t":"BatchDentalSeg (VISOR)","c":"Segmentation","s":"What is different in the results","x":"The label volume is named <scan>_Seg plus the extension (the identifier is editable), not <scan>_Segmentation.nii.gz , and the extension is compressed ( .nii comes back as .nii.gz , .gipl as .gipl.gz ). The label volume is no longer always written: it is only there if NIFTI is selected. A BatchDentalSeg_report.json accompanies the results. It lists each scan ( case_0000 , ... in order, not by patient name), whether it worked, and the table of label numbers for the model. A scan that cannot be read is reported as failed and the others are still segmented. Meshes are built differently from the…"},{"u":"visor/guide/CLIC.html","p":"CLIC with VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run CLIC through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . With a VISOR server the computation runs on the server, not on your workstation, and the CLIC panel is a form that sends your scans there. Details of the code are in the Atlas page ; connecting and running in general are in Using VISOR ."},{"u":"visor/guide/CLIC.html#panel","p":"CLIC with VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Nothing is installed in Slicer: no computing environment to build, no model to download. The trained model is already on the server, and you do not choose it. You provide a CBCT in .nii or .nii.gz , one file or a folder. Subfolders are searched, so a folder of one subfolder per patient works. Files in other formats ( .nrrd , .mha ...) are skipped; if nothing readable is left, the run is refused with a message saying so. Results land in the output folder you choose in the panel. You can also change the score threshold (0.7 by default: detections below it are not painted) and the suffix added…"},{"u":"visor/guide/CLIC.html#same","p":"CLIC with VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"What stays the same","x":"The method is unchanged: the volume is read slice by slice, each slice is classified on its own, and values 1, 2 and 3 mean buccal, bicortical and palatal. Neighbouring slices can still disagree about the same tooth, and nothing gives you one class per patient."},{"u":"visor/guide/CLIC.html#results","p":"CLIC with VISOR","t":"CLIC (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"What is different in the results","x":"Results come back as one archive, unpacked into your output folder. The folder tree of your input is reproduced, and each file is named <scan>_seg.nii.gz (the legacy module produced X.nii_seg.nii.gz inside an X.nii folder). The panel can load up to 12 of the segmentations into the scene when the run ends, so you no longer have to find and load them yourself. A CLIC_report.json file is written next to them. It names the three classes and says, per scan, which were detected and which scans failed. The panel does not display it: open the file. When a large folder is sent in several batches, the…"},{"u":"visor/guide/CNE.html","p":"CNE with VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run CNE through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . With a VISOR server the extraction runs on the server, not on your computer, and the CNE panel is a form that sends your notes there. Details of the code are on the Atlas page ; connecting and running in general are in Using VISOR . Your notes are uploaded The text of the notes you select leaves your workstation and is read by the server. The current client does not de-identify anything before sending, so the legacy guide's \"Nothing…"},{"u":"visor/guide/CNE.html#panel","p":"CNE with VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Nothing is installed in Slicer: no extra packages, no model download of several gigabytes. The model is on the server. The model is the one selected in the Model field, not one picked for your note type. Some client texts say the model can be left on \"automatic\" and that the server then picks the one for your note type. That is out of date: the field has no \"automatic\" entry, it lists the models hosted on the server, and nothing checks that your choice matches the note type beyond a warning in the report. Only the TMJ model is hosted at the moment. You can still choose Ortho as the note…"},{"u":"visor/guide/CNE.html#results","p":"CNE with VISOR","t":"CNE (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"What is different in the results","x":"For each note you get two files, Extraction_<note file name>.json and a readable .txt , with the source extension kept: B_001.txt gives Extraction_B_001.txt.txt . Notes sharing a name but not an extension no longer overwrite each other. A CNE_report.json file says which model ran, with which settings, which notes were extracted and why others were not. The status bar shows a one-line summary and the number of warnings. The model's answer is never saved as raw text when it cannot be read: the note is reported as failed. A scanned, image-only PDF is refused by name instead of producing an…"},{"u":"visor/guide/DOCShapeAXI.html","p":"DOCShapeAXI with VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run DOCShapeAXI through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . With a VISOR server the classification and the explainability maps are computed on the server, and the DOCShapeAXI panel is a form that sends your meshes there. Details of the code are on the Atlas page ; connecting and running in general are in Using VISOR . Your meshes are uploaded: what protects them is on Security and privacy in VISOR ."},{"u":"visor/guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#panel","p":"DOCShapeAXI with VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"There is no data-type list any more. You pick a checkpoint by its file name , and the name decides the anatomy, the task and the number of classes: condyles_4_class.ckpt ( condyle , 4 stages), cleft_4_class.ckpt (cleft bone defect, 4 stages), and for the airway airways_2_class.ckpt (obstruction yes or no), airways_4_class.ckpt (4 stages) and airways_4_regress.ckpt (a continuous ratio). For the airway, the three results are no longer chained in one run: one run gives one checkpoint, so you run it once per checkpoint you want. Nothing is installed or downloaded in Slicer: the checkpoints are…"},{"u":"visor/guide/DOCShapeAXI.html#results","p":"DOCShapeAXI with VISOR","t":"DOCShapeAXI (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"What is different in the results","x":"There is no csv. The predictions are in DOCShapeAXI_report.json : for each surface, the path relative to your input and either a class (an integer) or, for the ratio, a score . The report also names the anatomy, the task, the number of classes and the network. The meshes carrying the explainability maps are written directly in the output folder, with the suffix _pred added to their names, not in an explainability subfolder. The panel can load up to 12 of them into the scene when the run ends. The maps are read as in the legacy guide, Reading the result . Still true: no confidence score, the…"},{"u":"visor/guide/FlexReg.html","p":"FlexReg with VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run FlexReg through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through VISOR, the module is FlexReg Remote . The patch and the registration run on a server; the preview of the patch outline stays in Slicer. Using VISOR explains how to connect Slicer to a server; this page covers what is specific to FlexReg."},{"u":"visor/guide/FlexReg.html#visor-change","p":"FlexReg with VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What changes in the panel","x":"Joysticks. The four corners of the palatal patch are four 2D joysticks (ratio from the middle of the arch to the tooth, and shift forward or backward in millimetres), plus one joystick that moves the whole patch. A value outside their range is refused by the server. Preview. With \"Preview the patch while I drag\" checked (it is by default), the outline of the patch is redrawn in the 3D view as you move a joystick, without waiting for the server. It needs the arch to be a surface loaded in the Slicer scene; with a folder of arches, there is no preview. Only the outline is drawn: the filled…"},{"u":"visor/guide/FlexReg.html#visor-same","p":"FlexReg with VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What stays the same","x":"The construction of the butterfly patch and the rigid registration on it are the same code as in the legacy module, and the transformation is written as a .tfm in the same way."},{"u":"visor/guide/FlexReg.html#visor-diff","p":"FlexReg with VISOR","t":"FlexReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What is different in the results","x":"Nothing is overwritten. For each arch you get <name>_Reg (the suffix is editable) in the output folder, with your folder tree reproduced, plus the .tfm when you registered, plus a FlexReg_report.json . If a tooth needed by the patch is missing from an arch, that arch is reported as failed with a message; the legacy module wrote the arch back without a patch and looked successful. The output keeps your input extension but is always written as a VTK file. The reference must already carry its patch In \"Patch and register\", the patch is built on the arch you register, not on the reference. Both…"},{"u":"visor/guide/GreedyReg.html","p":"GreedyReg with VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run GreedyReg through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through a VISOR server the module is called GreedyReg Remote . You choose two folders in Slicer; they are sent to the server, which pairs the scans by patient, registers each T2 onto its T1 with Greedy, and sends the results back to a folder you choose. Nothing is installed or downloaded on your computer, and there is no Greedy binary to fetch. The panel is built from what the server publishes. Only part of the legacy module…"},{"u":"visor/guide/GreedyReg.html#visor-fournir","p":"GreedyReg with VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What you provide","x":"T1 and T2 : one folder of scans for each timepoint (T1 is the fixed image, T2 the moving one). A folder must hold a single timepoint. Patients are paired by name up to the timepoint word, so P1_T1_scan.nii.gz pairs with P1_T2.nii.gz , and names no longer need to start with letters then digits ( 01_T1.nii.gz and Patient-01.nii.gz are read as 01 and Patient_01 ). Sub-folders are searched. Scans can be .nii.gz , .nii , .nrrd , .gipl or .gipl.gz ; avoid .nrrd.gz , which is found but has no ITK reader. Advanced, optional : masks (one per patient; the metric is computed inside it; it must be in…"},{"u":"visor/guide/GreedyReg.html#visor-identique","p":"GreedyReg with VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What stays the same","x":"The same Greedy command and the same resampling of T2 into the T1 grid. A multi-segment mask is still flattened (any value above zero), and an identity start is still nudged by a thousandth of a unit so Greedy does not read it as \"no initialisation\". The matrix produced is in Greedy's own format, not a Slicer transform. AutoMatrix on VISOR accepts this plain 4x4 file, but takes the matrix as written, with no conversion: check the result before relying on it. Each Greedy call is limited to 10 minutes, as before."},{"u":"visor/guide/GreedyReg.html#visor-different","p":"GreedyReg with VISOR","t":"GreedyReg (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"What is different","x":"Results go to the output folder you choose, never into the T2 folder: <patient>_registered.nii.gz (with the default suffix), <patient>_transform.mat and GreedyReg_report.json . The input sub-folder layout is kept. If a folder holds both a patient's scan and an earlier result for that patient, the scan is used. The report lists patients present in one folder only, masks and initial transforms that matched nobody, and for each patient status and, if it failed, the reason. A failing patient does not stop the others. If none is registered the run ends with an error and no report. Defaults : the…"},{"u":"visor/guide/MRI2CBCT.html","p":"MRI2CBCT: not available with VISOR","t":"MRI2CBCT (VISOR)","c":"Recalage","s":"Introduction","x":"MRI2CBCT cannot be run through a VISOR server today. This page says what that means for you. The legacy module has its own page . MRI2CBCT is not available with VISOR. The server has no MRI2CBCT tool, and the VISOR client does not ship the module: its description says it is \"temporarily not shipped until it is ported to the server\" ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote CMakeLists.txt:10,31-32 , commit 19b95e8). The current version, the one-button rewrite described in the legacy guide , is ported nowhere. The VISOR extension has no MRI2CBCT panel at all. To register an MRI onto a CBCT , use the legacy…"},{"u":"visor/guide/MedX.html","p":"MedX: not available with VISOR","t":"MedX (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"MedX cannot be run through a VISOR server, and it is no longer in the legacy extension either. The archived module has its own page . MedX is not available with VISOR, and not in the current legacy extension either. It was dropped from SlicerAutomatedDentalTools on 2026-09-17 and kept on a separate archive branch. The VISOR server has no MedX tool and the VISOR client has no MedX module (commit 19b95e8). The VISOR client describes its CNE module as the successor of MedX ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote README.md:208 ). That is a similar use, not the same result: CNE extracts structured findings…"},{"u":"visor/guide/MedicalDataAnonymizer.html","p":"MedicalDataAnonymizer: local only with VISOR","t":"MedicalDataAnonymizer (VISOR)","c":"Texte","s":"Introduction","x":"The VISOR extension ships this module as a local copy: it never runs on a VISOR server, and it does not protect what other panels upload. The legacy module has its own page . The VISOR extension includes a Medical Data Anonymizer panel. It is an older copy of the same module that runs on your computer, installs the same Presidio and spaCy packages in Slicer, and sends nothing to the server ( AutomatedDentalToolsRemote README.md:28-30 , commit 19b95e8). It works on text documents only, with the same limits as described in the legacy guide : English only, a mapping file written next to your…"},{"u":"visor/guide/SurgMovPred.html","p":"SurgMovPred with VISOR","t":"SurgMovPred (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run SurgMovPred through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through VISOR, SurgMovPred runs on a server and Slicer only shows its panel. The prediction itself is the same: the same 112 models, applied row by row to your table. What changes is what you have to provide. One input instead of two folders. The legacy panel asked for an input folder and a model folder; here you give only the measurements, a single .csv , .xlsx or .ods table, or a folder of tables. There is no model folder to…"},{"u":"visor/guide/VFACE.html","p":"VFACE with VISOR","t":"VFACE (VISOR)","c":"Analyse","s":"Introduction","x":"What changes when you run VFACE through a VISOR server from 3D Slicer: what to provide, what is the same, what is different. The legacy module has its own page . Through VISOR, VFACE runs on a server and Slicer only shows its panel. The measurements, the classification and the chain of six modules are the ones described in the legacy guide ; what changes is where they run and what you have to provide. Only on servers built from two feature branches The server side of VFACE exists only on development branches (SADT-VISOR tool/vface and VISOR-serve conf/vface-deployment ), not on their main .…"},{"u":"visor/index.html","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"The rest of this atlas reads the legacy extension, where every algorithm runs inside Slicer. VISOR is the new generation that moves the computation to a server: a thin Slicer client, a generic inference server that knows no dental tool, and a catalogue of tools, each in its own folder with its own Python environment. This page is the overview; the pages listed at the bottom go into the machinery. Other languages The French, Spanish, Italian, Korean, Portuguese and Thai versions of this site describe only the legacy code ( SlicerAutomatedDentalTools , commit 3c67957 ). They have no VISOR…"},{"u":"visor/index.html#what","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"1. What VISOR is","x":"VISOR is three repositories that each own one layer. None of them is a fork of the legacy extension: the algorithms are ported ( \"a repackaging exercise, not a rewrite\" , SADT-VISOR CONTRIBUTING.md:309-315 ), the structure around them is new. Remote, the client AutomatedDentalToolsRemote , the client : a Slicer extension of 23 module folders, each a thin panel over the server. A panel is generated at run time from the server's tool descriptor, so a field added on the server appears in Slicer with no client release. Slicer installs nothing and downloads no model for a ported module.…"},{"u":"visor/index.html#why","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"2. Why it exists","x":"Legacy puts everything in Slicer: the widget, the model download, and the Python environment each module builds for itself. Two consequences recur across the legacy pages of this atlas: a laptop without a GPU cannot run a segmentation, and tools that pin incompatible libraries have to live in separate conda environments created on the user's machine. SADT-VISOR's own README lists reproducible conflicts in the upstream pins: SurgMovPred asks for numpy==2.4.0 where AREG, MedX and CLIC need numpy<2.0.0 , two conda environments are both named shapeaxi (Python 3.9 and 3.12), and torch is pinned…"},{"u":"visor/index.html#compare","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"3. Legacy and VISOR, side by side","x":"Only what the code of both sides shows. \"Legacy\" is SlicerAutomatedDentalTools at 3c67957 ; \"VISOR\" is the three repositories at the commits listed below . Question Legacy VISOR Where the computation runs On the user's machine, inside Slicer: widget, CLI modules, subprocesses of Slicer's Python On the server. SchemaTool.invoke always goes through dispatch ; the server never imports a packaged tool ( VISOR-serve server/registry/schema_tool.py:812-822 ) Python environments Built on the user's machine by ADTLib/env/conda.py for most modules; GreedyReg has none and uses Slicer's own Python One…"},{"u":"visor/index.html#tools","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"4. State of each tool","x":"\"Served\" means the folder is in the SADT-VISOR catalogue and VISOR-serve discovers and loads it; a running instance listed the same 23 names on 2026-10-20. It does not mean a production deployment, which nothing here establishes. \"Slicer module\" is the folder of AutomatedDentalToolsRemote that calls the tool. Each tool has two pages in this atlas: the VISOR page says what runs on the server and what changed, the legacy page describes the original Slicer module. Entry on the server Kind Slicer module VISOR page Legacy page ALI_CBCT packaged tool ALIRemote (calls the facade ALI ) ALI ALI…"},{"u":"visor/index.html#security","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"5. Security, in one sentence","x":"Transport is HTTPS with a certificate verified by the client and a shared Bearer token on most routes; de-identification is specified but not implemented, and attestation and key exchange exist only in a separate research prototype, not in these three repositories. The details and the evidence are on the security page ."},{"u":"visor/index.html#commits","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"6. What was read, and when","x":"This section is a snapshot : these pages read the working trees below on 2026-11-09, not the tip of every branch. The code moves; a line number or a count on these pages is true for the commit named here and may not be true next week. Where a repository's own documentation disagrees with its code, these pages follow the code and say so. Repository Branch read HEAD Commit date VISOR-serve conf/vface-deployment e9a7e5d 2026-09-30 SADT-VISOR tool/vface 6ba2a78 2026-09-30 AutomatedDentalToolsRemote main 19b95e8 2026-10-23 SlicerAutomatedDentalTools (legacy) read through git show 3c67957:<path>…"},{"u":"visor/index.html#lire","p":"VISOR: the next generation of the tools","t":"visor","c":"","s":"7. How to read the rest","x":"Architecture Client and server, the three rules, the registry, the schema, deployment.toml , facades, the call graph, the supervisor. A run, step by step Upload, staging, admission, execution, events, results, cancellation, review. Packaging and deployment The image, the environments, the compose profiles, updates. Security and privacy What is implemented, what is only specified, what is a prototype. Tests and provenance How a tool is tested, how names and provenance are checked. Using VISOR The Guide page: connect to a server, launch a tool, read the results. Every ported tool has four…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"What happens between a click in Slicer and the files coming back: the routes the server exposes, the fifteen stages one POST /run/{tool} goes through, the phases a watcher sees, how a run is admitted, detached, cancelled, paused and rewound, and which HTTP status each failure ends in. Every claim cites the line it was read from. Note This page describes VISOR , the server generation, not the legacy Slicer code that the rest of this site documents. Citations are file:line followed by the repository: VISOR-serve at commit e9a7e5d (lines of server/main.py unless a path is given)…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#endpoints","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"1. The routes","x":"Eighteen route handlers are declared in main.py , and two of them answer without a token. The decorators are the only authority: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:34-42 lists five, server/README.md:49 onward lists more but not all. “Gate” in the table is the maintenance.require_accepting dependency: while an operator has closed the door with POST /maintenance , the route answers 503 with Retry-After: 10 ( wire/maintenance.py:113-146 ). Reads, cancellation and deletion are deliberately not gated, so a run that is already going can always be watched and collected. Route Auth Line Role Notable answers GET…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#happy-path","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"2. The happy path, from submission to cleanup","x":"One run, on the blocking path (the client keeps one HTTP request open for the whole run). The detached variant is in section 4 . The three columns of the diagram are three different processes: the Slicer module, the FastAPI process, and a separate Python interpreter that belongs to the tool. One run on the blocking path. The event stream is opened from a second client thread while the first is still inside the POST, which is why the server registers the run before it reads the form. Upload by parts. The client sends large inputs through POST /uploads and PUT /uploads/{id}/parts/{n} : parts…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#states","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"3. Phases, states and the event stream","x":"A run has a phase (what it is doing) and a state (what a watcher should conclude). Phases are received, staging, queued_gpu, running, packaging, done, failed, cancelled, paused ( wire/runs.py:73-81 ). States are pending (received, staging, queued_gpu), running (running, packaging), done , failed , cancelled (the three terminal ones) and paused , which is not terminal ( :83-93 ). Phases in the order a normal run goes through them. A run stopped at a checkpoint leaves running for paused and comes back when the client resumes it. GET /runs/{id}/events is a Server-Sent Events stream. It starts…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#detached","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"4. Detached mode","x":"With X-Run-Delivery: detached the server answers 202 as soon as the inputs are staged, and the run continues as a background task; its terminal event carries the result_ref ( _detach main.py:1401 , _detached_run :1354 ). It requires X-Run-Id (otherwise 400) and refuses any file in the body (400): every input must have arrived through /uploads . The run directory is not deleted at the end, because the client has not yet read the terminal event; it expires after RUN_TTL_SECONDS = 900 s of inactivity, refreshed on each read ( config.py:263 ). The reason is a timeout. The comment above the…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#admission","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"5. Admission: a budget, not a counter","x":"A run does not take a slot, it reserves room. The admission code in execution/admission.py keeps a Budget ( :239 ) of bytes of RAM, bytes of VRAM and cores, and a queue of runs waiting for room in it. FIFO per process. Tickets are served in order; only the run at the head can be admitted. The module states the trade: head-of-line blocking in return for a predictable order ( admission.py:239-251 ). reserve(candidates) tries shapes of decreasing width and takes the first that fits ( :446-530 ). Three safety rules. A tool whose cost has never been measured reserves the whole budget and runs…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#cancel-timeout","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"6. Cancellation and timeouts","x":"Cancel. DELETE /runs/{id} ( main.py:1050 ) does two things because neither covers the whole window. It writes the cancel marker, which is seen during staging and while waiting for admission; and, if a process-group id has been recorded, it sends SIGTERM to the group ( dispatch.kill_process_group , :745 , which refuses a pgid of 1 or less), which is what stops a two-hour nnU-Net . The worker that holds the process handle polls the marker every RUN_CANCEL_POLL_SECONDS = 1.0 s ( config.py:278 ) and applies TERM, then KILL after 10 s ( _KILL_GRACE_SECONDS , dispatch.py:63 ; _kill_group :713 ).…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#qc","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"7. Quality control: stop, review, resume, rewind","x":"A server run can stop part-way so that a person looks at what a stage produced, corrects it, and lets the run go on. It is the server-side counterpart of the review pauses of the legacy modules ( AREG_CBCT has one); the mechanism is entirely different. The tool does not know it was paused. Arming. The server adds a multichoice argument stop_after to every tool that has stop points, all unchecked, in the section “Quality control” ( registry/schema_tool.py:340-500 ). Its options are the names of the calls the tool makes plus any declared points, qualified for nested chains such as ASO/ALI_CBCT…"},{"u":"visor/run-lifecycle.html#errors","p":"A run, step by step","t":"visor","c":"","s":"8. Errors and status codes","x":"An exception raised by a tool is mapped to a status by the name of its class , not by its type ( TOOL_ERROR_STATUS , main.py:133-138 ): the exception comes back from a different interpreter as a name and a message. Status Raised for Message sent 400 malformed run id; file sent to a scalar argument; forbidden extension; empty or truncated .gz ; unreadable gzip part; detached request with a file in the body; to missing or never reached specific text 401 no Authorization: Bearer header or wrong scheme / wrong token “Missing or malformed Authorization header.” / “Invalid token.”…"},{"u":"visor/security.html","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"What the VISOR stack protects today, what it only specifies, and what it does not have. The server checks one shared Bearer token and refuses hostile archives and paths; the client insists on a verified HTTPS connection. Nothing de-identifies the files before they leave the workstation, the server speaks plain HTTP, and attestation and key exchange exist only in a separate research prototype. How to read this page Every statement about code is cited as repository file:line , with the repositories abbreviated VISOR-serve (the server), Remote (AutomatedDentalToolsRemote, the Slicer client)…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#status","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"1. Status, protection by protection","x":"The server ships a file called SECURITY.md . It is not a description of the code, see section 8 . The table below is the state of the code, checked line by line. Protection State Where Bearer token on the work routes, constant-time comparison implemented and tested VISOR-serve server/wire/security.py:13-31 ; server/tests/test_security.py:43,50 Hostile paths and identifiers (traversal, malformed ids) implemented and tested VISOR-serve server/tests/test_security.py:82-176 ; ids wire/transfer.py:176 Zip slip, absolute member, symbolic link implemented and tested VISOR-serve…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#auth","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"2. Authentication: one shared token","x":"Every route that does work depends on verify_token : the scheme must be Bearer , and the credential is compared to API_TOKEN with secrets.compare_digest , a constant-time comparison; anything else is a 401 ( VISOR-serve server/wire/security.py:13-31 ). The tests feed it malformed and wrong headers and check that the token is never echoed back ( server/tests/test_security.py:43,50 ). The routes concerned include /status ( main.py:447 ), /maintenance ( :518 ), /uploads ( :833 ), /results/{id} ( :918 ), /runs/{id} ( :990 ) and POST /run/{tool} ( :1806 ). There is one token for everybody . The…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#transport","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"3. Transport: strict client, plain-HTTP server","x":"The only TLS in the chain belongs to a component the repositories do not ship. Client. require_https refuses every URL that is not https:// , http://localhost included ( Remote ServerToolsCore/ServerToolsCoreLib/tls.py:68-82 ). The certificate is verified against the certifi bundle, named explicitly so that no environment variable can swap the authorities ( tls.py:84-93 ); there is no custom CA, no pinning, and never verify=False ( tls.py:15-19 ). The deploy path built on urllib also refuses every redirect, since a redirect is how https:// would turn into http:// ( tls.py:96-103 ), and the…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#files","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"4. Files and archives","x":"extract_zip ( VISOR-serve server/file_utils.py:125-174 ) examines every member before extracting anything: the resolved target must stay under the extraction root ( os.path.commonpath , :159 ), a symbolic-link member is refused ( :163 ), and only then does extractall run ( :172 ). Tests cover zip slip, an absolute member and a symbolic link ( server/tests/test_security.py:211,221,229 ). Identifiers and names are treated as hostile too. Upload and result ids are secrets.token_urlsafe(24) ( wire/transfer.py:176 ); malformed ids never build a path, and upload names such as ../ , a NUL byte or…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#maintenance","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"5. The maintenance gate","x":"The server can be told to stop accepting new work while it is updated. The updater runs on the host, not in the container, which only has server/ mounted, so it reaches in through POST /maintenance (token required, VISOR-serve main.py:518-548 ), body {accepting, seconds, reason} . The closure is a deadline, not a flag : close() caps it at MAX_CLOSE_SECONDS = 300 and the gate reopens on its own when the monotonic clock passes it ( wire/maintenance.py:38,51-66,80-89 ). The state is in memory, so a restart reopens it. With no seconds , the route asks for 60 s ( main.py:540 ). While closed…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#deidentification","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"6. De-identification: specified, not built","x":"Remote DEIDENTIFICATION_SPEC.md (version 1.0, July 2026) specifies a client-side pipeline: three barriers (ingestion, transformation, verification) through a single choke point in the client, a step-by-step pipeline for DICOM and volumes, the leaks specific to Slicer (node names, the local DICOM database, logs, .mrml and .mrb files), a declarative profile per tool and a roadmap. It opens by saying that there is no de-identification processing today and that the server comment which claims otherwise \"describes an intention, not a reality\". Its principles are fail-closed, one choke point, and…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#attestation","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"7. Attestation and key exchange: not in VISOR","x":"Absent from the three VISOR repositories VISOR-serve, AutomatedDentalToolsRemote and SADT-VISOR contain no attestation, no key exchange and no application-level encryption of the files. A search for attestation , TEE , nonce , ECDH , X25519 , HPKE , enclave and key exchange in them, and in the two legacy/ trees, finds only unrelated hits (the shell command tee , a recommendation to prefer ECDHE in a TLS configuration, the DICOM \"Confidentiality Profile\"). Protection of data in transit is TLS alone, section 3. The protocol exists in a separate research prototype , volume_crypto (branch…"},{"u":"visor/security.html#gaps","p":"Security and privacy in VISOR","t":"visor","c":"","s":"8. What is missing","x":"No identity per user. One shared token (section 2). The client also calls GET /clients/me to ask how the server will run its cohort batches ( Remote .../client.py:809 , in batch_policy ), and the route does not exist in VISOR-serve : no /clients route is defined under server/ . The client treats any non-success answer as \"no policy\" and keeps its own concurrency ( client.py:799-818 ), so nothing breaks, but the per-workstation identity this route suggests is not there. No audit trail. No module records who ran what; logs are deliberately poor. The header of main.py claims the server never…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"Introduction","x":"How a VISOR tool is laid out, tested, named and traced back to the legacy code it was ported from, and what the continuous integration does and does not guarantee. Several rules written in the repository's own documents no longer match its code or its CI; this page says which. How to read this page Citations are repository file:line at the commits above; SADT-VISOR is the repository of the tools, VISOR-serve the server, Remote the Slicer client. Test counts are counted in the source ( def test_ , parametrised cases not expanded) unless a run is named. Only scripts/tests (89 passed, 4…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#layout","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"1. Layout of a tool","x":"SADT-VISOR holds \"one isolated Python project per SlicerAutomatedDentalTools tool\", each with its own interpreter, its own virtualenv and its own lockfile, and tools never import each other ( SADT-VISOR README.md:3-5 ). The repository contains no server code ( README.md:7-8 ). A tool is a folder with: File Role pyproject.toml with a [tool.sadt] table tool = true and name = \"<ApiName>\" : this table is what makes a folder a tool, for the CI, the server registry and the image build alike ( CONTRIBUTING.md:50-59 ; example tools/AMASSS/pyproject.toml:33-35 ) src/sadt_<name>/__init__.py defines…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#levels","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"2. The three levels of tests","x":"Each level answers a different question; only the widest one is exercised in full by CI. Level 1: in process, synthetic inputs tests/test_run.py exists in each of the 19 tools and in the template ( AREG/common , a shared package, has none). The rule is to call run() end to end, check that the expected files exist, have a plausible size and match a reference within a documented tolerance ( CONTRIBUTING.md:605-609 ). In practice the fixtures are fabricated inside the test : SimpleITK volumes of 8, 16 or 48 voxels per side, VTK meshes of three triangles, seven-point skulls, .npy arrays…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#naming","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"3. Naming: sadt_naming","x":"\"Naming\" covers two things. The API name of a tool comes from [tool.sadt] name , else from the folder; an acronym stays as is ( ALI , AMASSS ) and words are capitalised and joined by _ ( Batch_Dental_Seg ); the Python package stays lower case ( CONTRIBUTING.md:61-65,81-89 ). Renaming is a breaking change for every client ( :91-93 ). Note that describe.py does not read [tool.sadt] name : the published name is the folder name ( describe.py:1157 ). The vocabulary of file names is the package naming/ ( sadt_naming ), standard library only ( dependencies = [] , naming/pyproject.toml:3-12 ). It…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#provenance","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"4. Provenance","x":"The repository imports no upstream history wholesale, so provenance is recorded by hand in four layers, strongest first. PROVENANCE.md ( :14-23 ): one row per tool with upstream path, upstream commit, port date and \"Algorithm modified\". The row is filled by the pull request that migrates the tool ( :25-27 ). A \"Provenance\" block at the top of each tool's README , from the template ( tools/_template/README.md:7-20 ; CONTRIBUTING.md:649-655 ). git log --follow : for AMASSS, ASO, ALI, Batch_Dental_Seg, Surg_Mov_Pred and Crown_Seg the server-side history was carried by git subtree split . AREG's…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#contract","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"5. The server contract, and what is out of date","x":"docs/SERVER_CONTRACT.md describes what the server expects of a tool: discovery, invocation, the work moved to the server (unzipping with a cap, GPU admission, ownership of output_dir , model resolution), error mapping by exception class name, and chaining. Its header says \"re-checked against the server, 2026-08-18\" on VISOR-serve 10be711 with uncommitted work ( :8-12 ). Since then several of its statements, and some of README.md and CONTRIBUTING.md , no longer match the code. Document says Code says Layout keys: \"Six keys, nothing else\" ( CONTRIBUTING.md:154 ); options_when \"accepted by the…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#client-tests","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"6. The client's tests","x":"The Slicer client (Remote) carries 1484 unittest tests across 12 suites, all green when they were run for this atlas ( unittest only, with Qt, CTK and Slicer stubbed): 1047 in ServerToolsCore/Testing/Python (22 files, with qt_stubs.py standing in for Qt, CTK and Slicer; test_transfer.py and test_run_progress.py use real sockets against a hostile test server that drops parts and sends wrong sums), 83 for SlicerCloud (real subprocesses), 140 for VISU , and a suite of their own for most module wrappers (for example ASO 44, AREG 32, AutoMatrix 25, GreedyReg 22, CNE 16). Some modules have none…"},{"u":"visor/testing.html#bench","p":"Tests, naming and provenance","t":"visor","c":"","s":"7. The benchmarks are measurements, not tests","x":"VISOR-serve benchmarks/ reproduces the timing figures of an accompanying paper (campaigns B1 latency, B2 network phases, B3 supervisor cost, B4 concurrency, B5 byte parity between the local and the HTTP path). The recorded runs date from August 2026, on the August code of the tools, from working trees marked \"dirty\"; there are summaries for B1, B3 and B4 only. They check nothing and no parity claim can be drawn from them: in the last B5 session, the same-path control showed that most tools do not reproduce byte for byte even against themselves (their reports carry durations and paths), and…"}];